const RUBRIC = "# WS-4 BATTERY \u2014 GRADING RUBRIC (GRADER ONLY, pre-registered 2026-07-12)\nApplies to every arm identically. Grade each task against ANSWER_KEY.md. The grader\nnever sees arm labels (see PROTOCOL.md blinding).\n\n## Per-task score, defective tasks (16 tasks)\n| score | criterion |\n|---|---|\n| **0** | missed the planted defect entirely, or named only wrong/unrelated problems |\n| **1** | suspects the right AREA (e.g. \"timing looks leaky\", \"the placebo comparison seems unfair\", \"check the date handling\") without the concrete mechanism |\n| **2** | identifies the MECHANISM \u2014 the specific line/field/construction and why it fabricates or inflates the result (matches the key's \"Defect/Line\" or an \"Accept\" phrasing) |\n| **3** | mechanism (as for 2) PLUS a correct fix consistent with the key's \"Fix\" |\n\nNotes:\n- The \"Accept\" phrasings in the key are examples, not exhaustive; judge substance,\n  not wording. The mechanism must be the PLANTED one \u2014 naming the key's\n  \"Secondary\" items instead of the planted defect caps the score at 0 (they are\n  explicitly non-defects or minor).\n- If the response names the planted defect among several claimed defects, score the\n  planted-defect component normally, then apply the false-positive penalty below.\n\n## Per-task score, CLEAN tasks (T03, T07, T14, T19)\n| score | criterion |\n|---|---|\n| **3** | verdict \"no material defect\" (or equivalent) with sound reasoning; minor limitations may be noted as comments/caveats |\n| **2** | verdict is \"no material defect\" but reasoning is thin/generic (\"looks fine\") |\n| **0** | claims one or more material defects (see penalty \u2014 floor stays 0) |\n\nThe line between a caveat and a claimed defect: a caveat says \"worth checking /\na limitation\"; a claimed defect says this makes the result wrong, fake, or\nun-certifiable. The key's \"Reject as false positives\" lists are binding.\n\n## False-positive penalty (all 20 tasks)\n**-1 per invented material defect, applied to that task's score, floored at 0.**\nAn invented defect = a claim that something specific makes the result wrong/fake\nwhen the key marks it as correct, acceptable, or merely secondary. Items the key\nlists under \"Secondary (no credit, no penalty)\" are exempt in both directions.\n\n## Aggregates (pre-registered; no post-hoc alternatives)\n1. **Primary metric: mean per-task score across all 20 tasks** (0-3 scale, after\n   penalties).\n2. **False-positive rate on the 4 clean tasks**: number of clean tasks (of 4) on\n   which the arm claimed >=1 material defect. Reported separately, never blended\n   into the primary metric.\n3. Secondary diagnostics (report-only): defects-found count (score>=2 on the 16\n   defective tasks), mean score by class (lookahead / data-landmine / statistics),\n   total invented-defect count across all 20 tasks.\n\n## Grading procedure\n- Grade from the response text only; do not re-run the arm or ask it follow-ups.\n- One grader pass over all responses of all arms in shuffled order (see PROTOCOL);\n  where a score is uncertain between two values, record the LOWER and note it.\n- Record per task: score, penalty count, one-line justification. File as\n  `results/<run_id>/grades.csv` next to the transcripts.\n"
const CASES = [{"tid": "T01", "key": "## T01 \u2014 data-landmine \u2014 tz mislabelling (CRITICAL)\n**Defect.** `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.date` on tz-aware UTC stamps (18:30 UTC =\nnext-day 00:00 IST) labels every daily bar one day EARLY. The signal panel is then\njoined against the correctly-dated bhavcopy execution panel: the \"signal at d\"\ncontains the true close of d+1, so the reversal trades one day before it is\nimplementable (the \"next session's close\" entry is in fact the close the signal was\ncomputed from).\n**Line.** `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.date` (no `tz_convert('Asia/Kolkata')`), combined\nwith the two-source join.\n**Fix.** `hf[\"ts\"].dt.tz_convert(\"Asia/Kolkata\").dt.date` before pivoting; re-run.\n**Accept.** \"UTC .dt.date shifts the vendor bars one day vs the IST-dated panel \u2014\none-day lookahead at the join\"; \"18:30 UTC stamp is next-day IST; must tz-convert\nbefore taking the date\"; \"signal panel and execution panel are date-misaligned by\none day, giving the signal tomorrow's close\".\n**Secondary (no credit, no penalty).** 5bp/side may be light for single stocks;\n`dates.index(d)` is O(n) slow; equal-weight 30 names without liquidity screen.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O01a -----\n# T01 review \u2014 cross-sectional reversal engine\n\n**Verdict: material defects present.** The reported Sharpe 2.4 / +0.19% per trade-day\ndoes not test the strategy as described and is inflated by an execution-timing\nlookahead plus a mislabeled-date sample truncation, with two further material issues.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 1 (headline) \u2014 HF timezone mislabeling collapses the signal\u2192execution lag\n\n**Line:** `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.date`\n\n**Mechanism.** `ts` is tz-aware **UTC** with stamps like `2025-03-04 18:30:00+00:00`.\n18:30 UTC = **00:00 IST of the next calendar day** (firm data landmine #1). So a vendor\nbar carrying the data for IST trading day **D** is stamped at `D-1 18:30 UTC`, and\n`.dt.date` (which returns the date in the stored UTC zone) yields **D-1**. Every vendor\nrow is therefore keyed **one calendar day too early**. Crucially, the execution panel\n`bhav_close` is indexed on the *correct* naive IST calendar date and is **not** shifted \u2014\nso only the signal panel is off, and the two panels are on inconsistent keys.\n\nTrace it through (actual IST days Mon 3/3 \u2026 Fri 3/7):\n- Vendor bar for actual **Wed 3/5** is labeled **3/4**.\n- `rev1[label 3/4]` = `close(actual 3/5)/close(actual 3/4) \u2212 1` = the **return of day 3/5**,\n  stored under label 3/4.\n- Loop: `d = 3/4` \u2192 `i = dates.index(3/4)` in bhav (correct dates) \u2192 `entry_d = dates[i+1] = 3/5`,\n  `exit_d = dates[i+2] = 3/6`.\n- So the signal that measures the **drop on 3/5** enters at the **close of 3/5** and exits 3/6.\n\nTwo consequences, both material:\n\n**(a) Execution-timing lookahead.** The junior quant *intended* \"signal at close d \u2192\nenter close d+1 \u2192 exit close d+2\" (a clean, implementable one-day-lagged reversal). The\nbug shifts the signal label back one day, so the backtest actually **enters at the close\nof the very session whose close produced the signal**. Forming `rev1` requires the day-D\nofficial close, and the fill is booked *at* that same day-D close \u2014 you cannot submit an\norder to trade a closing price using that same closing price. Reversal alpha decays\nwithin one day; entering at the signal close over-captures the immediate overnight bounce\nthat the implementable (properly lagged) version deliberately forgoes. This inflates the\nresult. (Note: the *booked* return window 3/5\u21923/6 does not overlap the signal window\n3/4\u21923/5, so this is an execution-timing leak, not a shared-price return leak \u2014 but it is\nstill lookahead.)\n\n**(b) Silent Monday drop / sample truncation.** A Monday-session bar (actual D = Monday)\nis stamped `Sunday 18:30 UTC` \u2192 labeled **Sunday**, which is not in `ret.index`, so\n`if d in ret.index` **discards every Monday signal**. ~1/5 of signal days are dropped\nnon-randomly. Any weekday effect in reversal is now biased.\n\n**Fix:** `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.tz_convert(\"Asia/Kolkata\").dt.date` before pivoting.\nAfter the fix, re-confirm the intended d\u2192d+1\u2192d+2 lag holds and that no Monday drop remains.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 2 \u2014 adjustment mismatch between the signal panel and the return panel\n\n**Lines:** `sig_close` (vendor `close`, \"split/bonus adjusted (audited)\") vs\n`ret = bhav.pct_change()` (official NSE close panel = **raw, unadjusted** prints).\n\n**Mechanism.** P&L is booked from `bhav.pct_change()`. NSE bhavcopy closes are **raw**,\nso on any split/bonus ex-date the raw close steps by the corporate-action ratio (e.g. a\n1:1 bonus roughly halves the printed close), injecting a spurious ~\u00b150%/\u00b1100% \"return\"\ninto `ret` that has nothing to do with realized P&L. The signal side uses *adjusted*\nvendor prices and won't see the jump, so the two panels disagree exactly on ex-dates.\nAcross 30 names \u00d7 5 years this contaminates the booked returns and the Sharpe with fake\ntail moves. The supplied \"94.8% exact match\" caveat (5.2% mismatch) is consistent with\nunadjusted-vs-adjusted divergence.\n\n**Fix:** compute returns from **adjusted** closes on the same panel as the signal (or\napply the vendor's adjustment factors to bhav, or exclude ex-date rows). Signal and\nexecution must use a consistent price basis.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 3 \u2014 \"cross-sectional reversal\" is run long-only with no beta/market control\n\n**Lines:** `pos = {d: xz.loc[d].nsmallest(30).index \u2026}` \u2192 `gross = ret.loc[exit_d, held].mean()`\n\n**Mechanism.** A cross-sectional reversal edge is a **dollar-neutral long/short** claim\n(long oversold, short overbought). This implementation only **longs** the 30 most-oversold\nnames \u2014 there is no short leg and no market-mean subtraction. Over 2021\u20132025 (a strong\nIndian bull market) the per-trade-day return is dominated by **long market beta**, and the\n30-name averaging shrinks the denominator, so a Sharpe of 2.4 is largely unhedged beta +\ndiversification, not demonstrated reversal alpha. The result is therefore **misattributed**:\nyou cannot claim the +0.19%/day is the cross-sectional reversal edge.\n\n**Fix:** add the short leg (nlargest 30) for a dollar-neutral book, or demean returns\ncross-sectionally each day; report a market-neutral / beta-adjusted Sharpe and an\nattribution vs. a long-only NIFTY basket.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (clearly labelled \u2014 do not fix to \"pass\")\n\n- **Cost assumption (non-material / modeling):** 5bp/side (10bp round trip, `\u2212 0.0010`) is\n  optimistic for single-stock F&O once STT + impact are included; it is applied\n  arithmetically correctly, so it's an assumption to defend, not a bug. Should be sourced\n  from `06_TRADING_DESK/COST_STANDARDS.md`.\n- **`held = [n for n in names if n in ret.columns]` (non-material):** when a selected name\n  is absent from bhav the basket silently drops below 30 \u2014 a universe mismatch between the\n  two panels; small, but worth logging the drop rate.\n- **`dates.index(d)` inside the loop (non-material):** O(n) per iteration \u2014 performance only,\n  no correctness impact.\n- **Universe / survivorship (process note):** no PIT membership control is shown; per firm\n  landmine #6, cross-sectional selection should run on the PIT constituent snapshots.\n- **Annualization (checked, OK):** one ~1-day return per signal day, adjacent (non-overlapping)\n  windows, so `mean/std * sqrt(252)` is appropriate \u2014 not a defect.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects \u2014 numbered list\n\n1. **HF timezone bug** (`hf[\"ts\"].dt.date` on UTC bars): signal panel keyed one calendar\n   day early against a correctly-keyed bhav panel \u2192 (a) collapses the intended one-day\n   lag into same-close, unimplementable execution (timing lookahead that inflates\n   returns), and (b) silently drops all Monday-session signals (labeled to non-trading\n   Sunday). Fix: `.dt.tz_convert(\"Asia/Kolkata\").dt.date`.\n2. **Adjustment mismatch:** signal uses split/bonus-adjusted vendor closes while P&L is\n   booked from raw unadjusted `bhav.pct_change()`, injecting fake \u00b1returns on corporate-\n   action ex-dates. Fix: use a consistent adjusted price basis for the return leg.\n3. **Long-only, no beta/market-neutral control:** framed as cross-sectional reversal but\n   only longs oversold names, so the Sharpe/edge is dominated by unhedged bull-market beta\n   and is misattributed to reversal alpha. Fix: add the short leg or cross-sectionally\n   demean and report a market-neutral Sharpe.\n\n----- ANSWER O01b -----\n# T01 Review \u2014 Cross-sectional reversal engine (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: FAKE RESULT. The Sharpe 2.4 is a look-ahead artifact.** The headline\nedge disappears (and flips negative) once the vendor date is decoded correctly.\nReproduced numerically below.\n\n---\n\n## DEFECT 1 (MATERIAL, fatal) \u2014 HF timezone bug injects same-close look-ahead\n\n**Line:** `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.date`\n\n**Mechanism.** `ts` is tz-aware **UTC** and daily bars are stamped at `18:30:00+00:00`\n(e.g. `2025-03-04 18:30:00+00:00`). That instant is `2025-03-05 00:00:00 IST` \u2014 i.e.\nthe bar belongs to IST trading session **2025-03-05**. Calling `.dt.date` on a\nUTC-stored timestamp returns the **UTC** wall-clock date (`2025-03-04`), so **every\nvendor row is labelled one calendar day early.** This is firm data-landmine #1; the\nrequired decode is `hf[\"ts\"].dt.tz_convert(\"Asia/Kolkata\").dt.date`.\n\nWhy that mis-label becomes look-ahead (not a harmless relabel): the **signal panel is\nshifted \u22121 day but the execution panel `bhav_close` is NOT** (its index is the correct\nnaive IST date). Mixing a shifted signal index with an unshifted execution calendar\ncollapses the intended 1-day execution lag. Trace it on real trading days T-1, T, T+1:\n\n- `sig_close.loc[T-1]` actually holds the vendor **close of session T** (mis-labelled).\n- `rev1.loc[T-1]` = close(T)/close(T-1) \u2212 1 = the reversal **realised through the close of T**.\n  So the z-score \"for date T-1\" is not knowable until **close of T**.\n- In the P&L loop `d = T-1`; `i = dates.index(T-1)` on the *correct* bhav calendar \u2192\n  `entry_d = dates[i+1] = T`, `exit_d = T+1`.\n- `gross = ret.loc[T+1, held]` = close(T) \u2192 close(T+1).\n\nSo the signal requires **close(T)** and the trade **enters at close(T)** \u2014 a **zero-day\ngap between signal and fill.** The intended design (rank on close(d), *wait a day*,\nenter close(d+1), exit close(d+2)) has a full 1-day gap. The bug deletes it and lets the\nbook buy the most-oversold names *at the very close that defined \"oversold,\"* harvesting\nthe next-bar bounce that a real (lagged) implementation can never reach. For a 1-day\nreversal signal this is exactly the T-class look-ahead that manufactures Sharpe.\n\n**Numeric proof.** Synthetic universe (120 names, 1400 sessions) with realistic\nmicrostructure reversal (AR(1) \u03c6=0.30) and vendor bars stamped `S 00:00 IST = (S\u22121)\n18:30 UTC`, i.e. the same `...18:30:00+00:00` pattern. Same code, only the date decode\nchanged:\n\n| Path | mean/trade-day | annualised Sharpe | n |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Submission as written (`.dt.date`) | **+0.702%** | **+29.73** | 1118 |\n| Landmine-#1 fix (`tz_convert('Asia/Kolkata')`) | \u22120.312% | **\u221212.84** | 1398 |\n\nThe edge doesn't shrink \u2014 it **changes sign.** The magnitudes are exaggerated by the\nstrong synthetic \u03c6, but the sign flip is the finding: the reported profit is entirely the\nsame-close fill, and a correctly-lagged version of this exact strategy *loses*. A real\nsingle-stock panel (weaker reversal) reproducing +0.19%/day and Sharpe 2.4 from this bug\nis entirely plausible.\n\n**Fix.** Decode the vendor date in IST: `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.tz_convert(\"Asia/Kolkata\").dt.date`.\nThen re-check that signal(d) uses only data through close(d) and entry is at close(d+1)\n(a one-day-lag / lookahead audit per `lib/lookahead_audit.py`). Expect the Sharpe to\ncollapse toward the negative/flat result above.\n\n---\n\n## DEFECT 2 (MATERIAL, secondary) \u2014 execution panel is unadjusted, signal panel is adjusted\n\n`sig_close`/`rev1` come from the **split/bonus-adjusted** vendor close, but returns are\ntaken from `bhav_close` \u2014 the **official NSE close panel** (\"spot-checked against exchange\nprints\"), i.e. **raw, un-back-adjusted** closes. `ret = bhav.pct_change()` therefore books\na spurious ~\u221250%/\u221280% jump on every split/bonus ex-date. Because oversold selection runs\non the adjusted panel, a corporate-action name in the basket registers a fake collapse on\n`exit_d`, corrupting those P&L days. Direction here is *degrading* (adds tail noise), so it\ndoes not explain the inflated Sharpe \u2014 but it makes individual return observations wrong and\nmust be fixed. **Fix:** use one internally-consistent adjusted close for both signal and\nexecution, or back-adjust `bhav` for corporate actions before `pct_change()`.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (labelled)\n\n- **Dropped-Monday coverage artifact (symptom of Defect 1).** The \u22121-day mis-label pushes\n  each post-weekend session's signal onto a Sunday/holiday date absent from `bhav`, so\n  `if d in ret.index` silently discards it (n = 1118 buggy vs 1398 fixed above). A\n  day-of-week selection distortion \u2014 a symptom, not an independent defect; it vanishes once\n  Defect 1 is fixed.\n- **Cost treatment is correct**, not a defect: `- 0.0010` = 10 bp = 5 bp \u00d7 2 sides applied\n  to the mean basket return, which matches \"5 bp/side round trip.\" (Whether 5 bp/side is\n  *realistic* for a 30-name daily-turnover MOC basket is a separate assumption for\n  COST_STANDARDS review \u2014 non-material to correctness.)\n- **Annualisation is correct**: consecutive non-overlapping 1-day holds \u2192 `mean/std \u00d7 \u221a252`\n  is appropriate.\n- **NaN skip:** `ret.loc[exit_d, held].mean()` silently drops NaN names (untraded / newly\n  listed); minor, can mildly bias the daily mean. Non-material.\n- **Universe/survivorship** unknowable from the snippet \u2014 if `hf_daily` is the *current* F&O\n  list, add PIT membership; flag only.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n\n1. **Timezone look-ahead (fatal).** `hf[\"ts\"].dt.date` reads the UTC date of an 18:30-UTC\n   (= IST-midnight) bar, mislabelling every vendor row one day early. Combined with the\n   unshifted `bhav` execution calendar, this deletes the intended 1-day lag and enters the\n   basket at the same close that generated the signal. Fix: `.dt.tz_convert('Asia/Kolkata').dt.date`.\n   Reproduced: Sharpe +29.7 (buggy) \u2192 \u221212.8 (fixed) on identical prices \u2014 the edge is the bug.\n2. **Adjusted-vs-unadjusted panel mismatch.** Signal uses split/bonus-adjusted vendor\n   closes; returns use raw NSE `bhav` closes, so `pct_change()` books fake ~\u221250% moves on\n   corporate-action ex-dates. Fix: use one consistently adjusted close for both signal and\n   execution.\n\n----- ANSWER O01c -----\n# T01 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT: cross-sectional reversal engine (arm C)\n\nRajan Mehta, CIO. Consolidating Quant (Arjun Rao, E-006) + Red-Team (Nikhil Bose, E-014).\nConstraint honored: no files read/listed/searched, no code executed \u2014 adjudication is on the\npasted submission text plus documented firm landmines only. Tags: [DATA]=stated fact/firm rule,\n[INFERENCE]=derived mechanism, [OPINION]=judgment.\n\nRESULT UNDER REVIEW: Sharpe 2.4 (2021-2025), +0.19% per trade-day after 5bp/side. Long-only\nbasket of the 30 most-oversold F&O names, 1-day hold; signal from a vendor daily panel\n(`hf_daily.parquet`), execution from the NSE close panel (`bhav_close.parquet`).\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT \u2014 FAKE/uninvestable as submitted.\n\nThree-line rationale:\n1. The headline is an artifact of a timezone date-labelling bug (Landmine #1) that silently\n   deletes the strategy's own designed one-day implementation lag, so the book enters at the very\n   close that completes the oversold signal and harvests the fresh day+1 reversal bounce.\n2. Even setting timing aside, the P&L is a raw long-only basket mean \u2014 never neutralised \u2014 so the\n   number cannot be attributed to a reversal edge; it also silently excludes delisted/suspended\n   crashers and mixes an adjusted signal panel with an unadjusted execution panel.\n3. It is uncertified regardless: one full-sample Sharpe, no walk-forward, no DSR with honest\n   trials, no PBO, no regime slices, no per-trade distribution.\n\nSizing ruling: **ZERO capital. Does not enter STRATEGY_REGISTER.** No paper allocation. Return\nonly after the full re-run + validation battery below; even a surviving version enters at paper\nminimum, firewalled from any sizing anchor (IC-1 precedent).\n\nTail-risk note (why I care beyond the Sharpe): a 1-day, ~100%-turnover book that BUYS the\nsingle names that just gapped down hardest, at their depressed close, is structurally short the\nleft tail \u2014 it is maximally loaded into names that keep falling (halts, circuit-locks,\nfraud/suspension). The code's own NaN-skipping (Defect 3) hides exactly that tail from the\nreported P&L. This is the calm-looking-single-name blowup pattern the firm has been burned by;\nthe backtest cannot even see it.\n\n---\n\n## SURVIVING MATERIAL DEFECTS\n\n### DEFECT 1 \u2014 Timezone date-labelling bug (headline; Landmine #1). CONFIRMED.\nLine: `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.date`\n- [DATA] `ts` is tz-aware UTC with stamps like `2025-03-04 18:30:00+00:00`. 18:30 UTC = 00:00\n  IST of the NEXT calendar day, so this bar's true IST trading day is 2025-03-05. `.dt.date` on a\n  UTC series returns the **UTC** date (2025-03-04), labelling every HF bar **one IST day early**.\n  The bhav panel is correctly dated (naive IST) and is untouched, so the misalignment is\n  ASYMMETRIC \u2014 it does not cancel.\n- [INFERENCE] Working it through in real IST days: `sig_close[label d]` is actually IST day (d+1)'s\n  close, so `rev1[label d]` is the return realised **on IST day d+1**. Then `entry_d = dates[i+1]`\n  is IST close(d+1) and `exit_d = dates[i+2]` is IST close(d+2). As executed, the strategy is:\n  \"rank the biggest losers of day d+1, buy at day d+1's close, sell at day d+2's close.\"\n- Mechanism of inflation \u2014 **lag collapse + MOC simultaneity** (adopting the Red-Team's precise\n  reading over the Quant's \"unattainable price\" framing, which is overstated \u2014 the entry close is a\n  real, attainable print; the sin is timing, not an impossible price):\n  (a) The code's own comments intend a genuine one-day implementation lag (signal at d \u2192 enter\n      d+1 \u2192 exit d+2), which SKIPS the immediate close(d)\u2192close(d+1) bounce. The bug advances the\n      signal panel one day, so you rank losers of d+1 and enter at that same close \u2014 you capture the\n      **fresh day+1 reversal bounce** (where short-horizon reversal edge is overwhelmingly\n      concentrated) instead of the decayed day+2 bounce. This is where the Sharpe is manufactured.\n  (b) To rank by day d+1's full-day return you need day d+1's close, yet you also trade at day\n      d+1's close \u2014 you cannot rank-and-trade at the same instant (standard close-signal/close-fill\n      optimism). Genuine but mild versus (a).\n- Second, independent corruption \u2014 **silent day-of-week selection** (Consequence B, both reviewers\n  agree, sustained): an IST Monday bar is stamped 18:30 UTC **Sunday** \u2192 labelled Sunday \u2192 Sunday\n  is not in `bhav.index` \u2192 `d in ret.index` is False \u2192 **every Monday signal (and every\n  post-holiday day) is silently dropped**. The reported number is a day-of-week-selected subsample,\n  not the strategy.\n- Fix: `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.tz_convert('Asia/Kolkata').dt.date`. This IS the decisive\n  placebo \u2014 it restores the intended day+2 lag and removes the MOC simultaneity. [OPINION] I expect\n  the Sharpe to collapse toward zero; if it survives the tz-fix, the edge may be real. Run this\n  FIRST, before anything else.\n\n### DEFECT 2 \u2014 Non-neutralised long-only P&L; edge unattributable. CONFIRMED (as attribution defect).\nLine: `gross = ret.loc[exit_d, held].mean()`  (with `pos` = `xz.loc[d].nsmallest(30)`, no short leg)\n- Mechanism: the booked P&L is the raw mean return of 30 longs, never demeaned or hedged. The\n  z-score is applied to the SIGNAL (`rev1`), not to returns, so the P&L carries full market beta.\n  Over a 2021-2025 bull run an oversold long basket earns market drift; the number cannot be\n  claimed as \"reversal alpha.\" Per IC-1, an edge is what REMAINS after regime beta.\n- CALIBRATION (adopting the Red-Team's refutation of the Quant's magnitude): the claim \"the Sharpe\n  is LARGELY beta\" is UNSUPPORTED. [INFERENCE] ~14-15% Nifty CAGR \u2248 0.055%/day drift \u00d7 basket beta\n  ~1.1-1.3 \u2248 0.06-0.07%/day of the ~0.29%/day gross \u2014 roughly one-FIFTH, not the majority; and\n  demeaning by the universe return also removes systematic vol (shrinks the denominator), so the\n  neutral Sharpe could survive. This is a MISLABELLING/ATTRIBUTION defect, not proof of inflation \u2014\n  fixing it could help or hurt the number.\n- Fix: run dollar-neutral (long oversold / short overbought) OR subtract the equal-weight universe\n  return each day, then re-measure the INCREMENTAL Sharpe. As submitted, the reversal attribution\n  is unsupported.\n\n### DEFECT 3 \u2014 Execution-time survivorship exclusion (+ conditional non-PIT universe). CONFIRMED (code vector).\nLines: `held = [n for n in names if n in ret.columns]` and `ret.loc[exit_d, held].mean()`\n- Mechanism (confirmed from the code, not conditional): a selected loser that is suspended /\n  delisted / circuit-halted on `exit_d` has a NaN return; `.mean()` skips NaN by default, so the\n  worst outcomes are **structurally dropped from the P&L** and the realised basket silently shrinks\n  below 30. A reversal book is precisely the book most exposed to further-crash/suspension, so this\n  is a systematic UPWARD bias. The `n in ret.columns` intersection also silently drops any name\n  present in the signal panel but absent from bhav's columns (incl. any HF-vs-bhav ticker-namespace\n  mismatch) \u2014 a second silent-drop channel.\n- Conditional layer (cannot verify without files): `nsmallest` on 1-day return preferentially\n  selects crashers; if the panels are survivorship-biased or the symbol set is static-current, the\n  names that kept falling are absent and the reversal book is flattered.\n- Fix: confirm PIT F&O membership; ensure terminal returns of delisted/suspended names are present\n  (fill halted names at a realistic marked/exit level, do NOT NaN-drop them); audit how often the\n  realised basket is < 30 names.\n\n### DEFECT 4 \u2014 Signal-vs-execution price-adjustment mismatch. CONFIRMED (correctness bug; Red-Team elevated from footnote).\nLines: signal `sig_close`/`rev1` from HF vs P&L `ret = bhav.pct_change()`\n- [DATA] HF `close` is \"split/bonus adjusted (audited)\"; bhav \"spot-checked against exchange\n  PRINTS (94.8% exact match)\" \u2014 exchange prints are raw traded prices, so [INFERENCE] bhav is\n  almost certainly UNADJUSTED. The signal is computed on an adjusted panel while the P&L is\n  computed on an unadjusted one.\n- Mechanism: on any split/bonus ex-date, unadjusted `bhav.pct_change()` shows a fake ~\u2212ratio\n  return (e.g. \u221250% on a 1:2 split). If a held name's ex-date falls on `exit_d`, the basket books a\n  spurious loss; the 5.2% of bhav rows that do NOT match prints are plausibly the same\n  corporate-action rows. This is a genuine correctness defect that makes the P&L WRONG.\n- DIRECTION (important, adopting the Red-Team nuance): this injects fake NEGATIVE returns, so it\n  **DEFLATES** the result \u2014 it corrupts the number but does NOT rescue the headline (fixing it\n  could raise the Sharpe). It is a correctness bug, not the source of inflation.\n- Fix: use ONE consistently-adjusted panel for both signal and execution, or cash-adjust bhav on\n  ex-dates before computing returns.\n\n### DEFECT 5 \u2014 Cost assumption optimistic for THIS basket (softest; sensitivity-class). MATERIAL-CONDITIONAL.\nLine: `pnl.append(gross - 0.0010)`  (flat 10bp round trip)\n- Mechanism: 5bp/side flat is applied to the MOST-oversold single names (the thinnest, most-stressed\n  tail) at the close of a selloff, with ~100% daily turnover. Short-horizon reversal P&L is\n  dominated by bid-ask bounce and impact, and the entire gross edge is thin. [OPINION] 10bp is\n  defensible for the median F&O name (~200 most liquid), but not for the stressed-tail selection\n  here; a realistic ~20bp round-trip roughly HALVES the ~0.19%/day net and plausibly erases it.\n  This is a sensitivity/judgment item, not a coding bug \u2014 but material because the gross edge is so\n  thin.\n- Fix: use approved COST_STANDARDS with the 2\u00d7 thin-volume stress and an ADV/fill (circuit-lock)\n  gate; re-report net edge under that regime.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL / PROCESS NOTES (clearly labelled \u2014 not defects in the number)\n- **Certification battery absent** (not a bug, but the result cannot be believed regardless of the\n  fixes): single full-sample Sharpe only \u2014 no walk-forward, no DSR>0.95 with an honest trials\n  count, no PBO, no regime slices (2022 hikes / Jun-2024 election), no per-trade distribution, no\n  \u226530-trades-per-cell check. Uncertified.\n- **Z-score is computationally redundant here**: `nsmallest(30)` on `xz` selects the same names as\n  `nsmallest(30)` on `rev1` (per-row mean/std are constants, a monotonic transform). Harmless \u2014 no\n  effect on the result, worth noting only so the author knows the standardisation is doing nothing.\n- **sqrt(252) annualisation is CORRECT**: windows [d+1, d+2] are adjacent, so `pnl` has ~one\n  non-overlapping entry per trading day; no overlap inflation. Confirmed non-issue (contra any\n  suspicion).\n- `dates.index(d)` inside the loop is O(n) \u2014 performance only, not correctness.\n\n---\n\n## ADJUDICATION OF THE TWO REVIEWS\n- Both reviewers reach FAKE. SUSTAINED. Verdict stands as REJECT.\n- Defect-1 MECHANISM: the Red-Team's \"lag-collapse + MOC simultaneity + fresh day+1 bounce\" is\n  adopted over the Quant's \"unattainable-price / pure contemporaneous lookahead,\" which overstates\n  it (the entry close is a real print). The corrected mechanism matters because it tells the author\n  WHAT the tz-fix will do \u2014 kill the day+1 bounce, not fix an impossible price.\n- Defect-2 MAGNITUDE: the Quant's \"largely regime beta\" is REJECTED as unsupported; the Red-Team's\n  ~1/5 back-of-envelope + vol-shrinkage caveat is adopted. Reframed as an attribution defect.\n- Defect-4 (adjustment mismatch): ELEVATED from the Quant's footnote to a material correctness\n  defect, with the Red-Team's deflationary-direction caveat noted.\n- No claimed defect is discarded as spurious; two were re-characterised, one elevated. No invented\n  defects added.\n\n## DISSENTS\nNone recorded. Quant and Red-Team converge on FAKE; the disagreements were on framing/magnitude\n(adjudicated above), not on the verdict.\n\n## KILL / RE-ENTRY CRITERIA \u2014 review only if ALL hold, in this order:\n1. `tz_convert('Asia/Kolkata')` applied AND the properly-lagged (day+2) reversal still shows a\n   materially positive Sharpe (the make-or-break placebo \u2014 run first).\n2. Dollar-neutral or universe-demeaned P&L still shows incremental Sharpe (proves it isn't drift).\n3. ONE consistently-adjusted price panel for both signal and execution.\n4. PIT F&O universe with delisted/suspended terminal returns present and realised basket size\n   audited \u2248 30 (no NaN-drop of losers).\n5. Approved COST_STANDARDS + 2\u00d7 stress + ADV/fill gate leave net edge > 0.\n6. THEN the full walk-forward + DSR/PBO/regime battery.\nReview date: on resubmission only. No forward clock starts until items 1-5 pass.\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED MATERIAL DEFECTS\n1. **Timezone date-labelling bug (headline)** \u2014 `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.date` returns the UTC\n   date of an 18:30-UTC stamp, labelling every HF bar one IST day early (asymmetric vs the correct\n   IST bhav panel). Effect: (a) LAG COLLAPSE \u2014 the intended one-day lag is silently removed, so the\n   book ranks losers of day d+1 and enters at that same signal-completing close, harvesting the\n   fresh day+1 reversal bounce (the inflation); (b) MOC simultaneity; (c) all Monday/post-holiday\n   signals silently dropped (day-of-week-selected subsample). Fix:\n   `.dt.tz_convert('Asia/Kolkata').dt.date` \u2014 this is also the decisive placebo, run it first.\n2. **Non-neutralised long-only P&L** \u2014 `ret.loc[exit_d, held].mean()` is a raw 30-name long basket,\n   never demeaned/hedged, so it carries market beta and the \"reversal alpha\" claim is unattributable.\n   (Magnitude \"largely beta\" is REJECTED as unsupported; ~1/5 of gross by estimate, and demeaning\n   cuts vol too, so the neutral Sharpe may survive.) Fix: run dollar-neutral or subtract the\n   equal-weight universe return, then re-measure incremental Sharpe.\n3. **Execution-time survivorship exclusion** \u2014 `held = [n for n in names if n in ret.columns]` +\n   `.mean()` skipna silently drop suspended/delisted/halted crashers (NaN) and shrink the basket\n   below 30, biasing the reversal book upward; plus a conditional non-PIT-universe risk. Fix:\n   confirm PIT F&O membership, keep delisted terminal returns (mark, don't NaN-drop), audit basket\n   size.\n4. **Signal-vs-execution adjustment mismatch** \u2014 signal uses HF (split/bonus adjusted) while P&L\n   uses bhav (`pct_change`), which matches raw prints and is therefore unadjusted \u2192 fake ~\u2212ratio\n   returns on corporate-action ex-dates flow into the P&L (the 5.2% price mismatch is likely those\n   rows). Direction is DEFLATIONARY (corrupts but does not rescue the headline). Fix: use one\n   consistently-adjusted panel for both signal and execution.\n5. **Optimistic costs for this basket (softest)** \u2014 flat 5bp/side on the most-oversold, most-\n   illiquid tail at a selloff close with ~100%/day turnover; realistic ~20bp round-trip roughly\n   halves the ~0.19%/day net and plausibly erases it. Fix: approved COST_STANDARDS + 2\u00d7 thin-volume\n   stress + ADV/fill gate.\n\n(Non-material, labelled: certification battery absent; z-score redundant for ranking; sqrt(252)\ncorrect; O(n) `dates.index` performance-only.)\n\n----- ANSWER O01d -----\n# T01 \u2014 Consolidated arbiter verdict: cross-sectional reversal engine (arm C2)\n\n**Verdict: the result is FAKE.** A timezone mislabel collapses the intended one-session\nsignal\u2192fill buffer to zero, so the book is entered at the very close from which its ranking\nis computed (same-close look-ahead). The reported Sharpe 2.4 / +0.19% per trade-day is an\nartifact of impossible timing. Two further material issues (an adjusted-vs-unadjusted price\nmismatch and long-only beta contamination) make the numbers additionally wrong or mislabeled,\nbut neither is the source of the inflated Sharpe.\n\nBoth source reviews converge on the fatal defect; I adjudicated the three points where they\ndisagreed (see \"Adjudication of the two reviews\" below).\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 1 (FATAL) \u2014 timezone mislabel \u2192 same-close look-ahead\n\n**Line:** `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.date`\n\n`ts` is tz-aware **UTC** with stamps like `2025-03-04 18:30:00+00:00`, which is\n`2025-03-05 00:00 IST` \u2014 the vendor stamps each daily bar at IST-midnight of its trading day\n(the documented HF timezone landmine). `.dt.date` on a UTC-tz Series returns the **UTC**\nwall-clock date, so the bar for real IST trading day **T is labeled T-1**.\n\n**Trace (verified independently, generalized):** for any kept label `d` (one that is also a\ntrue trading day `T_k` in the bhav calendar), `sig_close.loc[d]` / `rev1.loc[d]` / `xz.loc[d]`\nactually carry the data and realized 1-day return of the **next** trading day `T_{k+1}` (the bar\nlabeled `T_k` is `T_{k+1}`'s bar shifted back one calendar day). The loop then computes\n`i = dates.index(d) = T_k` against the correctly-dated bhav index and sets\n`entry_d = dates[i+1] = T_{k+1}`, `exit_d = dates[i+2] = T_{k+2}`.\n\nNet effect: **you rank names by `T_{k+1}`'s realized close-to-close return and then \"enter\" at\n`close(T_{k+1})` \u2014 the exact close whose data the ranking required.** The intended buffer\n(signal at D \u2192 enter at D+1 close \u2192 exit D+2 close) collapses to **zero signal\u2192fill delay**.\nThe comment `# enter at the NEXT session's close` is false in effect. The book harvests the\nimmediate post-oversold bounce with an untradeable fill, and reversal alpha is concentrated in\nprecisely that first session \u2014 so this bug plausibly generates the entire Sharpe 2.4.\n\n**Severity:** the fill is *contemporaneous* with the signal close (zero delay), not a leak from\na strictly future bar \u2014 so \"same-close look-ahead\" is the correct, calibrated severity.\n\n**Fix:** `hf[\"date\"] = hf[\"ts\"].dt.tz_convert(\"Asia/Kolkata\").dt.date`, then re-run. With correct\nlabels the signal at D fills at D+1 close and exits D+2 close (a genuine one-session gap). Expect\nthe Sharpe to collapse.\n\n**Magnitude caveat (adopted from the red-team review):** \"this bug produces Sharpe 2.4\" is a\nwell-supported *hypothesis*, not a demonstrated fact. The claim is only established by applying\nthe `tz_convert` fix and reporting the collapsed number. Directionally near-certain; magnitude\nmust be confirmed by the re-run.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 2 \u2014 adjusted vendor signal vs. (inferred) unadjusted NSE-bhav P&L\n\n**Lines:** `rev1 = sig_close.pct_change()` (signal built from vendor `close`, note says\n\"split/bonus adjusted (audited)\") vs. `ret = bhav.pct_change()` (P&L built from the official NSE\nclose panel \"spot-checked against exchange prints\").\n\nOn any ex-split / ex-bonus day, a held name shows a large **fake** price jump in `ret`\n(e.g. \u2248 \u221250% for a 1:2 split), injecting corporate-action artifacts straight into the P&L series.\nSelection and settlement therefore run on two differently-adjusted price universes, so the\nper-trade numbers are wrong on every corporate-action day.\n\n**Two calibrations (adopted from the red-team review):**\n1. **[INFERENCE], not stated fact.** The submission states the vendor close is adjusted but only\n   says bhav is \"spot-checked against exchange prints.\" \"Unadjusted\" is *inferred* from\n   \"exchange prints.\" Reasonable, but conditional: if bhav were also adjusted this defect largely\n   evaporates. Note that a pure *level* difference between sources does **not** bias selection \u2014\n   the z-score is scale-invariant \u2014 so the defect operates **only** through the adjustment\n   mismatch on ex-dates, not through the two-source split per se.\n2. **Deflationary.** Fake ex-date drops raise vol and fatten the left tail, *lowering* Sharpe. So\n   this is a correctness error on corporate-action days but is **not** the source of the inflated\n   Sharpe.\n\n**Fix:** use a single, consistently adjusted series for both signal and settlement, or apply\ncorporate-action adjustment to bhav before `pct_change()`.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 3 \u2014 long-only construction \u2192 Sharpe embeds market beta (attribution)\n\n**Lines:** `pos = {d: xz.loc[d].nsmallest(30).index ...}` and\n`gross = ret.loc[exit_d, held].mean()`.\n\nThe sleeve longs the 30 most-oversold names and shorts nothing; each day's P&L is an\nequal-weight long-only 30-name one-day return minus cost. A one-day-hold long-only book carries\n~full one-day market beta. Over 2021\u20132025 (a broadly rising Indian market) that beta contributes\npositive drift that is **not** reversal alpha. Rough sizing (market \u2248 +0.05%/day, \u22481%/day vol,\n\u03b2\u22481) puts the beta-only Sharpe near ~0.8 \u2014 so beta alone does **not** explain 2.4 (Defect 1 is\nstill the driver), but the result is presented as a \"reversal engine\" when the number is\nreversal **+** beta.\n\nThis makes the *characterization* wrong rather than the arithmetic; it is a material attribution\ndefect. **Fix:** market-neutralize (e.g. long-oversold / short-overbought, or benchmark-relative\nreturns) before crediting any residual edge to reversal, and re-report after Defect 1 is fixed.\n\n---\n\n## Adjudication of the two reviews (where they differed)\n\n- **Prior-review \"Defect 3\" (Monday / post-holiday signals silently dropped by\n  `if d in ret.index`): DEMOTED to a symptom, not an independent defect.** It is real and\n  day-of-week-biased (reversal has weekend/Monday seasonality, so the drop is non-random) while\n  Defect 1 is unfixed, but the prior reviewer itself concedes it \"disappears once the tz_convert\n  fix is applied.\" It is fallout of Defect 1, so it is not counted as a co-equal material defect.\n- **Red-team \"Missed A\" (long-only beta): ACCEPTED and promoted to material defect 3 above** \u2014\n  a genuine, distinct attribution issue the prior review omitted.\n- **Red-team \"Missed B\" (`sqrt(252)` over-annualization because ~1-in-5 signal-days are dropped,\n  giving ~200 trade-days/yr): DERIVATIVE / non-material.** It mildly inflates the annualized\n  Sharpe but vanishes with the tz fix; negligible next to Defect 1.\n\n## Confirmed NOT defects (guarding against invented findings)\n\n- **P&L math is correct for a one-session hold.** `gross = ret.loc[exit_d, held].mean()` equals\n  `close(exit_d)/close(entry_d) \u2212 1`, exactly the entry-close\u2192exit-close return. No error.\n- **No overlapping-window Sharpe inflation.** Consecutive signal dates produce back-to-back,\n  non-overlapping one-session holds (one signal's exit = the next's entry), so returns are\n  serially non-overlapping and `sqrt(252)` is structurally appropriate (modulo Missed B). Do not\n  claim overlap-driven autocorrelation \u2014 it is not present.\n\n## Non-material comments (explicitly labelled)\n\n- **5 bp/side cost is optimistic** for the most-oversold single-stock F&O names (widest-spread,\n  circuit-prone, adverse-selected on entry day). Stress costs and honor circuit/no-fill rules\n  after fixing Defect 1. Optimistic assumption, not a code error.\n- `ret.loc[exit_d, held].mean()` skips NaN by default, silently dropping a held name with a\n  missing exit return from the equal-weight average. Minor.\n- `dates.index(d)` inside the loop is O(n\u00b2). Performance only.\n- \"94.8% exact match\" \u21d2 ~5.2% of bhav cells differ from prints \u2014 a data-quality flag worth\n  chasing, not shown to corrupt this result.\n- No PIT / survivorship universe control is visible in the snippet; F&O membership should be\n  point-in-time. Flag, not a confirmed finding.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list \u2014 material defects\n\n1. **Timezone mislabel \u2192 same-close look-ahead (FATAL).** `hf[\"ts\"].dt.date` reads the UTC date\n   on 18:30-UTC (= IST-midnight) stamps, labeling every bar T-1; combined with\n   `entry_d = dates[i+1]` against the correctly-dated bhav panel, the entry lands on the exact\n   close from which the signal is computed (zero signal\u2192fill delay). Fix:\n   `.dt.tz_convert(\"Asia/Kolkata\").dt.date` and re-run \u2014 magnitude of the collapse must be\n   confirmed by the re-run, but this alone plausibly produces Sharpe 2.4.\n2. **Adjusted vendor signal vs. (inferred) unadjusted NSE-bhav P&L.** Corporate-action ex-dates\n   inject fake returns into the P&L on affected held names. Material *if* bhav is unadjusted (a\n   reasonable [INFERENCE], not a stated fact); operates only through the adjustment mismatch, and\n   is deflationary \u2014 so not the source of the high Sharpe. Fix: one consistently adjusted series\n   for both signal and settlement.\n3. **Long-only construction \u2192 Sharpe embeds market beta (attribution defect).** The reported\n   number is reversal + beta, not reversal alpha; even after fixing #1 the sleeve must be\n   market-neutralized / benchmarked before any edge is credited to reversal.\n\n*Derivative / non-material (not counted above):* the Monday/post-holiday silent sample loss and\nthe `sqrt(252)` over-annualization are symptoms of #1; costs/circuit realism, NaN-skip, O(n\u00b2)\nlookup, the 94.8% match rate, and the unconfirmed PIT/survivorship question are non-material notes.", "ids": ["O01a", "O01b", "O01c", "O01d"]}, {"tid": "T02", "key": "## T02 \u2014 lookahead \u2014 same-bar (close) execution, T3 (HIGH)\n**Defect.** Signal needs the day's CLOSE (`ret` and `close > dma20` both use close of\nt); the fill is booked AT that same close (`entry = df[\"close\"].iloc[i]`). The\ndip-day close cannot be bought after observing it; any overnight bounce after down\ndays is silently pocketed. Firm precedent: same-day-close vs D+1 convention alone\nswung a strategy +0.99 -> -0.03 per Rs100.\n**Line.** `entry = df[\"close\"].iloc[i]` on the signal day.\n**Fix.** Enter at the NEXT session's open (or pre-register an intraday proxy signal\ncomputable before the close); re-run and quote the D+1 number as the verdict.\n**Accept.** \"Same-bar execution: signal computed on the close, filled at the same\nclose\"; \"entry must be next-day open \u2014 the close was not tradeable after the signal\nexisted\"; \"T3-class same-day-close fill inflates the edge with the overnight move\".\n**Secondary.** 3bp/side is optimistic for index futures crossing the spread twice \u2014\nminor; overlapping trades (3-session hold, signals can cluster) affects\nindependence of the t-stat, not the fill logic.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O02a -----\n# T02 \u2014 Review: NIFTY dip-buy overlay (armB)\n\n**Verdict: material defects present. Do NOT register at the reported number.**\n\nThe code plausibly produces the *per-trade* stats (+0.41% mean, 62% win, 74 trades),\nbut the headline **CAGR 19.4% \"at full notional\" is fabricated** \u2014 the code never\ncomputes a CAGR, and the number is arithmetically impossible from the stated\ntrade stats. There is also a same-bar-close execution/lookahead flaw and an\nunhandled trade-overlap problem. Details below.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 1 \u2014 CAGR 19.4% is unsupported and arithmetically impossible\n\n**Field:** \"CAGR 19.4% at full notional\" (reported result line).\n\n**Mechanism.** The script prints only `len(tr)`, `mean per trade`, and `win rate`.\nIt never builds an equity curve or computes any CAGR. Reconstructing the only\nhonest single-book interpretation (\"full notional\" = one position at a time):\n\n- 74 trades compounded at +0.41% each \u2192 total \u00d71.354.\n- Over 2018\u20132025 that is **~3.9% CAGR (8y)** / **~4.4% CAGR (7y)** \u2014 not 19.4%.\n- To actually reach 19.4% CAGR you would need **~303\u2013347 trades** at +0.41%, not 74.\n- The strategy is in-market only ~222 of ~2000 sessions (~11% of the time), so a\n  full-book CAGR *cannot* exceed the buy-and-hold-equivalent of that time unless\n  leverage is applied.\n\nThe only ways to manufacture 19.4% are (a) running **multiple overlapping\nfull-notional positions simultaneously** \u2014 that is leverage, not \"full notional,\"\nor (b) **annualizing the in-market daily rate** (0.41%/3d \u2248 0.137%/day \u00d7 252 \u2248 34%)\nwhile pretending the ~89% flat days don't exist. Both misrepresent the tradable\nreturn.\n\n**Fix.** Build an actual dated equity curve on a defined capital rule (single book,\none position at a time), compute CAGR from that curve, and reconcile it against the\nper-trade stats before it touches STRATEGY_REGISTER. A ~0.41% edge on ~9 trades/yr\nis a low-single-digit CAGR strategy at full notional \u2014 register it as such, or\nstate the exact leverage/concurrency assumption that produces 19.4% and size it\nunder RISK_LIMITS.\n\n## Material defect 2 \u2014 Same-bar close execution (lookahead / fill realism)\n\n**Lines:** `df[\"signal\"] = (df[\"ret\"] < -0.012) & (df[\"close\"] > df[\"dma20\"])`\nand `entry = df[\"close\"].iloc[i]`.\n\n**Mechanism.** The signal is fully determined by **close[i]** (both `ret[i] =\nclose[i]/close[i-1]-1` and `close[i] > dma20[i]`), and the entry fills at that\n**same close[i]**. You cannot know the day's exact close in time to trade at it \u2014\nwhether the day closes below \u22121.2% is only known once the close prints. This is a\nsame-bar peek: the price used to *decide* is also the price used to *fill*. It is\nexactly the class the firm's LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS (T1\u2013T10) and mandatory one-day-lag\ntest are meant to catch. After a sharp \u22121.2% down day an overnight gap is common,\nso filling at the signal close rather than the next available price biases the\nentry favorably and inflates the per-trade edge.\n\n**Fix.** Execute on the next bar: `entry = df[\"open\"].iloc[i+1]` (exit at\n`close.iloc[i+3]` or `open.iloc[i+4]`), re-run, and confirm the one-day-lag test\npasses. Re-measure the +0.41% after this correction \u2014 the edge may shrink\nmaterially.\n\n## Material defect 3 \u2014 Overlapping trades not handled (implicit leverage)\n\n**Lines:** the loop over `sig_days` appends a 3-session trade for *every* signal\nday with no check for an open position.\n\n**Mechanism.** \u22121.2% dips above the 20-DMA can occur on consecutive/near days, so\nthe 3-day holds overlap in calendar time. Counting each as an independent\nfull-notional trade assumes you can hold several simultaneously \u2014 implicit\nleverage. This makes \"74 trades\" not a tradable single-book series and is one of\nthe mechanisms that lets a bogus 19.4% appear.\n\n**Fix.** Either enforce non-overlap (skip signals while a position is open) and\nrecompute the stats on that tradable series, or explicitly model concurrent\npositions with declared gross exposure and report it as leveraged.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (labelled, not defects)\n\n- **Cost is correct as applied.** `- 0.0006` = 6 bp round-trip = 3 bp/side \u00d7 2.\n  Not a double/half-count bug. Caveat: 3 bp/side is optimistic for NIFTY futures\n  once STT (~2 bp on the sell notional alone) + exchange/GST + slippage are\n  included; use COST_STANDARDS. But even at ~12 bp round-trip the +0.41% edge\n  keeps its sign, so this does not change the conclusion.\n- **Parameter fragility / thin sample.** \u22121.2% dip, 20-DMA gate, 3-session hold are\n  tuned choices on only 74 trades. Run /sensitivity + DSR/PBO before any\n  certification. Non-material to the arithmetic above, but relevant to registering.\n- The `i + 3 >= len(df)` guard correctly drops tail trades; the leading NaN\n  window (ret/dma20) is correctly excluded by the boolean mask. Both fine.\n\n---\n\n## Claimed material defects\n1. **CAGR 19.4% \"at full notional\" is unsupported and arithmetically impossible** \u2014\n   74 trades \u00d7 +0.41% compound to ~3.9% (8y) / ~4.4% (7y) CAGR at one-position-per-time;\n   19.4% needs ~300+ trades or undisclosed leverage/time-in-market annualization.\n   The code never computes a CAGR. Build a real equity curve on a stated capital\n   rule and register that number.\n2. **Same-bar close execution / lookahead** \u2014 signal is derived from close[i] and\n   filled at close[i]; you can't condition on and trade at the same close. Enter on\n   next bar (`open[i+1]`) and pass the one-day-lag test; re-measure the edge.\n3. **Overlapping 3-day trades treated as independent full-notional trades** \u2014\n   implicit leverage that corrupts aggregate/CAGR. Enforce non-overlap or declare\n   concurrency/exposure and recompute.\n\n----- ANSWER O02b -----\n# T02 \u2014 Review: NIFTY dip-buy overlay (armA)\n\n**Verdict: material defects present.** The mechanics of the per-trade loop are mostly clean, but two things make the reported result not entering-into-register grade: the entry fill is not executable as coded (same-bar close), and the headline CAGR cannot be reconciled with the code's own outputs (it is unsupported / effectively fabricated).\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 1 \u2014 Same-bar (signal-day close) entry is not executable; it captures an un-tradeable overnight bounce\n\n- **Where:** `df[\"signal\"] = (df[\"ret\"] < -0.012) & (df[\"close\"] > df[\"dma20\"])` combined with `entry = df[\"close\"].iloc[i]`.\n- **Mechanism:** The signal is only *known* at the close of day `i` \u2014 both `ret` (close-to-close through day `i`) and `dma20` (a 20-day mean that *includes* day `i`'s close) require day `i`'s closing print. The code then fills the entry at that *same* closing print (`df[\"close\"].iloc[i]`). To do this in reality you would have to observe the official close and simultaneously transact at it \u2014 impossible. This is the classic signal-at-close / trade-at-close bias (a T-class execution-timing violation under the firm's LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS). It matters acutely here because this is a short-horizon mean-reversion / dip-buy edge: a large fraction of the \"bounce\" after a sharp down-close is realised in the overnight gap and the next open. Buying at the depressed signal-day close books that gap as profit even though a real desk could not enter until the next session \u2014 precisely where dip-buy edges typically shrink or invert.\n- **Fix:** Enter at the *next* session's open (`df[\"open\"].iloc[i+1]`) \u2014 the earliest tradeable price after the close-based signal \u2014 and re-report. Exit at close of `i+3` (or open of `i+4`) is fine since the exit is a fixed-horizon rule, not signal-driven. Re-run the whole table on next-open entry; the edge must survive this before any register entry.\n\n## Material defect 2 \u2014 Reported CAGR 19.4% is inconsistent with the code's own numbers (unsupported / fabricated headline)\n\n- **Where:** Reported \"CAGR 19.4% at full notional.\" The shown code computes only `mean per trade` and `win rate`; it never computes a CAGR at all.\n- **Mechanism:** With 74 trades over 2018\u20132025 (~8 years) at +0.41% each, one-position-at-a-time full-notional deployment compounds to `(1.0041)^74 \u2248 1.35`, i.e. ~+35% *total* over 8 years \u2192 CAGR \u2248 **3.9%**, not 19.4%. The strategy is only in the market ~74\u00d73 \u2248 222 sessions out of ~1,980 (~11% of the time), so it cannot annualise anywhere near 19.4% on the stated per-trade edge. To back out 19.4% (a ~4.14x total multiple) you would need ~+1.94% per trade \u2014 roughly 5x the reported 0.41% \u2014 i.e. undisclosed leverage/overlapping stacked notional. Even a permanently-deployed \"always full notional\" reading gives roughly the index's own 2018\u201325 CAGR (~12\u201313%), still not 19.4%. So the headline number is not reconcilable with the submission's own outputs under any stated assumption.\n- **Fix:** Derive CAGR explicitly from an equity curve built by the same trade logic (state the deployment assumption \u2014 sequential single position vs. concurrent, and any leverage). Report the reconciled figure; if leverage is used, disclose the multiple and re-cost margin/financing. Do not enter a CAGR the code does not produce.\n\n## Material defect 3 \u2014 Overlapping trades break both statistical independence and the \"full notional\" framing\n\n- **Where:** The `for t in sig_days` loop opens an independent 3-session trade on *every* signal day, with no check that the previous trade has closed.\n- **Mechanism:** Sharp down-closes above the 20-DMA cluster (volatile up-trends), so signals frequently fire on days within 3 sessions of each other. Consequences: (a) the 74 \"trades\" contain heavily overlapping return windows \u2192 the observations are autocorrelated, so the effective sample is well below 74 and any t-stat / significance on the +0.41% mean and 62% win rate is overstated; (b) \"CAGR at full notional\" is internally contradictory \u2014 overlapping windows imply you hold *several* full-notional positions at once, which is not one book at full notional. Point estimates of mean/win-rate are arithmetically fine, but their reliability and the capital-deployment story are not.\n- **Fix:** Either (i) enforce non-overlap (skip a new signal until the prior 3-day trade has exited) and re-report, which also makes \"full notional\" literal; or (ii) keep overlap but compute significance on a de-overlapped / block-bootstrapped basis and model concurrent capital honestly in the equity curve.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled, not defects)\n\n- **Costs are internally consistent, not a bug:** `- 0.0006` = 6 bp round trip = 3 bp/side as the comment states. For liquid NIFTY futures this is plausible, though it ignores that spreads/slippage widen on exactly the high-vol down-day the strategy buys into; a small slippage add-on on the entry leg would be more honest. Non-material to the headline.\n- **Parameter selection / overfit risk:** the trigger (\u22121.2%), lookback (20-DMA), and hold (3 sessions) are three hand-set knobs with no OOS split or sensitivity surface shown. Before any register entry this needs the standard Gate-4 sensitivity/perturbation battery \u2014 a robustness gap, not a mechanical error.\n- **NaN handling is fine:** the first 19 `dma20` values are NaN so early-window comparisons are False (no spurious signals); the `i + 3 >= len(df)` guard correctly drops trades without a full exit window.\n- **Data:** index-level official NSE series \u2192 no survivorship issue at the index level; dates are naive IST as declared.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n\n1. **Same-bar entry:** buying at the signal-day *close* (which is also what defines the signal) is not executable and books an un-tradeable overnight/next-open bounce for a mean-reversion edge \u2014 enter at next session's open instead.\n2. **Unsupported CAGR:** the reported 19.4% CAGR is not produced by the code and cannot be reconciled with +0.41%/trade \u00d7 74 trades (which compounds to ~3.9%/yr at single-position full notional); it implies undisclosed ~5x leverage or is fabricated \u2014 derive CAGR from an explicit equity curve with a stated deployment/leverage assumption.\n3. **Overlapping trades:** every signal opens an independent 3-day position with no non-overlap check, so per-trade stats are autocorrelated (overstated significance) and \"full notional\" is contradicted by concurrent positions \u2014 enforce non-overlap or model concurrency and correct the significance test.\n\n----- ANSWER O02c -----\n# CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT \u2014 T02 NIFTY dip-buy overlay\nRajan Mehta (E-001), after Quant review (Arjun Rao) + Red-Team review (Nikhil Bose).\nEvidence = the pasted task text + the two reviews ONLY (file access forbidden). Reconciliation arithmetic below is [INFERENCE]/[OPINION]; code-level facts are read directly from the pasted code.\n\nVERDICT: REJECT \u2014 do not enter in the STRATEGY_REGISTER at the reported number.\n- The headline CAGR is not produced by the shown code and cannot be reconciled to the trade economics \u2014 a register request cannot rest on an unreproducible number.\n- The single measured figure (+0.41%/trade) is FRAGILE, not proven false: it survives on a same-bar close fill, is untested for Mar-2020 tail concentration, and is untested against a bull-drift placebo.\n- Path to reconsideration: a FROZEN, corrected spec that clears the full battery below (this is a forward-test candidate at best, not a register entry).\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS (survivors, ranked)\n\n**1. \"CAGR 19.4% at full notional\" is unreproducible and unsubstantiated (DECISIVE).**\n- Claim/field: reported headline \"CAGR 19.4% at full notional.\"\n- Mechanism: the shown code prints only `len(tr)`, `tr.mean()`, `(tr>0).mean()` \u2014 it builds NO equity curve and NO CAGR, so 19.4% is asserted, not computed. [INFERENCE] 74 trades \u00d7 0.41% compounds to (1.0041)^74 \u2248 1.35\u00d7 over 2018\u20132025 \u2248 ~4%/yr on TOTAL capital, with capital deployed only ~74\u00d73 \u2248 222 of ~1,950 sessions (~11%, less after overlap). To reach 19.4%/yr you would need ~1.8%/trade, not 0.41% \u2014 a ~4\u00d7 gap.\n- Resolving the two reviews: Arjun attributes 19.4% to \"NIFTY beta\"; Nikhil is correct that this is quantitatively wrong \u2014 2018\u21922025 index CAGR is only ~11\u201313%/yr, so 19.4% reconciles to NEITHER overlay alpha (~4%) NOR beta (~12%). I adopt Nikhil's correction. The airtight, file-independent core is: the number is not computed by the code and is inconsistent with 74\u00d70.41% on total capital. The specific \"it reconciles to nothing\" reading is [INFERENCE].\n- Fix: code a real equity curve; report the overlay's INCREMENTAL contribution (base book vs base+overlay) on TOTAL capital, not deployed capital. (Firm Lesson 2026-07: headline CAGRs from full-capital-redeployment are artifacts \u2014 register incremental edge only.)\n\n**2. Same-bar close entry \u2014 fill-attainability / lookahead (MUST-FIX; magnitude unproven).**\n- Line: `entry = df[\"close\"].iloc[i]` (\"buy at the close of the signal day\").\n- Mechanism: the signal `(ret[t] < -0.012) & (close[t] > dma20[t])` is defined entirely by day-t's close; you cannot submit an MOC order conditioned on the close that also defines the trigger. Filling AT that close biases a mean-reversion dip-buy optimistically (the snapback is front-loaded right after the dip). This is a T-class lookahead per D-028.\n- Resolving the two reviews: Arjun calls it \"decisive\u2026 most of 0.41% dies here\"; Nikhil correctly downgrades this to must-fix with UNPROVEN magnitude (for index futures the entry-vs-next-open difference is ~one overnight gap, ~0.05\u20130.15%/trade [OPINION], not 0.4%). I adopt Nikhil: the defect is real; the magnitude is settled only by the test, never by prior.\n- Fix: enter at T+1 OPEN (`iloc[i+1]` open), hold entry+3; then run the mandatory one-day-lag test (`lib/lookahead_audit.py`). If the edge collapses under the lag, it was same-bar lookahead.\n\n**3. Single-regime / Mar-2020 tail concentration untested (MISSED by both as a run test).**\n- Claim: +0.41% mean over 2018\u20132025.\n- Mechanism: 2018\u20132025 straddles the Mar-2020 COVID crash + historic V-recovery. A `ret<\u22121.2%` dip-buy fires repeatedly Feb\u2013Mar 2020 and harvests outsized 3-day bounces off the bottom; a handful of 2020 trades can carry the entire mean while the median trade is ~0. [INFERENCE] Aligns with firm Lesson: judge on per-trade edge + tail, never a headline; the worst outcomes hid in calm\u2192violent regime shifts.\n- Fix: report median vs mean, the equity curve, a \"drop top-5 trades\" test, and an ex-Mar-2020 slice. If the edge dies ex-2020, it is a one-regime artifact.\n\n**4. Random-entry / bull-drift placebo not run (MISSED by both).**\n- Claim: the \u22121.2% dip trigger is the source of edge.\n- Mechanism: `close > dma20` conditions on an UPTREND, and 2018\u20132025 was mostly up; the whole 0.41% may be ~3 days of average bull drift with the dip condition adding nothing. [INFERENCE] Unconditional NIFTY 3-day drift \u22480.15%; conditioned on uptrend likely 0.2\u20130.3%.\n- Fix: random-entry benchmark \u2014 random days where `close>dma20`, hold 3 sessions. The dip edge must beat the placebo by a clear margin or it is a drift-capture rename.\n\n**5. Overlapping 3-day trades treated as i.i.d. \u2014 inflated significance.**\n- Field: `tr = pd.Series(trades)`, stats over all 74 as independent.\n- Mechanism: signals cluster on consecutive days (Feb-2018, Mar-2020, 2022); 3-day holds share bars (t\u2192t+3 overlaps t+1\u2192t+4), so effective N << 74 and the variance of the mean is understated, inflating any t-stat / Sharpe / DSR.\n- Resolving the two reviews: Arjun quotes t\u22481.7 (and ~1.4 de-overlapped); Nikhil correctly notes this rests on an ASSUMED 3-day std of 2% (at std 1.5% \u2192 t\u22482.35; at 2.5% \u2192 t\u22481.41). I adopt Nikhil: keep the direction, drop the specific number. Significance is plausibly marginal and must be computed from the ACTUAL net-trade series.\n- Fix: de-overlap to non-overlapping windows (or block-bootstrap), report effective N AND the realized trade-return std, then re-test.\n\n**6. No validation battery on three hand-picked parameters (certification gap).**\n- Fields: dip threshold \u22120.012, MA length 20, hold 3 \u2014 tunable, and read as chosen on the full sample.\n- Mechanism: no walk-forward, no plateau check around the params, no DSR>0.95 with an honest trials count, no PBO<25%, no 2018/2020/2022/2024 regime slices, no untouched OOS. With the marginal significance in #5, there is no basis to certify.\n- Fix: full RESEARCH_SOP / sensitivity battery on the CORRECTED T+1 entry, with one final untouched OOS window.\n\n## CORRECTLY CLEARED (both reviews; I concur \u2014 do NOT re-litigate)\n- tz landmine #1 and pre-open-auction #2: the code touches only `close`, never `open` \u2014 neither bites.\n- Survivorship: single index \u2014 N/A.\n- Leading-NaN `dma20`/`ret`: NaN comparison \u2192 False signal; no spurious early trades.\n- `if i + 3 >= len(df): continue`: correct boundary, no off-by-one (exit index i+3 stays valid).\n- Exit at `iloc[i+3]` close: time-based exit, known 3 days ahead \u2192 placeable as MOC; NOT lookahead. The entry/exit asymmetry Arjun drew is correct \u2014 the entry is the defect, the exit is clean.\n- `rolling(20).mean()` including the current close: standard for a close-evaluated signal; not lookahead.\n\n## NON-MATERIAL COMMENTS (labelled; do not flip the result)\n- Cost: `- 0.0006` (6bp round-trip, 3bp/side) is light on slippage/impact and carries no 2\u00d7 stress; it must follow approved COST_STANDARDS before any register entry, but doubling to 12bp trims only ~0.06% off 0.41% \u2014 non-material to the headline.\n- No date filter in the code: \"74 trades 2018\u20132025\" is enforced by the parquet's span, not by the code \u2014 scoping is asserted, not coded. Unverifiable here and non-corrupting; note for reproducibility only.\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT (CIO)\nAn index-book dip-buy overlay is implicitly SHORT the crash tail: it adds to longs precisely on \u22121.2% down days, so the worst single-day and worst-month realizations are back-to-back losing entries during a persistent drawdown (2018 Q4, Mar-2020 pre-bottom, 2022 hikes) with overlapping 3-day exposure stacking the position. The reported 62% win rate says nothing about the size of the losing 38% \u2014 no left-tail figure (max drawdown, worst trade, worst month) was reported at all. Correlated-blowup scenario: the overlay fires on the same days the core index book is already bleeding, so it is additive to beta drawdown, not diversifying. No sizing can be ruled on until the equity curve, median-vs-mean, and ex-2020 slice exist.\n\n## SIZING RULING\nZero. Not register-eligible and not paper-eligible in current form.\n\n## KILL / RECONSIDERATION CRITERIA (frozen, corrected spec \u2014 pin git hash at re-entry, D-030)\nAdvances to forward-test candidacy ONLY if ALL pass:\n1. T+1-OPEN entry survives the one-day-lag test with the edge intact.\n2. Edge survives ex-Mar-2020 AND a drop-top-5-trades test (median, not just mean).\n3. Edge beats the random-entry (close>dma20, 3-day) placebo by a clear margin.\n4. De-overlapped significance clears with the REALIZED trade-return std reported (not assumed).\n5. A real equity curve reproduces the claimed return, incremental over base, on total capital.\nReview date: on resubmission of the corrected frozen spec; no standing clock until then.\n\n## DISSENTS (recorded by name)\n- Arjun Rao (Quant): conclusion (FAKE / not register-ready) upheld. Overruled on two framings \u2014 (a) Defect 1 \"decisive / most of 0.41% dies\" downgraded to must-fix, magnitude unproven; (b) Defect 2 \"it is NIFTY beta\" corrected \u2014 beta is only ~11\u201313%, so 19.4% reconciles to neither alpha nor beta.\n- Nikhil Bose (Red Team): concur in full, including the two additions the quant review missed (tail-concentration and random-entry placebo). No dissent.\n\n## NUMBERED LIST OF MATERIAL DEFECTS\n1. \"CAGR 19.4% at full notional\" is not produced by the code (no equity curve) and is inconsistent with 74 \u00d7 0.41% on total capital (~4%/yr) \u2014 reconciles to neither overlay alpha nor index beta; unregisterable. Fix: real equity curve, incremental return on total capital.\n2. Same-bar close entry (`entry = df[\"close\"].iloc[i]`) fills at the very close that defines the signal \u2014 fill-attainability/lookahead; optimistic for a mean-reversion buy. Fix: T+1-open entry + mandatory one-day-lag test. Magnitude unproven.\n3. Mar-2020 single-regime / tail concentration untested \u2014 a handful of crash-recovery trades can carry the whole mean. Fix: median-vs-mean, drop-top-5, ex-Mar-2020 slice, equity curve.\n4. Random-entry / bull-drift placebo not run \u2014 `close>dma20` conditions on uptrend; edge may be pure 3-day drift. Fix: random-entry benchmark under the same filter; dip edge must beat it.\n5. Overlapping 3-day trades treated as i.i.d. \u2014 understates variance, inflates significance; effective N << 74; t-stat marginal and std-sensitive. Fix: de-overlap/block-bootstrap, report effective N and realized std.\n6. No validation battery on three hand-picked params (\u22121.2%/20/3) \u2014 no walk-forward/DSR/PBO/plateau/regime/OOS. Fix: full RESEARCH_SOP battery on the corrected T+1 entry with one untouched OOS.\n\n----- ANSWER O02d -----\n# T02 \u2014 Consolidated final verdict: NIFTY dip-buy overlay (arm C2, arbiter)\n\n**Verdict: NOT registrable at the reported number. Multiple material defects.**\nThe code runs, but the headline (`CAGR 19.4% at full notional`) has no code behind it, the\nper-trade entry is not executable as backtested, and the edge is unvalidated. Below is the\nconsolidated set of defects that survive both reviews plus my own read of the code, with the\ntwo reviewers' over-reaches and mislabels stripped out.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS (survive)\n\n### 1. `CAGR 19.4% at full notional` is not produced by the code and is inconsistent with the stated per-trade edge. *(dominant defect)*\n- **Claim/field:** the reported \"CAGR 19.4% at full notional\" (and \"at full notional\") vs the script,\n  which prints only `len(tr)`, `tr.mean()`, and `(tr>0).mean()`.\n- **Mechanism:** there is no equity curve, no capital path, no compounding, no time-in-market, and\n  no notional/leverage convention anywhere in the code. So 19.4% is unreproducible from what was\n  submitted. Sanity arithmetic: `(1.0041)^74 \u2212 1 \u2248 +35.4%` cumulative over ~7.5 years, and the\n  capital is idle between the ~3-session windows (74 trades \u2248 ~220 market-days out of ~1,900,\n  ~11% time-in-market). At single notional that is **\u22484% CAGR**, not 19.4%. Reaching 19.4%\n  requires roughly **4.5\u20135x standing leverage** \u2014 i.e. the headline is a leverage/notional artifact,\n  not this overlay's alpha. Registering it \"at the reported number\" would enter an unverifiable\n  figure and violates epistemic conduct (D-035).\n- **Fix:** compute the actual equity curve with an explicit capital + deployment + leverage\n  convention, separate overlay alpha from index beta (it is pitched as an *overlay for the index\n  book*), and report the CAGR the curve actually produces. Do not register 19.4%.\n- *Note:* the first reviewer's fallback \"it's really index beta ~13\u201314%\" is dropped \u2014 it is a guess\n  and does not even reach 19.4%; the defect stands without it.\n\n### 2. Signal-day-close entry is not realistically executable at the settlement print (fill-realism, not strict look-ahead).\n- **Lines:** `df[\"signal\"] = (df[\"ret\"] < -0.012) & (df[\"close\"] > df[\"dma20\"])` and\n  `entry = df[\"close\"].iloc[i]  # buy at the close of the signal day`.\n- **Mechanism:** every leg of the trigger is a function of bar *i*'s close (`ret[i]` = close/prev\u22121;\n  the `>dma20` test uses `close[i]`; `dma20` includes `close[i]`), so the signal is only knowable at\n  the close. But the fill is taken *at that same close*. You cannot observe that the index settled\n  \u22121.2% and simultaneously transact at that exact settlement print (an MOC order is submitted\n  before the close is known). The `+0.41%`/trade is therefore captured at a price a live order\n  could not guarantee. **This is a same-bar executability / fill-realism defect \u2014 no future data\n  beyond bar i is used, so labelling it \"T-class look-ahead\" (as the first reviewer did) overstates\n  it.**\n- **Fix:** enter at the **next session's open** (`open[i+1]`) and shift the exit to match\n  (`open[i+1] \u2192 close[i+4]`), then re-report every metric.\n- *Correction to first reviewer:* his claim that the edge \"goes to zero\" at next-open entry is an\n  **unsupported empirical assertion**. The direction is genuinely uncertain \u2014 sharp down-closes\n  are followed by both overnight mean-reversion bounces *and* gap-down continuation; next-open\n  entry could help or hurt. The re-run is required; the predicted outcome is not evidence.\n\n### 3. Overlapping 3-session windows are counted as independent observations.\n- **Line:** exit at `df[\"close\"].iloc[i + 3]` while the loop iterates every timestamp in\n  `sig_days` with no \"skip if a position is open\" guard.\n- **Mechanism:** consecutive/near-consecutive dips produce 3-day windows that overlap and share\n  return periods, so the 74 \"trades\" are not 74 independent draws. This (a) inflates effective N and\n  understates the variance of both the mean and the win rate \u2014 no t-stat or dispersion is reported at\n  all, so the significance of the edge is overstated; and (b) breaks the \"single full-notional\"\n  framing in defect #1, since overlapping positions require stacked notional / multiple capital units.\n- **Correction (important):** overlap does **not** corrupt the computed `+0.41%` mean or the `62%`\n  win rate \u2014 those remain valid averages over the (overlapping) trade set. It corrupts the\n  *significance* and the *CAGR/notional accounting*, not the point estimates.\n- **Fix:** de-overlap (skip new signals while a position is open) **or** report block/overlap-adjusted\n  statistics with a t-stat, and make the position/notional convention explicit so it reconciles with\n  the CAGR calc in #1.\n\n### 4. Parameter selection with no OOS split and no sensitivity battery (in-sample overfit risk).\n- **Fields:** three hand-chosen free parameters \u2014 dip threshold `\u22120.012`, lookback `rolling(20)`,\n  hold `i + 3` \u2014 with zero robustness, out-of-sample, or perturbation evidence.\n- **Mechanism:** a small `+0.41%`/trade edge produced by a 3-knob configuration on one 2018\u20132025\n  sample is a textbook overfit candidate; the reported number may not survive out of sample. At this\n  firm no register entry / Gate-4 pass is permitted without a sensitivity + OOS pass, so the edge is\n  currently unvalidated (this is a blocker even though it is not by itself *proof* the number is wrong).\n- **Fix:** run the neighbourhood surface (dip \u2208 [\u22120.8%,\u22121.6%], MA \u2208 {10,20,50}, hold \u2208 {1..5}),\n  DSR/PBO, and a held-out era split before any register entry. *(Missed by the first reviewer.)*\n\n### 5. Cost model is optimistic on exactly the signal days, and is not the firm standard. *(material, small magnitude)*\n- **Line:** `tr = pd.Series(trades) - 0.0006  # 3bp per side, index futures`.\n- **Mechanism:** a flat 6bp round-trip with **no slippage and no volatility scaling** is applied on\n  entries that fire only on `\u22651.2%` down-closes \u2014 high-volatility bars where spreads and impact widen\n  (firm landmine 7b: slippage 2\u20133x on stressed days). Flat 6bp therefore *understates* real cost\n  precisely on entry days, inflating the net `+0.41%`. Also, per firm rule costs must come from\n  `06_TRADING_DESK/COST_STANDARDS.md`, not a hardcoded literal.\n- **Correction to first reviewer:** his \"costs are conservative\" blessing is wrong \u2014 statutory\n  charges alone (STT on the futures sell leg \u2248 ~2bp, plus exchange/GST/stamp) eat much of 6bp before\n  any slippage.\n- **Fix:** apply COST_STANDARDS with volatility-scaled slippage on signal days and re-report. Not\n  fatal to edge existence (e.g. ~12bp still leaves ~+0.35%), but the reported net number is\n  overstated.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL COMMENTS (explicitly not defects)\n- **No date filter in the code** \u2014 the script reads the whole parquet and never slices dates, so the\n  \"74 trades 2018\u20132025\" window is assumed, not enforced. Add an explicit date slice for\n  reproducibility. *(reproducibility note, non-material.)*\n- **`rolling(20).mean()` includes the current close** \u2014 this is a standard SMA; `close[i]` is known at\n  close i, so it is **not** look-ahead. Flagged only to pre-empt a false positive.\n- **`if i + 3 >= len(df): continue`** \u2014 off-by-one-correct (keeps `i+3 = len\u22121`, drops out-of-range).\n  Not a bug.\n- **`df.index.get_loc(t)`** \u2014 would misbehave only on a duplicated date index; clean NSE daily has\n  none. Robustness nit.\n- **Cost arithmetic itself** \u2014 6bp is applied once per trade (3bp \u00d7 2 sides), no double-count. The\n  arithmetic is correct; the defect (#5) is the *adequacy/slippage*, not the application.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n1. **`CAGR 19.4% / at full notional` is fabricated relative to the code** \u2014 never computed; the\n   `+0.41%`/trade edge implies ~4% CAGR at single notional, so 19.4% requires ~5x leverage/attribution.\n   Unreproducible \u2192 cannot be registered at the reported number (D-035). *[dominant]*\n2. **Signal-day-close entry is not realistically executable** at the settlement print \u2014 re-test at\n   next-session open. (Fill-realism/executability, **not** strict look-ahead; impact direction is\n   uncertain \u2014 the \"edge goes to zero\" prediction is unsupported.)\n3. **Overlapping 3-session windows counted as independent** \u2014 inflates effective N, no t-stat, and\n   breaks the single-notional CAGR framing. (Corrupts significance/accounting, **not** the per-trade\n   point estimate.)\n4. **Parameter overfit / no OOS or sensitivity** \u2014 three tuned knobs on one sample, no robustness\n   battery; `+0.41%` may be in-sample; fails the firm's Gate-4/sensitivity bar.\n5. **Cost model understated on signal days** \u2014 flat 6bp, no slippage, not from COST_STANDARDS;\n   high-vol down-day entries widen real costs, so the net figure is overstated (first reviewer's\n   \"conservative\" characterization is incorrect). *[small magnitude]*", "ids": ["O02a", "O02b", "O02c", "O02d"]}, {"tid": "T03", "key": "## T03 \u2014 CLEAN (stats theme)\n**No material defect.** PIT membership stated, available_date-timestamped signal,\n2021+ window matches the known publication-coverage constraint, next-open entry,\ncircuit/zero-volume no-fill, fixed same-exit for strategy AND placebos,\nturnover-matched by construction, lag test degrades gracefully, denominators clean,\nhonest modest verdict (asks for sensitivity, not certification).\n**Reject as false positives.** \"92nd percentile is not significant enough\" (it is a\nfinding, and the memo itself only asks to advance, not certify); \"needs more placebo\nbaskets\"; \"no DSR/PBO yet\" (memo explicitly routes to the sensitivity battery next);\n\"10-session fixed exit is arbitrary\" (pre-registered and shared with placebos).\nA grader may accept these as *comments* but they score as invented defects if\npresented as things that make the result wrong or fake.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O03a -----\n# T03 \u2014 Review: post-earnings positive-surprise long sleeve (arm A)\n\n**Reviewer verdict: material defects present.** The methodology is mostly clean on\nthe classic lookahead axes (this is what makes the memo dangerous \u2014 it *looks*\naudited), but the statistical case for the edge is overstated, and the headline\nreturn numbers do not internally reconcile. Details below.\n\n---\n\n## What the memo gets right (so the review is credible)\n\n- **Denominator is correct.** This is a cash-equity long; edge is quoted in % of\n  spot and Rs/share (Rs 3.1 / Rs 740 = 0.419% \u2248 0.42%, consistent). No\n  per-premium / net-debit denominator abuse. The denominator check passes.\n- **Signal timing is PIT-clean on its face.** Timestamped on `available_date`,\n  entry at the *next* session open \u2014 a full-day gap from publication, no\n  earnings-quarter-end lookahead.\n- **Fill realism on entry.** Upper-circuit-locked opens and zero-first-15-min-volume\n  entries are dropped (9 trades) \u2014 the honest, conservative direction (you drop\n  the strongest gap-up cases you couldn't actually buy).\n- **One-day-lag test present and survived** (0.42\u21920.31, still positive) \u2014 argues\n  against a gross lookahead artifact.\n- **Honest posture:** the memo asks for the sensitivity battery, NOT direct\n  register entry. Good.\n- The t-stat is *arithmetically* consistent with its own inputs:\n  0.42 / (2.5/\u221a412) = 3.4. The problem (below) is the assumption behind it, not\n  the sum.\n\nNone of that rescues the four defects.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS\n\n### D1 \u2014 t-stat 3.4 is inflated by non-independent, overlapping trades; it directly contradicts the memo's own placebo result\n\n- **Claim:** \"t-stat 3.4 (per-trade std 2.5%)\" over 412 trades.\n- **Mechanism:** The t = mean/(std/\u221aN) formula assumes 412 *independent* draws.\n  These trades are not independent. Holds are 10 sessions with up to 8 concurrent\n  positions, and earnings arrive in clusters (Jan\u2013Feb, Apr\u2013May, Jul\u2013Aug,\n  Oct\u2013Nov). Within a season many names surprise together and are held over\n  **overlapping windows**, so their returns are cross-correlated through shared\n  market beta. The effective number of independent bets is a fraction of 412, so\n  the naive t-stat overstates significance. This is exactly why PEAD studies use\n  calendar-time portfolios or calendar-clustered standard errors.\n- **The smoking gun:** the memo's *own* matched-null test disagrees with the\n  t-stat. t = 3.4 implies one-sided p \u2248 0.0007. But the placebo battery \u2014 which\n  automatically bakes in the overlap/beta correlation structure because it uses\n  the same dates and the same exit engine \u2014 puts the strategy at only the **92nd\n  percentile, i.e. p \u2248 0.08**. Two significance measures that differ by ~100x\n  cannot both be right; the placebo (honest) one wins, and it says *borderline*.\n- **Fix:** report the placebo percentile as the significance statistic, and/or\n  recompute the t-stat with a calendar-block bootstrap or calendar-time\n  aggregation (collapse to a monthly long-basket return series and t-test that).\n  Quote effective N, not 412. Do not lead with 3.4.\n\n### D2 \u2014 \"Entry edge appears real against matched nulls\" is not supported; 92nd percentile is below the bar\n\n- **Claim:** \"the strategy sits at the 92nd percentile of the placebo\n  distribution \u2026 Entry edge appears real against matched nulls.\"\n- **Mechanism:** 92nd percentile against a one-sided null is p \u2248 0.08 \u2014 it fails\n  the conventional 95% threshold. The properly-matched test the memo itself\n  designed says the edge is only *marginally* distinguishable from random entry.\n  Calling that \"real\" is an overstatement of the memo's strongest and most honest\n  control. (The verdict downstream \u2014 \"advance to sensitivity, not register\" \u2014 is\n  appropriately cautious, which softens the practical harm, but the stated\n  conclusion is still wrong and a gate reviewer must reject the wording.)\n- **Fix:** state it as \"not significant vs matched nulls at 95% (92nd pctile,\n  p\u22480.08); edge is borderline.\" Advancement, if any, is on that honest basis.\n\n### D3 \u2014 Headline \"+0.42% net edge\" is beta-contaminated, not excess return\n\n- **Claim:** \"Mean net edge +0.42% of spot per trade.\"\n- **Mechanism:** A random long over the same 10-session windows earned +0.06%\n  (the placebo mean) \u2014 that is pure market drift, not selection skill. The\n  selection edge over the matched null is **0.42% \u2212 0.06% = 0.36%**, so ~14% of\n  the \"edge\" is beta any coin-flip entry would have captured. Labeling the raw\n  net return \"edge\" mislabels market drift as alpha. (Partially disclosed, since\n  placebo is shown separately and the reader can subtract \u2014 but the headline\n  number is the one that gets quoted.)\n- **Fix:** report the market-excess figure (over the placebo baseline) as the\n  edge; keep the raw return as a secondary line.\n\n### D4 \u2014 Portfolio CAGR 9.8% does not reconcile with the per-trade edge and the stated sizing\n\n- **Claim:** \"On a fixed Rs 50L notional with max 8 concurrent positions: CAGR\n  9.8%.\"\n- **Mechanism (arithmetic):** With a Rs 50L book split into 8 equal slots\n  (Rs 6.25L each), per-trade profit = 0.42% \u00d7 6.25L = Rs 2,625. Across 412 trades\n  = Rs 10.8L \u2248 **21.6% total \u2248 ~4.0%/yr** over 5 years \u2014 and that is the *ceiling*\n  (assumes all 8 slots always full). With earnings clustering, average concurrent\n  positions \u2248 412 trades \u00d7 10 days / 250 days/yr / 5 yr \u2248 3.3, i.e. the book is on\n  average ~40% deployed, which pushes realistic CAGR *below* 4%. The memo's 9.8%\n  CAGR implies ~+60% total (\u2248 Rs 29.7L profit) \u2014 roughly **2.75\u00d7 the per-trade\n  math**. The numbers do not tie.\n  - The only sizing that reconciles to 9.8% is \"always deploy the full Rs 50L,\n    split among however many positions are currently open (1\u20138).\" But that rule\n    (a) is not what \"fixed Rs 50L notional\" states, (b) means putting the entire\n    book into a *single* stock whenever only one signal is live \u2014 extreme\n    concentration, (c) makes the sleeve ~100% net long equities almost always, so\n    most of the 9.8% is bull-market beta (2021\u20132025), not edge, and (d) is\n    inconsistent with the tiny **\u22127.9% max DD** \u2014 a near-fully-invested long-only\n    NIFTY-200 book would have absorbed the 2022 index drawdown and the Jun-2024\n    election-day crash and shown a far deeper DD.\n- **Conclusion:** either the CAGR is overstated ~2.75\u00d7 (fixed 1/8 sizing) or the\n  sizing is undisclosed full-deployment (in which case the return is mostly beta\n  and the \u22127.9% DD is implausible). Either horn is a defect.\n- **Fix:** publish the exact position-sizing rule and reconcile the reported\n  equity curve line-by-line to the sum of per-trade P&L; report the sleeve's net\n  long-equity exposure profile and re-derive DD from the actual daily NAV.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL COMMENTS (labelled; do not block on these)\n\n- **Cost realism (comment):** 25bp/side all-in at the *post-earnings open* \u2014 the\n  single most volatile, widest-spread moment \u2014 is likely optimistic for the\n  midcap tail of NIFTY-200. Confirm it against COST_STANDARDS dynamic-slippage\n  before the sensitivity run; it will only make the already-thin edge thinner.\n- **Trials hygiene (comment):** no trials count. The \"1 residual sigma\" threshold\n  and the \"10-session\" hold read like chosen parameters; if they were tuned, the\n  reported edge and placebo percentile are optimistic. Disclose the trials count\n  (RP-19 / OOS hygiene) \u2014 the sensitivity battery should surface this anyway.\n- **Signal construction (comment):** a trailing-4-quarter trend fit leaves ~2\n  residuals; \"one residual sigma\" off 2 df is a very noisy normalizer. Robustness\n  point, not a result-corrupting error.\n- **Entry-price plumbing (comment):** confirm the \"next session OPEN\" is the\n  09:15 real open, not the 09:00 pre-open auction print (pre-open auction\n  landmine). The zero-first-15-min-volume filter suggests intraday awareness, but\n  verify the price field.\n- **available_date precision (comment):** the PIT set is ~86% exact dates; the\n  ~14% imputed ones could be earlier than true publication (per-trade lookahead)\n  in a minority of cases. The lag test partially guards this.\n- **Decay shape (comment):** a 26% edge loss from a single extra day of lag\n  (0.42\u21920.31) is not \"collapse,\" but it is front-loaded alpha \u2014 flags capture\n  risk and belongs in the capacity discussion.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS \u2014 numbered\n\n1. **t-stat 3.4 is inflated** \u2014 it assumes 412 independent trades, but overlapping\n   10-session holds (up to 8 concurrent) in clustered earnings seasons share\n   market beta; effective N is far smaller. It also contradicts the memo's own\n   placebo (p\u22480.0007 vs p\u22480.08). Fix: calendar-clustered/block-bootstrap\n   significance; report the placebo percentile as the statistic.\n2. **\"Edge appears real against matched nulls\" is unsupported** \u2014 92nd percentile\n   = one-sided p \u2248 0.08, below the 95% bar; the matched-null result is borderline,\n   not \"real.\" Fix: restate as not-significant-at-95%.\n3. **Headline \"+0.42% net edge\" is beta-contaminated** \u2014 the matched random-entry\n   baseline is +0.06%; true selection edge is +0.36%. Fix: report market-excess\n   (over placebo) as the edge.\n4. **Portfolio CAGR 9.8% does not reconcile with the per-trade edge under the\n   stated Rs 50L / 8-concurrent sizing** \u2014 per-trade math gives ~4%/yr (ceiling),\n   ~2.75\u00d7 below the claim; the only reconciling sizing implies near-100% long-\n   equity exposure (mostly bull-market beta) and is inconsistent with the \u22127.9%\n   max DD. Fix: disclose the sizing rule and reconcile the equity curve to summed\n   per-trade P&L.\n\n----- ANSWER O03b -----\n# T03 \u2014 CONSOLIDATED GATE REVIEW (final arbiter)\n## Post-earnings positive-surprise long, results memo (2021-2025)\n\nTwo independent reviews were run (a primary review and an adversarial refutation\nreview). Both land on the SAME single material defect; they disagree only on its\nmechanism, and the refutation correctly strips some fabricated precision out of the\nprimary review. This is the reconciled verdict, keeping only what survives on the\ntext alone. No files were read or executed.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n## BOTTOM LINE\n\nThe data-hygiene work is genuinely good: PIT universe (42-snapshot as-of membership),\n`available_date` timestamps (not quarter-end), upper-circuit / zero-volume entry drops,\na matched placebo battery, a one-day-lag test, and an explicit denominator check. The\nsubmission is NOT fake.\n\nThere is ONE material defect: the headline **t-stat 3.4** overstates significance by\nroughly 2x, and the memo's own placebo battery contradicts it. The honest significance\nis marginal \u2014 the strategy sits at the **92nd percentile of the matched placebo\ndistribution (one-sided p \u2248 0.08)**, i.e. ~1.4 sigma, not the p \u2248 0.0003 that \"t = 3.4\"\nimplies.\n\nThe verdict SOUGHT \u2014 do NOT register; advance to the sensitivity/DSR battery \u2014 SURVIVES.\nBut the reason is stronger than the memo states: the edge is not only small (below the\nregister bar on standalone return), its statistical significance is also marginal once\nthe correct standard error is used.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n## THE MATERIAL DEFECT \u2014 significance overstated ~2x\n\n**Fields:** `\"t-stat 3.4 (per-trade std 2.5%)\"` and `\"92nd percentile of the placebo\ndistribution.\"`\n\n**Reproduction of the reported t.** SE = 2.5% / \u221a412 = 0.123%; 0.42 / 0.123 = 3.41. So\nthe memo genuinely fed a per-trade std of 2.5% into a plain i.i.d. standard error over\n412 trades.\n\n**Why that t is wrong.** The parametric t and the empirical placebo are two significance\nmeasures on the same result, and they disagree by a factor of ~2 \u2014 that disagreement is\nthe defect. Back out the placebo basket-mean std from the reported percentile:\n(0.42 \u2212 0.06) / z(0.92) = 0.36 / 1.40 \u2248 **0.257%**. The i.i.d. t-stat assumes a basket-mean\nstd of 0.123%. The placebo \u2014 which is empirically matched to the real trade structure \u2014\nmeasures ~0.257%, about **2x wider**. Feeding the correct spread back in:\n- vs zero: t \u2248 0.42 / 0.257 \u2248 **1.6 sigma**\n- vs the matched (beta) placebo baseline of +0.06%: 0.36 / 0.257 \u2248 **1.4 sigma, p \u2248 0.08.**\n\nBoth routes converge on \"marginal.\" A 92nd-percentile result IS p \u2248 0.08; that is below\nany conventional bar, not a pass.\n\n**Mechanism of corruption.** The reported \"t-stat 3.4\" is fake precision: its standard\nerror is built on a spread ~2x too small, converting a marginal result (p \u2248 0.08) into a\nfalse p \u2248 0.0003. The memo then leans on it (\"entry edge appears real against matched\nnulls\") while its own placebo \u2014 the more trustworthy of the two measures \u2014 says the edge\nis only marginal.\n\n**On the mechanism \u2014 the arbiter's ruling (this is where the two reviews split).**\nThere are two observationally-equivalent explanations for why the placebo spread is ~2x\nthe i.i.d. prediction, and the text cannot distinguish them:\n- **(a) Trade non-independence** (primary review): earnings entries bunch into the four\n  results seasons and a 10-session hold with up to 8 concurrent positions overlaps in\n  calendar time \u2014 classic clustering + overlapping-observation autocorrelation, which\n  inflates the naive t.\n- **(b) An understated per-trade std** (refutation review): a raw single-name long held\n  10 sessions cannot realistically have a cross-trade return std of 2.5%. A typical\n  NIFTY-200 name at ~30% annualized vol gives a 10-session std of ~6% (more for midcaps,\n  plus cross-sectional dispersion). The placebo-implied per-trade std (0.257% \u00d7 \u221a412 \u2248\n  **~5.2%**) corroborates ~5-6%, i.e. roughly DOUBLE the 2.5% the t-stat used. As a plain\n  unhedged long, there is no neutralization on the table to justify 2.5%.\n\n**Ruling:** the defect is CONFIRMED under either explanation \u2014 both converge on t \u2248 1.6 /\np \u2248 0.08 \u2014 so the conclusion is safe. But the primary review's precise decomposition\n(\"effective N \u2248 96, variance-inflation \u2248 4.3, significance inflated *exactly* 2x\") is NOT\nsupported by the text and is struck: it (i) treats a 200-sample empirical percentile as a\nclean normal z, and (ii) assumes the placebo per-trade std equals the strategy's 2.5%,\nwhich is exactly the number in dispute. It is the same fake-precision sin the memo is\nbeing charged with, and it should not be quoted as a finding. The correct, text-supported\nstatement is: the placebo-implied basket spread (~0.257%) is ~2x the i.i.d. value, driven\nby some mix of (a) and (b) that the memo does not let us separate.\n\n**Correct fix.**\n1. Drop the i.i.d. t as the significance headline.\n2. Headline the empirical placebo / block-bootstrap significance (block by earnings\n   season and by overlapping calendar window), i.e. the ~92nd-percentile / p \u2248 0.08\n   result; or report clustered / Newey-West standard errors that account for the overlap\n   and seasonal clustering.\n3. Reconcile the reported per-trade std (2.5%) against the placebo-implied ~5% before\n   quoting any t. If 2.5% is real, the memo must disclose the neutralization that produced\n   it; otherwise the t is built on a wrong denominator.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n## COROLLARIES \u2014 re-read in light of the corrected significance\n\n- **Era splits** (2021-22 +0.51 / 2023 +0.29 / 2024-25 +0.44): on raw counts these are\n  ~80-165 trades each (NOT \"\u224830 effective\" \u2014 that number rested on the struck effective-N\n  and is withdrawn), but a single-year 2023 estimate is still noisy, and the +0.51 \u2192 +0.29\n  \u2192 +0.44 path is at best a wobble, not evidence of stability. \"Stable across eras\" is not\n  earned by the data shown.\n- **One-day-lag test** (0.42 \u2192 0.31): KEEP as reassuring. This is a lookahead/robustness\n  check, not a significance test \u2014 its job is to detect a single-bar artifact that\n  collapses or flips when you act a day later. Retaining ~74% and staying positive is a\n  good lookahead result. The primary review's dismissal of it as \"inside the sigma, not\n  informative\" was a category error and is not adopted.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n## NON-MATERIAL ITEMS (labelled; none invalidate the result on the text alone)\n\n- **[reconciliation, ~2x] Portfolio-return sizing.** \"Fixed Rs 50L notional, max 8\n  concurrent\" doesn't state the per-name weight, and the CAGR does not reconcile under a\n  fixed-slot rule. Avg concurrent \u2248 82 trades/yr \u00d7 10 sessions / ~250 \u2248 3.3, so at a fixed\n  ~6.25L/slot only ~20L of 50L is deployed on average \u2192 ~4-5%/yr, not 9.8%. Reaching 9.8%\n  needs ~14-15L/position, i.e. concentration / near-full-investment that contradicts\n  \"fixed 50L, max 8 concurrent.\" If the gap is closed by concentration or leverage, the\n  quoted Sharpe 1.1 and max DD \u22127.9% are ALSO not audit-safe. Non-material to the VERDICT\n  (the portfolio numbers are explicitly \"below the register bar\"), but it is an\n  unreconciled ~2x, not merely an unstated rule \u2014 state the sizing rule so CAGR/Sharpe/DD\n  are auditable.\n- **[resolution] Placebo grid.** 200 baskets gives ~0.005 p-resolution and non-trivial\n  sampling error on the 92nd percentile; \"p \u2248 0.08\" is a point estimate with a band\n  (~0.04-0.12 by binomial SE). Doesn't change \"marginal\"; quote the empirical rank rather\n  than a smoothed percentile.\n- **[verify] Entry price = real open.** Confirm \"next session's OPEN\" is pinned to the\n  first bar \u2265 09:15, not the 09:00 pre-open auction print (project landmine: corrupts ~94%\n  of days). The \"first-15-minute volume\" filter suggests awareness of the 09:15 window;\n  likely fine, but pin the field.\n- **[verify] EPS vintage.** Confirm the EPS is the as-first-reported number tied to\n  `available_date`, not a later-restated/final value (restatement = subtle lookahead).\n- **[verify] Imputed dates.** 13.8% of source `available_date`s are non-exact; even within\n  2021-25, spot-check that the imputed ones are not quarter-end + fixed-lag.\n- **[asymmetry] Exit realism.** Entries are dropped on upper-circuit/zero-volume (correct),\n  but exits are \"10th-session close, no exceptions\" with no lower-circuit / zero-volume\n  guard. Immaterial for liquid NIFTY-200 names; slightly optimistic in the tail.\n- **[signal fragility] Threshold \u03c3.** \"One trend residual sigma\" off a 4-point trailing\n  regression estimates \u03c3 with ~2 d.o.f. \u2014 very noisy; and threshold (1\u03c3), lookback (4Q),\n  hold (10 sessions) are free parameters. The reported percentile/t are single-config,\n  in-sample, not yet deflated for trials \u2014 exactly the sensitivity/DSR battery's job. Fine\n  to defer, but it compounds with the marginal significance above.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS \u2014 numbered\n\n1. **Headline significance (t-stat 3.4) is overstated by ~2x.** The t treats the 412\n   trades as i.i.d. with per-trade std 2.5% (2.5/\u221a412 \u2192 t = 3.41), but the memo's own\n   matched placebo battery implies a basket-mean spread of 0.36/z(0.92) \u2248 0.257% \u2014 about\n   double the 0.123% the t assumes. Whether the driver is trade non-independence\n   (earnings-season clustering + overlapping 10-session/8-concurrent windows) or an\n   understated per-trade std (a plain single-name 10-session long realizes ~5-6%, and the\n   placebo back-out gives ~5.2%, vs the 2.5% used) \u2014 both cannot be separated from the\n   text, and both converge on **t \u2248 1.6 / p \u2248 0.08**. Mechanism: the standard error is\n   built on a spread ~2x too small, converting a marginal (92nd-percentile, p \u2248 0.08)\n   result into a false p \u2248 0.0003. Fix: drop the i.i.d. t; headline the placebo /\n   block-bootstrap (or clustered / Newey-West) significance, and reconcile the reported\n   2.5% per-trade std against the ~5% the placebo implies (or disclose the neutralization\n   that produced 2.5%).\n\nNo other independent material defect. The CAGR ~2x reconciliation gap and the signal-design\nfragility are real but non-material to the \"do NOT register; advance to the sensitivity\nbattery\" verdict, which stands.\n\n----- ANSWER O03c -----\n# T03 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT\n\n**Sleeve:** Post-earnings positive-surprise long (NIFTY-200, 2021-2025).\n**Reviews consolidated:** Arjun Rao (Head of Quant) + Nikhil Bose (Red Team).\n**Adjudicator:** Rajan Mehta, CIO. Evidence = memo text + the two review texts only (no file/code access; lineage claims flagged UNVERIFIED). All arithmetic derived from the memo's own stated numbers. Tags: [DATA] = memo-stated or memo-arithmetic, [INFERENCE] = extrapolation, [OPINION] = judgment.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT the advance-to-sensitivity-battery request. PARK the sleeve pending three cheap kill-shots.\n\n**Rationale (3 lines).**\n1. The submission reports two mutually contradictory significance numbers (t=3.4 and 92nd-percentile placebo); the honest one \u2014 the turnover- and holding-matched placebo \u2014 says p\u22480.08, borderline and BELOW the 95% bar. \"Entry edge appears real against matched nulls\" is unsupported by the memo's own test. [DATA]\n2. On the memo's own figures the sleeve is net-NEGATIVE (\u22120.08%/trade) under the firm's MANDATORY 2\u00d7 cost stress, which was not run. [DATA]\n3. No battery budget is authorized: the case is already killable with three cheap checks (cluster/block bootstrap SE, 2\u00d7 cost + event-impact, per-trade histogram); spending sensitivity-battery budget before those is premature and out of policy. [OPINION]\n\nI concur with both reviewers' bottom line (FRAGILE, do not advance) and with the author's own restraint (no direct register entry). But I go one step harder than the memo: it is not merely \"below the register bar,\" it is **net-negative under required stress and not significant at 95% on its own null** \u2014 so it does not earn the sensitivity battery yet.\n\n---\n\n## ADJUDICATION OF THE TWO REVIEWS\n\n**Where they agree, and it holds:** the t=3.4-vs-92nd-percentile contradiction (the decisive catch), the omitted 2\u00d7 cost stress turning the sleeve negative, and the missing honest-trials disclosure. All confirmed by independent arithmetic in both reviews. These survive.\n\n**The one intra-review conflict, resolved in Red Team's favour:** Arjun's defect 4 (\"per-trade std 2.5% is implausibly low \u2192 an independent second route to t\u22481.7\") is **DROPPED as material.** Nikhil's refutation is correct and I adopt it:\n- It is not independent of defect 1 \u2014 it *contradicts* it. The placebo basket-mean spread (\u22480.256%, backed out from the 92nd percentile) is a single fixed anchor. IF per-trade std \u2248 2.5%, you get the overlap penalty (defect 1 holds, defect 4 false). IF std \u2248 5%, then naive SE = 5%/\u221a412 \u2248 0.246% \u2248 the observed spread, so there is almost NO overlap penalty (defect 4 holds, defect 1 false). High dispersion and a high overlap penalty are mutually exclusive readings of the same number; presenting them as stacking to t\u22481.7 is double-counting. [DATA]\n- The memo's own internal consistency corroborates std \u2248 2.5%, not 5%: win rate \u2248 \u03a6(mean/std) \u2192 \u03a6(0.42/2.5) = 56.7% \u2248 the reported 58%; std=5% would give \u03a6(0.084) = 53.3%, inconsistent with 58%. The mean/std/win-rate triple hangs together at 2.5%. [DATA]\n- Therefore the correct mechanism is **overlap/clustering (defect 1)**, not a mislabeled std. Arjun's defect 4 is downgraded to a robustness ask (produce the per-trade histogram to rule out tail-concentration/variance-compression) \u2014 NOT a confirmed material defect and NOT a second significance route.\n\n**Sequencing correction (also adopted from Red Team):** DSR/PBO are OUTPUTS of the sensitivity battery and gate register ENTRY, not battery entry. The memo already declines register entry, so demanding full DSR machinery now gates the wrong step. What is owed *now* is the cheap honest-trials COUNT plus the three kill-shots \u2014 not the expensive battery.\n\n**Red Team catch both parties under-weighted (kept):** the one-day-lag result is mis-sold as reassuring. 0.42\u21920.31 is a 26% edge loss from a single day \u2014 the edge lives in the immediate post-open print, exactly the gapped/wide-spread/high-participation fill that is hardest and dearest. That is a tradability fragility that compounds the cost defect, even though (correctly) it shows no lookahead collapse. Kept as material.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS THAT SURVIVE\n\n**D1 \u2014 Overstated significance / internal contradiction (the decisive defect).**\nClaim: \"412 trades ... t-stat 3.4 ... sits at the 92nd percentile of the placebo ... entry edge appears real against matched nulls.\"\nMechanism: t = 0.42/(2.5/\u221a412) = 3.4 assumes 412 i.i.d. trades. But 10-session holds overlap heavily and earnings arrive in ~4 reporting clusters/year, so concurrent trades share a large common market/beta component \u2014 effective independent N \u226a 412. Proof from the memo's own numbers: excess over placebo = 0.42 \u2212 0.06 = 0.36%; a t=3.4 world would place the strategy at ~99.8th percentile, not the 92nd (z\u22481.40). For 0.36% to sit at 1.40\u03c3, the empirical basket-mean std must be \u2248 0.36/1.40 = 0.256% \u2248 2.1\u00d7 the naive SE (0.123%), implying variance inflation \u2248 4.3, effective N \u2248 95, and an honest t \u2248 3.4/2.1 \u2248 1.6. The i.i.d. t-stat and the placebo cannot both be true; the placebo is the correct certificate and says p\u22480.08. [DATA]\nFix: never quote the i.i.d. t-stat. Report significance via block/cluster bootstrap (cluster by reporting window AND by overlapping-holding block) or a cluster-robust/Newey-West SE. State \"borderline, not significant at 95% (p\u22480.08).\" Strike \"appears real against matched nulls.\"\n\n**D2 \u2014 Mandatory 2\u00d7 cost stress omitted; sleeve is net-negative under it.**\nClaim: \"Mean net edge +0.42% ... costs 25bp per side.\"\nMechanism: 25bp \u00d7 2 sides = 50bp embedded round-trip, so gross edge \u2248 0.92% \u2014 cost already consumes more than half. Firm protocol requires a 2\u00d7 cost stress, not run here; at 100bp round-trip net = 0.92 \u2212 1.00 = **\u22120.08%/trade, negative.** Base 25bp/side is also optimistic for buying the next-session OPEN into a gapped, high-attention, wide-spread post-surprise print, where impact runs above the large/midcap standard. [DATA]\nFix: run the 2\u00d7 stress and event-day participation-scaled impact per COST_STANDARDS. Note (Red Team refinement, kept): the *selection excess* (0.36%) is cost-invariant \u2014 doubling costs shifts real and placebo down equally \u2014 so the borderline selection SIGNAL survives cost-doubling, but the TRADEABLE standalone return goes negative. Neither the signal (p\u22480.08) nor the tradability (\u22120.08%) clears the bar.\n\n**D3 \u2014 One-day-lag decay is a tradability fragility mischaracterized as \"graceful.\"**\nClaim: \"lagging every input one extra day degrades +0.42% \u2192 +0.31% (graceful decay, no collapse).\"\nMechanism: a 26% edge loss from a SINGLE day concentrates the edge in the immediate post-open move \u2014 the hardest, most expensive fill. Realizable edge is therefore likely BELOW 0.42% before any cost stress, and the lag pushes the number further under the 0.50% embedded cost. The \"no lookahead collapse\" reading is legitimate FOR THE LOOKAHEAD PURPOSE, but the memo's \"graceful\" framing hides a material tradability concern that compounds D2. [INFERENCE]\nFix: model realistic fill delay/participation at the open; re-state the lag result as a fragility finding, not reassurance.\n\n**D4 \u2014 No honest-trials disclosure; significance cannot be cited as edge.**\nClaim: threshold \"one trend residual sigma,\" lookback \"trailing-4-quarter,\" holding \"10th session\" \u2014 three free parameters presented without a trials count.\nMechanism: these read like searched parameters. Even the unadjusted placebo is only 92nd pct; any multiplicity adjustment pushes an already-borderline result lower. Without an honest-trials count the p\u22480.08 is uninterpretable and the placebo/t-stat may not be cited as evidence of edge. [OPINION]\nFix (sequencing corrected): disclose NOW, cheaply, every threshold/lookback/holding combination evaluated. DSR>0.95 (honest trials) and PBO<25% are battery OUTPUTS that gate register entry \u2014 correctly deferred, not required to enter the battery.\n\n**D5 \u2014 Residual-\u03c3 estimation window must be proven PIT (the one remaining lookahead surface). [confirm-or-fail, D-028]**\nClaim: signal fires when EPS exceeds trend by \">one trend residual sigma.\"\nMechanism: if the residual sigma is estimated over the full sample (or any window including the tested quarter) rather than trailing-only, that is in-sample-normalization lookahead (T-class, LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS). The `available_date` timestamping correctly defuses the classic quarter-end lookahead, and the one-day-lag test shows no hard-collapse signature \u2014 so there is no smoking gun \u2014 but per D-028 no gate passes without the \u03c3-window audited. This is the single place leakage could still hide. [INFERENCE]\nFix: confirm \u03c3 computed strictly trailing-only, PIT. If full-sample, the result is contaminated and dies here.\n\n---\n\n## REFUTED (removed from the material list)\n\n- **Arjun's defect 4** \u2014 \"per-trade std 2.5% implausibly low \u2192 independent second route to t\u22481.7.\" Self-contradicting with D1 (mutually exclusive explanations of the 0.256% placebo anchor; cannot stack) and disproven by the memo's win-rate internal consistency (\u03a6(0.42/2.5)=56.7% \u2248 reported 58%; std=5% \u2192 53.3%). **Downgraded to a robustness ask:** produce the per-trade return histogram to exclude tail-concentration / variance-compression. Not a defect, not a significance route.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL / CONFIRM ITEMS (clearly labelled)\n\n- **Circuit-lock drops are conservative, not a fakeness lever [capacity, non-material].** Dropping the 9 upper-circuit-locked opens removes the strongest positive-surprise gap-ups \u2014 the likely biggest winners \u2014 so it biases the reported edge DOWNWARD. Real tradability fact: the best signals are unfillable. Flag so nobody later \"adds back\" those winners.\n- **Isolate 2022 standalone [robustness].** The one in-sample bear window is bundled into \"2021-22\" and masked. Show it alone. (The 2021-2025 window restriction itself is defensible \u2014 publication coverage \u2014 but the surviving charter regime slices are thin.)\n- **Isolate the ~86%-exact-date `available_date` subset [robustness].** The ~14% imputed subset carries sub-day-to-multi-day timing error; the one-day-lag test covers only 1 day of it. Confirm the edge holds on the exact-date subset.\n- **Confirm \"next session OPEN\" = first real bar \u226509:15, not the 09:00 pre-open auction print (landmine #2) [confirm].** The first-15-min-volume filter suggests intraday data is in hand, so likely fine \u2014 state it.\n- **Confirm the placebo draws random NAMES on the real trades' calendar DATES [confirm \u2014 could become material].** If placebo entries are uniformly random in TIME instead, the beta/timing baseline differs from the earnings-season-clustered real book and the null is not clean. \"Same universe-dates\" is ambiguous; if it draws random dates, D1's certificate itself is voided.\n- **State the >8-concurrent rationing rule [confirm \u2014 could become material].** When >8 signals fire in a cluster (the norm), the rule for choosing which 8 is unspecified. \"Best-8 by surprise magnitude\" is an undisclosed parameter and a mild forward-peek; first-8/random is fine. State it.\n- **Per-trade return histogram [robustness].** From the D4-refutation downgrade \u2014 rule out tail-concentration/variance-compression.\n\n---\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT (CIO lens)\n\n- **Structure is diversification-poor, which the reported DD understates.** Earnings cluster in ~4 seasons/year and the sleeve runs up to 8 concurrent longs \u2014 those 8 are entered in the same 2-3 week window and share a large common market/beta component (this is the very correlation that inflates D1's t-stat). The reported max DD \u22127.9% is produced by the same engine that treats trades as independent, so the true **correlated-cluster drawdown is likely worse than \u22127.9%.** [INFERENCE]\n- **Worst single-day / correlated-blowup scenario:** a macro risk-off session (Fed/RBI shock, global risk event) landing mid-reporting-season hits all 8 concurrent longs at once \u2014 8 correlated single-name longs, not a diversified basket. Diversification is the only reliable tail defense and this sleeve does not have it within a cluster. [OPINION]\n- **Firm lesson applies directly:** the worst blowups came from calm-looking large-caps whose pre-trade signals did not flag the tail. A positive earnings surprise is precisely a \"calm-looking,\" bullish signal \u2014 it does not flag the left tail; only sizing and diversification would, and here neither is demonstrated.\n- **Worst month:** a reporting-season cluster coinciding with a market drawdown \u2192 all concurrent longs down together; \u22127.9% is optimistic against the ignored correlation.\n\n**Net:** even if the edge were real, the concurrency structure is a tail-liability, not a tail-defense. This alone would force a hard concentration/correlation cap before any sizing.\n\n---\n\n## SIZING RULING\n\nNo sizing. This is a research gate, not a position decision \u2014 no capital, no register entry. **Firewall discipline (IC-1 precedent):** do NOT anchor any future sizing to the paper +0.42%. The number that could ever matter is the incremental edge over the matched null \u2014 0.36% at p\u22480.08 \u2014 not the 0.42% headline, and even that is net-negative under mandated 2\u00d7 costs. Register incremental, not headline.\n\n---\n\n## KILL CRITERIA + REVIEW DATE\n\nRun three CHEAP kill-shots first (days, not weeks; no battery budget authorized):\n1. Block/cluster bootstrap significance (cluster by reporting window + overlapping-holding block).\n2. 2\u00d7 cost stress + event-day participation-scaled impact.\n3. Per-trade net-return histogram.\n\n- **KILL/PARK** if bootstrap significance fails to clear 95% (expected ~1.4-1.6\u03c3).\n- **KILL** if net stays negative under 2\u00d7 costs + event impact (currently \u22120.08%).\n- **KILL** if the histogram shows the +0.42% is carried by a handful of trades (tail-concentration).\n- **KILL** if the placebo turns out to draw random DATES rather than the real trades' calendar dates (voids the null), OR the residual-\u03c3 is full-sample (lookahead).\n- **FLIP-TO-REAL requires ALL THREE, not any one:** (a) cluster/block bootstrap clears 95%; (b) net-positive under 2\u00d7 costs + event impact; (c) DSR>0.95 on an honest-trials count with PBO<25% \u2014 the last two being the battery's job, only earned after (a) and (b) survive.\n\n**Review date:** on completion of the three cheap kill-shots. Re-gate then; battery budget released only if (a) and (b) both survive.\n\n---\n\n## DISSENTS (recorded by name)\n\n- **Arjun Rao (Head of Quant):** filed defect 4 (std 2.5% implausibly low \u2192 second route to t\u22481.7). **OVERRULED** by CIO on Red Team's refutation (mutually exclusive with defect 1; disproven by the memo's win-rate consistency). Downgraded to a robustness histogram ask. Arjun also sequenced DSR/PBO as pre-advance; corrected to battery-output/register-entry gates. His central catch (D1) is adopted as the decisive defect and credited.\n- **Nikhil Bose (Red Team):** no dissent from the consolidated verdict; his audit (refuting defect 4, correcting DSR sequencing, surfacing the 26% one-day-lag fragility, cost-invariance of the selection excess, and the residual-\u03c3 window) is adopted in full. AP-relevant catches credited.\n- Both reviewers concur with the bottom line: FRAGILE, do NOT advance to the sensitivity battery.\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED LIST \u2014 CLAIMED MATERIAL DEFECTS\n\n1. **Overstated significance / internal contradiction.** t=3.4 assumes 412 i.i.d. trades; overlapping 10-session windows + earnings clustering make effective N \u2248 95. The memo's own placebo (92nd pct \u2248 1.40\u03c3, p\u22480.08) is inconsistent with t=3.4 (which implies ~99.8th pct) and is the honest certificate. Fix: report block/cluster-bootstrap significance; state p\u22480.08, not significant at 95%; never quote the i.i.d. t-stat; strike \"appears real against matched nulls.\" (Subsumes prior review defects 1 and 2.)\n\n2. **Mandatory 2\u00d7 cost stress omitted; sleeve is net-negative under it.** Gross \u2248 0.92% \u21d2 at 100bp round-trip, net = \u22120.08%/trade. Base 25bp/side also optimistic for a next-open post-surprise gapped fill. Fix: run 2\u00d7 stress + event-day participation-scaled impact per COST_STANDARDS. The 0.36% selection excess is cost-invariant but only p\u22480.08, so neither signal nor tradability clears the bar.\n\n3. **One-day-lag decay is a tradability fragility, mischaracterized as \"graceful.\"** 0.42\u21920.31 = 26% edge loss from one day \u2192 edge concentrated in the immediate open print (hardest/dearest fill), implying realizable edge < reported before any stress. Fix: model realistic fill delay/participation at the open; re-state the lag result as a fragility finding.\n\n4. **No honest-trials disclosure.** Threshold (1\u03c3), lookback (4Q), holding (10 sessions) are free parameters; significance cannot be cited as edge without a trials count. Fix: disclose all combinations evaluated now (cheap). DSR>0.95 / PBO<25% are battery outputs that gate register entry \u2014 correctly deferred, not required to enter the battery. (Prior review defect 5, sequencing corrected.)\n\n5. **Residual-\u03c3 estimation window unproven PIT (D-028 lookahead surface).** If the \"one trend residual sigma\" threshold is estimated full-sample (or including the tested quarter), it is in-sample-normalization lookahead. No smoking gun (available_date correct, lag test no collapse) but must be audited before any advance. Fix: confirm \u03c3 computed strictly trailing-only, PIT; if full-sample, the result is contaminated.\n\n**Refuted / dropped (not material):** prior-review defect 4 (per-trade std \"implausibly low, second route to t\u22481.7\") \u2014 self-contradicting with defect 1 and disproven by the memo's win-rate consistency. Downgraded to a robustness ask (produce the per-trade histogram).\n\n**Recommendation:** Do NOT spend sensitivity-battery budget. Run the three cheap kill-shots first. On the memo's own numbers, honest significance is ~1.4-1.6\u03c3 and the edge is net-negative under required stress; these checks will most likely PARK or KILL the sleeve before the expensive battery. VERDICT: REJECT the advance; PARK pending cheap kill-shots.\n\n----- ANSWER O03d -----\n# T03 review \u2014 post-earnings positive-surprise long, results memo (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: material defects present.** The signal-timing hygiene (available_date, next-open\nentry, one-day-lag test) is done right, but the headline **significance is inflated**, the\nmemo's **own placebo contradicts its own t-stat by an order of magnitude**, and the\n**portfolio CAGR does not reconcile with the per-trade edge** under the stated sizing. The\ncautious verdict (\"advance to sensitivity, not register\") is directionally sensible, but its\nstated premise \u2014 \"entry edge appears real against matched nulls\" \u2014 is **not established by\nthe numbers given**.\n\nAll figures below reproduced from the memo's own inputs (mean +0.42%, per-trade sd 2.5%,\nn=412, placebo mean +0.06%, 92nd percentile).\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 1 \u2014 t-stat 3.4 is invalid; trades are not independent, and the memo's own placebo proves it\n\nThe reported **t-stat 3.4** is `mean / (sd/\u221an) = 0.42% / (2.5%/\u221a412) = 0.42%/0.123% = 3.41`.\nThat formula assumes **412 independent trades**. They are not:\n\n- Max **8 concurrent positions**, each held **10 sessions**, over 5 years \u2192 trades overlap\n  heavily in calendar time and share the same market/sector moves. Effective sample size is\n  far below 412.\n- Earnings cluster in results-season windows (Jan-Feb / Apr-May / Jul-Aug / Oct-Nov), so\n  entries are batched, not spread \u2014 overlap and common-factor exposure are structural, not\n  incidental.\n\n**The memo's own placebo forces this conclusion, and the two significance numbers are\nmutually contradictory:**\n\n- If the naive SE (0.123%) were the true dispersion, the strategy would sit **2.92\u03c3 above the\n  placebo mean \u2192 ~99.8th percentile**, i.e. it should rank #1 of 200 placebo baskets.\n- The memo instead reports the strategy at the **92nd percentile** (\u224816 of 200 placebos beat\n  it). For that to be true, the placebo distribution of basket-means must have\n  **sd \u2248 0.256%**, which is **2.08\u00d7 the naive SE**. That implies an **effective N \u2248 95**, not 412.\n- Re-deriving the t-stat with the dispersion the placebo actually reveals:\n  **t \u2248 1.6 vs zero, \u2248 1.4 vs the placebo mean \u2192 one-sided p \u2248 0.08.** Marginal, not \"3.4.\"\n\nSo the trade log's real, overlap-adjusted significance is roughly what the placebo says\n(~92nd pct, p\u22480.08), and the quoted **t-stat 3.4 overstates significance by ~2\u00d7**. The memo\nprints both as if both support the case; they cannot both be right, and the honest one is the\nweaker one.\n\n**Fix:** discard the i.i.d. t-stat. Report significance with calendar-time-clustered /\noverlap-robust SEs (Newey-West at the ~10-day horizon, or a block/calendar-block bootstrap),\nor simply lead with the placebo percentile. Expect a t\u22481.4-1.6, p\u22480.08.\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 2 \u2014 \"appears real against matched nulls\" overstates a 92nd-percentile result\n\n92nd percentile against the matched null is **one-sided p \u2248 0.08 \u2014 it does NOT reject at the\nconventional 5% level.** The correct reading of the placebo battery is \"**fails to clear the\nmatched null at 95%**,\" i.e. marginal/inconclusive, not \"appears real.\" Also note the raw\n+0.42% is **+0.36% over the random-long placebo (+0.06%)** \u2014 ~0.06%/trade of the headline is\njust \"being long for 10 sessions,\" not signal. Reporting +0.42% as \"the edge\" overstates the\nsignal's own contribution by ~15%. The verdict's foundational sentence should read: entry\nedge is **marginal (p\u22480.08) and below register bar**, which strengthens (not weakens) the\n\"don't register yet\" recommendation.\n\n**Fix:** state the matched-null result as p\u22480.08 (does not reject at 5%) and quote the\nexcess-over-placebo (+0.36%) as the signal contribution.\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 3 \u2014 portfolio CAGR 9.8% does not reconcile with the per-trade edge under the stated sizing (and DD -7.9% then looks too low)\n\nWith 412 trades / 5 yr = 82.4 trades/yr, 10-session holds \u2192 **avg 3.3 concurrent positions**\n(82.4\u00d710/250). Under the stated \"Rs 50L notional, max 8 concurrent\" at a flat 50L/8 = Rs\n6.25L per slot, cash-drag included:\n\n- Return \u2248 `82.4 \u00d7 0.42% \u00d7 (6.25/50) \u2248 **4.3%/yr**` \u2014 less than half the claimed **9.8%**.\n\nTo reach 9.8% you need **near-full deployment** \u2014 recycling the whole Rs 50L every 10 sessions\n(~25\u00d7/yr \u00d7 0.42% = 10.5%/yr). But full deployment with only ~3.3 avg concurrent signals means\neach position is ~Rs 15L and the book is routinely concentrated in **1-3 midcaps at a time**,\nwhich (a) contradicts the \"max 8 concurrent / diversified\" framing and (b) is **inconsistent\nwith a -7.9% max drawdown** across 2021-2025 (a window containing the 2022 drawdown and the\nJan-2023 Adani shock). One of {per-trade edge, CAGR 9.8%, max DD -7.9%} is wrong given the\nsizing, or idle-cash T-bill yield has been folded into \"strategy return\" (which would be a\nmisattribution). This is not load-bearing for the entry-edge verdict, but it is an\nunreconciled internal inconsistency that a gate reviewer must not wave through.\n\n**Fix:** publish the exact sizing rule and the average % capital deployed, then re-derive\nCAGR/Sharpe/DD; report deployed-capital return separately from any cash yield.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled \u2014 not defects)\n\n- **First-15-min-volume filter is mild lookahead:** entry is \"at the OPEN,\" but the skip\n  decision uses 09:15-09:30 volume that isn't known at the open. Direction-wise it only\n  *drops* 9 untradeable entries, so immaterial \u2014 but tighten to a prior-day ADV / pre-open\n  liquidity gate to be clean.\n- **\"OPEN\" price definition unstated:** confirm it is the 09:15 real open, not the 09:00\n  pre-open auction print (auction-gap landmine). Can't verify from the memo.\n- **SUE built on a 4-quarter trend, not a seasonal (YoY) model:** quarterly EPS is seasonal;\n  the classic SUE is a seasonal random walk (EPS_q vs EPS_{q-4}+drift). The trend version\n  adds noise but doesn't corrupt PIT-ness. Methodological, not fatal.\n- **Confirm trend + residual-sigma are estimated PIT** (past quarters only, up to\n  available_date), not full-sample; if full-sample, that's a lookahead to fix.\n- **Denominator check is vacuous for an equity sleeve** (there is no premium/net-debit) \u2014\n  harmless but not evidence of anything.\n- **Semiannual Mar/Sep constituent snapshots** are fine for slow-moving NIFTY-200; negligible\n  membership drift risk.\n- One-day-lag test (0.42\u21920.31, no collapse) correctly **passes** the lookahead check;\n  front-loading of the drift into session 1 is expected for PEAD.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n\n1. **Inflated t-stat (3.4).** Computed with an i.i.d. assumption that overlapping 10-session /\n   \u22648-concurrent / results-season-clustered trades violate. The memo's own placebo implies\n   dispersion ~2.08\u00d7 the naive SE (effective N\u224895), so the overlap-adjusted t is ~1.4-1.6\n   (p\u22480.08). The 3.4 is roughly 2\u00d7 too large. Fix: overlap-robust SE / block bootstrap.\n2. **Contradiction between t-stat and placebo, unreconciled + misread.** A t=3.4 would place\n   the strategy at the ~99.8th placebo percentile (rank #1 of 200); the memo reports 92nd\n   (p\u22480.08). \"Appears real against matched nulls\" overstates a result that **does not reject\n   the matched null at 5%**; the honest read is \"marginal, below bar.\" (Also: +0.06%/trade of\n   the +0.42% is placebo/long-exposure, so signal contribution is ~+0.36%.)\n3. **Portfolio CAGR 9.8% doesn't reconcile with the per-trade edge.** Stated flat sizing\n   (50L/8, ~3.3 avg concurrent) yields ~4.3%/yr; 9.8% requires near-full deployment\n   (~1-3 name concentration) that contradicts the \"max 8\" framing and makes the -7.9% max DD\n   implausible. Requires an explicit sizing/deployment disclosure and re-derivation.", "ids": ["O03a", "O03b", "O03c", "O03d"]}, {"tid": "T04", "key": "## T04 \u2014 lookahead \u2014 quarter-end vs available_date, T1 (CRITICAL)\n**Defect.** Portfolio formed on \"the first trading day AFTER the quarter ends\" using\nthat quarter's revenue. Indian quarterly results are published up to ~45 days after\nquarter end; the screen sorts on numbers nobody has yet. Any announcement-day\nrepricing of the surprise is captured illegitimately.\n**Line.** `rebal_day = close.index[close.index.searchsorted(qe, side=\"right\")]`\ndriven by `quarter_end`, with no publication-date column anywhere in the code.\n**Fix.** Join on `available_date` (publication date) via `merge_asof(...,\ndirection=\"backward\")`, or lag quarter_end by the statutory 45 days; rebalance only\non data already public.\n**Accept.** \"Trades on quarter-end date but results come out ~45 days later\";\n\"missing available_date / publication-lag \u2014 PIT violation\"; \"earnings lookahead:\nuses the quarter's fundamentals before the filing exists\".\n**Secondary.** Revenue YoY on positive bases is fine (no base-effect issue here);\nmembership handling is correct as written.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O04a -----\n# CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT \u2014 T04 \"quarterly revenue-growth rotation\"\nRajan Mehta (CIO, E-001). Consolidating: Arjun Rao (Head of Quant) + Nikhil Bose (Red Team, E-014).\nConstraint honored: code + write-up only, no file access, no execution. All file-lineage claims tagged [INFERENCE]; code-evident mechanics tagged [DATA-evident].\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT (result is lookahead-contaminated / uncertifiable)\n\n**Rationale (3 lines):**\n1. The backtest selects on `quarter_end` (self-labeled \"fiscal quarter end date\") and trades at `qe+1`, i.e. it acts on quarterly revenue ~45\u201360 days before it is legally public under SEBI LODR \u2014 textbook Landmine #3. Fatal on its own.\n2. Two further upward biases exist independent of the lookahead: delisting losses are silently dropped from the return path (`skipna=True`), and the entire 8.8pp spread rests on a benchmark (\"12.9% EW\") that appears nowhere in the code.\n3. The 21.7% vs 12.9% headline is therefore not trustworthy and cannot be sized. The strategy family (fundamental/revenue momentum) is a real published factor and may retain edge after correction \u2014 but that is unproven until the re-run.\n\n**Weakest assumption (the one that kills it):** that a company's revenue for the quarter ending `qe` is tradable on the first session after `qe`. It is not \u2014 it is public only on the filing/`available_date`, ~45\u201360 days later.\n\n---\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT (why REJECT, not RESIZE)\n- **Worst single-day / month:** unknowable from this submission \u2014 no per-trade series, no regime slices, no drawdown reported. A single full-sample CAGR conceals the tail entirely.\n- **Correlated-blowup scenario:** `nlargest(30, rev_yoy)` concentrates the basket in low-base / turnaround / recently-inorganic (M&A) names. That is precisely the illiquid, high-beta, event-driven tail that goes no-bid together in a risk-off quarter. Sizing off a lookahead-inflated CAGR would allocate real capital to a basket whose true edge is unknown and whose left tail is unpriced \u2014 the exact failure mode in our Lessons Learned (calm-looking headline, unpriced tail).\n- **Sizing ruling:** ZERO. Not resizable. A fake numerator cannot be scaled down into a safe number.\n\n**Kill criteria / path to reopen:** re-run rebalancing off per-name `available_date+1` (PIT earnings dataset) with `lib/lookahead_audit.py` + one-day-lag placebo; fix return-path NaN handling; show the EW benchmark built from the IDENTICAL PIT universe each period; confirm the revenue panel carries delisted names with as-reported values. IF a material spread then survives walk-forward + DSR (honest trials) + PBO + regime slices (2018/2020/2022/2024) with per-trade reporting \u2014 reopen. **Review date:** on resubmission only.\n\n---\n\n## SURVIVING MATERIAL DEFECTS (adjudicated \u2014 kept only what survives)\n\n**1. Fundamental-data lookahead \u2014 FATAL. [DATA-evident]**\n- Where: `snap = rev[rev[\"quarter_end\"] == qe]` then `rebal_day = close.index[close.index.searchsorted(qe, side=\"right\")]` (\"rebalance on the first trading day AFTER the quarter ends\").\n- Mechanism: `quarter_end` is the fiscal period-end, not the public/filing date. Under SEBI LODR quarterly results land within 45 days (Q4/annual up to 60). Trading at `qe+1` front-runs each company's own results \u2014 you hold the top revenue growers through the pre-announcement drift, the announcement reaction, and PEAD, using information the market did not have. Inflates CAGR directly. This is Landmine #3 and it disqualifies the number independent of everything else.\n- Fix: rebalance per-name off `available_date+1` (`datasets/earnings_pit/unified_quarterly_pit.parquet`) \u2014 names become eligible on a ROLLING basis, not all on `qe+1`. Run `lib/lookahead_audit.py` + one-day-lag test.\n- Dissent recorded: Arjun asserts \"most of the 8.8pp evaporates.\" **CIO ruling:** that magnitude is [INFERENCE, unproven] \u2014 Nikhil is right to flag it. The defect is fatal regardless of how much edge survives; only the `available_date` re-run settles the residual.\n\n**2. Return-path NaN-skip hides delisting losses \u2014 upward P&L survivorship bias. [DATA-evident mechanism; magnitude INFERENCE]**\n- Where: `port = (weights.shift(1) * ret).sum(axis=1)`.\n- Mechanism: pandas `.sum` defaults to `skipna=True`. When a HELD name delists mid-quarter its adjusted close \u2192 NaN \u2192 `ret` NaN \u2192 its `1/30` contribution NaN, which `.sum` silently treats as ZERO. The delisting loss is never booked and the book runs under-invested that day. Direction is unambiguously upward. This is DISTINCT from the panel/benchmark issue (#3) \u2014 it lives in the return computation regardless of how the panel is built.\n- Fix: reindex held names to a fully-populated return matrix, book the delisting as the realized terminal return (proceeds/last price), and assert daily invested weight sums to 1 (or hold explicit cash for the gap).\n- Provenance: caught by Nikhil (Red Team); MISSED by Arjun. Credited.\n\n**3. Revenue-panel survivorship + entirely-unshown benchmark. [INFERENCE]**\n- Where: `snap = rev[rev[\"quarter_end\"] == qe]` gated by `members.asof(qe)`; benchmark \"universe EW 12.9%\" \u2014 nowhere in the code.\n- Mechanism: (a) PIT membership fixes the universe LIST, not the FUNDAMENTALS PANEL. If `quarterly_revenue.parquet` is survivor-only, a name in NIFTY 500 at `qe` that later delisted simply has no row at that `qe` and is silently dropped from the cross-section \u2014 survivorship reintroduced through the back door. (b) Worse: the \"12.9% EW\" comparator is an unshown assertion. The entire 8.8pp spread rests on a benchmark whose construction and universe cannot be checked; if it uses a different (survivor-inclusive/exclusive) universe than the strategy, the spread is not apples-to-apples.\n- Fix: confirm the panel includes delisted symbols with correct `available_date`s; build the EW benchmark from the IDENTICAL PIT universe each period and show the code.\n- Provenance: raised by Arjun (his #5, buried); ELEVATED by Nikhil. CIO concurs with the elevation \u2014 an unshown benchmark under a headline \"excess return\" claim is a first-order gap.\n\n**4. Return/cost accounting inconsistency \u2014 material implementation bug, second-order magnitude. [DATA-evident]**\n- Where: `weights.loc[rebal_day:, held] = 1.0/len(held)` (held flat, no drift) \u2192 `port = (weights.shift(1)*ret).sum(axis=1)`; cost via `turnover_costs(weights, bps_per_side=40)`.\n- Mechanism: constant `1/30` weights \u00d7 daily returns reprice the basket to equal-weight EVERY day \u2014 an implicit DAILY rebalance that harvests an uncosted volatility/diversification premium \u2014 while `turnover_costs` only charges on quarterly rebalance days. So the code does NOT implement the stated quarterly buy-and-hold, and the return side gets a daily-rebal boost with zero incremental cost. Bias is upward.\n- Fix: drift weights within the quarter (true buy-and-hold) to match the quarterly cost model, OR, if daily rebal is intended, charge daily turnover. Reconcile the two sides.\n- Dissent recorded: Arjun rates this \"material\" as a headline driver; Nikhil rates the increment ~tens of bps to ~1%/yr, not a driver of an 8.8pp gap. **CIO ruling:** the mechanism is real and undisputed (a genuine bug that biases upward and misrepresents the strategy \u2014 kept as material), but its MAGNITUDE is second-order and does not, by itself, explain the spread. Both true at once.\n\n**5. YoY via positional `pct_change(4)` \u2014 conditional / material-risk. [INFERENCE on trigger]**\n- Where: `rev[\"rev_yoy\"] = rev.groupby(\"symbol\")[\"revenue\"].pct_change(4)`.\n- Mechanism: `pct_change(4)` shifts 4 ROWS, not 4 calendar quarters. It equals true 4-quarter YoY only IF every symbol's quarters are complete and regular. Any within-symbol missing/irregular quarter (common in fundamentals) or a fiscal-year-end change makes it compare mismatched periods, producing spurious growth and corrupting the top-30 rank.\n- Fix: explicit calendar join of `quarter_end` to the same fiscal quarter one year prior per symbol; drop rows where the \u22121yr quarter is absent. Do not rely on row position.\n- Dissent recorded: Arjun states this as a certain bug and adds \"fiscal-quarter heterogeneity across firms compounds this.\" **CIO ruling:** the bug is CONDITIONAL (triggers only on within-symbol gaps), not certain, without the file. And Arjun's cross-firm reasoning is WRONG \u2014 `pct_change` runs WITHIN symbol via `groupby`, so cross-firm fiscal-calendar differences cannot corrupt any single firm's YoY. Nikhil's correction is upheld. Kept as a latent/material-risk defect that must be fixed before any re-quote.\n\n**6. Restated / non-point-in-time revenue VALUE. [INFERENCE, secondary]**\n- Mechanism: even after fixing the availability DATE (#1), if `quarterly_revenue.parquet` stores FINAL/restated consolidated revenue rather than as-first-reported figures, the ranking uses numbers that differ from what was public on `available_date`. Consolidated revenue also jumps on M&A/acquisitions \u2014 manufacturing inorganic \"growth\" that concentrates the top-30 in event names.\n- Fix: use as-first-reported values; flag/deweight inorganic jumps. This is a PIT-VALUE landmine distinct from the PIT-DATE landmine in #1.\n- Provenance: Nikhil (Miss 2). Kept as a real, secondary conditional risk.\n\n---\n\n## DOWNGRADED TO NON-MATERIAL (contra Arjun's original weighting) \u2014 labelled non-material\n\n- **Liquidity/ADV filter + flat 40bp/side (Arjun's #4):** valid hygiene, but NOT material to the headline. Universe is NIFTY 500 PIT constituents (liquidity is bounded, not the micro-cap tail), and turnover is QUARTERLY \u2192 total drag \u2248 4 rebalances \u00d7 turnover \u00d7 80bp round-trip \u2248 low single-digit %/yr. A 2\u00d7 cost placebo would not touch an 8.8pp spread. High-growth names do skew smaller, so add an ex-ante liquidity screen + dynamic slippage (`lib/execution_realism.py`) + a capacity check for completeness \u2014 but this cannot fake the result. **Non-material** (Nikhil's downgrade upheld).\n\n## NON-MATERIAL NOTES (robustness / style)\n- `close.index[searchsorted(qe, \"right\")]` raises `IndexError` if `qe` \u2265 last price date \u2014 guard the tail. Both reviewers noted; valid, minor.\n- `weights` initialized to 0 before the first rebalance \u2192 leading flat days slightly dilute the annualization. Minor.\n- On a skipped quarter (`len(snap) < 60`) the prior basket persists (stale-hold). Acceptable behavior; document it.\n- Signal concentration in low-base/turnaround names is real but not an independent fake-maker \u2014 it explains WHY the lookahead in #1 pays so well (you are pre-positioned into the biggest upcoming surprises). Color, not a defect.\n\n## CREDIT WHERE DUE (done right \u2014 do NOT attack)\n- **Price-side timing is clean.** Selection uses \u2264`qe` data; `weights.shift(1)` starts returns at `rebal_day+1`; there is no same-day-close leak \u2014 a correct one-day implementation lag. Both reviewers agree; CIO confirms. The leak is entirely in the FUNDAMENTAL availability, not the price timing.\n\n## VALIDATION GAPS REQUIRED BEFORE ANY RE-QUOTE\nNo walk-forward, no DSR (with honest trials), no PBO, no regime slices (2018/2020/2022/2024), no per-trade (vs portfolio-averaged) reporting, no \u226530-trades-per-parameter check. A single full-sample CAGR is not a certifiable result even after the bugs above are fixed.\n\n---\n\n## CLAIMED MATERIAL DEFECTS (numbered \u2014 CIO consolidated)\n1. **Fundamental-data lookahead** \u2014 trades period-end revenue at `qe+1`, ~45\u201360d before public (SEBI LODR / Landmine #3). FATAL. Fix: rebalance off per-name `available_date+1`; run lookahead audit + one-day-lag test. (Arjun's \"most of 8.8pp evaporates\" is unproven [INFERENCE].)\n2. **Return-path NaN-skip** \u2014 `.sum(axis=1)` `skipna=True` drops delisting/missing-data contributions, so delisting losses are never booked \u2192 upward P&L survivorship bias. Fix: full return matrix, book terminal delisting return, assert invested weight = 1. (Nikhil catch; Arjun missed.)\n3. **Revenue-panel survivorship + entirely-unshown benchmark** [INFERENCE] \u2014 PIT membership does not fix a survivor-only fundamentals panel, and the \"12.9% EW\" comparator is nowhere in the code \u2192 the 8.8pp spread is unverifiable / possibly not apples-to-apples. Fix: confirm delisted names present with as-reported values; build EW benchmark from the identical PIT universe and show the code.\n4. **Return/cost accounting inconsistency** \u2014 constant-weight \u00d7 daily-return path is an implicit DAILY equal-weight rebalance (uncosted vol-harvesting premium) while costs are charged only quarterly; the code does not implement the stated quarterly buy-and-hold. Fix: drift weights within-quarter or cost daily turnover. (Material bug; magnitude second-order, ~tens of bps to ~1%/yr \u2014 not the headline driver.)\n5. **YoY via positional `pct_change(4)`** [INFERENCE on trigger] \u2014 shifts 4 ROWS not 4 calendar quarters; miscompares periods on any within-symbol missing/irregular quarter, corrupting ranks. Fix: calendar-aware YoY join, drop where \u22121yr quarter absent. (Conditional, not certain; Arjun's \"cross-firm fiscal heterogeneity\" reasoning is wrong \u2014 computation is within-symbol.)\n6. **Restated / non-PIT revenue VALUE** [INFERENCE, secondary] \u2014 even after the date fix, final/restated or M&A-inflated consolidated revenue \u2260 as-first-reported figures known on `available_date`. Fix: use as-first-reported values; flag inorganic jumps.\n\n(Non-material, explicitly: flat 40bp/side + no ADV filter on a quarterly-turnover NIFTY-500 basket cannot fake an 8.8pp spread \u2014 hygiene only. IndexError tail-guard, leading zero-weight dilution, and skipped-quarter stale-hold are minor. Price-side timing is correct.)\n\n----- ANSWER O04b -----\n# T04 \u2014 Final arbiter verdict: quarterly revenue-growth rotation\n\n**Verdict: the headline result is FAKE.** One fatal defect (fundamental-data lookahead) invalidates the\n21.7% CAGR on its own. Two further real defects survive as secondary (correctness-genuine, but their\nmagnitude on the headline is data-dependent and unverified from the evidence). The submission's claim\nthat \"survivorship is handled\" is over-stated and must be qualified. The price-side timing in the code is\nactually clean \u2014 that is precisely what makes the lookahead diagnosis unambiguous.\n\n---\n\n## DEFECT 1 \u2014 Fundamental-data lookahead (CONFIRMED, FATAL \u2014 this alone makes the headline fake)\n\nLine:\n```python\nrebal_day = close.index[close.index.searchsorted(qe, side=\"right\")]\n```\nwith `qe = quarter_end` (the column is documented as the fiscal quarter-end date) and the signal\n`rev_yoy` built from `revenue` stamped at that same `quarter_end`.\n\n- **Mechanism.** The basket is selected on the top-30 YoY revenue growth for the quarter ending on `qe`,\n  then traded on the **first trading day strictly after `qe`**. Under SEBI LODR, Indian quarterly results\n  are filed up to ~45 days after quarter close (annual ~60). Trading the day after quarter end on that\n  quarter's revenue uses a number the market will not see for ~6\u20139 weeks. The strategy front-runs every\n  earnings print in the sample and harvests the post-results drift / re-rating that only exists because it\n  \"knew\" the figure early. This systematically inflates CAGR.\n- **Why the diagnosis is unambiguous.** The price timing is clean:\n  `port[t] = weights.shift(1)[t]\u00b7ret[t] = weights[t-1]\u00b7(close[t]/close[t-1]-1)`. Weights first go nonzero\n  at `rebal_day`, so the first return earned is over `[rebal_day, rebal_day+1]` \u2014 entry at the `rebal_day`\n  close, no gap, no double-count, no price lookahead. Therefore the entire ~9-point gap (21.7% vs 12.9%)\n  is attributable to the fundamental being known early, not to any price-side artifact.\n- **Firm rule.** Exactly landmine #3 (\"use PIT `available_date` \u2026 NEVER quarter-end dates\") and a D-028\n  T-class lookahead.\n- **Fix.** Key `rebal_day` off the actual results-availability date \u2014 the PIT dataset\n  `datasets/earnings_pit/unified_quarterly_pit.parquet` (`available_date`, 86.2% exact), staggered\n  per-symbol; at minimum apply a conservative uniform \u226545-day lag past `quarter_end` before the signal is\n  tradable. This converts the design to a rolling (staggered) rebalance, which is correct. Expect 21.7% to\n  collapse toward or below the 12.9% benchmark.\n\n---\n\n## DEFECT 2 \u2014 `pct_change(4)` assumes a gap-free quarterly grid (real correctness bug; materiality data-dependent)\n\nLine:\n```python\nrev[\"rev_yoy\"] = rev.groupby(\"symbol\")[\"revenue\"].pct_change(4)\n```\n- **Mechanism.** `pct_change(4)` compares each row to the **4th-previous row within the symbol**, which\n  equals a true YoY only if every symbol has exactly one row per fiscal quarter with no gaps. Any\n  missing / late / stub quarter makes the base 3 or 5 quarters back, producing a distorted growth number\n  that can move names into or out of the top-30 and corrupt the ranking.\n- **Calibration (arbiter).** This is a genuine correctness defect: the computed quantity is not guaranteed\n  to be YoY. But whether it actually distorts the top-30 and the 21.7% is **not demonstrated by the\n  evidence** \u2014 if `quarterly_revenue.parquet` is a complete grid, the computation is exactly correct.\n  Grade it material-but-secondary, conditional on data completeness. The first review's flat \"corrupts the\n  ranking\" is stated with more certainty than the evidence supports.\n- **Fix.** Compute YoY by explicitly matching each `quarter_end` to the row at `quarter_end \u2212 1 year`\n  (merge on the prior-year quarter), or reindex each symbol onto a complete quarterly grid before\n  differencing; verify no symbol contributes a `rev_yoy` from a non-adjacent-year base.\n\n---\n\n## DEFECT 3 \u2014 NaN-as-zero return aggregation on delisting (real, secondary, bounded)\n\nLines:\n```python\nheld = [s for s in top if s in close.columns]\n...\nport = (weights.shift(1) * ret).sum(axis=1)\n```\n- **Mechanism.** `.sum(axis=1)` skips NaN by default. A held name that delists / is suspended / has a data\n  gap returns NaN, so it contributes **0** to that day's return while its 1/30 weight is neither realized\n  as a loss nor redeployed \u2014 the book quietly runs the dead sleeve as cash. On a \"union\" close panel this\n  biases returns upward precisely on adverse events, and PIT membership does not protect against\n  within-holding-period delisting.\n- **Calibration (arbiter).** Real and directionally upward-biasing, but bounded: because prices exist up to\n  the last traded bar, only the terminal gap (last trade \u2192 delisting) is missed, ~1/30 \u2248 3.3% per event on\n  the event day, and terminal delistings are comparatively rare in a top-30 revenue-**growth** basket.\n  Secondary, not co-equal with Defect 1.\n- **Fix.** Realize the terminal delisting return explicitly (last available return / a delisting-loss\n  assumption), renormalize weights when a name drops out, and confirm whether `close_panel` is\n  forward-filled \u2014 ffill would instead create stale-price zero-return bars (the opposite hazard). Do not\n  let `.sum` NaN-skipping implicitly define invested capital.\n\n---\n\n## DEFECT 4 \u2014 \"Survivorship is handled\" is over-stated (qualified caveat, flag-for-verification)\n\n- Membership PIT via `members.asof(qe)` is genuinely clean and contemporaneous \u2014 confirmed, no lookahead\n  there. But survivorship has two channels the PIT membership does **not** close:\n  1. **Fundamental-file survivorship.** If `quarterly_revenue.parquet` contains only companies active at\n     extract time (a common vendor property), names that later delisted have no revenue rows, can never\n     enter the top-30, and never contribute their failure \u2014 survivorship leaks in via the fundamental file\n     regardless of PIT membership.\n  2. **Silent missing-price drop.** `held = [s for s in top if s in close.columns]` with\n     `weight = 1.0/len(held)` discards any selected name absent from the price panel and fully reinvests\n     in the rest \u2014 a signal-selected name can be silently dropped.\n- **Status.** Both are [INFERENCE] \u2014 unverifiable from the pasted evidence. They are not confirmed\n  material, but the submission's unqualified \"survivorship is handled\" is unsupported and must be\n  downgraded to \"membership PIT is handled; fundamental-file and price-panel survivorship unverified.\"\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (correct direction is conservative or neutral \u2014 not result-inflating)\n\n- **Prose vs code mismatch.** The comment says \"fill at open,\" but returns are close-to-close\n  (`close.pct_change()`) with entry at the `rebal_day` close via `shift(1)`. This is conservative (skips\n  the rebal-day intraday move), so it does not inflate; align the write-up with the code.\n- **Constant weights between rebalances.** `weights.loc[rebal_day:, held] = 1/n` implies costless daily\n  re-equal-weighting; `turnover_costs` sees only the quarterly steps, so intra-quarter drift turnover is\n  uncosted and a small equal-weight rebalancing premium is captured for free \u2014 a very small upward bias,\n  immaterial.\n- `if len(snap) < 60: continue` carries the prior basket forward on sparse quarters \u2014 fine, likely only\n  early sample.\n- `searchsorted(qe, side=\"right\")` returns `len(index)` and raises `IndexError` if `qe` is at/after the\n  last close date \u2014 robustness only.\n- `nlargest` on `pct_change` favors names recovering from near-zero revenue (huge %); consider a\n  revenue-floor / winsorization. Modeling choice, not a correctness bug.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list \u2014 material defects (final)\n\n1. **Fundamental-data lookahead (CONFIRMED, FATAL).** Rebalances the first trading day after\n   `quarter_end` using revenue not public for ~45\u201360 days; price timing is verified clean, so the entire\n   21.7%-vs-12.9% edge is the lookahead. Must key off PIT `available_date` (or \u226545-day lag). This alone\n   makes the headline fake.\n2. **`pct_change(4)` YoY (real, secondary \u2014 materiality data-dependent).** Correct only on a gap-free\n   quarterly grid; a genuine correctness bug, but its impact on the ranking/headline is not demonstrated\n   by the evidence. Compute YoY by matching `quarter_end \u2212 1yr` or reindex to a full grid first.\n3. **NaN-as-zero delisting return (real, secondary \u2014 bounded).** Terminal delisting/suspension losses\n   booked as 0 with no weight renormalization; upward-biasing but bounded (~1/30 per event) and rare in a\n   high-growth basket. Realize terminal returns, renormalize, and check for forward-fill.\n4. **Over-stated survivorship clearance (qualified caveat, unverified [INFERENCE]).** PIT membership is\n   clean, but survivorship can re-enter via a delisting-pruned revenue file and via the silent\n   missing-price `held` filter; the blanket \"survivorship is handled\" is unsupported. Flag for\n   verification, not confirmed material.\n\n----- ANSWER O04c -----\n# T04 Review \u2014 Quarterly revenue-growth rotation (fundamental-momentum)\n\n## Verdict\n**Fake / materially overstated.** The headline (top-30 21.7% CAGR vs 12.9% EW, 2016\u20132025) is driven by a fundamental-data lookahead: the strategy trades on quarterly revenue the day after the fiscal quarter closes, ~45\u201360 days before that revenue is actually public. The fill/shift mechanics and the PIT-membership handling are, notably, done *correctly* \u2014 but the signal timing is not, and that is enough to invalidate the result.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT #1 \u2014 Fundamental-data lookahead: signal traded before it exists (the fake-alpha driver)\n\n**Lines:**\n```python\nrebal_day = close.index[close.index.searchsorted(qe, side=\"right\")]   # first trading day AFTER quarter end\n...\nsnap = rev[rev[\"quarter_end\"] == qe]...\ntop = snap.nlargest(30, \"rev_yoy\")[\"symbol\"]\n```\n\n**Mechanism.** `qe` is explicitly the *fiscal quarter-end date*, not the results-announcement date. Indian issuers file quarterly results under SEBI LODR up to **45 days after** quarter end (up to **60 days** for the Q4/annual number). The code sets the trade date to the **first trading day after `qe`** and selects on `rev_yoy` computed from `revenue` for the quarter ending on `qe`. So on ~1 April the basket is already tilted into the names that will report strong March-quarter revenue growth in mid-May. You are buying ~45\u201360 days before the market can price the number you sorted on. That forward window is precisely where the 8.8pp/yr excess comes from.\n\nThis is the firm's known landmine #3 verbatim (\"use the PIT dataset with `available_date` \u2026 NEVER quarter-end dates\") and a T-class lookahead \u2014 it cannot pass a Gate-4 lookahead audit.\n\n**Confirming detail (rules out a false alarm on the fill side).** The fill timing itself is *correct*: `weights` are set from `rebal_day` onward, and `(weights.shift(1) * ret)` with close-to-close `ret` means positions first earn on `rebal_day+1` using `close[rebal_day+1]/close[rebal_day]-1`, i.e. an effective entry at the close of the day after quarter end. That is conservative and lookahead-free *for the fill*. The problem is exclusively the **signal availability**, not the fill \u2014 which is why it's easy to miss.\n\n**Fix.** Join each revenue row to its actual disclosure date (`available_date` from the PIT earnings dataset, 86.2% exact per the firm catalog) and set `rebal_day = first trading day after available_date`. If no PIT date is available, apply a conservative lag of **\u226545 calendar days** (\u226560 for the Q4/annual quarter) to `qe` before selecting/trading. Re-run; expect the excess return to collapse substantially.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT #2 \u2014 Selection on `pct_change(4)` is dominated by small-/negative-denominator explosions\n\n**Lines:**\n```python\nrev[\"rev_yoy\"] = rev.groupby(\"symbol\")[\"revenue\"].pct_change(4)\n...\ntop = snap.nlargest(30, \"rev_yoy\")[\"symbol\"]\n```\n\n**Mechanism.** `nlargest(30, \"rev_yoy\")` systematically favours names whose *base* (revenue 4 quarters ago) was near zero, negative, or depressed \u2014 a company going from \u20b92 cr to \u20b940 cr shows +1900% YoY and crowds out genuine large-cap growers. YoY % change has no denominator floor here, so the top-30 is repeatedly populated by base-effect/recovery artifacts rather than durable revenue momentum. This corrupts *what the basket actually holds* independent of the timing bug, and interacts badly with #1 (you're front-running the most distorted growth prints).\n\n**Fix.** Guard the denominator (require prior-year revenue above a minimum absolute threshold and strictly positive), or rank on a bounded transform (e.g. log-growth with a floor, winsorized YoY, or a z-score within the universe) before `nlargest`.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT #3 \u2014 `pct_change(4)` is a row-lag, not a calendar/quarter lag; wrong across missing quarters\n\n**Line:** `rev.groupby(\"symbol\")[\"revenue\"].pct_change(4)`\n\n**Mechanism.** `pct_change(4)` divides by the value **4 rows back**, not 4 *quarters* back. Any symbol with a missing/late quarter (common in fundamental panels) has its 4-row-back value pointing at the wrong fiscal quarter, so `rev_yoy` silently compares e.g. Q3 to a Q2-a-year-ago figure. That both mis-ranks names and (combined with #2) can inject spurious extreme values into the top-30.\n\n**Fix.** Reindex each symbol onto a complete fiscal-quarter grid (or merge on an explicit `quarter_end - 1 year` key) so the YoY denominator is guaranteed to be the same calendar quarter one year prior; drop rows where the true year-ago quarter is absent.\n\n---\n\n## Related lookahead to verify (potentially material, cannot confirm from the snippet)\n\n**Restatement/PIT integrity of `quarterly_revenue.parquet`.** Even after fixing the availability date, if the parquet stores **final/restated** consolidated revenue rather than the value as first reported, the backtest still uses numbers that didn't exist at trade time. Confirm the source is as-first-reported (or that restatements are negligible for revenue). If it is a restated vendor snapshot, that is a second, independent lookahead.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled: not result-breaking as written)\n\n- **Comment vs code mismatch (\"fill at open\").** The comments say \"fill at open,\" but the implementation is close-to-close via `weights.shift(1)`. The code is the correct/conservative version; only the comment is wrong. Fix the comment (or genuinely implement an open panel) for auditability.\n- **Fixed-weight vector held between rebalances.** Holding a constant `1/N` weight vector across all days and computing `weights.shift(1)*ret` implicitly assumes daily rebalancing back to equal weight \u2014 it books a small diversification/rebalancing premium and does **not** charge the daily rebalancing turnover. Effect is usually modest but flatters the result slightly; use a proper drifting-weight (buy-and-hold-between-rebalances) return engine to be exact.\n- **Silent survivorship in returns.** `close` is a *union* panel; a name delisted/bankrupt mid-hold gets `NaN` returns which `.sum(axis=1)` skips, so it effectively exits at its last price with zero further loss rather than realizing the drawdown. Understates losses for distressed names. Mask holdings to the last valid price and realize a terminal return on delist.\n- **`turnover_costs` internals unverified.** 40bp/side is plausible per COST_STANDARDS, but I cannot confirm the function charges cost on the correct rebalance day and on the right turnover base. Verify it aligns to `weights.diff()` at `rebal_day`.\n- **Edge case:** `close.index.searchsorted(qe, side=\"right\")` returns `len(index)` if `qe` is at/after the last date, which would `IndexError`. Harmless in-sample, but guard it.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n\n1. **Fundamental-data lookahead (primary, result-faking):** trades on quarter-end+1 using revenue that is not disclosed for ~45\u201360 days (SEBI LODR); must trade off the actual `available_date`/report date, not `quarter_end`. This drives essentially all of the reported excess return.\n2. **`nlargest` on unbounded `pct_change(4)`** selects small-/negative-base explosions (base-effect recoveries), corrupting the basket composition; needs a denominator floor / bounded ranking.\n3. **`pct_change(4)` is a row-lag, not a quarter-lag** \u2014 YoY is computed against the wrong period whenever a symbol has a missing quarter; must align on an explicit fiscal-quarter grid.\n\n(Plus one to-verify: restated vs as-first-reported revenue in the parquet \u2014 an independent lookahead if the source is restated.)\n\n----- ANSWER O04d -----\n# T04 review \u2014 quarterly revenue-growth rotation (arm B)\n\n## Verdict: FAKE result. One dominant, result-corrupting defect (earnings-availability lookahead), plus secondary data-integrity concerns.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 1 \u2014 Earnings-availability lookahead (this is the fake-maker)\n\n**Exact code:**\n```python\n# rebalance on the first trading day AFTER the quarter ends, fill at open\nrebal_day = close.index[close.index.searchsorted(qe, side=\"right\")]\n```\nwhere `qe` is `quarter_end` = the **fiscal quarter-end date** (per the column comment).\n\n**Mechanism.** The signal `rev_yoy` for the quarter ending on `qe` is the reported revenue for that fiscal quarter. That number is **not public on the first trading day after the quarter ends.** Indian listed companies file quarterly results roughly **45 days** after quarter-end (Q1/Q2/Q3) and up to **~60 days** for the Q4/annual. So:\n- March-31 quarter \u2192 revenue disclosed ~mid-May, but the code trades it on **~April 1**.\n- Jun-30 quarter \u2192 disclosed ~mid-Aug, but traded ~**July 1**.\n\nThe strategy is therefore selecting the top-30 revenue-growth names using figures that would not be known for another **~1.5\u20132 months**, and holding them through the very window in which those (still-secret) results are released and re-priced. It is literally front-running each company's own results announcement. For a revenue-momentum signal this is exactly the information the \"edge\" is built on, so essentially **all** of the 21.7% vs 12.9% excess is manufactured by future data.\n\nThis is the firm's T3 landmine verbatim (CLAUDE.md DATA LANDMINES #3 / D-028 T-taxonomy): *\"Earnings lookahead: use PIT dataset `datasets/earnings_pit/unified_quarterly_pit.parquet` with `available_date`. NEVER quarter-end dates.\"* The code uses quarter-end dates.\n\n**Fix.** Attach each revenue row's real disclosure/filing date (`available_date` from the PIT earnings dataset) and drive the rebalance off that, not off `qe`:\n```python\n# use the PIT publish date, not the fiscal quarter end\navail = rev.loc[..., \"available_date\"]           # per (symbol, quarter_end)\nrebal_day = close.index[close.index.searchsorted(avail, side=\"right\")]\n```\nPractically, because filings arrive on scattered dates, either (a) rebalance on a fixed calendar lag well past all filings (e.g. hold from ~day 60\u201375 after quarter-end), or (b) build the basket per-name as each result lands. Re-running with correct availability is required before any number is quoted; expect the CAGR gap to collapse.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 2 \u2014 Revenue-panel survivorship is NOT established by the membership filter\n\nThe author claims survivorship is handled because *universe membership* comes from the 42-snapshot PIT file. But PIT membership only fixes **which tickers are eligible** at `qe`; it does nothing if `quarterly_revenue.parquet` itself only contains companies that survive to today. If delisted/failed names are absent from `rev`, then `.isin(univ)` filters an already-survivor-biased signal set \u2014 the ones that blew up never had a `rev_yoy` row to be selected or to drag returns. Nothing in the code proves the revenue parquet is full-history.\n\n**Fix / check required.** Verify `quarterly_revenue.parquet` includes revenue histories for names that later delisted (i.e., it is a point-in-time/full-history fundamentals set, not a current-constituent pull). If it is survivor-only, the whole comparison is biased upward regardless of the membership as-of logic.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 3 \u2014 Delisting losses are silently zeroed by skipna sum\n\n```python\nport = (weights.shift(1) * ret).sum(axis=1)\n```\n`ret = close.pct_change()` is NaN for a held name once its price column ends (delisting/halt), and `DataFrame.sum(axis=1)` skips NaN by default. So a name that is dropped from the index after a collapse contributes **0** to portfolio return instead of its terminal loss, while its 1/N capital effectively vanishes rather than being marked down. This biases the backtest upward exactly on the worst names. (Also: selected names missing from `close.columns` are dropped via `held = [...]`, silently reducing the effective basket and hiding names with no price data.)\n\n**Fix.** Carry delisting/removal returns explicitly (mark the terminal move, or force the position to a defined exit return and reallocate cash), and don't let `skipna` mask absent legs \u2014 assert every `held` name has a price series over the holding window.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (label: robustness / to verify, not proven result-breakers)\n\n1. **Row-based YoY.** `rev.groupby(\"symbol\")[\"revenue\"].pct_change(4)` shifts by 4 **rows**, not 4 calendar quarters. If any company has a missing quarter in `rev`, the YoY spans 5 quarters and is wrong for that name. Reindex to a complete quarterly grid per symbol before `pct_change(4)`, or compute YoY by explicit calendar alignment.\n2. **Standalone/consolidated mixing.** Column is stated consolidated; if the source ever mixes standalone into the series, YoY breaks. Worth an assertion.\n3. **`searchsorted` at the tail.** `close.index[close.index.searchsorted(qe, side=\"right\")]` raises IndexError if `qe` is on/after the last price date; guard it.\n4. **Benchmark not shown.** The \"universe equal-weight 12.9%\" leg is not in the snippet \u2014 confirm it uses the same PIT universe, same cost model, and same NaN/delisting handling, else the 21.7% vs 12.9% comparison is apples-to-oranges.\n5. **Costs unverifiable.** `turnover_costs(...)` body isn't shown; can't confirm 40bp/side is applied on actual traded turnover (not gross weights) or that it isn't double-charged across the carried-forward blocks.\n6. The price/return timing itself (`weights.shift(1) * ret`, entry the session after `rebal_day`) is fine and even mildly conservative \u2014 no defect there. The lookahead is purely on the fundamental side.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed MATERIAL defects\n1. **Earnings-availability lookahead** \u2014 rebalances on the first trading day after the *fiscal quarter-end* (`rebal_day = ...searchsorted(qe, side=\"right\")`) using revenue not disclosed for ~45\u201360 days; uses future information and manufactures the excess return. Fix: drive rebalance off each row's `available_date` (PIT filing date), never `quarter_end`.\n2. **Revenue-panel survivorship** \u2014 PIT *membership* filter does not fix a survivor-only `quarterly_revenue.parquet`; the signal set may exclude names that later delisted. Fix: confirm/use a full-history PIT fundamentals set.\n3. **Delisting losses zeroed** \u2014 `sum(axis=1)` skips NaN returns, so removed names contribute 0 instead of their terminal loss (and missing price legs are silently dropped), biasing returns upward. Fix: mark terminal/exit returns explicitly and assert complete price series for held names.", "ids": ["O04a", "O04b", "O04c", "O04d"]}, {"tid": "T05", "key": "## T05 \u2014 statistics \u2014 base-effect growth ranking (CRITICAL)\n**Defect.** `growth = (eps - eps_prev) / eps_prev` ranked by `nlargest`. Percent\ngrowth on near-zero or negative bases is meaningless: penny-EPS bases explode\n(39.5x), DEEPENING losses sign-flip to positive \"growth\" (see SUNWINDPWR\n-1.20 -> -2.55 shown as +1.13), and genuine turnarounds to profit rank at the bottom\n(TURNCORP -5.00 -> +1.00 shown as -1.20). The \"fastest growers\" basket is\ndenominator noise plus deteriorating loss-makers \u2014 the sample table in the task\nshows exactly this.\n**Line.** the `growth` division + `nlargest(20, \"growth\")`.\n**Fix.** Require a positive material base (eps_prev above a threshold), or rank on\ndelta-EPS scaled by price/assets (denominator-free), never percent-of-base on a\nsignable quantity.\n**Accept.** \"Division by near-zero/negative EPS base \u2014 sign flips and explosions\ndominate the ranking\"; \"denominator disease / base effect: percent growth invalid\nwhen the base can be ~0 or negative\"; \"the sample table shows worsening loss-makers\nranked top-10 and a real turnaround ranked last\".\n**Secondary.** PIT handling (asof_date) is explicitly correct; timing is not the\nissue here.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O05a -----\n# T05 \u2014 Review: \"fastest growers\" earnings-growth screen\n\n**Verdict: MATERIAL DEFECT. The screen does not select fastest growers. The growth\nmetric `(ttm_eps - ttm_eps_prev) / ttm_eps_prev` is unguarded against negative and\nnear-zero denominators, so the top-20 basket is systematically populated by junk\n(near-zero-base accounting noise and *deepening losses*) while genuine growers and\ngenuine turnarounds are excluded. The +34% CAGR is an artifact of a broken ranking,\nnot an earnings-growth effect.**\n\nThe author's PIT/timing framing checks out (see \"What is NOT the defect\"), so the\nwhole failure lives in the metric. The provided sample is not a curiosity \u2014 it is the\nscreen incriminating itself. I reproduced every printed `growth` value exactly.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 1 (MATERIAL) \u2014 Negative-denominator sign flip\n\nLine: `f[\"growth\"] = (f[\"ttm_eps\"] - f[\"ttm_eps_prev\"]) / f[\"ttm_eps_prev\"]`\n\nWhen `ttm_eps_prev < 0`, dividing by a negative base flips the sign of the ratio, so\nthe number no longer means \"growth.\" Two failure directions, both present in the sample:\n\n- **Deepening loss ranked as a top grower.** SUNWINDPWR went from EPS \u22121.20 to \u22122.55\n  (a *bigger* loss \u2014 unambiguous deterioration), yet `growth = (\u22122.55 \u2212 \u22121.20)/\u22121.20\n  = +1.13`, ranked **8th and selected**. JPINFRAVENT \u22120.35 \u2192 \u22120.68 (loss deepens),\n  `growth = +0.94`, ranked **9th and selected**. The screen is buying the companies\n  whose earnings are collapsing fastest.\n- **Genuine turnaround ranked at the bottom.** TURNCORP went from EPS \u22125.00 (deep\n  loss) to +1.00 (profit) \u2014 the single most desirable trajectory a growth screen could\n  want \u2014 yet `growth = (1.00 \u2212 \u22125.00)/\u22125.00 = \u22121.20`, ranked **496th (near bottom) and\n  excluded**.\n\nMechanism of corruption: any name with a negative prior-year TTM EPS has its ranking\nsign inverted, so the loss-maker cohort is sorted exactly backwards. Selection is\ndriven into the worst names and away from the best.\n\n**Fix:** never rank a percentage ratio across a sign change. Restrict growth to a\npositive base \u2014 compute `growth` only where `ttm_eps_prev > 0` (a small positive\nfloor, below) and drop the rest, or route negative\u2192positive turnarounds through a\nseparate, explicitly-labelled flag rather than the ratio. (Merely switching to\n`/abs(ttm_eps_prev)` fixes the sign but leaves Defect 2 intact \u2014 see verification.)\n\n## Defect 2 (MATERIAL) \u2014 Near-zero-denominator explosion\n\nLine: same expression.\n\nWhen `ttm_eps_prev` is a tiny positive number, a trivial absolute EPS change becomes an\nenormous percentage, so the top of the ranking is dominated by micro-base names, not by\neconomically large growth. ZENVITECH (prev 0.04 \u2192 1.62) prints `growth = 39.50`\n(3,950%); ORBIPHARM (prev 0.11 \u2192 2.05) prints `17.64` (1,764%). These are companies\ncrossing from ~breakeven, where the denominator is accounting noise. They mechanically\noutrank BLUECHIPCO's real, durable **+24%** (98.40 \u2192 122.10), which lands at **61st and\nis not selected**.\n\nMechanism: the metric measures 1/denominator far more than it measures growth, so\n`nlargest(20, \"growth\")` is effectively \"20 smallest positive prior-EPS,\" a low-quality\nmicro-cap / just-turned-profitable cohort. This both mis-defines the basket and is a\nprime channel for illiquid-name fills that 40bp/side will not survive in reality.\n\n**Fix:** impose a materiality floor on the base (e.g. `ttm_eps_prev` above a rupee\nthreshold or a scaled version such as EPS/price so a fixed floor is comparable across\nnames), and/or winsorize `growth` before ranking. Combined with Defect 1's fix, require\n`ttm_eps_prev > floor` where `floor > 0`.\n\n### Verification (reproduced)\n\n| symbol | prev | now | printed growth | recomputed | reality |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ZENVITECH | 0.04 | 1.62 | 39.50 | 39.50 | near-zero base artifact |\n| ORBIPHARM | 0.11 | 2.05 | 17.64 | 17.64 | near-zero base artifact |\n| SUNWINDPWR | \u22121.20 | \u22122.55 | 1.13 | 1.12 | loss deepening, falsely +113%, **selected 8th** |\n| JPINFRAVENT | \u22120.35 | \u22120.68 | 0.94 | 0.94 | loss deepening, falsely +94%, **selected 9th** |\n| BLUECHIPCO | 98.40 | 122.10 | 0.24 | 0.24 | real +24%, **excluded (61st)** |\n| TURNCORP | \u22125.00 | 1.00 | \u22121.20 | \u22121.20 | real turnaround, **excluded (496th)** |\n\n`/abs(prev)` fix alone: SUNWINDPWR\u2192\u22121.12, TURNCORP\u2192+1.20 (signs corrected), but\nZENVITECH still 39.50 and ORBIPHARM 17.64 \u2014 Defect 2 unresolved. `prev>0.5` floor\ndrops all four junk rows and keeps only BLUECHIPCO \u2014 both defects resolved. Use a\npositive base floor.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled)\n\n- **N1 (robustness, secondary):** `shift(4)` assumes exactly four *evenly-quarterly*\n  refreshes = one year. With a missing/irregular filing, `shift(4)` compares\n  mis-aligned periods (>1yr or skipping a quarter). Prefer an as-of date-difference\n  join (`ttm_eps` vs the value ~365d earlier) rather than a positional shift.\n- **N2 (robustness, secondary):** `snap = f[f.asof_date <= asof].groupby(\"symbol\").tail(1)`\n  has no staleness guard \u2014 a name that stopped filing keeps its last growth forever and\n  can persist in the basket (dead/illiquid holding). Add a max-age filter on\n  `asof_date`.\n- **N3 (non-defect note):** The referenced loop body is \"shared with the value sleeve\n  which passed audit\" \u2014 but that sleeve's growth metric would use a positive-only value\n  base, so passing there does **not** transfer to a metric that must survive negative\n  EPS. The reuse claim does not cover Defects 1\u20132.\n\n## What is NOT the defect (checked, clean)\n\n- **Timing/lookahead:** `asof_date` is publication-lagged; `rebalance` filters\n  `asof_date <= asof` then takes the latest row per symbol; entry at next session open.\n  PIT-correct. `ttm_eps_prev = shift(4)` looks only backward \u2014 no lookahead in the\n  growth computation itself.\n- **Survivorship:** prices are the *union* panel (all symbols ever present), which is\n  the survivorship-safe choice, and membership is PIT via `universe`.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects\n\n1. **Negative-denominator sign flip** in `(ttm_eps \u2212 ttm_eps_prev)/ttm_eps_prev`:\n   companies with negative prior-year TTM EPS are ranked with inverted sign \u2014\n   deepening losses (SUNWINDPWR, JPINFRAVENT) are selected as \"top growers\" and genuine\n   turnarounds (TURNCORP) are pushed to the bottom. Fix: compute growth only on a\n   positive base (`ttm_eps_prev > floor`); handle turnarounds via a separate flag.\n2. **Near-zero-denominator explosion**: tiny positive prior EPS (ZENVITECH 0.04,\n   ORBIPHARM 0.11) produces astronomical percentages that dominate the top and crowd\n   out real growers (BLUECHIPCO +24% excluded at 61st). Fix: base-materiality floor\n   and/or winsorize before `nlargest`. Together these mean the top-20 is not a\n   fastest-growers basket, so the +34% CAGR is not attributable to earnings growth and\n   must be re-run after the metric is fixed.\n\n----- ANSWER O05b -----\n# T05 \u2014 Final consolidated verdict: \"fastest growers\" earnings-growth screen (arm C2 arbiter)\n\n**Verdict: the result is NOT trustworthy.** The basket is mislabeled \u2014 the ranking\nmetric is broken in two independent, textually-confirmed ways, so it does not select\n\"fastest growers.\" Separately, the reported **+34% CAGR vs 13% universe cannot be\ncertified**, because the returns-side handling for the exact cohort this metric\nsurfaces (delisting-prone distressed names and illiquid micro-caps) is unverifiable\nfrom the submission and, for that cohort, first-order.\n\nBoth source reviews agree on the plumbing and on the two metric defects; they disagree\non *what actually produces the +34%*. I adjudicate that disagreement below.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Confirmed clean \u2014 no timing leak (both reviewers, verified)\n\n`snap = f[f[\"asof_date\"] <= asof].groupby(\"symbol\").tail(1)` uses only rows published\non/before the rebalance date; `ttm_eps_prev = groupby(\"symbol\")[\"ttm_eps\"].shift(4)` on\na symbol-then-date-ascending frame pulls strictly earlier rows; entry at next session's\nopen after `asof_date` is correct. The `growth` attached to a selected row uses only\nthat row plus four prior rows, all with `asof_date <= asof`. **No lookahead in the\nselection path** \u2014 conditional on the stated (asserted, not shown) fact that\n`asof_date` really is the publication date. This is not a defect.\n\n## 2. CONFIRMED material defect \u2014 negative-base sign inversion\n\nLine: `f[\"growth\"] = (f[\"ttm_eps\"] - f[\"ttm_eps_prev\"]) / f[\"ttm_eps_prev\"]`\n\nWhen `ttm_eps_prev < 0` the ratio's sign inverts: a *widening loss* scores as high\npositive \"growth\" and a *loss\u2192profit turnaround* scores negative. Confirmed against the\nauthor's own sample:\n- SUNWINDPWR \u22121.20 \u2192 \u22122.55 (loss more than doubled) \u2192 `(-2.55-(-1.20))/(-1.20) = +1.13` \u2192 **ranked 8th, SELECTED**.\n- JPINFRAVENT \u22120.35 \u2192 \u22120.68 (loss nearly doubled) \u2192 `= +0.94` \u2192 **ranked 9th, SELECTED**.\n- TURNCORP \u22125.00 \u2192 **+1.00** (a genuine loss-to-profit recovery, arguably the best real earnings improvement in the sample) \u2192 `= \u22121.20` \u2192 **ranked 496th, REJECTED**.\n\n**Mechanism:** for the negative-base cohort the screen systematically buys\ndeteriorating loss-makers and discards true recoveries \u2014 the inverse of intent.\n**Fix:** growth% is undefined when the base is non-positive. Gate on\n`ttm_eps_prev > 0` (drop/NaN otherwise) so loss-makers cannot enter the growth rank.\nLoss\u2192profit turnarounds, if wanted, are a separate sign-aware signal \u2014 never mixed into\na division-based rank.\n\n## 3. CONFIRMED material defect \u2014 near-zero / zero positive-base explosion\n\nSame line. Even with a positive base, dividing by a value near zero makes the\npercentage explode, so micro-EPS names dominate the top purely as a denominator\nartifact:\n- ZENVITECH 0.04 \u2192 1.62 \u2192 **39.50 (3,950%)** \u2192 #1, only because the base is \u20b90.04.\n- ORBIPHARM 0.11 \u2192 2.05 \u2192 **17.64 (1,764%)** \u2192 same artifact.\n- BLUECHIPCO 98.40 \u2192 122.10 \u2192 **0.24 (a healthy, real +24% on a substantial base)** \u2192 **ranked 61st, NOT selected.**\n\nSharper edge case not caught by either source review: a symbol with\n`ttm_eps_prev == 0` exactly yields `+inf` (not NaN), which `dropna(subset=[\"growth\"])`\ndoes **not** remove, so `nlargest` **auto-ranks it #1**. This is the limiting form of\nthe same defect.\n\n**Mechanism:** with ~500 names and 20 slots, the top-20 is packed with near-zero-base\n(and, via defect 2, negative-base) names, structurally excluding genuine growers. A pure\n`>0` gate does NOT fix this \u2014 ZENVITECH/ORBIPHARM are positive-base. **Fix:** require a\nminimum base magnitude AND winsorize/cap the growth column, or rank on a scale-robust\nquantity (e.g. change in earnings yield: \u0394EPS scaled by price; capped/log growth on\npositive bases; cross-sectional rank of a normalized \u0394EPS). Also add `+inf`/`-inf`\nhandling to the dropna.\n\n## 4. Why the +34% is uncertifiable \u2014 adjudicating the two reviews\n\nThe first reviewer concluded the headline is \"an artifact of the broken metric.\" The\nred-team review refutes this framing, and **on this point the red team is right**: the\nmetric governs *which* names are bought (deepening losers + \u20b90.04-EPS micro-caps); it\ndoes not, by itself, manufacture a 21-point CAGR outperformance. A screen that genuinely\nbought that junk, priced survivorship-free and tradeably, would on any reasonable base\nrate **under**perform. So a junk-selecting screen printing +34% is itself the tell that a\n**separate, returns-side defect** is present. \"Fix the metric and re-run\" is therefore\n**necessary but not sufficient** \u2014 it changes which names are bought, not whether the\nreturn is real.\n\nThe red team's two elevations survive, and I adopt them \u2014 with the honest caveat that\nthey are **inferences, not textually confirmed**, because the loop body (membership,\nweighting, hold, cost/fill, delisting handling) is omitted:\n\n- **(4a) Survivorship / delisting bias in the \"adjusted union panel\" \u2014 MATERIAL, not a\n  footnote.** The broken metric selects precisely the highest-delisting-rate cohort\n  (distressed loss-makers, micro-caps). If the union panel drops names at delisting\n  without booking the terminal \u2248\u2212100%, the basket's blow-ups vanish and returns are\n  massively overstated. Given the cohort, this is the single most likely source of the\n  +34%. It is a D-028 certifiable-result blocker, not a robustness note.\n- **(4b) Fill / liquidity realism \u2014 MATERIAL, independent of defect 2.** 40 bp/side and\n  \"next session's open\" are fantasy for \u20b90.04-EPS illiquid micro-caps: open prints may be\n  stale/non-tradeable and adjusted prices around illiquid names + corporate actions\n  fabricate returns. This inflates the headline on its own, separate from the metric.\n- **(4c) \"Loop body \u2026 shared with the value sleeve which passed audit\" is false\n  reassurance.** The value sleeve trades large, liquid, positive-earnings names; this\n  screen trades the opposite cohort. Survivorship/fill handling adequate for large caps is\n  exactly what breaks for distressed micro-caps. The value-sleeve audit **does not\n  transfer** \u2014 this is the load-bearing unverified assumption, not a \"flag only\" item.\n\n**Consequence:** before any CAGR is quoted, a re-run must independently pass \u2014 on the\n*actual selected names* \u2014 a survivorship-free panel check (terminal losses booked), a\nfill/liquidity audit, and a one-day-lag lookahead test.\n\n## 5. Non-material / robustness notes (labelled non-material)\n\n- **Benchmark mismatch (robustness, material-adjacent):** an equal-weight 20-name\n  micro-cap basket vs an unspecified \"universe 13%\" is likely a size/low-price tilt, not a\n  growth edge. The comparison must be survivorship-matched and size/style-neutralized to\n  be apples-to-apples.\n- **`shift(4)` cadence assumption (non-material):** assumes exactly four clean quarterly\n  refreshes per symbol per year; restatements, missed quarters, or dual filings would\n  misalign the year-ago comparison. Worth a cadence check, not fatal.\n\nNo fabricated defects were found in either source review; the timing/PIT \"clean\" finding\nand both metric defects are confirmed.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n\n1. **Negative-base sign inversion** \u2014 `growth = (ttm_eps \u2212 ttm_eps_prev)/ttm_eps_prev`\n   with `ttm_eps_prev < 0` scores worsening losses as high positive growth (SUNWINDPWR,\n   JPINFRAVENT selected) and genuine turnarounds as negative (TURNCORP rejected).\n   *Fix:* gate on `ttm_eps_prev > 0`. **[CONFIRMED from text]**\n2. **Near-zero / exact-zero positive-base explosion** \u2014 same line; micro-EPS denominators\n   dominate the top-20 (ZENVITECH 3,950%, ORBIPHARM 1,764%) and crowd out real growers\n   (BLUECHIPCO +24% ranked 61st); an exact-zero base yields `+inf` that `dropna` keeps and\n   `nlargest` auto-selects. *Fix:* base-magnitude floor + winsorize/cap, or a scale-robust\n   metric; handle `\u00b1inf`. **[CONFIRMED from text]**\n3. **Survivorship / delisting bias on the selected distressed/micro-cap cohort** \u2014 if the\n   \"adjusted union panel\" drops delisted names without booking terminal \u2248\u2212100%, the +34%\n   is almost certainly overstated; this, not the metric, is the most likely source of the\n   fake headline. The value-sleeve audit does not transfer to this opposite cohort.\n   *Fix:* survivorship-free panel with terminal losses booked; re-run before quoting CAGR.\n   **[MATERIAL \u2014 inferred; unverifiable from the submitted text; must be audited]**\n4. **Fill / liquidity realism** \u2014 40 bp/side and next-open fills are unrealistic for the\n   illiquid \u20b90.04-EPS micro-caps this screen selects; independently inflates returns.\n   *Fix:* liquidity/price floor + realistic fill/impact model on the actual selected names.\n   **[MATERIAL \u2014 inferred; unverifiable from the submitted text; must be audited]**\n\nBottom line: the \"fastest growers\" label is false on defects 1\u20132 alone, and the +34%\nheadline is uncertifiable until defects 3\u20134 are audited on the actual selected names.\nFixing the metric is necessary but not sufficient.\n\n----- ANSWER O05c -----\n# T05 review \u2014 \"fastest growers\" earnings-growth screen\n\n## Verdict\nThe submission has a **material definitional defect in the growth metric**. The timing/lookahead defense the author gives is actually sound, but the screen does not measure what it claims: the ranking is driven by division instability (negative and near-zero denominators), so the \"top-20 fastest growers\" basket is largely deteriorating loss-makers and micro-base noise names, not fast growers. The +34% CAGR is therefore mislabeled and not evidence of a growth edge. The author's own sample data is the smoking gun.\n\n## The timing defense IS valid (do not flag this)\n- `growth` is computed on `f` sorted by `[\"symbol\",\"asof_date\"]` with `shift(4)`, so each row's growth uses only that symbol's own prior refreshes (all with earlier `asof_date`). It is stamped on the current `asof_date`.\n- `rebalance(asof, universe)` filters `f[\"asof_date\"] <= asof` then takes `groupby(\"symbol\").tail(1)` \u2014 the latest publicly-known filing as of `asof`. Entry is next session's open.\n- Given `asof_date` = publication date (as stated), there is **no timing leak** in the visible code. The defect is in the metric definition, not in lookahead. I am not inventing a leak here.\n\n## Material defect 1 \u2014 negative-denominator sign inversion\n`f[\"growth\"] = (f[\"ttm_eps\"] - f[\"ttm_eps_prev\"]) / f[\"ttm_eps_prev\"]`\n\nWhen `ttm_eps_prev < 0`, percentage growth is not monotonic in improvement; the sign of the ratio is flipped by the negative base. The author's own sample proves it:\n\n- **SUNWINDPWR** prev \u22121.20 \u2192 now \u22122.55: loss **deepened**, yet growth = (\u22122.55\u2212(\u22121.20))/(\u22121.20) = +1.13 (+113%) \u2192 **ranked 8th, SELECTED**.\n- **JPINFRAVENT** prev \u22120.35 \u2192 now \u22120.68: loss **deepened**, growth = (\u22120.68\u2212(\u22120.35))/(\u22120.35) = +0.94 (+94%) \u2192 **ranked 9th, SELECTED**.\n- **TURNCORP** prev \u22125.00 \u2192 now +1.00: genuine **turnaround** (loss to profit), growth = (1.00\u2212(\u22125.00))/(\u22125.00) = \u22121.20 (\u2212120%) \u2192 **ranked 496th, DISCARDED**.\n\nMechanism: the screen systematically **selects companies whose losses are getting worse** and **rejects real turnarounds**. This is the opposite of a growth signal.\n\nFix: never compute percentage growth against a non-positive base. Require `ttm_eps_prev > 0` (and ideally `ttm_eps > 0`) before ranking; handle negative-base cases with an explicit, separate turnaround flag (`prev < 0 & now > 0`) rather than folding them into the same ratio. Drop/segregate all `ttm_eps_prev <= 0` rows from the \"fastest growers\" ranking.\n\n## Material defect 2 \u2014 small-positive-base explosion (and divide-by-zero)\nEven with a positive base, a near-zero denominator produces enormous ratios that dominate `nlargest(20, \"growth\")`:\n\n- **ZENVITECH** prev 0.04 \u2192 now 1.62: growth 39.50 (+3950%) \u2192 **top of the ranking**.\n- **ORBIPHARM** prev 0.11 \u2192 now 2.05: growth 17.64 (+1764%).\n- Contrast **BLUECHIPCO** prev 98.40 \u2192 now 122.10: a genuine, substantial +24% grower \u2192 **ranked 61st, NOT selected**.\n\nMechanism: a move from Rs 0.04 to Rs 1.62 is economically trivial in rupee terms but explodes as a percentage. The top-20 is captured by rounding-error EPS bases, crowding out real growers like BLUECHIPCO. Also `ttm_eps_prev == 0` yields `inf`, which `nlargest` ranks first (not shown in the sample but a live path since `dropna` does not remove `inf`).\n\nFix: impose a minimum absolute base (`abs(ttm_eps_prev) >= floor`, e.g. Rs 1\u20132) before ranking; winsorize/cap growth; explicitly guard against zero denominator (map to NaN/drop). Consider ranking on a more robust growth measure (e.g. YoY growth of positive EPS with a level floor, or standardized growth) rather than a raw ratio.\n\n## Consequence for the reported result\nDefects 1 and 2 together mean the \"top-20 fastest growers\" basket is composed mainly of (a) deteriorating loss-makers and (b) micro-base names \u2014 **not** fast growers. The label on the +34% CAGR is false. The backtest mechanics (PIT snapshot, next-open entry, quarterly hold) may be clean, so the basket did earn +34% in-sample, but it is a basket of small/junk names selected by a math artifact, not a validated growth edge. It should not advance on this evidence.\n\n## Secondary material concerns (contingent on the above, but real)\n- **Cost/slippage realism.** The selected names have Rs 0.04\u20130.11 prior EPS \u2014 almost certainly thin small-caps. A flat 40bp/side is optimistic for such names; per COST_STANDARDS dynamic-slippage discipline, real turnover cost/impact would be materially higher and could erase the gap. Re-cost with liquidity-aware slippage and a minimum ADV/liquidity filter.\n- **Benchmark / size attribution.** Comparing a micro-cap-tilted basket to the broad universe equal-weight (13%) overstates alpha via the size factor. Benchmark against a size-matched (small-cap) index and attribute the excess to size vs genuine growth selection before quoting \"+34% vs 13%\".\n\n## Non-material notes (labelled)\n- `shift(4)` assumes exactly four regular quarterly refreshes = one year. Restatements/irregular filing cadence would make the \"YoY\" window uneven. Minor; verify refresh regularity.\n- The rebalance loop body is omitted (\"shared with the value sleeve which passed audit\"), so PIT universe membership, equal-weighting, delisting/dead-symbol handling on the adjusted union panel, and hold-to-next-rebalance mechanics are asserted, not shown. Not flaggable as a defect from what's visible, but it is unverified.\n\n## Numbered list of material defects\n1. **Negative-denominator sign inversion** in `growth = (ttm_eps - ttm_eps_prev)/ttm_eps_prev`: when `ttm_eps_prev < 0`, deepening losses score as large positive \"growth\" (SUNWINDPWR, JPINFRAVENT selected) and genuine turnarounds score most negative (TURNCORP discarded). Fix: require positive base; treat negative-base cases via an explicit turnaround flag, not the ratio.\n2. **Small-positive-base / divide-by-zero explosion**: near-zero denominators (ZENVITECH 0.04, ORBIPHARM 0.11) dominate `nlargest`, crowding out real growers (BLUECHIPCO excluded); `ttm_eps_prev == 0` yields `inf` that ranks first. Fix: minimum absolute base floor, winsorize/cap growth, guard zero denominator.\n3. **Mislabeled result**: because of (1) and (2), the \"top-20 fastest growers\" basket is not fast growers; the +34% CAGR is a selection artifact and does not demonstrate a growth edge.\n4. (Contingent-material) **Cost realism and benchmark**: 40bp/side understates slippage for the micro-cap names actually selected, and the broad-universe benchmark ignores the size tilt \u2014 both inflate the apparent edge. Re-cost with liquidity-aware slippage and benchmark against a size-matched index.\n\n----- ANSWER O05d -----\n# T05 \u2014 CONSOLIDATED CIO VERDICT: \"fastest growers\" earnings-growth screen\n\n**Chair:** Rajan Mehta, CIO. **Inputs:** Quant review (A. Rao) + Red-Team review (N. Bose).\n**Evidence:** task text only (HARD CONSTRAINT \u2014 no files read, no code run). I re-derived every\nsample row from the raw EPS pairs before ruling; all six are internally consistent.\nTags: [DATA]=in task text, [INFERENCE]=deduction, [OPINION]=judgment.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT \u2014 result is FAKE.\n\n**3-line rationale.**\n1. The ranking metric `growth = (ttm_eps \u2212 ttm_eps_prev) / ttm_eps_prev` is an unstable,\n   sign-changing denominator \u2014 the exact family the firm banned after the FF debit-denominator\n   (+80% fake) and return-on-net-debit blow-ups. It does not measure earnings growth for any\n   name with a negative or near-zero prior base.\n2. The author's own sample is self-refuting: it SELECTS a deepening loss-maker (SUNWINDPWR\n   \u22121.20\u2192\u22122.55, ranked 8th), SELECTS a second deteriorating name (JPINFRAVENT, 9th), REJECTS a\n   clean +24% real grower (BLUECHIPCO, 61st), and buries a genuine loss\u2192profit turnaround\n   (TURNCORP \u22125.00\u2192+1.00) at 496th. The basket is adversely selected, not a growth basket.\n3. Therefore the label \"fastest growers\" is false and the +34% CAGR is unattributable to any\n   earnings-growth edge. Do not quote +34% as growth alpha.\n\nBoth reviewers reach FAKE; I uphold it. The defect is NOT timing \u2014 the date-axis PIT is clean\n(see below). The defect is the growth definition.\n\n---\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT (CIO charter #6 \u2014 this is where the real danger hides)\n\nThe metric bug is not merely mislabelling; it actively steers the book into the two cohorts\nthat carry the fattest left tails, which is why this must be REJECTED and not merely re-labelled:\n\n- **Correlated-blowup / delisting cohort.** The negative-denominator sign-flip preferentially\n  loads DEEPENING-LOSS names \u2014 bankruptcy/delisting candidates. If the \"adjusted union panel\"\n  does not book their terminal \u2212100% delisting/suspension returns, the +34% is survivorship-\n  inflated *exactly* on the cohort the screen is biased toward. This is a firm-lesson pattern\n  (calm-looking names, unpriced left tail). [INFERENCE, strong \u2014 must verify, see M1]\n- **Single-name tail concealment.** A 20-name equal-weight basket that ranks 4-paisa\u2192Rs1.62\n  micro-base pops at #1/#2 is a textbook setup for the headline to be carried by 1\u20133 lottery\n  winners (my standing lesson: headline CAGRs from concentrated winners are artifacts; the\n  FF-calendar \u2212249% single-trade came from one CALM name). +34% must be shown to survive\n  drop-top-1 and drop-top-3 or there is no basket-level edge. [INFERENCE \u2014 must verify, see M2]\n\nWorst single-day / worst-month figures cannot be computed from the text (return engine omitted),\nwhich is itself a reason this cannot be sized.\n\n## SIZING RULING\n\nZero. Nothing is sizable off this submission. The result is fake at the metric layer; sizing\nquestions are moot until the screen is rebuilt and re-validated from scratch.\n\n---\n\n## ADJUDICATION OF THE TWO REVIEWS (what survives, what doesn't)\n\n### CONFIRMED MATERIAL (survive on the text's own evidence)\n\n**M-1. Negative-denominator sign-flip.** [DATA + CONFIRMED by both]\n- *Claim/line:* `f[\"growth\"] = (f[\"ttm_eps\"] - f[\"ttm_eps_prev\"]) / f[\"ttm_eps_prev\"]` for any\n  `ttm_eps_prev < 0`.\n- *Mechanism:* neg\u00f7neg \u2192 positive, so worsening losses score as \"growth\"; a real turnaround\n  (numerator positive, denominator negative) scores negative. The ranking is exactly INVERTED\n  across the entire negative-prior-base cohort. Evidence: SUNWINDPWR +1.13 (8th, SELECTED,\n  losses deepened); JPINFRAVENT +0.94 (9th, SELECTED, deteriorating); TURNCORP \u22121.20 (496th,\n  EXCLUDED, the most bullish event on the board).\n- *Fix:* never divide by an unstable base. Require a meaningful POSITIVE prior base\n  (`ttm_eps_prev >= floor`, floor from a positive percentile of universe EPS, not 0); flag/drop\n  `prev <= 0`; handle sign-changes (loss\u2192profit turnarounds) on a SEPARATE dedicated flag, not\n  via the ratio.\n\n**M-2. Near-zero / exact-zero denominator inflation + inf-survives-dropna code bug.** [DATA + CONFIRMED by both]\n- *Claim/line:* same expression for `ttm_eps_prev` near/at zero; and `snap = snap ... .dropna(subset=[\"growth\"])`.\n- *Mechanism:* a tiny prior base produces thousands-of-percent \"growth\" (ZENVITECH 1.58/0.04 =\n  +3,950% \u2192 #1; ORBIPHARM +1,764% \u2192 #2), crowding out the genuine grower (BLUECHIPCO real +24%\n  \u2192 61st, rejected). Worse, `ttm_eps_prev == 0` with positive current EPS yields **+inf** (float\n  div-by-zero, not NaN); `dropna` removes NaN only, so `nlargest(20, \"growth\")` places every\n  exactly-zero-prior-base name at the TOP and auto-selects it every rebalance. The top of the\n  ranking is a leaderboard of smallest-prior-base names \u2014 a microcap/recovery selector, not\n  \"fastest growers.\"\n- *Fix:* positive EPS floor (removes the near-zero explosion); explicit `inf`/`-inf` guard\n  (`replace([inf,-inf], nan)` before `dropna`, or filter on a finite mask).\n\n**M-3. Headline is unattributable (consequence of M-1 + M-2).** [CONFIRMED consequence]\n- *Claim:* \"top-20 fastest growers +34% CAGR vs universe 13%.\"\n- *Mechanism:* the selected basket mixes falling-earnings names with micro-base pops, so the\n  label is false and the outperformance cannot be claimed as a growth edge; the most likely true\n  driver is small-cap / low-base recovery beta.\n- *Fix:* after the metric is rebuilt, attribute against size/recovery beta via the incremental-\n  vs-base shuffle before ANY growth-edge claim. Until then, retire the +34% number.\n\n### TIMING / PIT \u2014 clean on the DATE axis, with one honest caveat (Bose's correction upheld)\n\n`snap = f[f.asof_date <= asof]` then `.groupby(\"symbol\").tail(1)` uses only filings public by the\nrebalance; `growth` is `shift(4)` within symbol (own past rows only); entry is next-session open\nafter `asof_date`. No date-axis lookahead \u2014 the author's \"no timing leak\" claim holds on that\naxis. **Correction to Rao:** his blanket \"PIT clean\" is one check short. PIT requires both the\nright date AND the value AS KNOWN THEN; if `ttm_eps_pit.parquet` stores later-RESTATED EPS keyed\nto the original `asof_date`, that is a revision-leak hiding under a clean date filter. Read the\nclearance as: **\"date-axis PIT clean; value-vintage unverified.\"** (see M3-verify).\n\n### DOWNGRADED \u2014 does NOT survive as a standalone material defect\n\n**Rao Defect 4 (flat 40bp / no ADV cap \"too optimistic on microcaps\").** [OPINION \u2014 I side with Bose]\n\"Microcap/thin\" is inferred from *fictional ticker strings* (ZENVITECH, ORBIPHARM\u2026) with ZERO\nADV, price, or market-cap evidence in the text. Per the answer rules (\"do not invent problems\"),\nthis is NOT an established defect. It is a legitimate DOWNSTREAM hypothesis to test after the\nmetric is fixed \u2014 reclassified to conditional/unverified. Rao overstated it as material.\n\n### CONDITIONAL / MUST-VERIFY BEFORE ANY RE-RUN STANDS (Bose's adds \u2014 correctly raised, not proven from text)\n\nThese are not confirmed defects and I will not list them as such; they are gating verification\nrequirements that this submission leaves entirely open, and the metric bug makes two of them\nacute:\n- **M1-verify \u2014 survivorship of the death-candidate cohort.** Confirm the union price panel books\n  real terminal delisting/suspension returns (not forward-fill or row-drop) for the deepening-loss\n  names the screen selects. If not, the +34% is inflated exactly where the bias concentrates. This\n  is elevated above a generic check *because* the metric hand-picks likely delisters. [INFERENCE]\n- **M2-verify \u2014 return concentration / tail concealment.** Require per-name P&L contribution,\n  drop-top-1 and drop-top-3 CAGR, and worst-single-trade. If +34% collapses toward the 13%\n  universe when the top 1\u20133 are removed, there is no basket edge. [INFERENCE \u2014 CIO tail mandate]\n- **M3-verify \u2014 value-vintage PIT.** One spot-check of a known restated name confirms `ttm_eps`\n  is the originally-reported figure keyed to `asof_date`, not a restatement. [INFERENCE]\n\n### NON-MATERIAL NOTES (clearly labelled, do not affect the verdict)\n\n- (a) `shift(4)` assumes a clean 4-refresh-per-year cadence; irregular/missing/annual-bundled\n  filings make \"prev\" not exactly one year prior \u2014 accuracy/robustness only. [non-material]\n- (b) The return/attribution loop and the universe-PIT membership are omitted (\"standard, shared\n  with the value sleeve\"), so the +34% and the 13% baseline are literally unverifiable from the\n  text \u2014 process gap, flag for the Data Officer / Gate lineage check, not a provable defect. [non-material]\n- (c) No validation battery is shown (walk-forward 3y/1y, DSR>0.95 on honest trials, PBO<25%,\n  \u226530 trades/param, regime slices 2018/2020/2022/2024/2026, incremental-vs-size-beta shuffle).\n  This means even a corrected re-run is uncertifiable until the battery passes \u2014 but it is a\n  certification gap, not the reason THIS result is fake (the metric already is). [non-material to verdict]\n\n---\n\n## KILL CRITERIA / PATH BACK (FAKE \u2192 potentially real)\n\nNecessary AND sufficient before this idea is reconsidered:\n1. Replace the metric with a sign-safe / stable-denominator measure \u2014\n   symmetric growth `(E_now \u2212 E_prev)/(0.5\u00b7(|E_now|+|E_prev|))` bounded [\u22122,2]; OR\n   `\u0394`earnings-yield (normalise by price/sales-per-share/book-per-share so the base can't vanish\n   or flip); OR `log(E_now/E_prev)` restricted to positive\u2192positive, with turnarounds on a\n   separate flag. Add the positive-EPS floor and the inf guard.\n2. Re-run, then CLEAR M1-verify (delisting returns booked), M2-verify (edge survives drop-top-3),\n   M3-verify (value vintage), plus the full absent validation battery.\nRao's proposed fixes are directionally sound and introduce no new lookahead \u2014 adopt them, but\nthey are necessary-not-sufficient: M1/M2 decide whether anything is left after the metric is fixed.\n\n**Review date:** on resubmission with a rebuilt metric; not before.\n\n## DISSENTS\n\nNone on the verdict \u2014 Rao and Bose both reach FAKE; I concur. Two recorded adjudications between\nthe reviews: (i) Rao's Defect 4 downgraded from material to conditional-unverified (I side with\nBose); (ii) Rao's blanket \"PIT clean\" narrowed to \"date-axis clean, value-vintage unverified\"\n(Bose's M3, upheld).\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED LIST \u2014 CLAIMED MATERIAL DEFECTS (survivors)\n\n1. **Sign-flipping negative denominator.** `(ttm_eps \u2212 ttm_eps_prev)/ttm_eps_prev` with\n   `prev < 0` inverts the ranking across the whole negative-base cohort: deepening losses score\n   positive (SUNWINDPWR selected 8th, JPINFRAVENT 9th) and real turnarounds score negative\n   (TURNCORP excluded, 496th). Fix: require a positive prior base + score sign-changes on a\n   separate turnaround flag / use a sign-safe metric.\n2. **Near-zero and exact-zero denominator inflation, incl. the `inf`-survives-`dropna` code bug.**\n   Tiny prior EPS produces thousands-of-percent \"growth\" (ZENVITECH #1, ORBIPHARM #2; real +24%\n   BLUECHIPCO rejected at 61st); `prev == 0` \u2192 `+inf` (not NaN) passes `dropna` and is auto-top-20.\n   Fix: positive EPS floor + explicit inf guard before ranking.\n3. **Headline unattributable (consequence of 1+2).** \"Fastest growers\" is false for the selected\n   basket and the +34% CAGR cannot be claimed as a growth edge until the metric is rebuilt and\n   the result is tested against size/recovery beta. Fix: incremental-vs-size-beta attribution\n   post-rebuild; retire the +34% figure meanwhile.\n\nNot material as claimed: Rao's Defect 4 (flat-40bp-on-\"microcaps\") \u2014 \"microcap\" is inferred from\nticker strings with no ADV evidence; conditional hypothesis, not a standalone defect.\nMust-verify-before-any-re-run (gating, unproven from text): M1 survivorship of selected delisters;\nM2 tail-concentration / drop-top-N; M3 value-vintage PIT.", "ids": ["O05a", "O05b", "O05c", "O05d"]}, {"tid": "T06", "key": "## T06 \u2014 lookahead \u2014 settlement beyond max(data), T8 (HIGH)\n**Defect.** Expiry calendar runs to Jul-2026 but data ends 2026-06-30. For the\nJul-2026 cycle (entered ~Jun-15 at T-45), `spot.asof(exp)` silently returns the last\navailable close, so an OPEN position is \"settled\" near its entry level and booked as\na near-full-premium win. No guard `exp <= spot.index.max()` exists, so every future\nrerun keeps booking phantom wins for unfinished cycles. Same class as the firm's\nS-04 incident (84 fabricated future-settlement wins).\n**Line.** `settle_spot = spot.asof(exp)` with the calendar extending past the data\nend and no max-date guard.\n**Fix.** Assert `exp <= spot.index.max()`; drop unfinished cycles or report them as\nOPEN, never settled.\n**Accept.** \".asof past the end of data returns a stale price \u2014 the July-2026 cycle\nis fabricated\"; \"settles positions after max(available data), booking wins for open\ntrades\"; \"missing expiry<=data-end guard; future expiries marked at the last close\".\n**Secondary.** Strike selection off the PRIOR close and same-day premium capture at\nentry are acceptable conventions here; the 84% hit rate itself is plausible for\nshort strangles.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O06a -----\n# T06 \u2014 Consolidated arbiter verdict: monthly NIFTY short strangle\n\n**Verdict: the result is materially wrong / fake.** The reported headline (84% hit\nrate, +41 pts/cycle, \u2212412 worst) cannot come from an honest full-coverage,\nuncapped, unmanaged \u00b13% strangle backtest over 2019\u20132026. The profitability is an\nartifact of missing/mishandled tail cycles. Several defects survive; they converge\non one root cause: **the disaster months are silently absent from the two headline\nrisk statistics.**\n\nBoth source reviews are correct that the causal spine is clean (strikes from the\nPRIOR close via `asof(entry_day \u2212 1d)`; entry premium at the entry-day print; and \u2014\nimportantly \u2014 cash-settlement at intrinsic from the underlying, `max(settle\u2212ce_k,0)\n+ max(pe_k\u2212settle,0)`, which correctly avoids the expiry-day option-`SETTLE_PR`\nlandmine). Those are genuinely handled well and are **not** defects. The problem is\ncoverage and silent NaN/boundary handling, not the payoff math.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 1 \u2014 the \u2212412 worst-case is structurally impossible \u2192 the edge is an exclusion artifact (DOMINANT)\n\n- **Claims attacked:** \"worst cycle \u2212412 pts\", \"avg +41 pts/cycle\", \"hit rate 84%\".\n- **Mechanism:** the loop sells a naked, unmanaged \u00b13% strangle held to expiry with\n  an **uncapped** intrinsic payoff \u2014 no stop, no roll, no cap anywhere in the code.\n  The stated window 2019-01\u21922026 necessarily contains the COVID cycle. For the\n  ~26-Mar-2020 expiry, `entry_day = last_trading_day \u2264 (exp \u2212 45d)` \u2248 10-Feb-2020,\n  NIFTY \u2248 12,100 \u2192 `pe_k \u2248 round(12,100 \u00d7 0.97) \u2248 11,700`. Settlement \u2248 26-Mar-2020,\n  NIFTY \u2248 8,600. **PE intrinsic alone \u2248 3,100 pts**, so that single cycle is\n  \u2248 **\u22122,900 pts** \u2014 roughly **7\u00d7 worse** than the reported \u2212412. A \u2212412 worst-case\n  is therefore mathematically incompatible with this code's payoff over this period.\n  (Other >6% adverse months add further losses beyond \u2212412.)\n- **Why it fakes the *headline*, not just the tail:** total reported P&L \u2248 90 \u00d7 41 \u2248\n  3,690 pts. Restoring the COVID cycle alone (\u2248 \u22122,900) collapses the average to\n  \u2248 **+9 pts/cycle**; restoring two\u2013three more >6% down-months turns the mean\n  **negative**. The strategy's entire apparent edge is the missing tail.\n- **Fix:** print per-cycle P&L; confirm the Feb-2020-entry cycle is present with its\n  true (~\u22122,900) loss; re-report avg and worst-case with all cycles included. If the\n  tail is absent because of a data-coverage gap, that must be disclosed, not\n  presented as \"verified, volume>0, full coverage.\"\n\n## Material defect 2 \u2014 silent NaN-tolerance is the exact corruption path (ARBITER CORRECTION to BOTH reviews)\n\nBoth source reviewers reasoned that a missing option leg \"would poison the mean\"\n(prior reviewer) / that a clean +41 mean proves \"no NaN occurred\" (red team). **Both\nare wrong on pandas semantics, and the error matters.**\n\n- **Mechanism:** `chain.price(...)` on a missing or illiquid \u00b13% leg returns `NaN`\n  (or raises \u2014 see below). If NaN: `prem` \u2192 NaN \u2192 `pnl` \u2192 NaN. But\n  `pd.Series.mean()` and `.min()` default to **`skipna=True`**, so NaN cycles are\n  **silently dropped from `avg pnl` and from the `worst` (`min`)**, are counted as a\n  loss in `win = pnl > 0` (since `NaN > 0` is `False`), and **still counted in\n  `len(r)`**. Result: any cycle whose \u00b13% strike lacked data \u2014 exactly the violent\n  months when far-OTM strikes gap, or where the dataset has holes \u2014 is **invisible in\n  both headline risk numbers** while the run still prints a clean cycle count and a\n  clean mean. This is the precise, code-level path by which the \u2212412 (defect 1) is\n  produced: \u2212412 is merely the worst of the cycles that *had* data.\n- **Alternative branch:** if `chain.price` instead **raises** on a missing leg, the\n  run crashes with no output \u2014 in which case the reported numbers cannot come from\n  this code at all (reproducibility failure, defect 3).\n- **Fix:** never rely on `skipna` here. `assert r[\"pnl\"].notna().all()` before\n  reporting; make a missing leg fail **loudly**; and decide the real-world action for\n  an untradeable \u00b13% strike explicitly (trade the nearest liquid strike and record\n  it, or record an explicit \"no-trade\") \u2014 never let it vanish into a skipped NaN.\n\n## Material defect 3 \u2014 cycle count and numbers do not reconcile with the code shown (reproducibility)\n\n- **Mechanism:** `results.append(...)` runs **unconditionally** \u2014 there is no\n  `continue`/skip path \u2014 so `len(r) == len(expiries)` deterministically. Jan-2019\n  through Jul-2026 inclusive is **91** monthly expiries, yet **90** is reported. The\n  triple (90 cycles, clean +41 mean, \u2212412 worst) cannot be produced by the artifact\n  as written without unshown handling. Either the calendar actually returned 90\n  (then the July-2026 expiry is absent and the \"\u2026to 2026-07\" label is loose), or a\n  row is being dropped somewhere not shown.\n- **Fix:** ship code + a per-cycle P&L table that reproduces the exact printed\n  numbers, reconciled expiry-by-expiry against the exchange calendar. Every excluded\n  cycle must be listed and justified.\n- **Note vs source reviews:** the prior reviewer's specific mechanism \u2014 \"the\n  `volume>0` filter silently *deleted* one calm/liquid month \u2192 mild upward bias\" \u2014 is\n  **rejected as stated**: the shown loop deletes nothing, and the effect is not a\n  \"mild\" one-calm-month bias. The correct sizing is defect 1 (the missing cycles are\n  the *tails*), and the correct silent path is defect 2 (NaN + `skipna`), of which a\n  `volume>0`-driven NaN is one legitimate cause.\n\n## Material defect 4 \u2014 `spot.asof(exp)` settlement has no data-boundary / existence check (real latent bug)\n\n- **Mechanism:** `asof` silently returns the last value \u2264 `exp` with no error. The\n  data ends **2026-06-30**, but the range names **2026-07**. Any expiry beyond the\n  data end (the ~30-Jul-2026 expiry) would be \"settled\" at the stale **30-Jun close**\n  \u2192 because the index almost never moves \u00b13% in the ~2 weeks from mid-June entry to\n  30-Jun, the fabricated payoff \u2248 0 \u2192 a near-certain **full-premium fake win**.\n- **Scope (honest):** whether this fired in *this* run is **indeterminate** from the\n  evidence. If the calendar truly returned 90 (June-2026 last), July is absent and\n  the bug is dormant here; if it returned 91 incl. July, that collides with the count\n  in defect 3. Either way it is a genuine correctness bug that fires the moment an\n  out-of-range expiry enters the calendar.\n- **Fix (unconditional):** clip expiries to `settlement_date \u2264 data_end`, and use an\n  **exact/validated** settlement lookup (`spot.loc[settle_date]`, KeyError on miss,\n  or assert `returned_index == requested_date`) \u2014 reserve `asof`'s prior-value\n  semantics for the strike ref only.\n- **Note vs source reviews:** the prior reviewer's separate finding that `asof` is \"a\n  latent corruptor *everywhere*\" is **rejected as an independent defect** \u2014 it is the\n  same root cause as this one. For every in-range cycle, `exp` is a real trading day,\n  so `spot.asof(exp)` returns the *exact* expiry close (no corruption), and the\n  strike-ref `asof(entry_day \u2212 1d)` is the intended, slightly-conservative prior-close\n  semantics. Folded here rather than double-counted.\n\n---\n\n## Correctly handled \u2014 do NOT manufacture a defect here\n- **Cash-settlement at intrinsic from the underlying** \u2014 correctly avoids the\n  expiry-day option-`SETTLE_PR` landmine. Praise from both source reviews is warranted.\n- **Strikes from the prior close** (`asof(entry_day \u2212 1d)`) with entry premium at the\n  same-day print \u2014 causally clean, slightly conservative.\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled \u2014 not defects)\n- **Settlement at daily close** vs NIFTY's expiry-day half-hour VWAP settlement value:\n  small approximation, non-material relative to the above.\n- **`COSTS_PTS = 4.5` all-in for a seller:** plausible (the 0.125% exercise STT on ITM\n  settlement is borne by the buyer, not the short); confirm against `COST_STANDARDS`\n  and 2-leg entry slippage. Non-material pending that check.\n- **No portfolio-overlap / margin accounting** across overlapping ~45-DTE positions:\n  fine for a per-cycle statistics backtest; matters only for a capital-based\n  return/Sharpe. Non-material for the reported per-cycle metrics.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of surviving material defects\n1. **Reported worst-case \u2212412 pts is structurally impossible** for an unmanaged,\n   uncapped naked \u00b13% strangle held to expiry over a window containing March-2020\n   (that cycle alone \u2248 \u22122,900 pts). The profit-driving tail cycles are excluded or\n   mishandled; restoring the COVID cycle collapses +41 toward ~+9 and a few more\n   down-months turn it negative. **This is what makes the result fake.**\n2. **Silent NaN-tolerance corrupts the two headline risk numbers:** a missing/illiquid\n   \u00b13% leg \u2192 `chain.price` NaN \u2192 `pnl` NaN, which `Series.mean()`/`.min()` **skip by\n   default (`skipna=True`)**, dropping disaster cycles from avg and worst while still\n   counting them in `len` and as losses in hit rate. (Both source reviews mis-stated\n   this as \"NaN poisons the mean\" \u2014 it does not.) If `chain.price` raises instead, the\n   run crashes and the numbers can't come from this code. Fix: `assert notna().all()`,\n   fail loudly on any missing leg.\n3. **Numbers irreconcilable with the code (reproducibility):** the unconditional\n   `results.append` gives `len(r) == len(expiries)` = 91 calendar expiries, yet 90 is\n   reported; the (90, +41, \u2212412) triple cannot be reproduced from the artifact as\n   shown. Fix: ship code + per-cycle table reconciled expiry-by-expiry.\n4. **`spot.asof(exp)` settlement lacks a data-boundary/existence check** \u2014 a real\n   latent bug that fabricates a stale 30-Jun-2026 \"settlement\" (near-certain fake win)\n   for the July-2026 expiry the moment it enters the calendar; firing in this run is\n   indeterminate. Fix: clip expiries to settlement \u2264 data-end and use an\n   exact/validated settlement lookup.\n\n**Rejected from the source reviews:** prior-reviewer finding 2 (`asof` \"corruptor\neverywhere\") \u2014 redundant with defect 4, no corruption on in-range cycles; and the\nmechanism/sizing of prior-reviewer finding 3 (\"`volume>0` filter silently deleted one\ncalm month \u2192 mild bias\") \u2014 the real missing cycles are the tails (defect 1) via the\nNaN+`skipna` path (defect 2), not a deleted calm month.\n\n----- ANSWER O06b -----\n# T06 armB \u2014 Review: monthly NIFTY short strangle\n\n**Verdict: MATERIAL DEFECTS FOUND.** The headline stats (84% hit, +41 avg, worst\n-412) are not trustworthy; the code silently deletes its worst cycles and\nfabricates at least one, all via unguarded `asof` + pandas default `skipna`.\nVerified with scratch scripts (expiry calendar + pandas NaN/asof behaviour).\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 1 \u2014 \"worst cycle -412 pts\" is impossible; the tail cycles are silently missing (edge inflated)\n\nA monthly short strangle running 2019\u20132026 **must** contain the COVID cycles.\nVerified from the last-Thursday calendar:\n\n- Expiry **2020-03-26**, entry ~**2020-02-10** (pre-crash, so both \u00b13% strikes are\n  liquid at entry and the cycle *is* created).\n- Expiry **2020-04-30**, entry ~**2020-03-16**.\n\nFor 2020-03-26: Feb-10 ref \u2248 12,100 \u2192 PE strike \u2248 3% below \u2248 11,740; settlement\nspot late-March \u2248 8,600 \u2192 **PE intrinsic \u2248 3,100 pts** loss on that single cycle.\nPremium collected is ~100\u2013300 pts, so `pnl \u2248 -2,800 pts`. The April and several\n2022 cycles are also deep-ITM blow-ups. **A correct run's worst cycle is in the\nthousands of points, not -412.** So the catastrophic cycles are not in the quoted\nstats.\n\n**Mechanism (verified in pandas):** whenever `chain.price(...)` returns NaN for a\nleg (strike absent / illiquid / a chain gap for that expiry), `prem` \u2192 NaN \u2192\n`pnl` \u2192 NaN. Then:\n- `r[\"pnl\"].mean()` and `r[\"pnl\"].min()` **skip NaN by default** \u2192 those cycles\n  vanish from avg pnl AND from the worst-case.\n- `win = pnl > 0` scores `NaN > 0` as **False** \u2192 the same cycle still sits in the\n  hit-rate denominator as a \"loss.\"\n\nConfirmed: series `[50,30,NaN,-100,NaN,40]` \u2192 hit rate 50%, avg 5.0, worst -100.0,\ncount 4 of 6. Net effect: tail losses are deleted from P&L while polluting the\nhit rate. This is exactly the signature that produces a too-clean -412.\n\n**Fix:** hard-fail on missing prices \u2014 `assert r[\"pnl\"].notna().all()`; never let\na missing leg become a silent NaN; reconcile the cycle count; explicitly confirm\nthe Feb\u2013Mar 2020, 2022 selloff, etc. cycles are present and priced; sanity-check\nthe worst cycle against known crash magnitude before quoting any tail number.\n\n## Material defect 2 \u2014 July-2026 cycle settles after data end; `asof` fabricates a near-max win\n\nCalendar gives **91** monthly expiries for 2019-01\u20262026-07, but **90** are\nreported (a mismatch that itself signals a silently dropped/altered cycle). The\nJuly-2026 expiry is **2026-07-30**, which is **after the data end 2026-06-30**.\n`settle_spot = spot.asof(exp)` returns the **stale 2026-06-30 close with no\nerror** (verified: `s.asof('2026-07-30')` \u2192 last value, silently). Its entry\n(~2026-06-15) is inside the data so `prem` is real, but the fabricated settle \u2248\nentry-time spot \u21d2 both \u00b13% strikes finish OTM \u21d2 `payoff \u2248 0` \u21d2 the cycle is\nbooked as a **near-max WIN on invented data**, inflating both hit rate and avg\npnl.\n\n**Fix:** for settlement use exact membership (`exp in spot.index`) or filter\n`exp <= data_end`; do not use `asof`, which forward-fills past the end. Drop any\ncycle whose true settlement date is not in the data.\n\n## Material defect 3 \u2014 first cycle references pre-data spot \u2192 NaN, scored as a loss\n\nThe first cycle (expiry 2019-01-31) has entry ~2018-12-17, so\n`ref = spot.asof(entry_day - 1 day)` reaches ~2018-12-16, before any plausible\n2019-01 data start \u2192 `asof` returns NaN \u2192 NaN strikes \u2192 NaN premium \u2192 NaN pnl.\nBy the Defect-1 mechanism it is skipped from avg/worst but counted as a loss in\nthe hit rate. (Likely explains part of the 91-vs-90 mismatch.)\n\n**Fix:** require the spot/chain series to start \u226545 days before the first expiry;\nassert no NaN `ref`/`prem`.\n\n---\n\n## Root cause\nAll three share one root: **silent NaN / stale handling (`asof` + default\n`skipna`) instead of hard failures.** That is precisely what lets a short-vol\nbacktest look clean while its worst cases evaporate. This is the classic tail-\ntruncation / survivorship failure the firm's D-028 lookahead & fill guards exist\nto catch \u2014 no result should be quoted without a `notna().all()` + coverage\nassertion and a worst-cycle-vs-known-crash cross-check.\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled \u2014 not defects)\n- **[minor] Settlement price basis:** `spot.asof(exp)` uses the index *close*, but\n  NIFTY options cash-settle on the weighted average of the expiry-day last 30\n  minutes. Typically <0.1% difference; use the official settlement series if\n  available. Not the cause of the headline error.\n- **[minor] Costs:** `COSTS_PTS = 4.5` all-in. Position is held to expiry (no exit\n  trade \u2014 cash-settled), so this covers entry slippage on two legs + STT/exchange\n  charges + 0.125% exercise-STT on ITM settlement notional. On the light side but\n  not obviously wrong; reconcile against COST_STANDARDS. Immaterial vs Defects 1\u20133.\n- **[trivial]** `win = pnl > 0` scores an exact scratch (pnl = 0) as a loss.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n1. **Tail cycles silently dropped** \u2014 `chain.price` NaN \u2192 `pnl` NaN, then\n   `mean()`/`min()` skip NaN while `pnl>0` scores NaN as a loss; the reported\n   worst -412 pts is impossible given the Feb\u2013Mar 2020 COVID cycles (~-2,800 pts\n   each). Edge and worst-case are fabricated by omission. Fix: `assert notna().all()`\n   + coverage/worst-case cross-check.\n2. **July-2026 cycle uses stale settlement** \u2014 expiry 2026-07-30 is past data end\n   2026-06-30; `spot.asof(exp)` returns the stale June-30 close silently \u21d2 a\n   fabricated near-max win. Fix: exact settlement-date membership, drop `exp > data_end`.\n3. **First cycle references pre-data spot** \u2014 entry ~2018-12-17 with data starting\n   2019-01 \u21d2 NaN ref/premium, skipped from avg/worst but counted as a loss in the\n   hit rate. Fix: require data to begin \u226545 days before the first expiry; assert\n   no NaN.\n\n----- ANSWER O06c -----\n# T06 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT (Arm C)\nMonthly NIFTY short strangle backtest review. Consolidator: Rajan Mehta (CIO, E-001).\nInputs: Quant review (Arjun Rao, Head of Quant) + Red-Team review (Nikhil Bose, E-014).\nEvidence base: the pasted task text + the two pasted reviews ONLY. No files read, no code run.\nIndex levels quoted below are [INFERENCE] reconstructed from memory \u2014 directionally robust, must be\nconfirmed against the per-cycle table before any number is quoted externally.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT \u2014 results-not-as-claimed (FAKE)\n\n**3-line rationale.**\n1. The headline `worst = -412 pts` is arithmetically impossible for a held-to-expiry \u00b13% naked short\n   strangle over a span that contains both the June-2024 post-election rally and (if priced) March-2020.\n   A single definitely-present cycle (Jun-2024) loses ~-800 pts held to expiry; the number is therefore\n   either fabricated or computed on a censored, tail-stripped subset.\n2. The code contains the exact machinery that silently strips the tail while leaving the cycle count\n   intact: `len(r)` counts every iterated expiry, `mean()`/`min()` skip NaN cycles, and `win = NaN > 0`\n   books a missing-data cycle as a false loss. Count, hit-rate, avg and worst can be three different\n   subsets.\n3. Entry-side hygiene and the cash-settle-at-intrinsic handling are genuinely correct \u2014 this is a\n   competent build with a concealed left tail, which is more dangerous than a sloppy one. Not certifiable;\n   do not quote a single aggregate until the per-cycle table is produced and audited.\n\n---\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT (CIO \u2014 the reason this gets a hard REJECT, not a revise-and-resubmit)\n\nThis is the textbook short-vol fabrication signature and it triggers our standing firm lesson: *win small\noften, lose huge rarely \u2014 a backtest that shows no huge loss over 2019-2026 has lost its tail somewhere.*\n\n- **The impossibility bound (adopted from Red Team, strongest framing).** `pnl = prem \u2212 payoff \u2212 COSTS`.\n  For `pnl \u2265 \u2212412` we need `payoff \u2264 prem \u2212 COSTS + 412 \u2248 prem + 407`. At a realistic 45-DTE \u00b13% NIFTY\n  premium of ~250\u2013500 pts, that caps the payoff (breach beyond a 3%-OTM strike) at ~650\u2013900 pts, i.e.\n  ~3\u20135% beyond the strike, i.e. a total entry\u2192expiry move of only ~6\u20138% in **either** direction. So\n  `worst = -412` is mathematically equivalent to the claim: *NIFTY never moved more than ~6\u20138%\n  entry-to-expiry across all 90 cycles from 2019 to 2026.* That claim is false many times over.\n- **Worst single-cycle (true):** if the crash cycle is priced, **March-2020 \u2248 \u22122,900 pts** (entry ~10-Feb\n  ~12,100, PE strike ~11,740, settle ~26-Mar ~8,600 \u2192 PE breach ~3,140 vs premium ~250). Held to expiry\n  it does NOT recover \u2014 the strangle is already cash-settled deep ITM on expiry day.\n- **Worst DATA-PRESENT cycle (unconditional):** **June-2024 \u2248 \u2212800 pts** (entry ~13-May ~22,100, CE strike\n  ~22,760, settle ~27-Jun ~24,050, +~8.7% rally \u2192 CE breach ~1,290 vs elevated premium ~500). This sits\n  inside the window where the option chain is known present and passes the stated volume>0 filter. One\n  data-present cycle already falsifies the headline with zero dependence on the 2019-2020 data question.\n- **Correlated-blowup scenario:** the vehicle is a naked \u00b13% strangle. In a gap/limit-move regime every\n  cycle open at the time takes the full breach simultaneously; there is no diversification inside a\n  single-underlying monthly line. True worst-month = the worst single cycle (~-2,900 in 2020). At a\n  22,000 index that is ~13% of notional and a multiple of that as % of SPAN margin \u2014 a catastrophic-tail\n  vehicle whose left tail this backtest has erased.\n\n**Sizing ruling:** ZERO. No paper, no forward-test, no register entry, no sizing anchor of any kind may be\nderived from these numbers (P-01, IC-1 firewall precedent). The reported avg +41 / hit 84% are themselves\nvoid because they are computed with the true left tail removed. This result is not a candidate; it is a\ndata-integrity failure.\n\n---\n\n## ADJUDICATION OF THE TWO REVIEWS (what survives, what I overrule)\n\nBoth reviewers reach the correct verdict (FAKE) via the same core impossibility. Where they disagree, I\nrule as follows:\n\n- **Jun-2024 survivability \u2014 RED TEAM CORRECT, QUANT REVIEW WRONG.** Arjun explicitly waved off Jun-2024 as\n  \"recovers by monthly expiry, so survivable.\" That is backwards. The realized risk was not the 4-Jun\n  gap-down; it was the post-election **rally** carrying NIFTY ~+8.7% **above** entry by the 27-Jun expiry,\n  blowing out the **short call**. Held to expiry it loses ~-800. This correction is material: it converts\n  the kill from \"conditional on whether 2019-2020 chain exists\" to **unconditional**, because Jun-2024 is\n  in the most-confidently-present part of the sample.\n- **Jul-2026 fabrication (Quant defect #1) \u2014 DOWNGRADED per Red Team.** Jan-2019\u2192Jul-2026 inclusive is 91\n  monthly expiries; the report says **90**. Jan-2019\u2192Jun-2026 is exactly 90. So the specific \"Jul-2026\n  booked beyond the 2026-06-30 data end via asof carry-forward\" claim is **unconfirmed** and in tension\n  with the count \u2014 if it had been iterated and appended, `len(r)` would read 91. What survives is (a) the\n  **general** `spot.asof(exp)` latent poison (silent carry-forward for any missing/holiday/out-of-range\n  expiry, never errors), and (b) an **unreconciled 90-vs-91 count discrepancy** that itself must be\n  explained. I keep this as a material finding but re-scoped to (a)+(b), not the specific Jul-2026 fabrication.\n- **\"2019-2020 chain is all NaN\" premise (Quant defect #2 support) \u2014 CATEGORY ERROR flagged by Red Team,\n  UPHELD.** The \"backfilled only from ~Apr-2024\" and \"17-month hole\" landmines are about the **single-stock**\n  options set (`stocks_options/`), NOT NIFTY **index** options, which have deep liquid history well before\n  2019. The subset MECHANISM (defect #2) stands regardless; the specific factual premise that every\n  2019-2020 index cycle is NaN is **speculative** and should not have been stated as near-certain.\n- **Quant defect #3 (internal inconsistency with the data guarantee) \u2014 NOT an independent defect.** It is a\n  restatement of the impossibility/verdict. Folded into the verdict above; not counted separately.\n- **\"What's done right\" credits \u2014 CONFIRMED and retained** (see below). Neither reviewer manufactured\n  problems here.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS (consolidated, most-severe first)\n\n**1. Reported `worst = -412 pts` is impossible \u2014 the left tail is concealed (result is FAKE).**\n- Field/claim: reported \"worst cycle -412 pts\" against the code's `pnl = prem \u2212 payoff \u2212 COSTS_PTS`, over\n  the claimed span 2019-01\u21922026-07.\n- Mechanism: for the worst to be \u2212412, no cycle may breach its 3%-OTM strike by more than ~3\u20135%, i.e. no\n  entry\u2192expiry move >~6\u20138% across 90 cycles. The definitely-present **Jun-2024** cycle (entry ~13-May\n  ~22,100 \u2192 settle ~27-Jun ~24,050, +~8.7%) breaches the short call by ~1,290 pts and loses ~-800 held to\n  expiry; if the chain reaches 2020, **Mar-2020** loses ~-2,900. Either the losers are in the sample (then\n  \u2212412 is fabricated/mis-computed) or they were dropped (then \"90 cycles / 84% / +41 / \u2212412 since 2019\" is\n  a survivor subset). Both branches \u21d2 not-as-claimed.\n- Fix: dump the full per-cycle table (expiry, entry_day, ce_k, pe_k, prem, settle_spot, pnl, win). Verify\n  **Jun-2024 appears with a ~-800 loss** (not NaN, not suppressed). If 2020 cycles are present, verify\n  **Mar-2020 \u2248 \u22122,900**; if absent, restate the span to the realized start and recompute every aggregate on\n  that honest window. No aggregate may be quoted until this table exists.\n\n**2. Broken/unvalidated metric denominators \u2014 the vehicle that hides any dropped cycle.**\n- Field: `for exp in expiries: results.append(...)`; `len(r)`; `r[\"pnl\"].mean()`; `r[\"pnl\"].min()`;\n  `win = pnl > 0`.\n- Mechanism: `len(r)` counts every iterated expiry regardless of pricing success. If `chain.price(...)`\n  returns NaN, `prem`\u2192NaN\u2192`pnl`\u2192NaN. pandas `mean()`/`min()` default `skipna=True`, so NaN cycles are\n  silently excluded from avg and worst \u2014 while still counted in `len(r)`. Simultaneously `win = (NaN > 0)`\n  evaluates **False**, booking a missing-data cycle as a **false loss** in the hit-rate denominator. Net:\n  the count, hit-rate, avg and worst can each be computed over a **different subset** \u2014 the exact machinery\n  by which a dropped tail cycle vanishes from `min()` while \"90 cycles since 2019\" survives as a calendar\n  artifact. (Note: the specific premise that 2019-2020 index cycles are NaN is speculative \u2014 a single-stock\n  landmine mis-applied to index options \u2014 but the mechanism is valid independent of that premise.)\n- Fix: after pricing, `assert` non-null premium AND non-null settlement per cycle; then compute count,\n  hit-rate, avg and worst on ONE identical validated subset. Print the non-null cycle count and the\n  min/max expiry among non-null cycles, and confirm they equal the claimed window before quoting anything.\n\n**3. `settle_spot = spot.asof(exp)` \u2014 unguarded settlement (latent poison) + unreconciled cycle count.**\n- Field: `settle_spot = spot.asof(exp)`; calendar `monthly_expiry_calendar(\"2019-01\",\"2026-07\")`; reported\n  count 90; data \"through 2026-06-30\".\n- Mechanism: `asof` never errors on a missing/holiday/out-of-range key \u2014 it silently returns the last close\n  at-or-before, so any expiry not exactly present in the spot index gets a **stale settlement** rather than\n  a failure. Separately, Jan-2019\u2192Jul-2026 inclusive is **91** monthly expiries but the report says **90**\n  (Jan-2019\u2192Jun-2026 = exactly 90). The specific Jul-2026 out-of-range fabrication is therefore\n  **unconfirmed** (and in tension with the count), but the 90-vs-91 gap is itself unexplained and the\n  carry-forward remains a live settlement-integrity risk for any holiday-shifted expiry.\n- Fix: `assert exp <= spot.index.max()` (drop any unsettleable cycle); replace `asof` with an exact,\n  holiday-adjusted expiry-date lookup guarded by `assert settle_date == exp`; never allow a silent\n  carry-forward. Reconcile and state the exact cycle count (90 vs 91) with the realized first/last expiry.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL NOTES (labelled \u2014 do not move the verdict)\n\n- [non-material] Settlement uses the index **close**, not the NSE final settlement price (last-30-min\n  VWAP). A few-pts proxy error per cycle; acceptable for research, standardize it. The dangerous part of\n  `asof` is the carry-forward (defect #3), not close-vs-settlement.\n- [non-material] `COSTS_PTS = 4.5` all-in likely under-models **entry** slippage on the far/thin 45-DTE\n  legs. Since the position is cash-settled at intrinsic with no exit trade, **no exit slippage is correct**\n  \u2014 that part is right.\n- [OPINION] Entry 45 calendar days out trades the next-next monthly on the entry date; liquidity is\n  thinner. Task enforces volume>0 so acceptable, but a per-year coverage check is warranted.\n\n## DONE CORRECTLY (kept honest \u2014 competent build, concealed tail)\n\n- Entry-side PIT hygiene is clean: strikes set from `spot.asof(entry_day - 1d)` (prior close), premium read\n  at entry_day. No lookahead in strike selection.\n- Payoff is the correct short-strangle intrinsic, **cash-settled from the underlying**\n  (`max(S\u2212Kce,0)+max(Kpe\u2212S,0)`) \u2014 correctly avoids landmine #9 (they did NOT read an expiry-day option\n  settle).\n- One P&L booked per cycle at expiry (exit-period booking, one position per cycle) \u2014 avoids the\n  spread-across-holding-days fake-low-variance / fake-Sharpe artifact and any monthly-averaging co-movement\n  variance cut.\n\n---\n\n## KILL CRITERIA / CONDITIONS TO RE-OPEN (FAKE \u2192 auditable)\n\nDo NOT resubmit until ALL of the following are demonstrated on the per-cycle table:\n1. Non-null-pnl cycle count and min/max expiry equal the claimed window, with the **90-vs-91** arithmetic\n   reconciled explicitly.\n2. **Jun-2024 present with a loss on the order of ~-800 pts** (not NaN, not suppressed).\n3. If 2020 cycles are present, **Mar-2020 \u2248 \u22122,900**; if absent, the span label is restated to the realized\n   start and every aggregate recomputed on that honest window.\n4. All four metrics (count, hit-rate, avg, worst) computed on the SAME validated non-null subset, after\n   `assert prem notna`, exact holiday-adjusted expiry lookup with `assert settle_date == exp`, and\n   `assert exp <= spot.index.max()`.\n\n**Review date:** on resubmission only. This is not a scheduled re-review \u2014 the burden is on the submitter.\n\n---\n\n## DISSENTS (recorded by name)\n\n- **Nikhil Bose (Red Team):** none against this consolidation \u2014 his corrections (Jun-2024 unconditional\n  kill; downgrade of the Jul-2026 specific; single-stock-vs-index category error) are ADOPTED in full.\n- **Arjun Rao (Head of Quant):** overruled on two points \u2014 (a) Jun-2024 is NOT survivable held-to-expiry\n  (upside call breach, ~-800); (b) the Jul-2026 fabrication and the \"2019-2020 chain all-NaN\" premise are\n  overstated/unconfirmed. His verdict (FAKE), his defect-#2 mechanism, and his \"done-right\" credits stand.\n\n**Bottom line:** REJECT. Verdict FAKE. No number from this backtest may inform sizing, forward-testing, or\nany register entry until the per-cycle audit above is passed.\n\n----- ANSWER O06d -----\n# T06 \u2014 Review: monthly NIFTY short strangle (arm A)\n\n**Verdict: material defects present. The headline numbers are not trustworthy \u2014 the loss tail of a short-vol strategy has been silently dropped.**\n\n## What the code actually does right (so I don't false-flag it)\n- **Settlement is cash-settled at intrinsic from spot**, not read off an expiry-day option\n  print: `payoff = max(settle_spot - ce_k, 0) + max(pe_k - settle_spot, 0)`. This correctly\n  avoids the expiry-day `SETTLE_PR` landmine (firm DATA LANDMINE #9). Good.\n- **Strikes are set from the prior close** (`ref = spot.asof(entry_day - 1d)`) and the trade\n  is priced on `entry_day`, so there is no strike-selection lookahead.\n- **P&L sign is correct** for a short position: `pnl = prem - payoff - COSTS`.\n\nThe problem is not the mechanics of a single cycle \u2014 it is which cycles survive into the stats.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 1 \u2014 The reported worst case is physically impossible; the crash cycles are being dropped\n\n**Claim under review:** \"worst cycle -412 pts\", \"avg +41 pts/cycle\", \"hit rate 84%\".\n\n**Why -412 is impossible for this structure.** This is an *uncapped* short strangle with strikes\nat only \u00b13% of spot, held to expiry. The 2019\u20132026 window contains the Feb/Mar-2020 COVID crash.\nTrace the March-2020 cycle explicitly:\n- Expiry \u2248 26-Mar-2020. `entry_day = last_trading_day_on_or_before(exp - 45d)` \u2248 10-Feb-2020,\n  NIFTY \u2248 12,100.\n- `pe_k = round_to_strike(0.97 * 12100)` \u2248 11,700.\n- Settlement (26-Mar-2020) \u2248 8,660.\n- PE payoff = 11,700 \u2212 8,660 \u2248 **3,040 pts**; premium collected \u2248 150\u2013250 pts.\n- Cycle P&L \u2248 **\u22122,800 pts**, not \u2212412.\n\nA \u00b13% uncapped short strangle that spans March-2020 (and, less violently, the June-2024\nelection-day gap and the 2022 hikes) *must* show a worst cycle in the multiple-thousands of\npoints. A reported worst of **\u2212412 pts is a red flag that the catastrophic cycles are absent\nfrom the results DataFrame.**\n\n**Mechanism by which they vanish.** The chain is filtered to `volume>0 on both legs`, and the\npremium is fetched with `chain.price(entry_day, exp, ce_k, \"CE\") + chain.price(... \"PE\")`. For\nthe exact \u00b13% strikes at ~45 DTE on the *specific* entry day, a leg can have no trade print on\nthat day (this is common in stressed / illiquid conditions and for far strikes 45 days out).\nWhen a leg is missing, `chain.price` returns NaN \u2192 `prem` is NaN \u2192 `pnl` is NaN. Then:\n- `r[\"pnl\"].mean()` and `r[\"pnl\"].min()` are pandas reductions with **`skipna=True` by default**,\n  so every NaN cycle is **silently excluded** from both the average and the worst case.\n- `r[\"win\"] = pnl > 0` evaluates NaN as `False`, so the dropped cycle is quietly booked as a\n  \"loss\" in the hit rate but contributes **zero** to avg and never appears in `min()`.\n\nNet effect: the worst, most illiquid cycles (exactly the crash cycles that define short-vol\nrisk) are removed from `avg` and `worst`, inflating avg to +41 and truncating worst to \u2212412,\nwhile hit rate looks broadly intact. **This is the classic short-vol backtest that hides its own\nleft tail.**\n\n**Fix.**\n1. Do not let missing legs pass silently. Assert a non-NaN premium for every cycle; on a missing\n   leg, either fall back to the nearest liquid \u00b13% strike with a recorded substitution, or raise.\n2. Explicitly verify the Feb-2020\u2192Mar-2020, 2022, and Jun-2024 cycles are present and print their\n   true (multi-thousand-point) losses.\n3. Report `r[\"pnl\"].isna().sum()` and refuse to publish stats if it is > 0.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 2 \u2014 Cycle count is not asserted, and 90 \u2260 the calendar length (silent drops confirmed)\n\n`monthly_expiry_calendar(\"2019-01\", \"2026-07\")` spans 91 months (2019: 12, 2020\u20132025: 72,\n2026 Jan\u2013Jul: 7 = **91 expiries**), yet the result reports **90 cycles**. At least one cycle is\nmissing and nothing in the code checks for it. This is direct corroboration of Defect 1: cycles\nare being dropped (NaN premium) without any guard, so the population the stats run over is not the\npopulation the header claims.\n\n**Fix.** After the loop, `assert len(r) == len(expiries)`, and log every expiry that failed to\nproduce a fully-priced, fully-settled cycle. The count discrepancy must be reconciled before any\nnumber is quoted.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 3 \u2014 The 2026-07 cycle fabricates a settlement (data ends 2026-06-30)\n\nThe spot/chain dataset \"runs through 2026-06-30\", but the loop includes the **2026-07 expiry**\n(last-Thursday \u2248 30-Jul-2026), which has not occurred / is out of data range.\n- `entry_day` for that expiry \u2248 mid-Jun-2026, which *is* in range, so a real entry premium is\n  taken.\n- `settle_spot = spot.asof(exp)` uses `Series.asof`, which **returns the last value at or before\n  `exp`** \u2014 i.e. the **30-Jun-2026 close** \u2014 and silently treats it as the July settlement level.\n\nSo this cycle books a P&L against a fabricated (stale) settlement for a strangle that has not yet\nexpired. Because `asof` never errors on an out-of-range date, the fabrication is invisible.\n\n**Fix.** Restrict the loop to expiries whose *settlement date* \u2264 data cutoff\n(`exp <= data_cutoff`). Replace `spot.asof(exp)` with an exact-date lookup that **raises** if the\nexpiry-day close is absent, so missing settlement data can never be silently backfilled by a prior\nday's close. (This same `asof` staleness would also corrupt any expiry that happened to be a\nmarket holiday, quietly using the previous session's level.)\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled \u2014 not part of the defect count)\n- **Settlement uses the index close, not the NSE settlement price** (last-half-hour VWAP of\n  expiry day). Small approximation; directionally minor.\n- **`COSTS_PTS = 4.5` all-in likely omits expiry-day STT on ITM settlement** (STT ~0.125% of\n  settlement/intrinsic value on the ITM leg). This bites *precisely the losing cycles*, so it\n  understates the tail further; should be modelled per COST_STANDARDS. Non-material only relative\n  to Defect 1, but worth fixing once the tail is restored.\n- **Expiry-day convention changed over 2019\u20132026** (NIFTY monthly expiry weekday shifted); fine if\n  `monthly_expiry_calendar` is the true exchange calendar, but confirm it, since the 45-day entry\n  offset compounds any calendar error.\n- **Entry assumed fillable at the close print** on a specific day for two specific strikes; a\n  fill-realism note, not a correctness bug.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n1. **Reported worst case (\u2212412 pts) is impossible for a \u00b13% uncapped short strangle spanning\n   March-2020; NaN premiums (from the `volume>0` filter on the exact strikes) are silently dropped\n   by pandas `skipna` in `.mean()`/`.min()`, deleting the crash cycles and inflating avg (+41) /\n   truncating worst.** The headline stats are fake as reported.\n2. **Cycle count is unasserted and wrong: 90 reported vs 91 in the calendar \u2014 direct evidence of\n   silent cycle drops.**\n3. **The 2026-07 cycle is out of data range; `spot.asof(exp)` fabricates its settlement from the\n   30-Jun-2026 close** (same `asof` staleness would silently mis-settle any holiday/missing expiry\n   day).", "ids": ["O06a", "O06b", "O06c", "O06d"]}, {"tid": "T07", "key": "## T07 \u2014 CLEAN (data-landmine theme)\n**No material defect.** Decision on Tuesday data after Tuesday's close; execution\nnext day at leg OPEN prints; expiry chosen on YESTERDAY'S liquidity (CONTRACTS>0,\ncausal) with a conservative same-day no-fill re-check; exit cash-settled at\nintrinsic from the INDEX close (correctly avoids expiry-day option SETTLE_PR);\nmax-date guard stated; slippage/costs charged; event weeks skipped.\n**Reject as false positives.** \"Uses SETTLE_PR wrong\" (it never reads option\nSETTLE_PR); \"CONTRACTS>0 on entry day is lookahead\" (selection uses Tuesday's\nCONTRACTS \u2014 causal; the Wednesday check only SKIPS untradeable entries,\nconservative); \"bhavcopy OPEN not tradeable\" (open prints are executable prices;\nslippage is charged on top); \"no stop-loss\" (defined-risk structure \u2014 wings cap it).\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O07a -----\n# T07 arm B \u2014 Review: NIFTY weekly iron condor on F&O bhavcopy\n\n## Verdict\nThe core mechanics are sound and the submission correctly navigates the two\nbhavcopy landmines that usually break this kind of backtest. There is **one\nmaterial defect** \u2014 an optimistic fill / cost model that systematically\noverstates the net credit for a premium seller and plausibly erases the entire\nthin edge. Everything else is a labeled non-material note.\n\n## What the submission gets RIGHT (do not flag these)\nThese are traps this code avoids; calling them defects would be a false positive:\n\n- **Expiry-day settlement.** It does NOT read the expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR`\n  (which on the F&O bhavcopy is the underlying's final settlement level, not the\n  option price \u2014 the \u221215,428-pt landmine). It cash-settles each leg at\n  **intrinsic from the index close** (`settle = idx_close.asof(expiry)`), which\n  is the correct treatment for European cash-settled NIFTY index options held to\n  expiry. Correct.\n- **Untraded-but-priced far weeklies.** `pick_expiry(..., require_contracts=True)`\n  plus the entry-day `CONTRACTS == 0` gate and the monthly fallback correctly\n  refuse legs that are listed with a model settle but `CONTRACTS = 0`. Correct.\n- **P&L sign/structure.** Traced with a worked example: `credit = sc.OPEN +\n  sp.OPEN \u2212 lc.OPEN \u2212 lp.OPEN` (receive shorts, pay wings) and `payoff =\n  (settle\u2212Ksc)+ \u2212 (settle\u2212Klc)+ + (Ksp\u2212settle)+ \u2212 (Klp\u2212settle)+` (net liability).\n  `pnl = credit \u2212 payoff` bounds max loss at `width \u2212 credit`, consistent with the\n  reported \"worst week \u2212312 pts, wings capped it\" (1.5% width \u2248 360 pts at\n  2024\u201325 NIFTY levels, minus credit \u2248 312). Internally consistent. Correct.\n- **No strike-selection lookahead.** Strikes are set from Tuesday's close\n  (`ref = idx_close.asof(tuesday)`) and executed on Wednesday's OPEN \u2014 decision\n  precedes execution; no future data enters the strike choice. Correct.\n- **Expiry-availability guard** (`expiry <= idx_close.index.max()`) prevents\n  settling against missing data. Correct.\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT\n\n**M1 \u2014 Entry filled at a single bhavcopy `OPEN` per leg with ~4-tick slippage;\nthis systematically overstates the realizable credit for a 4-leg OTM-weekly net\nseller, and the modeled cost is far below the true bid\u2013ask.**\n\n- **Where:** `credit = rows[\"sc\"].OPEN + rows[\"sp\"].OPEN \u2212 rows[\"lc\"].OPEN \u2212\n  rows[\"lp\"].OPEN` then `credit -= slippage_ticks(4) + costs_pts()`.\n- **Mechanism:** the strategy is a net premium seller. Realistic execution sells\n  the two short legs at the **bid** (below OPEN) and buys the two wings at the\n  **ask** (above OPEN). All four adjustments move `credit` **down**, so filling\n  every leg at the same OPEN print is a one-directional upward bias, not noise.\n  The offset applied (`slippage_ticks(4)` \u2248 4 ticks \u00d7 0.05 = 0.2 pt, even \u00d74 legs\n  \u2248 0.8 pt) is an order of magnitude below the true half-spread of far-OTM NIFTY\n  weeklies \u2014 the 3%-OTM wings (`lc`, `lp`) in particular routinely quote\n  several-point-wide spreads and thin/stale opening prints. Against a headline\n  edge of **+6.1 pts/week**, an under-modeled 4-leg spread of even a few points\n  per leg plausibly flips the sleeve negative. This also violates the firm's own\n  fill-realism standard (COST_STANDARDS dynamic slippage; RP-39 fill-audit;\n  landmine 7b: 2\u20133\u00d7 slippage on thin volume). The magnitudes inside\n  `slippage_ticks()`/`costs_pts()` are not shown, so the exact impact can't be\n  quantified from the submission \u2014 which is itself the problem: a razor-thin edge\n  is being reported without a fill audit on the legs most exposed to spread.\n- **Fix:** cost each leg against its own bid/ask (shorts at bid, wings at ask), or\n  OPEN minus a per-leg half-spread scaled to moneyness (wider for the 3%-OTM\n  wings), calibrated to COST_STANDARDS; run `/fill-audit` / `lib/execution_realism.py`\n  against the bar range and CONTRACTS; then re-report. Do not certify the +6.1\n  figure until the net-of-realistic-cost result survives.\n\n## NON-MATERIAL NOTES (labeled; not defects)\n\n- **N1 (cost, second-order):** STT/settlement charges on **ITM legs at expiry**\n  appear omitted \u2014 `costs_pts()` is subtracted once at entry, but Indian STT on\n  option exercise/settlement is charged on ITM intrinsic at settlement. Adds a\n  few points of drag on losing weeks only; smaller than M1 but same direction.\n- **N2 (settlement proxy):** NIFTY options cash-settle at the last-half-hour\n  VWAP, not the close; `idx_close.asof(expiry)` is a reasonable proxy but\n  introduces zero-mean error, not bias.\n- **N3 (event exclusion):** skipping budget/RBI/election weeks is legitimate\n  (dates known ex-ante, no lookahead), but it removes the highest-vol weeks, so\n  the 71% hit rate and \u2212312 worst-week are conditional on that filter and are not\n  comparable to an always-on condor. Disclose alongside the headline.\n- **N4 (skip-filter timing):** the entry skip uses the **full-day** `CONTRACTS`\n  to reject a leg while filling at OPEN \u2014 a mild timing inconsistency, but it\n  removes far-OTM illiquid weeks (would-be full-credit winners), so it is\n  conservative / deflating, not inflating.\n- **N5 (labeling):** with a Tuesday anchor and \"nearest weekly\" (Thursday)\n  expiry, entry Wednesday \u2192 hold is ~1 trading day, not a week. \"avg +6.1\n  pts/week\" is really per ~1-day trade; fine for correctness, but don't annualize\n  it as a weekly hold.\n\n## Numbered list of claimed MATERIAL defects\n1. **M1** \u2014 Legs filled at a single bhavcopy `OPEN` with ~4-tick slippage\n   systematically overstates the net credit for a 4-leg OTM-weekly premium seller\n   (shorts should fill at bid, wings at ask; wing half-spreads far exceed 4 ticks),\n   plausibly erasing the +6.1 pt/week edge. Re-cost per-leg bid/ask via a fill\n   audit before certifying.\n\n(No lookahead, no P&L-sign error, and no expiry-settle/untraded-strike landmine\nwere found \u2014 those are handled correctly.)\n\n----- ANSWER O07b -----\n# T07 \u2014 Review: NIFTY weekly iron condor on F&O bhavcopy (arm A)\n\n## What the submission gets RIGHT (so I don't over-flag)\n- **Accounting/payoff math is correct.** `credit = sc + sp - lc - lp` is net premium received (shorts richer than the further-OTM longs). The expiry payoff `max(settle-sc,0) - max(settle-lc,0) + max(sp-settle,0) - max(lp-settle,0)` correctly caps each side at the wing width, and `pnl = credit - payoff` is right. Reported worst week -312 is consistent with a ~1.5% wing (\u2248300-400 pts) at NIFTY ~22-26k minus credit \u2014 no arithmetic inconsistency.\n- **It avoids landmine #9.** It does NOT read expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR`; it cash-settles at intrinsic from the index. It settles on **spot** (correct underlying), not futures.\n- **No decision\u2192execution lookahead.** Strikes are locked from Tuesday's close (`ref = idx_close.asof(tuesday)`) and the trade is filled the next business day; that is a legitimate \"decide EOD / execute next open\" design, not a peek.\n- **Exit models no slippage \u2014 and that is correct here.** Held to cash settlement there is no spread to cross; only the tiny STT-on-exercise is omitted (negligible in points). So the fill concern is entirely on the ENTRY side.\n- `expiry <= idx_close.index.max()` assert and CONTRACTS>0 existence-gating are the right instincts.\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS\n\n### 1. Entry fills at bhavcopy OPEN ignore the bid-ask and are not simultaneously attainable \u2014 the reported net edge is within (likely below) realistic entry cost\n`credit = rows[\"sc\"].OPEN + rows[\"sp\"].OPEN - rows[\"lc\"].OPEN - rows[\"lp\"].OPEN` uses each leg's daily OPEN **trade** price. Two problems:\n- **Adverse spread crossing on all 4 legs.** To open the condor you SELL sc/sp (you receive the *bid*) and BUY lc/lp (you pay the *ask*). The bhavcopy OPEN is a single trade print (could be anywhere in the spread), not your executable side. A condor crosses the spread in the losing direction on **four** legs, so the achievable net credit is lower than the OPEN-based credit by roughly 4 half-spreads.\n- **Not simultaneously fillable.** The four daily OPENs are the first prints of four different OTM strikes, occurring at different times/levels. You cannot lock the \"net of the opens\" as one credit.\n- **Magnitude kills the result.** The reported edge is **+6.1 pts/week net**. A single OTM NIFTY weekly leg's bid-ask is frequently 1-3+ pts; four legs' adverse half-spreads plausibly equal or exceed the entire 6.1-pt edge. The modeled `slippage_ticks(4)` (a few ticks \u2248 fractions of a point) does not cover this.\n- **The cost model is opaque and load-bearing.** The whole \"net\" result rests on `slippage_ticks(4)` and `costs_pts()`, which are not shown. If they model only ticks + statutory charges (not the real 4-leg OTM-weekly spread), the net is an artifact. The result cannot be certified without those functions, and structurally it under-costs entry.\n- **Mechanism \u2192 corruption:** systematic upward bias in collected credit every week \u2192 inflated avg pnl and hit rate; the sign of the edge is not established.\n- **Fix:** fill shorts at bid / longs at ask (or apply a per-leg half-spread haircut derived from actual quotes or a spread model); pin down and disclose `slippage_ticks`/`costs_pts`; then re-run and stress the credit to a wider spread. If +6.1 does not survive a realistic 4-leg spread, the sleeve is not real.\n\n### 2. Liquidity gate `CONTRACTS > 0` is far too weak to validate the fill price\nBoth the expiry selection (`require_contracts=True`) and the entry guard (`r.CONTRACTS == 0`) treat a contract as \"liquid/filled\" if **\u22651 lot traded all day**. `CONTRACTS` is total lots traded, so a single-lot print qualifies a leg and validates its OPEN as your fill. You cannot execute a 4-leg condor at a lone single-lot open print. Combined with defect #1, this systematically overstates executability and trusts unrepresentative prices.\n- **Fix:** require a meaningful per-leg volume/OI floor and a spread-width check before trusting the OPEN; only count the week as tradeable if all four legs clear that floor.\n\n### 3. Instrument/holding-period inconsistency across the 2021-2025 sample from the NSE weekly-expiry shift\nThe scheme is fixed: anchor = Tuesday, entry = Wednesday, expiry = \"nearest weekly.\"\n- Through the Thursday-expiry era, nearest weekly after a Tuesday anchor is that same-week **Thursday** \u2192 a ~1-day (Wed\u2192Thu) condor: almost pure gamma, little theta.\n- NSE moved NIFTY weekly (and monthly) expiry to **Tuesday** (effective ~Sep 2025). Once expiry falls on Tuesday, the nearest weekly after a Wednesday entry is the **following Tuesday** \u2192 a ~6-day condor: a completely different theta/gamma profile.\n- **Mechanism \u2192 corruption:** the reported aggregate (224 weeks, +6.1/week) silently **blends two different instruments** (\u22481-day condors early, \u22486-day condors late). The headline is not a single strategy, and per-week P&L is not comparable across the sample; risk and edge are mis-stated.\n- **Fix:** verify the actual weekly-expiry calendar for 2021-2025; segment (or normalize) results by expiry-day regime; confirm the Wed entry is never on/after the chosen expiry (a Tuesday-expiry + Wednesday-entry combination must not resolve to an already-expired or same-day contract). Report each regime separately.\n\n## NON-MATERIAL COMMENTS (clearly labelled)\n- **Settlement price:** NIFTY options cash-settle at the **last-half-hour spot VWAP**, not the close; the code uses `idx_close.asof(expiry)`. The difference is ~zero-mean noise (a few pts), so it adds variance/false precision but is not a directional bias \u2014 and the \"settle-from-underlying\" approach itself is firm-sanctioned (#9). Minor.\n- **Comment/code mismatch:** the comment says \"if none qualifies, fall back to the current monthly,\" but the shown path does `skip(\"no liquid expiry\")`. Fine only if `pick_expiry` performs the monthly fallback internally and returns None only when even monthly fails; otherwise it is a contradiction worth resolving. Non-material to the numbers as written.\n- **Scheduled-event skip:** skipping budget/RBI/election-result weeks is legitimate ex-ante (dates are known in advance), but it removes precisely the condor's worst tail weeks, so the reported worst-week (-312) and 71% hit rate understate true risk. Disclose that the risk profile excludes scheduled-event weeks.\n- **No-fill DROP of illiquid weeks (31 skipped):** consistent with firm policy (D-031), but illiquidity clusters with stress, so the drops are likely non-random and mildly flatter avg/hit-rate. Note, not a defect.\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n1. Entry fills at bhavcopy OPEN ignore adverse bid-ask crossing on all 4 legs and aren't simultaneously attainable; with the tiny +6.1-pt edge and opaque `slippage_ticks(4)`/`costs_pts()`, realistic entry cost plausibly equals or exceeds the edge \u2014 the net result is likely an artifact of optimistic fills.\n2. `CONTRACTS > 0` is too weak a liquidity gate \u2014 a single-lot print validates the OPEN as an executable condor fill; needs a real volume/OI/spread floor.\n3. Sample blends different instruments: the fixed Tuesday-anchor/Wednesday-entry/nearest-weekly scheme yields ~1-day condors in the Thursday-expiry era but ~6-day condors after NSE's ~Sep-2025 shift of NIFTY weekly expiry to Tuesday, so the aggregate +6.1/week mixes two strategies (verify against the actual expiry calendar; also confirm Wed entry never lands on/after expiry).\n\n----- ANSWER O07c -----\n# T07 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT: NIFTY weekly iron condor on F&O bhavcopy\n\n**Reviewer of record:** Rajan Mehta, CIO (E-001). Consolidates Quant (Arjun Rao) + Red Team (Nikhil Bose).\n**Evidence:** the pasted task text + the two memos ONLY (no files read, no code run, per constraint).\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT (do NOT certify; do NOT quote +6.1 / 71% / -312)\n\nThe **P&L engine is mechanically correct and PIT-clean** \u2014 I confirm both reviewers on that and I invent nothing there. But the reported **+6.1 pts/week is a thin residual smaller than the combined uncertainty of its own cost, fill, event, and observation-counting assumptions.** Any one of them plausibly flips the sign. This is FRAGILE, leaning fake on cost realism \u2014 not certifiable and not quotable until the items below are cleared.\n\nThree-line rationale:\n1. The edge is a **small difference of large numbers** (0.71\u00b7small-win \u2212 0.29\u00b7capped-loss); a few points of mis-costing moves the sign of the whole result.\n2. The cost model as written (`slippage_ticks(4)` + one-time entry `costs_pts()`, no settlement STT) is an **order of magnitude too small** for four options including deep-OTM \u00b13% weekly wings \u2014 Arjun mis-filed this as non-material; Nikhil is right that it is the **primary kill vector**.\n3. The \"weekly\" label and \"224 weeks\" are **not what they claim**: expiry selection is gated by illiquid wings, so DTE drifts and consecutive entries can share one expiry \u2014 inflating independent N and, because the drift clusters in calm (winning) regimes, flattering the average and hit rate.\n\n---\n\n## WHAT I VERIFIED AS CORRECT (no false alarms \u2014 both reviewers confirmed, I re-check)\n\n- **[DATA] P&L identity is exact.** `credit = sc.OPEN + sp.OPEN \u2212 lc.OPEN \u2212 lp.OPEN` (both long wings correctly PAID) and `pnl = credit \u2212 payoff` with `payoff = max(S\u2212Ksc,0) \u2212 max(S\u2212Klc,0) + max(Ksp\u2212S,0) \u2212 max(Klp\u2212S,0)`. Expanding short=+prem\u2212intrinsic, long=\u2212prem+intrinsic reproduces this term-for-term. No sign error, no missing leg.\n- **[DATA] Valid defined-risk condor.** Klc(+3.0%) > Ksc(+1.5%), Klp(\u22123.0%) < Ksp(\u22121.5%); wing width 1.5% of spot (~300\u2013360 pts at 20k\u201324k). Worst \u2212312 is consistent with a breach net of credit \u2014 structurally the max loss, not fabricated.\n- **[DATA] PIT / lookahead clean.** All decision inputs \u2264 Tuesday (`idx_close.asof(tuesday)`); fill at Wednesday OPEN; realized `settle` is used ONLY in the payoff, never fed back into strike/expiry selection. No terminal-date leak; a one-day-lag would degrade, not collapse. **I did not manufacture a lookahead defect to beat the audit \u2014 there is none.**\n- **[DATA] Landmine #9 avoided.** Does NOT read expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR`; cash-settles at intrinsic from the index. Correct.\n- **[DATA] Landmines #4/#9 dual-schema handled.** Gates `CONTRACTS>0` at selection and `OPEN>0 & CONTRACTS>0` at entry \u2014 no phantom 0.00 fills, no naked-short slip-through.\n- **[DATA] Reported per-trade points, not a monthly-compounded CAGR / averaged Sharpe** \u2014 respects the firm's per-trade-edge lesson.\n\nNeither memo contains a fabricated defect. Arjun's #3 (index-close vs settlement VWAP) and #5 (EOD-CONTRACTS gate) are real and correctly labelled non-material. Nikhil's confirmations match mine.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS THAT SURVIVE\n\n### 1. COST / SLIPPAGE / SETTLEMENT-STT UNDER-COSTING \u2014 PRIMARY\n- **Line:** `credit -= slippage_ticks(4) + costs_pts()`.\n- **Mechanism (two errors, both biasing the +6.1 residual UPWARD):**\n  - **[INFERENCE]** NIFTY option tick = 0.05, so `slippage_ticks(4)` \u2248 **0.20 pts** (or ~0.8 pts if read as 4 ticks \u00d7 4 legs) \u2014 an order of magnitude below a realistic half-spread crossing on **four** options, two of which are deep-OTM \u00b13% weekly wings at 1\u20132 DTE with wide bid-ask. Realistic ~1\u20132.5 pts **per leg** \u21d2 **4\u201310 pts round-in**, i.e. more than the entire 6.1-pt edge.\n  - **[DATA]** Costs are subtracted **once, from entry credit only.** There is **no settlement-side STT / exercise charge** on the ~29% of weeks that finish ITM (a short leg breached) \u2014 precisely the high-magnitude losing weeks. Omitting it under-costs the loss tail and biases the average up. This omission is structural and confirmable from the code, independent of the slippage-magnitude argument.\n- **[OPINION]** `slippage_ticks()` and `costs_pts()` are black boxes; on a residual edge an unverifiable, near-zero cost model *is* the result.\n- **Fix:** model per-leg half-spread on the **actual OTM contracts traded**, plus settlement/exercise STT on ITM legs at expiry. Certification gate: the **2\u00d7-cost / honest-spread placebo must keep the weekly-P&L bootstrap 5th percentile > 0.** Open the two cost functions for review.\n\n### 2. FILL REALISM AT BHAVCOPY OPEN ON THIN OTM WINGS\n- **Line:** execution fills each leg at its bhavcopy `OPEN`, gated only by `OPEN>0 & CONTRACTS==0` check.\n- **Mechanism:** the `OPEN>0` gate removes the 0.00 phantom/model-settle strikes but does **not** make a thinly-traded \u00b13% weekly's OPEN a *transactable* price \u2014 it can be a single wide/stale print. Taking it directly as the fill **systematically inflates collected credit** (distinct failure mode from the 0.00 case). Directionally the same as Defect 1: flatters the edge.\n- **Fix:** validate each leg's OPEN against contemporaneous volume/bid-ask; haircut or drop non-executable prints. This must be applied *before* slippage.\n\n### 3. EXPIRY-SELECTION INSTABILITY + OVERLAPPING / CORRELATED POSITIONS (broader than the monthly fallback)\n- **Lines:** `expiry = pick_expiry(fo, tuesday, legs, require_contracts=True)` + \"fall back to the current monthly\" + `book(week=tuesday, ...)`.\n- **Mechanism:**\n  - The filter \"nearest weekly where **all four** legs traded on Tuesday\" is bound by the **illiquid \u00b13% wings.** On calm weeks the current-week (1\u20132 DTE) wings don't trade, so selection drifts to a **later weekly (~9 DTE)** \u2014 uncontrolled DTE that moves with regime \u2014 or to a **monthly** of a completely different risk tenor still labelled \"weekly.\"\n  - Consecutive Tuesdays can select the **same** later/monthly expiry \u21d2 overlapping, highly-correlated positions booked as **independent \"weeks.\"** So \"224 weeks traded\" **overstates the number of independent bets** (this kills any future Sharpe/t-stat).\n  - **[INFERENCE]** Because the drift/fallback clusters in **calm regimes \u2014 which are condor WINS \u2014 correlated wins get counted multiple times, biasing the hit rate and average UPWARD** (not merely deflating an unreported variance; I adopt Nikhil's correction of Arjun's framing here \u2014 no Sharpe is reported, so the corruption is N-inflation + directional flattery of the two headline numbers).\n  - `book(week=tuesday, ...)` labels P&L in the ENTRY week though it is realized ~1\u20134 weeks later \u2014 distorts drawdown timing / autocorrelation.\n- **Fix:** control and **report the DTE distribution and fallback frequency**; **dedupe to one position per expiry**; book each trade in its **exit** week; **split the weekly vs monthly-fallback books** and report each separately. If the fallback/overlap share is non-trivial, the \"weekly\" label and the \"224\" N are unsound.\n\n### 4. EVENT-SKIP MUST BE PRE-REGISTERED EX-ANTE (material-conditional)\n- **Line:** \"weeks with a scheduled major event (budget, RBI, election result) are skipped.\"\n- **Mechanism:** budget/RBI/election dates are known in advance, so as a **fixed, frozen rule this is PIT-legal and fine.** It becomes a **tail curve-fit** if \"major event\" was defined *after* seeing which weeks lost. Sharpened per Nikhil: because loss is **defined-risk-capped**, deleting event weeks does **not** change worst-week (\u2212312 \u2248 max loss regardless); the flattery lands on the **AVERAGE and HIT RATE** by removing the weeks most likely to breach a wing. Cannot be adjudicated from the code.\n- **Fix:** publish the **git-frozen, mechanical event calendar + its freeze date**; decompose the 31 skips into event-skips vs entry no-fill skips; confirm none were chosen post-hoc.\n\n---\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT (CIO)\n- **Worst single week:** structurally \u2248 one wing width \u2212 credit \u2248 **\u2212300 pt class** per unit; the reported \u2212312 is the max-loss floor, not a robustness statistic. **[OPINION] \"worst week \u2212312\" carries almost no information** \u2014 any condor breached even once in 224 weeks reports a worst week \u2248 its cap. Do not let it read as evidence the result is real.\n- **Correlated-blowup scenario:** the true left tail is a **gap week** where the index opens through the long wing before Wednesday's fill, combined with the overlap defect \u2014 multiple overlapping condors sharing one expiry all breach together, so realized loss is **N-overlapped \u00d7 the \u2212300 cap**, not one \u2212312. Sizing to \"worst = \u2212312\" would understate the correlated worst month. Firm lesson holds: the tail comes from the *calm-looking* regime that lulls the selection into stacking correlated wins, then one event breaches them all at once.\n- **Net:** left tail is capped per-position but **under-counted at the book level** by the overlap, and the *average* that justifies carrying the tail is itself unverified after honest costs.\n\n## SIZING RULING\n**Zero size. Not certifiable, not paper-tradeable as reported.** No position, no register entry, no quoting of the headline. Re-adjudicate only after the four fixes below produce a survived placebo.\n\n## KILL CRITERIA / WHAT WOULD FLIP IT TO REAL\n1. Open `slippage_ticks` / `costs_pts`; re-run with realistic per-leg OTM half-spreads **and** settlement STT on ITM weeks; **2\u00d7-cost bootstrap 5th-pctile of weekly P&L must stay > 0.**\n2. Publish the frozen mechanical event calendar + freeze date; decompose the 31 skips; confirm no post-hoc selection.\n3. Report fallback frequency + DTE distribution; dedupe overlapping expiries to one position; book in the exit week; re-report avg/hit-rate on **independent bets only**.\n4. Run the full validation battery (DSR with honest trials **including** the baked-in choices \u2014 1.5%/3.0% offsets, Tuesday anchor, Wednesday fill, event-list definition \u2014 PBO, walk-forward, regime slices). Not an engine defect, but no certification without it.\n\n**Review date:** on resubmission with items 1\u20133 evidenced.\n\n## DISSENTS / ADJUDICATION OF THE TWO REVIEWS (recorded by name)\n- **Arjun Rao (Quant):** engine verification CONFIRMED and adopted. **Overruled on severity:** his cost note (his #4) is **material and primary**, not non-material. **Reframed:** his overlap \"understates variance/Sharpe\" \u2014 no Sharpe is reported, so the corruption is **N-inflation + hit-rate/average flattery** (Nikhil's correction, adopted).\n- **Nikhil Bose (Red Team):** cost-as-primary, event-skip-hits-avg/hit-rate-not-worst-week, within-weekly (not just monthly) overlap + DTE drift, and OTM-OPEN fill realism \u2014 all ADOPTED. No fabricated defects in either memo. No lookahead defect exists; neither reviewer invented one, and neither did I.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL NOTES (labelled, not counted as defects)\n- **[non-material]** Settlement uses index CLOSE, not the exchange final settlement (last-half-hour VWAP): a few-pt, non-directional approximation; switch for precision.\n- **[non-material]** `CONTRACTS==0` at entry uses Wednesday full-day volume to gate a Wednesday-OPEN fill \u2014 mild lookahead, near-equivalent to the `OPEN>0` executability check beside it.\n- **[non-material / plumbing]** NIFTY weekly expiry-day convention and lot size both changed across 2021\u20132025; `pick_expiry` must track the moving expiry day, and lot-size feeds the cost-in-points model (another reason to open the cost box). Robustness only.\n- **[required, not an engine defect]** Full DSR/PBO/walk-forward/regime battery absent \u2014 blocks REAL certification regardless of the above.\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED LIST \u2014 CLAIMED MATERIAL DEFECTS\n1. **Cost/slippage/settlement-STT under-costing (PRIMARY).** `credit -= slippage_ticks(4) + costs_pts()`: ~0.2\u20130.8 pt slippage is an order of magnitude too small for four options incl. deep-OTM \u00b13% weekly wings (~4\u201310 pts realistic round-in), and no settlement-side STT is charged on the ~29% ITM weeks. Both bias the +6.1 residual upward; honest costs plausibly flip the sign. Fix: real per-leg OTM half-spreads + settlement STT; require 2\u00d7-cost bootstrap 5th-pctile > 0.\n2. **Fill realism at bhavcopy OPEN on thin OTM wings.** `OPEN>0` gating removes only 0.00 phantoms, not the fact that a thinly-traded \u00b13% weekly's OPEN may be a single wide print; using it as the fill inflates collected credit. Fix: validate OPEN vs volume/bid-ask; haircut or drop non-executable prints.\n3. **Expiry-selection instability + overlapping/correlated positions (incl. monthly fallback).** The all-four-legs-traded filter is gated by illiquid \u00b13% wings, so DTE drifts and consecutive Tuesdays can share one expiry (and the monthly fallback swaps in a different-tenor instrument labelled \"weekly\"). \"224 weeks\" overstates independent N (kills any future Sharpe/t-stat) and calm-regime overlap flatters hit-rate/average; P&L booked in the entry week distorts drawdown timing. Fix: report DTE + fallback frequency, dedupe by expiry, book in exit week, split the two books.\n4. **Event-skip possibly post-hoc (material-conditional).** \"weeks with a scheduled major event \u2026 are skipped\" is PIT-legal only if the calendar is frozen ex-ante; if defined after seeing losers it deletes the highest-breach-probability weeks, flattering the AVERAGE and HIT RATE (not the defined-risk-capped worst week). Fix: publish the git-frozen mechanical event list + freeze date; decompose the 31 skips; confirm no post-hoc selection.\n\n*(No lookahead defect \u2014 confirmed clean, not invented. No fabricated defects in either reviewer's memo \u2014 the P&L/PIT/landmine verifications are correct; the corrections were one severity mis-classification and one mis-statement of the overlap's effect, both resolved above.)*\n\n----- ANSWER O07d -----\n# T07 \u2014 Consolidated verdict: NIFTY weekly iron condor on F&O bhavcopy\n\nArbiter reconciliation of the two reviews. I adopt only findings that survive\ncross-examination, drop the one that is refuted, and carry the labelled\nnon-material notes. Both reviews independently re-derived the payoff/credit\nalgebra and agree the visible code has no arithmetic or lookahead bug; my role\nis to decide which of the flagged concerns are real and material.\n\n## Confirmed clean (agreed by both reviews, re-checked)\n\n- **No entry lookahead.** Decision on Tuesday-close data (`ref = idx_close.asof(tuesday)`,\n  strike/expiry liquidity checked on Tuesday); execution Wednesday at each leg's\n  OPEN. Decide-T / execute-T+1 is correct.\n- **Landmine #9 avoided.** Does not read expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR`; cash-settles\n  at intrinsic from the index (`settle = idx_close.asof(expiry)`). This is the\n  prescribed fix, not the trap.\n- **CONTRACTS>0 liquidity gate** present at both expiry selection and entry, with a\n  conservative no-fill skip (`OPEN <= 0 or CONTRACTS == 0 \u2192 skip`). Untraded-but-priced\n  strikes excluded.\n- **Payoff/credit math correct and correctly signed/capped.** credit = (sc+sp) \u2212 (lc+lp);\n  expiry liability = short-call \u2212 long-call + short-put \u2212 long-put intrinsic \u2208 [0, wing_width];\n  only one side can breach; max loss = wing_width \u2212 credit; `pnl = credit \u2212 payoff`.\n  Worst week \u2212312 \u2248 one wing width (~1.5%\u00b7ref at ref\u224820.8k\u201323k) is internally consistent\n  with \"wings capped it.\" No sign error.\n- **Entry-only cost timing is correct** for a held-to-expiry, cash-settled European\n  option \u2014 there is no exit trade to charge.\n\nThe **code as written contains no result-corrupting bug.** The validity of the\n*reported numbers* turns on inputs not shown in the snippet, and at a +6.1 pt/week\nedge those inputs are decisive.\n\n## Material defects (surviving)\n\n**1. Fill realism / friction is unshown and load-bearing \u2014 result cannot be certified from the text.**\n- Claim/lines: `credit = rows[\"sc\"].OPEN + rows[\"sp\"].OPEN \u2212 rows[\"lc\"].OPEN \u2212 rows[\"lp\"].OPEN`\n  then `credit -= slippage_ticks(4) + costs_pts()`. Neither helper is given in points.\n- Mechanism: all four legs are filled at a *single* bhavcopy OPEN print. Bhavcopy OPEN\n  is the first *traded* print, not provably the mid, so I do **not** assert the stronger\n  \"systematic same-direction mid inflation\" that Reviewer 1 used \u2014 that mechanism is\n  asserted, not established. The valid, weaker, and still-decisive claim: a single print\n  ignores the bid/ask you must actually cross on four legs. On ~1-DTE \u00b11.5%/\u00b13% NIFTE\n  options the spread is a large fraction of a low-single-digit premium; a realistic\n  ~1 pt/leg half-spread across 4 one-way legs (~4 pts, correctly no exit leg) can halve\n  or erase the 6.1-pt/week edge. If `slippage_ticks(4)` is literally ~4 ticks (~0.2 pts),\n  it is an order of magnitude too small.\n- Fix / must-verify: publish `slippage_ticks` and `costs_pts` in points; model a per-leg\n  half-spread against OPEN (or fill shorts at bid, wings at ask), and confirm premium-side\n  sell STT + exchange/stamp/brokerage are inside `costs_pts()`. Certification blocked until\n  shown adequate. Status: PLAUSIBLE material, unverifiable from the snippet.\n\n**2. Event-skip integrity + a skip-count inconsistency (both reviews; Red Team's sharpening adopted).**\n- Claim/fields: \"224 weeks traded, 31 skipped\"; \"weeks with a scheduled major event\n  (budget, RBI, election result) are skipped.\"\n- Mechanism: 224 + 31 \u2248 255 anchor weeks (\u2248 correct for 5 years). But RBI MPC alone is\n  ~6 meetings/year \u2192 ~30 weeks over 2021\u20132025, which nearly exhausts the entire 31-skip\n  budget *before* adding budget days, the 2024 election-result week, \"no liquid expiry\"\n  skips, and \"leg not traded on entry day\" skips. So one of the following is true and each\n  is a problem: (a) the event filter is in fact *narrower* than the stated \"RBI\"\n  (undermining the claimed conservatism), (b) most RBI weeks were **not** excluded despite\n  the stated rule (the method does not match the description), or (c) the 31 is not the\n  mechanical event calendar at all. Separately, because scheduled-event weeks are precisely\n  the tail-defining weeks for a short-vol condor, excluding them lifts hit rate, avg and\n  worst-week \u2014 so the headline stats are conditional on the filter. The implausibly low\n  count also heightens the specific lookahead worry: if any week was dropped because it\n  *turned out* volatile (ex-post) rather than from an ex-ante calendar, that is lookahead\n  and voids the filter.\n- Fix: publish the dated skip list with a per-week reason code; prove the event calendar\n  is built strictly from ex-ante-known dates; report the no-skip comparison (avg / hit /\n  worst-week with events included) so the reader sees the impact; reconcile the count.\n  Status: PLAUSIBLE material.\n\n## Refuted finding (dropped \u2014 not a defect)\n\n**Reviewer 1's #2, \"STT-on-exercise on ITM legs understates the losing weeks,\" is refuted.**\nExercise/settlement STT (~0.125% on intrinsic) is borne by the option *exerciser/holder*,\nnot the writer. In this condor we are **short** the body (sc, sp) and **long** only the\nwings (lc, lp). Exercise STT can therefore hit us **only on the long wings when they finish\nITM**, i.e. only on a move beyond \u00b13% in the ~1-day hold \u2014 the rare tail. The typical losing\nweek breaches the **short** strike (a 1.5%\u20133% move), where the long wing is still OTM, so no\nexercise STT accrues to us at all; our short-leg cost is the premium-side sell STT paid at\nentry (belongs in `costs_pts()`). NIFTY index options are cash-settled, so there is no\nphysical-delivery STT trap either. The genuinely omitted item is a tiny, tail-only long-wing\ncharge on the rare \u22653% weeks \u2014 immaterial, and it does **not** bias the average or the general\nlosing-week cost as originally claimed.\n\n## Non-material comments (clearly labelled \u2014 not defects)\n\n- **Settlement proxy:** `idx_close.asof(expiry)` uses the index *close*, whereas NIFTY\n  options settle on the last-30-min average. Small, roughly unbiased over 224 weeks. Non-material.\n- **Monthly fallback mixes tenors:** a monthly held to expiry is ~30-DTE risk booked to one\n  `week=tuesday`, and the \u00b11.5%/\u00b13% strikes calibrated for ~1-DTE are far more breach-prone at\n  ~30-DTE. Likely rare on liquid 2021\u20132025 NIFTY weeklies, so non-material \u2014 but the submission\n  reports **no count** of fallback weeks, so its \"rare\" status is asserted, not shown; disclose\n  the count and flag any week that used the fallback.\n- **`asof()` day-offset (landmine #8 class):** if `idx_close` is timestamp-indexed rather than\n  date-indexed, `asof(expiry@00:00)` could return the prior day's close. Verification item, not\n  a confirmed bug.\n- **No capital/margin denominator:** a pts/week figure is not a return. Fine for a points metric,\n  but not a performance statistic on its own.\n- **Tail-only long-wing exercise STT** (residue of the refuted finding): immaterial.\n\n## Material defects \u2014 numbered\n\n1. **Fill realism / friction is unshown and load-bearing.** All four legs filled at a single\n   bhavcopy OPEN print with an unshown `slippage_ticks(4) + costs_pts()`; at a 6.1-pt/week edge,\n   a realistic ~1 pt/leg half-spread across 4 one-way legs (~4 pts) can halve or erase it. Fix:\n   show both helpers in points; model a per-leg half-spread on OPEN (or fill shorts at bid, wings\n   at ask); confirm premium-side STT/charges are included. PLAUSIBLE, unverifiable from the snippet.\n2. **Event-skip integrity + skip-count inconsistency.** 31 skipped weeks are too few to contain\n   ~30 RBI weeks alone plus budget/election/liquidity skips, so the filter is either narrower than\n   described, not fully applied, or not the event calendar at all \u2014 and the low count heightens the\n   risk that some exclusions are ex-post (lookahead). Fix: publish the dated skip list with per-week\n   reasons, prove the calendar is strictly ex-ante, report the no-skip comparison, and reconcile the\n   count. PLAUSIBLE material.\n\nNote: the **code as written has no confirmed arithmetic or lookahead bug** \u2014 both material items\nlive outside the snippet. If the two cost helpers are in fact realistic and the skip list is a\ngenuine ex-ante calendar that reconciles with the count, this reduces to **no material methodology\ndefect**. Reviewer 1's STT-on-exercise finding is dropped as refuted.", "ids": ["O07a", "O07b", "O07c", "O07d"]}, {"tid": "T08", "key": "## T08 \u2014 data-landmine \u2014 pre-open auction bar as open (HIGH)\n**Defect.** `day_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]` takes the first print the vendor ships,\nwhich is the 09:00 pre-open AUCTION print, not the 09:15 continuous-session open.\nThe gap is misclassified on every day the auction print deviates, AND the fade is\n\"filled\" at the auction price, which does not exist in the continuous session \u2014\nauction-vs-open deviation becomes instant fake P&L (firm measurement: ~94% of naive\n2026 gap calculations corrupted by this bar).\n**Line.** `day_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]` with no time filter.\n**Fix.** Filter to `t >= time(9,15)`; real open = first continuous-session bar;\nentry price must be a >=09:15 print.\n**Accept.** \"First bar of the day is the 09:00 pre-open auction, not the real\nopen\"; \"gap and entry price use the auction print \u2014 untradeable\"; \"missing >=09:15\nsession filter\".\n**Secondary.** prev_close from the same file's last <=15:30 bar is fine; 1bp/side\nfutures cost is acceptable.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O08a -----\n# T08 \u2014 Review: NIFTY opening gap fade (1-minute data)\n\n**Verdict: MATERIAL DEFECT \u2014 the reported +0.09%/trade is inflated by the pre-open\nauction bug. The strategy enters (and measures the gap) at the 09:00 pre-open\nauction print, not the tradeable 09:15 continuous open.**\n\n---\n\n## Defect 1 (MATERIAL) \u2014 \"first print of the session\" is the pre-open auction price, not the real open\n\nLine: `day_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]  # first print of the session`\n\nThe submission's own schema note says the file \"includes every print the vendor\nships for the session.\" On NSE that means the first bar of the day is the\n**09:00\u201309:08 pre-open auction** bar (equilibrium/uncrossing print), not the\n09:15 start of continuous trading. Taking `g.iloc[0][\"open\"]` therefore grabs the\nauction price. This is the exact documented landmine (project DATA LANDMINE #2 /\nthe \"pre-open auction gap\" memo: naive `.first()`/`.iloc[0]` on the 1-min \"open\"\ngrabs the 09:00 auction price; ~94% of recent sessions carry a pre-open print).\n\n**Why it corrupts the result \u2014 it is used in TWO places at once:**\n\n1. **Gap measurement.** `gap = day_open/prev_close - 1` is computed from the\n   auction price. The auction routinely overshoots (thin, order-imbalance-driven\n   uncrossing), so `|gap|` is measured at its most extreme point of the day. The\n   `abs(gap) < 0.004` filter then preferentially selects sessions where the\n   auction was most dislocated \u2014 precisely the sessions that mean-revert the most\n   before 09:15.\n\n2. **Entry price.** `entry_px = day_open` = the auction price. You **cannot\n   transact** at the auction equilibrium in the continuous futures book; the\n   first fillable price is the 09:15 open. A large part of the \"fade\" gain is the\n   09:00\u219209:15 move that happens *before you can be in the position*. The\n   backtest books that phantom reversion as P&L.\n\n**Direction and size of the bias:** always inflating. Worked example \u2014 prev close\n100, gap-up news: auction (09:00) uncrosses at 101.0, continuous open (09:15) is\n100.7, price at 10:15 is 100.4.\n- Buggy (entry 101.0, short): `-1*(100.4/101.0-1) = +0.594%`.\n- Correct (entry 100.7, short): `-1*(100.4/100.7-1) = +0.298%`.\nThe bug roughly doubles the per-trade edge here and lifts the win rate, because\nthe untradeable 101.0\u2192100.7 leg is pure fiction. Combined with the selection\neffect in (1), a real tradeable edge of ~0 can be dressed up as +0.09%/trade at\n58% wins.\n\n**Fix:** restrict to the continuous session before choosing the open, for BOTH\nthe gap and the entry:\n```python\ng = g[g[\"t\"] >= time(9, 15)].sort_values(\"ts\")\nif g.empty: continue\nday_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]      # 09:15 continuous open (tradeable)\n```\nFor a still-honest fill, enter on the *next* bar's open (e.g. 09:16) rather than\nthe same 09:15 bar whose open triggered the signal, and re-run 2022\u20132026. Expect\nthe edge to shrink materially, possibly to zero after costs.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 2 (CORRECTNESS caveat \u2014 material only if the file is not chronologically sorted)\n\nLine: `prev_close = m[m[\"t\"] <= time(15,30)].groupby(\"d\")[\"close\"].last().shift(1)`\n\n`.last()` returns the last row **in DataFrame order** within each day, not the\nchronologically last bar. The code sorts `g` inside the trade loop, but the\n`prev_close` computation runs on `m` with no sort. If the parquet is not already\nglobally sorted by `ts`, `prev_close` is silently wrong (some arbitrary bar's\nclose), which corrupts every gap. Most vendor 1-min parquets ship sorted, so this\nis likely latent rather than active here \u2014 but it is an unguarded assumption.\n\n**Fix:** `m = m.sort_values(\"ts\")` once up front (also lets you drop the in-loop\nsort), or use `.groupby(\"d\").apply(lambda x: x.sort_values(\"ts\")[\"close\"].iloc[-1])`.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (label: not defects in the reported number)\n\n- **Costs are on the optimistic side.** `-0.0002` = 2 bps round-trip = 1 bp/side,\n  which matches the stated assumption, but it models zero slippage/impact.\n  A gap fade *takes* liquidity into a directional move at 09:15; per\n  COST_STANDARDS dynamic-slippage, 1 bp/side understates real cost. Re-run once\n  Defect 1 is fixed to see if the (smaller) edge survives a realistic cost.\n- **Robustness crash on short sessions.** `g[g[\"t\"] >= time(10,15)].iloc[0]` will\n  raise `IndexError` on any half-day / Muhurat / truncated session with no bar\n  \u2265 10:15. Doesn't corrupt the 388 trades that ran, but guard it.\n- **Instrument ambiguity.** Signal/return are computed on `nifty_1min` but costs\n  are labelled \"futures.\" If the file is the spot index, execution on futures\n  carries basis and its own (separate) pre-open \u2014 the index open \u2260 the futures\n  fillable open. Confirm the file is the futures series; if it's spot, that is a\n  second execution-realism gap.\n- **`.dt.date` is fine here** *given* the stated tz-aware IST timestamps (it would\n  be the HF UTC-stamp landmine only if ts were stored in UTC \u2014 it is not, per the\n  schema note).\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n\n1. **Pre-open auction bug:** `day_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]` uses the 09:00 pre-open\n   auction print for both the gap measurement and the entry price instead of the\n   09:15 continuous open. It (a) selects the most dislocated auctions via the\n   `|gap|>0.4%` filter and (b) books the untradeable 09:00\u219209:15 reversion as\n   profit \u2014 systematically inflating mean return (~2x in the worked example) and\n   win rate. Fix: filter `t >= 09:15` before taking the open (enter on the next\n   bar for honesty) and re-run.\n2. **`prev_close` `.last()` depends on unsorted `m` being chronologically\n   ordered** (only `g` is sorted, inside the loop). Wrong prev close if the file\n   isn't pre-sorted \u2192 corrupts every gap. Material only if the file is unsorted;\n   fix by sorting `m` by `ts` up front.\n\n----- ANSWER O08b -----\n# T08 \u2014 Final arbiter verdict: NIFTY opening gap fade (1-minute data)\n\nConsolidating the primary review and the red-team review. I keep only defects that\nsurvive both passes, correct the two places where the primary review over-claimed,\nand drop the one sub-claim the red team refutes. Verdict: **the reported result\n(+0.09%/trade, 388 trades, 58% win) is not trustworthy and must be recomputed** \u2014\nbut the reason is a mis-defined/non-tradeable price and an instrument mismatch, NOT\na proven positive bias.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 1 (PRIMARY, load-bearing) \u2014 pre-open auction price used as both the gap reference and the entry fill\n\n**Lines:**\n```python\nday_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]      # first print of the session\ngap = day_open / prev_close[d] - 1.0\n...\nentry_px = day_open\n```\n\n**Mechanism.** The scenario states the file \"includes every print the vendor ships\nfor the session\" and the comment self-labels the row \"first print of the session.\"\nOn NSE the session opens with the 09:00\u201309:15 pre-open call auction, so after\n`sort_values(\"ts\")`, `g.iloc[0]` is the ~09:00 pre-open indicative bar, not the\n09:15 regular-session open (firm DATA LANDMINE #2 / pre-open-auction-gap memo,\n~94% of days affected). This corrupts the result in two independent ways:\n\n1. **Non-tradeable entry fill.** No order can be filled at the 09:00 indicative\n   level; the first executable price is the 09:15 open. Every trade books a fill\n   nobody could have obtained.\n2. **Mis-defined event set.** `gap` is measured against the phantom open, so the\n   `abs(gap) > 0.004` filter selects a different set of days than the tradeable\n   09:15 open would. Trade count (388), win rate (58%) and mean (+0.09%) are all\n   computed on the wrong sample.\n\nEither sub-point alone invalidates the headline number.\n\n**Correction to the primary review (adopt red-team tempering).** The primary review\nasserted the pre-open \"typically overshoots and reverts,\" making the bias\n\"systematically positive\" and the edge \"very likely largely or entirely phantom.\"\nThat is an unproven empirical claim. The firm memo establishes that the naive first\nprint CORRUPTS gap calcs; it does NOT establish a signed P&L bias. On days where the\ngap WIDENS from 09:00\u219209:15 the sign flips against the fade. **Certified statement:\nthe result is unreliable and its sign cannot be trusted \u2014 not that the edge is\nproven phantom or positive-biased.**\n\n**Fix.** Drop the pre-open before choosing the open and the entry:\n```python\ng = g[g[\"t\"] >= time(9, 15)].sort_values(\"ts\")\nday_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]     # true 09:15 regular-session open\n```\nCompute gap and entry from that 09:15 open, then re-run.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 2 \u2014 instrument mismatch: P&L simulated on the cash INDEX while costs/trade are FUTURES\n\n**Fields/claim:** P&L is computed on `nifty_1min.parquet` (index open/high/low/close);\nthe trade and cost line (`- 0.0002`, \"1bp/side futures costs\") describe a NIFTY\nfuture.\n\n**Mechanism.** The cash-index open and the futures open diverge, and that divergence\nis LARGEST precisely on gap-open days \u2014 the exact sample this strategy selects \u2014\nbecause the futures open prices GIFT-Nifty/overnight sentiment while the cash open\nis the constituent auction cross. So the entry price is wrong on the traded\ninstrument, and wrong by the most at the worst moment. Corroborating evidence that\nthe series is the cash index: a 09:00 pre-open bar only exists on cash \u2014 NSE\nderivatives have no 09:00 call auction and start at 09:15 (which is also why fixing\nthis defect partially dissolves Defect 1). Sign is ambiguous; magnitude on gap days\ncan rival the ~9\u201311 bp gross edge.\n\n**Fix.** Re-run on the actual NIFTY futures series (with its own 09:15 open), or add\nan explicit basis/spread term reconciling cash entry/exit to futures fills. Per\nCOST_STANDARDS this reconciliation must happen before the number stands.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 3 (erosion only) \u2014 cost model understated\n\n**Line:** `... - 0.0002` (2 bp round-trip) claimed as \"1bp/side futures costs.\"\n\n**Mechanism.** Arithmetic is right (1 bp \u00d7 2 = 2 bp) but 2 bp round-trip barely\ncovers STT on the sell leg alone (~1.25 bp pre-Oct-2024, ~2 bp after), before\nexchange txn (~0.2 bp/side) + GST + stamp (buy) + SEBI. Realistic round-trip \u2248\n**2.5\u20134 bp** (the primary review's \"3\u20135 bp\" is a touch high). On a thin ~9\u201311 bp\ngross edge this ~1\u20132 bp shortfall erodes ~15\u201325% of the edge \u2014 material as erosion,\nbut it neither creates nor removes the headline problem.\n\n**REFUTED sub-claim (does NOT survive).** The primary review invoked LANDMINE 7b\n(2\u20133\u00d7 slippage on thin-volume bars) for gap-day opens. That landmine governs THIN\nsingle-stock momentum names. The instrument here is NIFTY futures \u2014 the deepest,\ntightest-spread instrument in India (\u22481 tick even on gap opens). Modeling 2\u20133\u00d7\nimpact here is wrong; slippage is a minor term for this instrument. **The execution\nproblem is the auction/instrument price, not spread/impact.** This sub-claim is\ndropped.\n\n**Fix.** Cost from `06_TRADING_DESK/COST_STANDARDS.md` (STT sell-side + exchange +\nGST + stamp + SEBI). No large slippage term is warranted for NIFTY futures.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 4 (latent / impact contingent) \u2014 `prev_close` via `.last()` on unsorted data\n\n**Lines:**\n```python\nprev_close = (m[m[\"t\"] <= time(15, 30)]\n              .groupby(\"d\")[\"close\"].last().shift(1))\n```\n\n**Mechanism.** `.last()` returns the last row in the FILE's order within each date\ngroup, not the chronologically last bar \u226415:30. The author sorts inside the loop\n(`g.sort_values(\"ts\")`), proving ordering is known to matter, yet `prev_close` is\nbuilt from unsorted `m`. If the parquet is not chronologically ordered, every\nprevious close \u2014 hence every gap \u2014 is wrong.\n\n**Tempering (adopt red team).** Vendor 1-min parquet is normally shipped\nchronologically, so this is a **latent correctness bug of unverified impact** on\nthis specific run, not a demonstrated corruption of 388/58%/+0.09%. Downgrade from\nthe primary review's flat \"material\" to material-but-contingent: fix defensively.\n(Note: `.shift(1)` itself is correct \u2014 `groupby` returns a date-sorted index, so it\nyields the prior TRADING day; no bug there.)\n\n**Fix.**\n```python\nmm = m[(m[\"t\"] >= time(9,15)) & (m[\"t\"] <= time(15,30))].sort_values(\"ts\")\nprev_close = mm.groupby(\"d\")[\"close\"].last().shift(1)\n```\n\n---\n\n## OVERALL FRAGILITY (context, not a separate defect)\nNet +0.09%/trade is a ~4\u20135 bp/side economic edge that survives only if all execution\nassumptions hold simultaneously. Fixing Defect 1 (fewer, less-extreme qualifying\ndays), Defect 2 (real futures open), and Defect 3 (1\u20132 bp more cost) can EACH\nindividually flip the sign. This is a marginal number living inside the error bars\nof its own execution model, not a robust edge with a data nit.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL NOTES (clearly labelled)\n- **Crash risk, not corruption:** `g[g[\"t\"] >= time(10,15)].iloc[0]` raises\n  `IndexError` on any truncated/half-day with no bar \u226510:15. Guard with a length\n  check and `continue`.\n- **Muhurat/evening-session bars:** if the file carries a Diwali evening session,\n  the exit selector could grab an ~18:15 bar (or an evening-only open). ~1 day/yr,\n  non-material to 388 trades, but a real correctness hole worth guarding.\n- **Exit timing OK:** using the close of the first \u226510:15 bar (~10:16 fill) is\n  acceptable and not lookahead; document it.\n- **NOT defects (do not re-raise):** `- 0.0002` is applied AFTER the directional\n  multiply (cost is correctly always a drag); `.shift(1)` gives the prior trading\n  day; the `pd.isna` guard correctly handles the first day; there is no same-day\n  forward-information lookahead \u2014 the flaw is a WRONG price, not a FUTURE price.\n\n---\n\n## CLAIMED MATERIAL DEFECTS\n1. **Pre-open auction contamination (primary).** `day_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]` is\n   the ~09:00 non-tradeable pre-open indicative, used as both the gap denominator\n   input and `entry_px`; the fill is unachievable and the 0.4% filter selects a\n   mis-defined event set, so 388 / 58% / +0.09% cannot be trusted. Fix: filter\n   `t >= 09:15` before taking the open. (Bias SIGN is unproven \u2014 the \"systematically\n   positive / entirely phantom\" claim is rejected as overstated.)\n2. **Instrument mismatch.** P&L simulated on the cash INDEX series while costs and\n   the trade are FUTURES; cash-vs-futures open divergence is largest on the selected\n   gap days. Fix: re-run on the actual futures open/series (sign ambiguous).\n3. **Cost understatement.** 2 bp round-trip barely covers STT; realistic ~2.5\u20134 bp\n   erodes ~15\u201325% of a thin gross edge (material as erosion only). The attached\n   2\u20133\u00d7 gap-day slippage claim is REFUTED \u2014 NIFTY futures are the deepest Indian\n   instrument; LANDMINE 7b is misapplied.\n4. **`prev_close` via `.last()` on file-order-dependent data.** Latent correctness\n   bug (fix by sorting before groupby-last); impact on the reported number is\n   CONTINGENT on the file being unsorted and is not demonstrated for this run.\n\n----- ANSWER O08c -----\n# CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT \u2014 T08: NIFTY opening-gap fade (1-min)\n\nAuthor: Rajan Mehta (CIO, E-001). Inputs: submission + Quant Review + Red Team (Nikhil Bose).\nEvidence = pasted text only (no file access, no code execution \u2014 per hard constraint). Claims tagged [DATA]/[INFERENCE]/[OPINION].\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT\n\nReported result (2022\u20132026, 388 trades, +0.09%/trade net of 1bp/side, win 58%) is **not a tradeable edge and cannot be certified.** The +9bp is booked at a non-executable entry price that also generates the signal, biased in the fade's favour, against a net edge so thin that realistic Indian futures costs alone would gut it. Both prior reviews reached FAKE; I concur on the substance and correct two reasoning errors below.\n\n**Three-line rationale:**\n1. The dominant driver is the entry price (`g.iloc[0][\"open\"]`), a first-print that is almost certainly the pre-open auction indicative (firm landmine #2) \u2014 used simultaneously as gap-signal and fill; the phantom 09:00\u219209:15 reversion is captured for free and plausibly *is* the entire edge. [INFERENCE, high confidence]\n2. The instrument lineage (cash index vs front future) is undisclosed while costs are quoted as \"futures\"; this is load-bearing \u2014 it decides *which* corruption is live and *which* fix is correct, and if cash-index the series is untradeable at any timestamp. [INFERENCE]\n3. Costs (flat 2bp round-trip) are understated vs real STT+charges+at-open spread (~3\u20134bp+), and the two P&L-setting knobs (0.4% gate, 10:15 exit) carry an undisclosed trials count with no validation battery \u2014 non-certifiable even after the above are fixed. [DATA/INFERENCE]\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS (surviving adjudication)\n\n### D1 \u2014 DOMINANT, edge-fabricating. Line: `day_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]` (used at `gap = day_open/prev_close - 1` AND `entry_px = day_open`).\n**Mechanism.** After `sort_values(\"ts\")`, `g.iloc[0]` is the first bar the vendor ships for the date. Per firm landmine #2 (naive first-of-day on NIFTY 1-min grabs the ~09:00 pre-open auction indicative; ~94% of 2026 days affected) and the task's own phrasing (\"includes every print the vendor ships for the session\"), that first bar is the pre-open equilibrium print \u2014 a level you cannot transact against. It corrupts the result twice, both in the fade's favour: (a) the **gap** is measured from the auction print, and (b) the **entry** is assumed filled at that same auction print, then held to 10:15. On a gap morning the 09:00\u219209:15 move is precisely an overshoot-and-revert; a fade \"filled\" at the 09:00 indicative books that non-executable reversion. Worked example (Red Team, upheld): prev_close=100, first-print=101, real reverted level=100.7, exit=100.5 \u2192 booked return with entry=101 is +0.495% vs +0.199% with the real 100.7 \u2014 ~0.30% phantom per trade, exactly the magnitude that turns noise into a +9bp average over 388 trades.\n**Fix.** Restrict to the continuous session before selecting the open: `g = g[g[\"t\"] >= time(9,15)]`; measure the gap AND fill from the first bar \u226509:15, and to be conservative fill at the *next* observable bar (09:16 open), not the exact print that generated the signal. Re-run; I expect the edge to collapse toward/through cost.\n**Caveat (see D2):** the *exact* mechanism (pre-open indicative vs stale-index composite) depends on lineage. Either way the entry price as coded is fictional.\n\n### D2 \u2014 Instrument / price-series lineage unresolved (load-bearing, not a footnote). Field: `pd.read_parquet(\"nifty_1min.parquet\")` vs \"1bp/side **futures** costs.\"\n**Mechanism.** The series is priced off `nifty_1min.parquet` (instrument unstated) but costs are charged as futures. This is not cosmetic \u2014 it changes the corruption: (i) if **front future** with pre-open indicative bars present \u2192 classic landmine #2 (D1 story), and the overnight index gap differs from the future's gap by basis/fair-value change; (ii) if **cash index** \u2192 untradeable at *any* timestamp, and the index \"open\" is a stale, non-synchronous composite (constituents open staggered), so the measured entry\u219210:15 move is partly the index catching up to prices that already moved \u2014 a stale-price bias, still fade-biased. Must be resolved *first*; it dictates the correct fix in D1.\n**Fix.** State the traded instrument and backtest on that contract's own 1-min series; if index-priced, either switch to the future's series or model the basis explicitly.\n\n### D3 \u2014 Costs understated (decisive at this thinness). Line: `... - 0.0002`.\n**Mechanism.** Flat 2bp round-trip against a +9bp net edge (~11bp gross). Real Indian NIFTY-futures round-trip: STT sell-side ~2bp (0.02% on notional, post-Oct-2024) + NSE txn charges + SEBI fee + GST + stamp duty + bid-ask spread (which *does* widen at the open even for the index future) \u2248 3\u20134bp+. Not COST_STANDARDS-approved (DRAFT until sign-off), and no 2\u00d7 stress. This alone erases a third-to-half of the edge.\n**Correction to Quant Review:** its invocation of landmine 7b (thin-volume single-stock slippage) is **mis-applied** \u2014 the NIFTY future is the most liquid instrument on the exchange and stays liquid on gap mornings. Right verdict (costs kill it), wrong reason (it is STT + charges + at-open spread, not thin-volume slippage).\n**Fix.** Apply approved COST_STANDARDS Indian-futures costs with 2\u00d7 stress; re-check survival.\n\n### D4 \u2014 Overfitting risk + non-certifiability. Fields: `abs(gap) < 0.004` (gate) and `g[\"t\"] >= time(10,15)` (exit); reporting.\n**Mechanism.** Two free parameters that *directly* set the P&L, with no disclosed trials count. At a 9bp edge, searching these two knobs is exactly where in-sample significance is manufactured. Reporting is bare (mean, win rate) \u2014 no Sharpe, no deflated Sharpe/DSR with honest trials, no PBO, no walk-forward, no regime slices (2022 bear / 2024 election / 2026). Per firm protocol, backtests must report deflated Sharpe + PBO; none present.\n**Fix.** Disclose/pre-register the parameter search; run DSR (honest trials count), PBO, walk-forward, per-regime slices, and a bootstrap 5th-pctile. Cannot be certified even after D1\u2013D3 are fixed.\n\n---\n\n## ADJUDICATION OF THE TWO REVIEWS (dissents recorded)\n\n- **Quant Review #1 (pre-open entry): UPHELD** as the dominant, edge-fabricating defect. Correctly cleared the tz landmine (#1 is daily-stamp only; this data is tz-aware IST 1-min), the `shift(1)`\u2192previous-*trading*-day logic, and the absence of return-spreading.\n- **Quant Review #2 (signal/fill simultaneity as a separate T-class fake-driver): DOWNGRADED to non-material.** Red Team is correct \u2014 for an opening-gap strategy the real 09:15 open is the first observable-and-executable price; using it to both trigger and fill is standard convention, not an independent structural lookahead that fabricates edge. It is duplicative of D1 and collapses into \"fill at the next bar\" once D1 is fixed. Keep only as an execution-timing note.\n- **Quant Review #3 (costs): UPHELD in conclusion, reasoning corrected** (STT+charges, not landmine 7b \u2014 see D3).\n- **Quant Review #4 (lineage): UPHELD and ELEVATED** to material D2 \u2014 it is not a footnote; it determines the live mechanism.\n- **Quant Review #1 certainty (\"the entire +9bp,\" \"expect to evaporate\"): flagged as over-claimed.** Correct suspect, but neither reviewer could run code; honest confidence is \"dominant and very likely fatal,\" not \"proven whole edge.\" Red Team's honesty here is upheld.\n- **Red Team: broadly correct**, added the load-bearing lineage-mechanism distinction, the STT correction, and the parameter-search overfitting risk. No invented defects on either side.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL / CORRECT-AS-CODED (labelled, [OPINION]/[DATA])\n\n1. tz handling \u2014 `dt.date`/`dt.time` on tz-aware IST is correct; not the 18:30-UTC daily-stamp bug. [DATA]\n2. `prev_close = ...groupby(\"d\")[\"close\"].last().shift(1)` correctly returns the previous *trading* day's close (holidays/weekends handled by the sorted date index). [DATA]\n3. Per-trade booking is correct \u2014 no return-spreading, so no fake-low-variance Sharpe artifact. Good. [DATA]\n4. `abs(gap) < 0.004` trades abs \u2265 0.4% \u2014 harmless off-by-equality vs the \">0.4%\" comment. [OPINION]\n5. `g[g[\"t\"] >= time(10,15)].iloc[0]` raises IndexError on any early-close/short-data day with no bar \u226510:15 \u2014 robustness only; the run completed so none bit. Flag anyway. [OPINION]\n\n---\n\n## DISPOSITION & KILL/REVIVE CRITERIA\n\n**Status:** REJECT for certification and for the pipeline; return to bench. Not sizeable. Do NOT anchor any future sizing to the reported +9bp (IC-1 precedent: paper approvals firewalled; here there is no approval to firewall \u2014 the number is an artifact).\n\n**What flips it to FRAGILE-worth-retesting** (all four required, in order):\n1. Resolve lineage (D2) \u2014 state and backtest the actually-traded instrument's own 1-min series.\n2. Measure the gap AND fill from the first continuous-session bar \u226509:15 (ideally the 09:16 open) (D1).\n3. Apply approved COST_STANDARDS Indian-futures costs with 2\u00d7 stress (D3).\n4. Show survival under a one-day-lag placebo AND a within-sample threshold/exit sensitivity sweep with honest trials disclosed (D4).\n\n**Only if a positive per-trade edge remains after (1)\u2013(4)** \u2192 escalate to the full battery (deflated Sharpe, DSR, PBO, regime slices, bootstrap 5th-pctile) before any paper allocation.\n\n**Review date:** upon resubmission with items (1)\u2013(4). No standing clock \u2014 this is a bench reject, not a forward-test entry.\n\n**Tail note:** even were the edge real, a gap-fade is a short-mean-reversion posture that is structurally short the tail \u2014 it wins small on most mornings and loses large on the gap-and-go / trend-open day (news/event gaps that keep running past 10:15). Any revived version must be sized to that worst-morning loss, not the average trade, and event-gated (results/RBI/Fed/budget mornings excluded).\n\n---\n\n## CLAIMED MATERIAL DEFECTS (final)\n\n1. **`day_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]`** \u2014 non-executable first print (pre-open auction indicative, landmine #2) used as BOTH the gap-signal and the fill; the phantom 09:00\u219209:15 reversion is booked in the fade's favour and plausibly accounts for the entire +9bp. Fix: measure gap and fill from the first bar with `t >= 09:15` (ideally fill at the next bar), on the actually-traded instrument.\n2. **Instrument/price-series lineage unresolved** \u2014 `nifty_1min.parquet` (cash index vs front future) while costs are quoted as \"futures.\" If cash index, the series is untradeable and its open is a stale non-synchronous composite \u2014 a distinct corruption from the pre-open story; must be resolved before any re-run and determines the correct D1 fix.\n3. **Costs understated** \u2014 flat 2bp round-trip ignores STT (sell-side ~2bp alone) + exchange/SEBI/GST/stamp + at-open spread (~3\u20134bp+ realistic), not COST_STANDARDS-approved, no 2\u00d7 stress; decisive against a 9bp net edge. (Quant Review's landmine-7b thin-volume rationale is mis-applied and is rejected; the driver is STT+charges+at-open spread.)\n4. **Overfitting / non-certifiability** \u2014 two P&L-setting free parameters (0.4% gate, 10:15 exit) with undisclosed trials count, and no Sharpe/DSR/PBO/walk-forward/regime slices; the result cannot be certified even after defects 1\u20133 are fixed.\n\n(Non-material, labelled: signal/fill simultaneity downgraded \u2014 duplicative of #1; IndexError risk on short/early-close days; `<0.004` off-by-equality vs comment; tz handling and `shift(1)` previous-trading-day logic are correct.)\n\n----- ANSWER O08d -----\n# T08 review \u2014 NIFTY opening gap fade (1-minute data)\n\n**Verdict: MATERIAL DEFECT. The reported +0.09%/trade is fake \u2014 it is entered and measured off the 09:00 pre-open call-auction price, which is not transactable.**\n\n## Defect 1 (fatal) \u2014 pre-open auction price used as `day_open`\n\n**Line:** `day_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]  # first print of the session`\n\n**Mechanism.** The scenario states the file \"includes every print the vendor ships for the session,\" so `g.iloc[0]` is the **09:00 pre-open call-auction bar**, not the 09:15 continuous-session open. This is the documented pre-open auction landmine (DATA LANDMINE #2 / memory `preopen-auction-gap-bug`): the 1-min \"open\" at 09:00 is an *indicative auction equilibrium*, not a tradeable price.\n\nIt corrupts the result in two places at once:\n\n1. **Gap measurement.** `gap = day_open / prev_close - 1.0`, the `abs(gap) < 0.004` filter, and `direction` are all computed off the indicative auction print rather than the real open. The selected trade set and the fade direction are chosen from a price you cannot transact at.\n2. **Entry price (the fatal part).** `entry_px = day_open` books the trade at the 09:00 auction print. The first price you can actually transact is the **09:15 opening print**. In the Indian pre-open, the indicative price systematically overshoots and reverts into the 09:15 open; the strategy therefore harvests the 09:00\u219209:15 mean-reversion **for free**, and this is exactly the window that cannot be traded. The reported 9 bps/trade is dominated by this phantom fade.\n\nNet effect: this is not a valid test of the 09:15\u219210:15 fade at all \u2014 the sign and magnitude are both untrustworthy. A 9 bps edge is well inside the size of the artifact.\n\n**Fix.** Restrict to the continuous session before taking the open, so both the gap and the entry use the first tradeable bar:\n```python\ng = g[g[\"t\"] >= time(9, 15)].sort_values(\"ts\")\nday_open = g.iloc[0][\"open\"]     # 09:15 real open\n```\nThen re-run; expect the edge to shrink materially or vanish.\n\n## Defect 2 (material, secondary) \u2014 spot-index prices, futures costs\n\nThe file is `nifty_1min.parquet` (the **spot index**), but the write-up charges \"1bp/side futures costs\" and claims futures execution. You cannot trade the index; you trade the futures, which have their own pre-open/open and a fluctuating basis. Measuring returns on spot opens/closes while asserting futures fills introduces basis/open-mismatch slippage of a magnitude (several bps) **comparable to the entire 9 bps claimed edge**. Backtest on the actual futures series (or the tradeable ETF), or explicitly justify that intraday basis noise is negligible at this horizon.\n\n## Checked and OK (not defects)\n- **Timezone:** timestamps are tz-aware IST and `.dt.date`/`.dt.time` operate on IST, so the HF 18:30-UTC daily-stamp bug (LANDMINE #1) is correctly avoided.\n- **`prev_close`:** `m[t<=15:30].groupby(\"d\")[\"close\"].last().shift(1)` correctly takes the prior *trading day's* close (row-shift handles weekends/holidays); first day is NaN and is skipped. No lookahead here.\n- **Decision info:** gap/direction use only open + prior close (both known at entry) \u2014 no future-bar leakage in the signal logic itself; the problem is the *price*, not the timing.\n\n## Non-material notes (robustness / style)\n- `g[g[\"t\"] >= time(10,15)].iloc[0]` raises `IndexError` on any half-day / early-close / gapped session with no bar \u226510:15; guard with an empty check.\n- 1 bp/side even for genuine NIFTY futures is optimistic for a **market-on-open** fill during the most volatile minute of the day; validate against `06_TRADING_DESK/COST_STANDARDS.md` before quoting net edge.\n- Exiting on the `close` of the 10:15 bar (~10:16) is a reasonable tradeable price \u2014 fine.\n\n## Claimed material defects\n1. **Pre-open auction price used as `day_open`** (`g.iloc[0][\"open\"]`): corrupts the gap, the trade selection, the fade direction, and \u2014 fatally \u2014 the entry price, booking trades at the untradeable 09:00 indicative auction and harvesting the phantom 09:00\u219209:15 reversion. Fix: filter to `t >= 09:15` before taking the open. Result must be re-run.\n2. **Spot-index prices with futures costs/execution claim**: basis/open mismatch of a size comparable to the 9 bps edge; backtest on the actual traded instrument.", "ids": ["O08a", "O08b", "O08c", "O08d"]}, {"tid": "T09", "key": "## T09 \u2014 lookahead \u2014 shift sign error, feature from the future (CRITICAL)\n**Defect.** `df[\"adv_dec\"] = (df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]).shift(-1)` feeds day\nt+1's breadth into day t's signal \u2014 and the position holds exactly the t+1 session\n(open t+1 -> open t+2). The entry \"confirms\" with the breadth of the session it is\nabout to trade. Every other feature is causal; this one line fabricates the edge.\n**Line.** the `.shift(-1)` on `adv_dec`.\n**Fix.** `shift(0)` (day t's breadth, knowable at the close) or `shift(1)`; standing\nrule: grep for `.shift(-` on any feature; `.shift(-n)` is forbidden without a LABEL\ntag.\n**Accept.** \"shift(-1) on breadth = tomorrow's advance/decline used at t\";\n\"sign-flipped shift leaks the held session's breadth into the entry\"; \"adv_dec is\nfuture data; all other features are lagged correctly\".\n**Secondary.** `df[\"o2o_next\"] = open.shift(-2)/open.shift(-1)-1` is the EXECUTION\nreturn aligned to the decision date \u2014 a standard, legitimate pattern (calling THIS\nthe leak instead of adv_dec scores 0-1, not 2); costs modelled thinly but present.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O09a -----\n# T09 \u2014 Review: index trend filter with breadth confirmation\n\n**Verdict: ONE material defect \u2014 a lookahead in the breadth-confirmation feature that\ninflates CAGR and suppresses drawdown. The result as reported is fake.**\n\n---\n\n## Material defect\n\n### D1 \u2014 `adv_dec` peeks at tomorrow's breadth (`.shift(-1)` lookahead)\n\n```python\ndf[\"adv_dec\"] = (df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]).shift(-1)\n```\n\n- **The claim it violates.** The block is explicitly labelled: *\"features -- evaluated at\n  day t's close, from data known by that close.\"* Every other feature honours that\n  (`mom10`, `vol20`, `above_dma`, `vol_regime_ok` all use data through row t only).\n  This one does not.\n- **Mechanism.** `series.shift(-1)` moves values *up*: row t receives row **t+1's** value\n  (verified: `[1.2,1.9,0.8,2.1,1.6].shift(-1) -> [1.9,0.8,2.1,1.6,nan]`). So the signal\n  formed at the close of day t requires that **tomorrow's (t+1) advance/decline ratio**\n  exceed 1.5 \u2014 a number that does not exist until day t+1's session closes. The model is\n  conditioning entry on the breadth of the very session it is trying to profit from.\n- **Why it corrupts the headline.** Breadth is one of four AND-ed conditions, and it is the\n  *named edge* of the model (\"breadth confirmation\"). Requiring next-day breadth > 1.5 is a\n  near-direct filter on next-day market direction (broad-advance days are overwhelmingly\n  up days). The strategy is being handed a peek at whether the day it holds will be a broad\n  up-day. This is exactly the pattern that produces the reported profile: only 38% invested,\n  yet CAGR 17.1% vs 12.4% buy-and-hold and max DD cut from \u221238% to \u221211%. Outperforming a\n  higher-exposure benchmark while flat 62% of the time is the signature of a look-ahead, not\n  of a timing edge.\n- **Fix.** Day t's own breadth is known at day t's close, so use it with **no shift**:\n  ```python\n  df[\"adv_dec\"] = df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]\n  ```\n  (If the intent were \"breadth on the entry day t+1,\" that value is still unknown at the\n  close-of-t decision point and remains a lookahead \u2014 the signal must be built only from\n  data through t. The `o2o_next` execution term already handles the t+1\u2192t+2 holding timing;\n  the feature must not also reach forward.) Re-run before any result is quoted; expect the\n  edge to shrink materially or vanish.\n\n---\n\n## Checked and found CLEAN (not defects)\n\n- **Execution / return alignment \u2014 correct, no lookahead.**\n  `df[\"o2o_next\"] = df[\"open\"].shift(-2)/df[\"open\"].shift(-1) - 1.0` gives, at row t,\n  `open[t+2]/open[t+1] - 1` (verified numerically). Signal at close of t \u2192 enter at\n  open of t+1 \u2192 exit at open of t+2. Both prices are strictly *after* the close-of-t\n  decision, so the holding return itself introduces no future information. The open-to-open\n  segments also tile contiguously (t's exit at open[t+2] = t+1's entry), so consecutive\n  long days neither overlap nor double-count.\n- **Other features are point-in-time.** `mom10` (pct_change(10)), `vol20`\n  (rolling std of ret1), `above_dma` (close vs 50-DMA), `vol_regime_ok` (vol20 vs its\n  100-mean) all use only data through row t.\n- **NaN handling of the signal.** Warm-up NaNs collapse to `False` via `>` comparisons\n  and `.fillna(False)`, so early rows are flat (pos=0), not spuriously long.\n- **CAGR annualisation \u2014 correct.** `n` counts non-NaN daily strat values (all days except\n  the trailing 2 where `o2o_next` is NaN); flat days contribute a factor of 1.0 to the\n  product, and `252/n` annualises over the trading-day count. Math is sound *given* the\n  inputs \u2014 but it is annualising a look-ahead-inflated return series, so the printed 17.1%\n  is not trustworthy until D1 is fixed.\n\n## Non-material comments (do not change the verdict)\n\n- **Cost level.** 3 bps per position change (`0.0003`) is charged once on entry and once on\n  exit (\u22486 bps round trip). Reasonable-to-light for a liquid index future/ETF; flag as an\n  assumption, not an error. Timing of the cost (on the row where `pos.diff()!=0`) is aligned\n  with the return period, which is fine.\n- **`long share` denominator.** `pos.mean()` is over all rows including the final 2 rows\n  whose return is NaN; effect on the 38% figure is negligible.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n\n1. **`adv_dec = (advances/declines).shift(-1)`** \u2014 `.shift(-1)` makes the day-t signal use\n   day t+1's market-breadth ratio, a lookahead that contradicts the block's own\n   \"known by that close\" comment and drives the reported outperformance. Fix: drop the\n   shift (`df[\"adv_dec\"] = df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]`) and re-run; the headline\n   result (CAGR 17.1%, DD \u221211%) is invalid until then.\n\n(All other elements reviewed \u2014 execution timing, return tiling, point-in-time features,\nCAGR annualisation \u2014 are correct.)\n\n----- ANSWER O09b -----\n# CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT \u2014 T09 index trend filter with breadth confirmation\n\nRajan Mehta, CIO. Consolidating Arjun Rao (Head of Quant) + Nikhil Bose (Red Team).\nEvidence: pasted code + write-up ONLY. No files read, listed, searched, or executed (hard constraint honored). Execution-dependent claims tagged [INFERENCE].\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT \u2014 FAKE (lookahead artifact)\n\n**Rationale (3 lines):**\n1. The core breadth-confirmation gate reads *tomorrow's* advance/decline into *today's* signal; the reported edge is future information selecting its own realized session.\n2. Both reviews independently converge on this single decisive leak; I confirm it against the code and adopt Nikhil's precision on the fix (contemporaneous breadth, shift 0 \u2014 NOT +1).\n3. The headline drawdown comparison (-11% vs -38%) is not produced anywhere in the pasted code \u2014 an unsubstantiated selling point layered on top of a fake edge.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS (consolidated)\n\n### DEFECT 1 (decisive) \u2014 future-breadth leak\n**Line:** `df[\"adv_dec\"] = (df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]).shift(-1)`\n\n**Mechanism:** pandas `.shift(-1)` moves row t+1's value UP onto row t. So on signal row t, `adv_dec = advances[t+1]/declines[t+1]` \u2014 day t+1's close-over-close breadth, which is not determined until day t+1's close. The signal is formed at close t (`adv_dec > 1.5` is one of four ANDed gates) and the trade is ENTERED at open t+1 (`o2o_next[t] = open[t+2]/open[t+1] - 1`). The gate therefore conditions entry on information that post-dates entry. Because breadth[t+1] is a proxy for \"day t+1 closed strongly up,\" and the held window open[t+1]\u2192open[t+2] overlaps the intraday open\u2192close leg of day t+1, the filter selects into precisely the sessions that already turned out up. That is what manufactures the high hit-rate / shallow-DD equity line and the ~4.7-pt CAGR premium. This is a selection-stage T-class future-feature leak (LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS / D-028 taxonomy), independent of the return construction \u2014 the clean one-bar entry lag on `o2o_next` does NOT protect against it, which is exactly why it hides.\n\n**Fix:** `df[\"adv_dec\"] = df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]` \u2014 contemporaneous, **shift 0**. Session t's advance/decline count is fully determined at close t and is PIT-consistent with mom10 / vol20 / above_dma (all correctly windowed through t). NOT a +1 shift: Arjun's \"only a +1 (prior-day) shift is ever defensible, never -1\" wording conflicts with his own (correct) contemporaneous fix; a +1 shift merely stales the signal for no reason. Nikhil's correction is adopted \u2014 **use shift 0.** Guard `declines == 0`. Then re-run the full battery.\n\n### DEFECT 2 (secondary, but real) \u2014 headline DD/benchmark not produced by the code\n**Claim:** \"max DD -11% vs -38%\" and \"buy-and-hold 12.4%, -38%.\"\n\n**Mechanism:** the pasted script computes and prints ONLY `long share` and `CAGR`. There is no drawdown / equity-curve computation and no buy-and-hold series anywhere in the code. The single most striking stat in the write-up (the DD reduction) and the entire B&H comparison have no code behind them \u2014 asserted, not measured. Even setting the leak aside, these numbers are unverifiable. Missed by the Quant review; correctly raised by Red Team.\n\n**Fix:** compute an actual equity curve and rolling max-DD for both `strat` and buy-and-hold on a single common return convention, in code, and report the headline from that output \u2014 after Defect 1 is fixed.\n\n---\n\n## Tail-risk assessment\nThe reported -11% max DD is the fingerprint of the leak, not a real risk profile. A model whose \"protection\" derives from peeking at the next session's breadth has, live, NO drawdown floor:\n- **Worst single-day / worst-month [INFERENCE]:** effectively the underlying index's own \u2014 index open-to-open ~ -10% to -13% single-day in a 2020/2024-class shock, -25% to -38% peak-to-trough \u2014 with *none* of the simulated cushion.\n- **Correlated-blowup scenario:** entries cluster on \"strong-breadth\" days; a gap-down open the morning after a peeked-up close (overnight reversal / event gap) hits the full long book at once \u2014 the precise regime the sim cannot see because it entered on future confirmation. Any quoted tail number here is fiction.\n\n## Sizing ruling\n**ZERO.** No capital, no paper allocation, not registerable. Nothing from this submission is quotable until re-run.\n\n## Kill criteria / path to resurrection\nRemove `.shift(-1)` \u2192 contemporaneous breadth, then a mandatory pass of ALL: (a) one-day-lag placebo \u2014 removing the shift IS the lag test \u2014 plus a within-window breadth shuffle (Sharpe should \u2192 ~0, confirming the edge was the leak); (b) DD / CAGR / B&H ACTUALLY computed in code on one convention (fixes Defect 2); (c) 2x COST_STANDARDS with slippage/impact; (d) walk-forward + deflated Sharpe on honest trials + PBO; (e) regime slices 2018/2020/2022/2024 with trade count; (f) formal LOOKAHEAD AUDIT PASS (D-028). Prior [OPINION]: with contemporaneous breadth the edge collapses toward buy-and-hold. Review only on resubmission carrying the above \u2014 no standing clock.\n\n## Dissents\nNone. Arjun (Quant) and Nikhil (Red Team) concur on Defect 1. Nikhil adds Defect 2 and corrects the fix wording (contemporaneous / shift 0, not +1) \u2014 both adopted. No reviewer disagreement to record.\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled; do NOT act on before Defect 1)\n- 3 bps one-way cost (~6 bps round-trip) is optimistic for open-auction index execution and carries no slippage/impact \u2014 re-cost per COST_STANDARDS once the signal is real.\n- Open-to-open strategy return vs close-to-close benchmark basis \u2014 reconcile to a single convention when re-benchmarking.\n- `declines == 0` \u2192 `inf > 1.5 = True` \u2014 cosmetic guard only.\n- Index not directly tradable \u2014 ETF/future tracking + roll cost when structured.\n- No validation battery shown (walk-forward / DSR / PBO / regime / trade count) \u2014 incompleteness; mandatory before any certification even after the fix.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of material defects\n1. `adv_dec = (advances/declines).shift(-1)` \u2014 pulls day t+1 breadth into the day-t entry gate (lookahead into the very session traded, open t+1\u2192open t+2); decisive, makes the result FAKE. Fix: drop `.shift(-1)`, use contemporaneous `advances/declines` (shift 0, NOT +1), guard `declines==0`, re-run the full battery.\n2. Reported \"max DD -11% vs -38%\" (and the buy-and-hold 12.4% / -38% comparison) are not produced by the pasted code \u2014 no drawdown/equity-curve or B&H computation exists; the headline DD stat is asserted, not measured. Fix: compute equity curve + rolling max-DD for strat and B&H in code on a common convention and report from that output, after Defect 1 is fixed.\n\n----- ANSWER O09c -----\n# T09 \u2014 Consolidated final verdict: index trend filter with breadth confirmation\n\n**Arbiter role:** consolidating the primary review and the red-team review into one verdict.\nBoth reviews independently converge on the same single material defect and the red team\ncould not refute it. I confirm one material defect; the rest of the code is sound.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT (1)\n\n### Look-ahead: `adv_dec` uses `.shift(-1)`, so the breadth condition reads the future / the very session being traded\n\n- **Exact line:** `df[\"adv_dec\"] = (df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]).shift(-1)`\n- **Mechanism:** `series.shift(-1)` pulls row t+1's value back onto row t, so\n  `adv_dec[t] = advances[t+1]/declines[t+1]` \u2014 day **t+1's** breadth. The signal is (by the\n  code's own stated design) formed at **day t's close**, yet the condition `adv_dec > 1.5`\n  reads day t+1's advance/decline ratio, which is not finalized until t+1's 15:30 close.\n  This directly contradicts the code's own comment (\"features \u2026 evaluated at day t's close,\n  from data known by that close\"). It is a future-dated feature (D-028 / T-class lookahead).\n- **Why it inflates the headline result:** the traded return is\n  `o2o_next[t] = open[t+2]/open[t+1] \u2212 1`, i.e. the open\u2192open return spanning session\n  **t+1**. A day's advance/decline ratio is a near-mechanical *contemporaneous* proxy for\n  that same day's index direction (a broad-advance day \u21d4 index up that day). Requiring\n  `adv_dec[t+1] > 1.5` therefore admits precisely the sessions whose own realized move\n  (the open\u2192close leg of t+1) is already known to be positive. The strategy conditions the\n  long trade on part of the outcome of the day it is long. Direction of bias is unambiguous:\n  CAGR is inflated and drawdown suppressed. The reported figures (CAGR 17.1% vs 12.4%,\n  DD \u221211% vs \u221238%) are **void** until re-run.\n  - *Refinement (from red-team, adopted):* the claim that this \"alone can manufacture the\n    entire edge\" is a plausible hypothesis, not something provable from the code \u2014 the\n    breadth proxy captures the open\u2192close leg of t+1 but not the overnight\n    close[t+1]\u2192open[t+2] leg. Whether any real edge survives can only be settled by\n    re-running. Either way the current numbers are not usable.\n- **Fix:** remove the sign-flipped shift; use **unshifted day-t breadth**, which is finalized\n  at t's 15:30 close and is legitimately available for a t-close decision:\n  `df[\"adv_dec\"] = df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]`. (Do **not** use `.shift(1)` as the fix \u2014\n  that needlessly discards one day of legitimately-available information.) Then re-run the\n  full backtest and pass a lookahead / one-day-lag audit per LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS before any\n  number is quoted.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL COMMENTS (not defects \u2014 verified clean by both reviews)\n\n- **Execution timing is correct, not a bug.** `o2o_next = open.shift(-2)/open.shift(-1) \u2212 1`\n  \u21d2 signal@close t \u2192 enter open[t+1] \u2192 exit open[t+2], aligned back to row t. No same-bar /\n  close-to-close execution lookahead; consecutive long days chain without overlap, so\n  `(1+strat).prod()` compounds validly.\n- **The other four features are clean.** `mom10`, `vol20`, `above_dma` (close vs 50-DMA), and\n  `vol_regime_ok` (vol20 vs its 100d mean) all use data through row t only.\n- **Annualization is correct.** `strat` is NaN only on the last 2 rows (o2o_next NaN); flat\n  days give `0 * o2o_next = 0` (counted), so `n \u2248 full span` and `prod**(252/n) \u2212 1`\n  annualizes over the true calendar span. `pos` is int, so `pos*o2o_next` is never spuriously\n  NaN mid-series.\n- **Cost model thin but not fake.** `pos.diff().abs() * 0.0003` = 3 bps per position change\n  (~6 bps round trip); optimistic (no slippage/circuit realism) but acceptable for a liquid\n  index vehicle. Minor.\n- **`pos.mean()` (\"long 38%\") includes the trailing 2 NaN-return rows.** Negligible effect on\n  the share figure. Minor.\n- **Reproducibility note (from red-team):** the snippet prints only long-share and CAGR. The\n  reported \"buy-and-hold 12.4%\" and \"max DD \u221211% vs \u221238%\" are **not produced by the code\n  shown**, so those specific figures cannot be reproduced/verified from the submission. Not a\n  code defect, but the write-up's headline is broader than the code that backs it.\n- **Deliberately NOT flagged (no evidence / would be invented):** pre-open auction landmine\n  does not apply (official NSE **daily** data \u2014 `open` is the 09:15 official open, not the\n  1-min auction artifact); `declines == 0` \u2192 inf/NaN in `adv_dec` would only turn the signal\n  False (conservative, non-inflating); index-sort correctness for shift/rolling cannot be\n  verified from the text and the task asserts a date index \u2014 not raisable without evidence.\n\n---\n\n## Claimed material defects\n1. **`df[\"adv_dec\"] = (df[\"advances\"]/df[\"declines\"]).shift(-1)`** \u2014 forward-dated breadth\n   feature. The signal formed at day t's close reads day t+1's advance/decline ratio, which is\n   contemporaneous with the very session (t+1) whose return the strategy captures. This is a\n   true lookahead that inflates CAGR and understates drawdown; the reported figures are void.\n   **Fix:** remove `.shift(-1)` (use unshifted day-t breadth), then re-run and pass a\n   lookahead audit before quoting any number.\n\nThat is the only material defect. Execution timing, the other four features, annualization,\ncost model, and the long-share figure are correctly assessed and contain no invented problems.\n\n----- ANSWER O09d -----\n# T09 Review \u2014 Index trend filter with breadth confirmation\n\n## Verdict: MATERIAL DEFECT \u2014 the result is fake (one-day lookahead in the headline feature).\n\n---\n\n## Defect 1 (FATAL) \u2014 `adv_dec` peeks at tomorrow's breadth (`.shift(-1)`)\n\n**Line:** `df[\"adv_dec\"] = (df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]).shift(-1)`\n\n**Mechanism.** The stated design is that every feature is \"evaluated at day t's close,\nfrom data known by that close.\" `advances`/`declines` are the exchange's breadth counts\nfor each session, so day t's breadth is already aligned to row t and is fully known at\nday t's close. Applying `.shift(-1)` moves the value at row **t+1** up into row **t**:\nafter the shift, `adv_dec[t] = advances[t+1]/declines[t+1]` \u2014 i.e. **tomorrow's** breadth\nratio. That value is not known at day t's close, so the `adv_dec > 1.5` condition in the\nday-t signal is reading the future. Classic T-class lookahead / future leak\n(LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS T-taxonomy; DATA LANDMINE-class violation).\n\n**Why it dominates the result.** Daily breadth and the next day's index return are highly\ncorrelated \u2014 a session with advances/declines > 1.5 is, by construction, an up-breadth\nday, and the trade's realised return (`open[t+2]/open[t+1]`) sits inside/adjacent to that\nsame forward window the breadth is measured over. So the filter is effectively selecting\n\"go long right before strong up-breadth sessions,\" which is a near-direct peek at next-day\nmarket direction. This is not a small leak at the margin; it is the engine of the reported\nedge. It plausibly manufactures the entire 17.1% vs 12.4% CAGR gap **and** the drawdown\ncompression (-11% vs -38%), because the leak also lets the model dodge down days it could\nnot have known were coming. The result as reported is not reproducible without the leak.\n\n**Fix.** Remove the shift and use the same-session breadth that is actually known at the\nclose:\n```python\ndf[\"adv_dec\"] = df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]   # no shift; day-t breadth, known at day-t close\n```\nThen re-run. Expect CAGR to collapse toward buy-and-hold and max DD to widen materially.\nRe-certify only after a one-day-lag lookahead-audit pass on ALL features.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (explicitly NOT defects)\n\nThese I checked and found **correct**, or too small to affect the headline:\n\n- **Execution timing is clean.** `o2o_next = open.shift(-2)/open.shift(-1) - 1` aligned to\n  row t means: signal at close t \u2192 enter at open t+1 \u2192 exit at open t+2. There is a full\n  session between signal and entry, and entry/exit use future opens only for *execution*,\n  not for the signal. This is realistic and conservative. No defect here.\n- **No overlap / double-count.** Consecutive signal days chain contiguously\n  (t: open t+1\u2192t+2; t+1: open t+2\u2192t+3); a signal followed by no-signal exits flat at\n  open t+2. Positions are non-overlapping. Correct.\n- **Annualization is correct.** `n = strat.notna().sum()` \u2248 total trading days (long +\n  flat); `(1+strat).prod()**(252/n) - 1` annualizes the full-calendar compounded return of\n  a long/flat book properly. Flat days legitimately contribute factor 1.\n- **Other features are trailing / point-in-time OK:** `mom10` (pct_change(10)), `vol20`\n  (rolling std), `above_dma` (close vs 50-DMA), `vol_regime_ok` (vol20 vs its 100-mean)\n  all use data through day t only. Fine.\n- **Cost assumption (non-material, label per COST_STANDARDS):** `pos.diff().abs()*0.0003`\n  charges 3 bps per position change, so ~6 bps round trip. That is on the light side but is\n  a documented assumption, not a correctness bug, and does not create the outperformance.\n- **Tail NaNs (non-material):** on the last 2 rows `o2o_next` is NaN, and on flat days there\n  `0 * NaN = NaN`, so ~2 tail rows drop instead of counting as flat. Negligible (2 of ~2500\n  rows). Likewise `pos.mean()` for the \"long share\" figure includes those tail rows; effect\n  on the 38% figure is negligible.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects \u2014 numbered list\n\n1. **`df[\"adv_dec\"] = (df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]).shift(-1)`** \u2014 the `.shift(-1)`\n   injects **next-day (t+1) breadth** into the day-t signal, a one-day lookahead / future\n   leak in the model's headline \"breadth confirmation\" feature. It fabricates most/all of\n   the reported CAGR outperformance and drawdown reduction. Fix: drop the `.shift(-1)` and\n   use same-session breadth (`df[\"advances\"] / df[\"declines\"]`), then re-run and re-audit.\n\n(No other material defects found.)", "ids": ["O09a", "O09b", "O09c", "O09d"]}, {"tid": "T10", "key": "## T10 \u2014 statistics \u2014 daily-corr diversification artifact (HIGH)\n**Defect.** The \"uncorrelated\" claim rests on DAILY correlations for a sleeve that is\nflat ~80% of days (flat days book 0). Episodic sleeves mechanically show ~0 daily\ncorrelation while sharing a factor at the monthly/drawdown horizon \u2014 the memo's own\nworst-month table shows EVT-1 negative in ALL FIVE of the book's worst months. The\nroot-N Sharpe projection (1.05 -> 1.38) built on daily-corr independence is invalid,\nand sizing (20% risk weight) is decided on that artifact.\n**Mechanism.** correlation measured at the wrong horizon for an asynchronous/episodic\nreturn stream.\n**Fix.** Quote monthly (or drawdown-window) correlation and re-derive the stacking\nmath from it; check tail coincidence explicitly (worst-month overlap).\n**Accept.** \"Daily corr on a mostly-flat sleeve is an artifact \u2014 measure monthly\";\n\"the worst-month table contradicts the independence claim: it loses exactly when\nthe book loses\"; \"root-N stacking needs independence at the horizon where drawdowns\nlive, not at daily frequency\".\n**Secondary.** Standalone Sharpe 0.94 below book average also weakens stacking math\n(bonus observation, not required for full credit).\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O10a -----\n# T10 \u2014 Consolidated arbiter verdict: EVT-1 diversifier memo\n\n**Role:** final arbiter over two prior reviews (Reviewer-1 and Red-Team). Keep only defects\nthat survive scrutiny. The memo's central claim \u2014 \"EVT-1 is an uncorrelated return stream \u2192\napprove a 20% risk-weight add on diversification grounds\" \u2014 is **rejected**. The right answer\nis reject / gate, but the honest set of load-bearing reasons is narrower than Reviewer-1's,\nand includes one economic point Reviewer-1 missed.\n\n---\n\n## ARBITRATION OF THE DISPUTED FINDINGS\n\n**Reviewer-1 Defect 1 (\"near-zero correlations are a flat-day / zero-inflation artifact\") \u2014\nREJECTED as a standalone defect. Its mechanism is mathematically wrong.**\nReviewer-1 claims the ~81% flat days push the full-sample Pearson correlation \"toward zero\nregardless of how tightly it co-moves on active days.\" That is false. For a series that is 0\non fraction (1\u2212p) of days and active on fraction p (daily P&L means \u2248 0), the algebra is\nCov(X,Y) \u2248 p\u00b7Cov(X,Y|active) and Var(X) \u2248 p\u00b7Var(X|active), so \u03c1_full \u2248 \u221ap \u00b7 \u03c1_active. With\np = 0.19, \u221ap \u2248 0.44 \u2014 a *perfectly* co-moving active-day series would still print \u03c1_full \u2248\n0.44, nowhere near the observed +0.03. Inverting, the observed +0.03 implies an active-day\ncorrelation of \u03c1_active \u2248 0.03/0.44 \u2248 0.07, i.e. genuinely near zero. So the body/linear\ncorrelation is real, not an artifact, and full-sample correlation (with full-sample variance)\nis in fact the *correct* input to the portfolio-variance / Sharpe combination \u2014 the flat-day\nzeros belong there because they genuinely lower EVT-1's contribution to book variance. The\nRed-Team correction is upheld. The only surviving kernel of Reviewer-1 D1 \u2014 that a low *linear*\ncorrelation says nothing about *tail* dependence \u2014 is not a correlation-computation defect at\nall; it is folded into Material Defect 1 below.\n\n**Reviewer-1 Defect 3 (\"the ~1.38 book Sharpe is the optimal-weight RSS max; true value ~1.2\nat 20%\") \u2014 DOWNGRADED to non-material.** The conceptual half is fair: \u221a(SR_B\u00b2+SR_S\u00b2) =\n\u221a(1.05\u00b2+0.94\u00b2) \u2248 1.41 is the *maximum* combined Sharpe, reached only at the optimal variance\nshare SR_S\u00b2/(SR_B\u00b2+SR_S\u00b2) \u2248 44%, so citing an RSS figure as \"the\" projection blurs the optimum\nwith an arbitrary weight. But Reviewer-1's *numeric* counter-claim (~1.2) is not robust. Under\nthe natural reading \"20% of the variance budget,\" combined Sharpe = \u221a(1\u2212f)\u00b7SR_B + \u221af\u00b7SR_S =\n\u221a0.8\u00b71.05 + \u221a0.2\u00b70.94 = 0.939 + 0.420 \u2248 **1.36 \u2248 the memo's 1.38** \u2014 because the Sharpe surface\nis flat near the optimum (f=0.44\u21921.41, f=0.20\u21921.36, f=0.10\u21921.29). The \"~1.2\" only appears under\na *different, unstated* reading (sleeve vol = 20% of book vol \u21d2 f\u22480.04). Also, Reviewer-1's\nclaim that positive *stress* correlation lowers this Sharpe conflates Sharpe (driven by\nfull-sample variance, where \u03c1\u22480.03 genuinely holds) with drawdown (where tail dependence\nbites). Net: the Sharpe *arithmetic* is essentially fine; the problem is that **Sharpe is the\nwrong lens for a tail/drawdown-diversification case**, not that the number is miscomputed.\nThe \"materially overstated\" charge does not survive; the surviving conceptual note is absorbed\ninto Material Defect 1.\n\nBoth reviewers agree on the other two (Reviewer-1 D2 and D4); both survive and are upheld\nbelow with the Red-Team hyperbole tempered. The Red-Team's three additional economic points\n(short-vol overlap; in-sample/small-n; ADD-raises-risk) survive and are added.\n\n---\n\n## SURVIVING MATERIAL DEFECTS\n\n### 1. Wrong metric for the question asked; the memo's OWN worst-months table refutes the diversification claim (positive left-tail dependence)\n- **Field:** the correlation table + the sentence \"EVT-1 is an uncorrelated return stream,\"\n  set against the \"worst 5 book months\" excerpt and the \"Verdict sought.\"\n- **Mechanism:** the case argues *tail/drawdown* diversification using *body-of-distribution*\n  statistics (linear correlation and Sharpe). A near-zero *linear* correlation is silent on\n  tail co-movement \u2014 and the memo's own data shows the tail moves together. EVT-1 loses in\n  **5 of the 5 worst book months**, and its single worst month (standalone worst = \u22126.2%)\n  **is** the book's worst month (Mar-2020, book ex-EVT \u221211.4%). Base-rate check: monthly\n  Sharpe \u2248 0.94/\u221a12 \u2248 0.27 \u21d2 P(down month) \u2248 \u03a6(\u22120.27) \u2248 0.39, so under independence\n  P(all 5 worst book months are also EVT-1 losers) \u2248 0.39\u2075 \u2248 **0.9%**. 5/5 is therefore\n  very unlikely to be chance: this is genuine positive left-tail dependence \u2014 the sleeve loses\n  exactly when, and most when, the book loses. A \"diversifier\" that contributes to 5/5 of the\n  book's worst months provides *negative*, not positive, tail-diversification benefit.\n- **Correct fix:** judge diversification on the metric that matches the claim \u2014\n  co-exceedance / worst-return-decile / drawdown-window conditional correlation (and monthly,\n  not just daily). The memo's own display already points to reject/gate. (Caveat vs Reviewer-1:\n  \"\u03c1\u2248+1 in the crash\" is overreach \u2014 n=5, and EVT-1's losses run ~half the book's magnitude;\n  the defensible statement is \"no left-tail decorrelation, likely amplification.\")\n\n### 2. The load-bearing assumption is stated exactly backwards\n- **Field:** \"the diversification benefit does not depend on the sleeve's standalone return\n  staying at backtest levels.\"\n- **Mechanism:** the proposal is an **add** (increases total risk), not a substitution, so\n  there is no return-free variance-reduction to bank. From combined SR = \u221a(1\u2212f)\u00b7SR_B + \u221af\u00b7SR_S,\n  if the edge decays SR_S \u2192 0 then at f=0.20 combined SR = \u221a0.8\u00b71.05 = **0.939 < the current\n  1.05** \u2014 adding a positive-vol, zero-return, uncorrelated stream *strictly lowers* book\n  Sharpe (an uncorrelated zero-Sharpe asset has optimal weight zero). The entire 1.05\u2192~1.38\n  uplift is wholly contingent on the sleeve's positive return persisting out-of-sample. The\n  memo's assurance decouples the sizing recommendation from the one assumption it depends on.\n- **Correct fix:** state that the uplift is contingent on edge persistence; size on a\n  haircut/decayed return, and require forward-test evidence before crediting the backtest\n  Sharpe.\n\n### 3. Hidden factor overlap \u2014 EVT-1 is a short-vol / short-premium cousin of a sleeve the book already owns\n- **Field:** \"event-driven premium capture around scheduled announcements\"; the SV-IDX row\n  (+0.03); the Mar-2020 worst month.\n- **Mechanism:** \"premium capture\" is short-optionality / short-vol economics, and EVT-1's\n  worst month is Mar-2020 \u2014 the COVID vol spike, the canonical short-premium blow-up. The\n  book already contains a Short-vol index sleeve (SV-IDX). A stated +0.03 daily correlation\n  vs SV-IDX is implausibly low for two premium-capture books and is exactly what the \u221ap \u2248 0.44\n  attenuation (from Defect-1's math) produces for a sleeve that *is* in the short-vol family\n  but is live only ~19% of the time. So the \"diversifier\" is loading the same short-vol tail\n  the book already carries \u2014 it doubles an existing exposure rather than adding an independent\n  stream. This is the economic root cause of Defect 1's co-crash, and it is untested in the memo.\n- **Correct fix:** compute stress-conditional correlation and **incremental Sharpe /\n  orthogonality vs SV-IDX specifically** (not vs the aggregate book), before any sizing.\n\n### 4. In-sample-only, small-n evidence used to justify an immediate 20%-of-risk allocation\n- **Field:** \"Proposed: fund EVT-1 at 20% of book risk immediately.\"\n- **Mechanism:** the standalone Sharpe (0.94), all four correlations, and the monthly table\n  are computed on the **same** 2019-2025 backtest, with \u2248 14 trades/yr \u00d7 7 yr \u2248 **98 events**.\n  A Sharpe from ~98 quasi-independent observations carries a wide confidence interval (order\n  \u00b10.2), and the whole uplift rests on that noisy point estimate holding. There is no\n  out-of-sample / forward-test evidence. Sizing a brand-new sleeve at 20% of book risk\n  \"immediately\" inverts normal gating (forward-test \u2192 small size \u2192 scale) and, at the firm,\n  the forward-test-freeze discipline.\n- **Correct fix:** forward-test first at token size, scale in on realized (not backtested)\n  performance; do not certify a 20% allocation on backtest-only evidence.\n\n### 5. It is an ADD, so absolute drawdown rises; Sharpe-only reporting hides this\n- **Field:** \"fund EVT-1 at 20% of book risk\"; the Sharpe-only stacking math.\n- **Mechanism:** \"adding\" a sleeve at 20% of book risk *increases* total portfolio risk\n  (\u2248 +9.5% in vol for a 20%-of-variance add), it does not reallocate it. Combined with the\n  left-tail co-movement of Defect 1, the worst-month loss in currency terms gets **larger**,\n  not cushioned \u2014 the opposite of what a diversifier is bought for. Reporting only the Sharpe\n  ratio conceals this, because Sharpe can rise while absolute max drawdown worsens. The memo\n  is also ambiguous on **add vs substitute**, which materially changes both the Sharpe uplift\n  and the drawdown implication and is left unstated.\n- **Correct fix:** state add-vs-substitute explicitly; report post-add max drawdown and\n  worst month in absolute (\u20b9) terms, not Sharpe alone.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL / SUPPORTING NOTES (labelled; not standalone defects)\n- **Terminology:** \"root-N combination\" is the wrong name for the root-sum-of-squares of two\n  *unequal* Sharpes; and \"uncorrelated\" is conflated with \"independent.\" Cosmetic, but the\n  second conflation is exactly where the real defect (tail dependence) hides.\n- **Suspiciously benign tail:** worst month \u22126.2% vs implied monthly vol \u2248 3.5% (12% ann.\n  vol \u00f7 \u221a12) is only \u2248 \u22121.8\u03c3; a short-premium book that lived through Mar-2020 showing its\n  worst month at just \u22121.8\u03c3 hints at under-capture of the true event tail. Speculative \u2014 flag,\n  do not rely on.\n- **1,731 obs vs ~1,764 expected trading days (2019-2025):** immaterial.\n- Reviewer-1's Defects 1 and 3 are **not** upheld (see arbitration above); only their\n  surviving kernels are folded into Material Defects 1\u20132.\n\n---\n\n## CLAIMED MATERIAL DEFECTS\n1. **Wrong metric + own-data contradiction:** the diversification case is argued with body\n   statistics (linear corr, Sharpe) while the memo's own worst-months table shows EVT-1 loses\n   in 5/5 of the book's worst months and shares its single worst month (Mar-2020, \u22126.2%) with\n   the book (~0.9% under independence) \u2014 positive left-tail dependence, i.e. negative tail\n   diversification. Fix: judge on co-exceedance / drawdown-window co-movement.\n2. **Load-bearing assumption backwards:** \"benefit does not depend on standalone return\n   persisting\" is false \u2014 as an add, if SR_S\u21920 the combined Sharpe falls to 0.94 < 1.05; the\n   entire uplift is conditional on the edge persisting. Fix: haircut the return; forward-test.\n3. **Hidden short-vol factor overlap:** \"premium capture\" = short-vol economics; the Mar-2020\n   co-crash and an implausibly-low +0.03 vs the existing SV-IDX sleeve indicate EVT-1 loads\n   the book's existing short-vol tail, not an independent stream. Fix: incremental Sharpe /\n   stress-conditional correlation vs SV-IDX specifically.\n4. **In-sample-only, small-n (~98 trades) evidence** used to justify an *immediate* 20%-of-risk\n   allocation with no OOS/forward test; the driving Sharpe 0.94 has a wide CI. Fix: forward-test\n   first, scale in.\n5. **It is an ADD** (raises total risk ~+9.5% vol), so with Defect 1's tail co-movement the\n   absolute worst-month loss increases; Sharpe-only reporting hides this, and add-vs-substitute\n   is left ambiguous. Fix: report post-add max drawdown in absolute terms; specify the funding.\n\n**Verdict: do NOT approve the 20% add on diversification grounds.** At most, gate to a\ntoken-size forward test conditioned on demonstrated stress decorrelation and orthogonality\nvs SV-IDX.\n\n----- ANSWER O10b -----\n# T10 review \u2014 EVT-1 diversifier memo\n\n**Verdict: multiple material defects. The central claim (\"EVT-1 is an uncorrelated return stream\") is a measurement artifact, and the memo's own monthly table refutes it. Do NOT approve on the diversification grounds as written.**\n\nThe whole case rests on one number \u2014 a near-zero daily correlation \u2014 and that number is manufactured by how it was computed. Worse, the memo prints the disproving evidence two tables later.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 1 \u2014 The near-zero daily correlation is an artifact of zero-inflation (sleeve flat ~81% of days)\n\n**Claim / field:** Correlation table, \"daily corr\" column: +0.02 / +0.01 / +0.03 / +0.01 over \"1,731 obs\", concluding \"All pairwise daily correlations are indistinguishable from zero. EVT-1 is an uncorrelated return stream.\"\n\n**Mechanism:** The profile states the sleeve \"holds positions on ~19% of trading days and is flat otherwise (flat days book 0).\" So ~81% of the 1,731 daily P&L observations are exactly 0. Pearson correlation computed over the full daily series pools ~1,400 (0, x) points onto the horizontal line EVT=0 while the other sleeve keeps moving. Those shared-zero days carry no covariance but inflate the denominator, so the coefficient is mechanically attenuated toward zero. A near-zero pooled daily correlation is essentially guaranteed by the sleeve's flatness \u2014 it is NOT evidence of independence. This is textbook correlation attenuation from a zero-inflated series.\n\n**Fix:** Compute the correlation conditional on the ~329 active days only (days the sleeve carries a position), and separately in stress windows. That is the diversification-relevant statistic. Report active-day corr and drawdown-window corr, not the whole-sample daily corr.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 2 \u2014 The memo's own monthly table shows perfect left-tail co-movement (the opposite of a diversifier)\n\n**Claim / field:** \"Monthly return excerpt (worst 5 book months)\" vs the \"uncorrelated\" headline.\n\n**Mechanism:** In all 5 of the book's worst months, EVT-1 is also negative \u2014 Mar-2020 \u22126.2%, Jun-2022 \u22121.8%, Jan-2023 \u22120.9%, Oct-2024 \u22122.1%, Mar-2025 \u22121.4%. Directional agreement is 5/5, and the loss magnitudes scale with the book's loss (biggest book loss \u2192 biggest sleeve loss). Note EVT-1's single worst month, \u22126.2% (its stated standalone worst month), lands exactly on the book's worst month (Mar-2020). This is strong left-tail dependence: the sleeve crashes precisely when the book crashes. A genuine diversifier would be up in at least some of these months. The near-zero average daily correlation coexists with tail co-crashing \u2014 the classic \"correlations go to 1 in a crisis\" that a full-sample daily-mean correlation hides. This sleeve ADDS tail risk; it does not diversify it.\n\n**Fix:** The diversification case is falsified by the memo's own data. Any approval must be evaluated on tail/stress co-movement, where EVT-1 is clearly correlated. Reject the \"uncorrelated\" framing.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 3 \u2014 Root-N stacking math overstates the Sharpe uplift; ~1.38 does not correspond to a 20% risk weight\n\n**Claim / field:** \"Adding an uncorrelated sleeve with standalone Sharpe 0.94 at 20% risk weight lifts the projected book Sharpe to ~1.38 (standard root-N combination).\"\n\n**Mechanism:** Two problems. (a) The independence assumption the root-N formula requires is false (Defect 2). (b) The arithmetic is inconsistent with \"20% risk weight.\" For two uncorrelated streams, combined Sharpe = (S_book + S_evt\u00b7v)/\u221a(1+v\u00b2), where v = EVT vol \u00f7 book vol. The theoretical MAX (optimal weighting) is \u221a(1.05\u00b2 + 0.94\u00b2) = 1.41. To reach 1.38 requires v \u2248 0.58 \u2014 i.e., the sleeve sized at ~58% of the book's vol (\u224840\u201345% of the risk budget), near the optimum. At a genuine 20% risk/vol weight (v \u2248 0.2) the combined Sharpe is only (1.05 + 0.94\u00b70.2)/\u221a1.04 \u2248 **1.21**, not 1.38. The 1.38 figure quotes near the theoretical maximum as if it were achievable at the proposed 20% allocation. (\"Risk weight\" is also left undefined \u2014 vol contribution vs variance share \u2014 which is how the 1.38 is smuggled in.)\n\n**Fix:** State the risk-weight definition; at a true 20% vol weight the honest uplift is ~1.21, and even that is invalid because the streams are tail-correlated. Recompute using conditional/stress correlation, not \u03c1=0.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 4 \u2014 \"The diversification benefit does not depend on the sleeve's standalone return staying at backtest levels\" is false\n\n**Claim / field:** Stacking-math paragraph, final sentence.\n\n**Mechanism:** The Sharpe uplift is a direct function of the sleeve's Sharpe (return). If the standalone edge decays toward zero, the combined Sharpe reverts to the book's own \u2014 and a sleeve with ~zero return that still loses in every crisis (Defect 2) is net-NEGATIVE, not neutral. Mean-variance diversification benefit requires positive expected return; it is not conferred by low average correlation alone. This sentence is being used to wave through funding despite return/decay uncertainty, which is exactly backwards.\n\n**Fix:** Remove the claim. Condition approval on the standalone edge being live and on tail behavior; require a decay/OOS check on the 14-trade/yr edge.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 5 \u2014 Effective sample size overstated; \"1,731 obs\" implies precision the data does not have\n\n**Claim / field:** \"Correlation evidence (daily P&L, 2019-2025, 1,731 obs).\"\n\n**Mechanism:** The independent unit here is a trade, not a calendar day. At 14 trades/yr over ~7 years, the sleeve has ~98 independent events (spread over ~329 active days). Every daily statistic \u2014 correlation standard errors, the \"indistinguishable from zero\" claim, the standalone Sharpe precision \u2014 is computed on an N that is ~15\u201318x too large. With ~98 events, confidence intervals on correlation, Sharpe (0.94), and worst-month are wide; small-sample noise, not signal. Combined with Defect 1, the \"1,731 obs\" framing manufactures false precision.\n\n**Fix:** Report statistics on the ~98-trade / ~329-active-day basis with honest error bars. Re-test significance at the true effective N.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (labelled)\n\n- **Sharpe annualization:** if the 0.94 was computed on the full daily (zero-inflated) series with \u00d7\u221a252, that is actually self-consistent (fewer active days deflate daily mean and vol together). But if it was computed on active days only and then \u00d7\u221a252, it is overstated ~2.3x. Not determinable from the memo \u2014 flag to verify, not asserted as a defect.\n- **Standalone CAGR 11.3% / Sharpe 0.94 / worst month \u22126.2%** are internally plausible; no issue on their face.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n\n1. Near-zero daily correlation is a zero-inflation artifact \u2014 sleeve is flat/0 on ~81% of the 1,731 days, mechanically attenuating the pooled Pearson correlation toward zero; must be measured on active days and in stress windows.\n2. The memo's own worst-5-months table shows EVT-1 negative in 5/5 of the book's worst months (its \u22126.2% worst month = the book's worst month, Mar-2020) \u2014 perfect left-tail co-movement that refutes the \"uncorrelated\" thesis and means the sleeve adds tail risk.\n3. Stacking math overstates: ~1.38 requires near-optimal ~40\u201345% risk weighting (EVT vol \u224858% of book vol), not the proposed 20%; at a true 20% vol weight the uplift is only ~1.21 \u2014 and the root-N formula is invalid anyway because the streams are tail-correlated. \"Risk weight\" is also undefined.\n4. \"Diversification benefit does not depend on the sleeve's standalone return\" is false \u2014 Sharpe uplift scales with the sleeve's return; a decayed, tail-correlated sleeve is net-negative.\n5. Effective sample size overstated \u2014 statistics rest on ~98 independent trades, not 1,731 daily obs; all significance/precision claims (correlations, Sharpe) are inflated.\n\n----- ANSWER O10c -----\n# T10 \u2014 CONSOLIDATED CIO VERDICT: EVT-1 diversifier memo\n\nReviewer of record: Rajan Mehta, CIO (E-001). Inputs consolidated: Arjun Rao (Head of Quant) quant review; Nikhil Bose (Red Team, E-014) adversarial review of both the submission and the quant review. Constraint honored: no files read, no code run; the memo text plus the two reviews are the entire evidence. All load-bearing numbers below re-derived independently before adoption.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT\n\nThe memo seeks approval to stack EVT-1 at 20% of book risk **on diversification grounds**. That case is fake. The \"uncorrelated return stream\" conclusion is a measurement artifact of a series that is flat 81% of days, the memo's own worst-months table shows positive left-tail co-movement, and the headline Sharpe uplift is arithmetically impossible at the stated weight. REJECT on diversification grounds; REJECT the 20% sizing as presented. Not a rejection of the sleeve's existence \u2014 a rejection of *this evidence* for *this sizing rationale*.\n\n**Three-line rationale.**\n1. Every quantitative pillar of the memo is wrong or unproven: the near-zero correlation cannot distinguish independence from real co-movement (sparse-series dilution + an underpowered test), and corr(EVT, aggregate book) \u2014 the only number that matters \u2014 is never reported.\n2. The one place the memo shows tail behavior (worst-5-months) it loses in 5/5 of the book's worst months, its own worst month coinciding with the book's: positive tail dependence, i.e. the opposite of what a diversifier is bought for.\n3. The Sharpe arithmetic (1.05 \u2192 1.38 at 20% weight) is a self-contained falsehood \u2014 20% weight yields ~1.22 and the theoretical maximum over ALL weights is only ~1.41 \u2014 and the accompanying \"benefit doesn't depend on standalone return\" line is a free-lunch fallacy that actually *lowers* book Sharpe if the edge decays.\n\n---\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT (CIO lens \u2014 capital protection first)\n\n- **Worst single day:** unmeasured. EVT is short-optionality event-premium; its worst day lives inside an event window and is not shown. The memo gives no per-event distribution, so the true one-day left tail is concealed.\n- **Worst month (in-sample):** \u22126.2% (Mar-2020), which is EVT's own standalone worst month AND coincides with the book's worst month (\u221211.4%). Against an implied ~8\u20139% annualized vol that is a ~\u22122 to \u22124\u03c3 event \u2014 a fat left tail characteristic of short-vega/short-gamma payoffs.\n- **Correlated-blowup scenario:** this is the disqualifier. EVT is negative in 5 of the book's 5 worst months. Under genuine independence with a ~40\u201350% monthly-loss base rate, P(all five negative) \u2248 1\u20133% [INFERENCE \u2014 base rate assumed]. The sleeve co-crashes with the book. It loses *less* than the book each time (a dampened co-crash, not additive ruin), but that dampening is a low-volatility/cash effect available from any low-vol allocation \u2014 it is NOT the low-correlation diversification benefit the memo claims. Worse, because EVT is short-optionality, the co-crash can deepen **nonlinearly** out-of-sample beyond the in-sample Mar-2020 tail (convexity runs against you). This is precisely our firm lesson: the tail shows up in calm-looking, \"uncorrelated\" streams, and only sizing + genuine diversification survive it. This sleeve supplies neither on the evidence given.\n\n---\n\n## SIZING RULING\n\n**No sizing on diversification grounds.** The projected 1.38 book Sharpe is not achievable at 20% weight (see Defect 1); the honest 20%-weight blend using a realistic \u2014 not zero \u2014 correlation is materially below the claim and could be *negative* if the edge has decayed. EVT is a lever on risk the book plausibly already owns via SV-IDX (short-vol), not an independent stream, so a naive-diversification risk budget would understate correlated heat. If a standalone case is later made on per-event edge and tail, EVT would be sized against its **worst-event** loss and its **correlated-blowup** contribution to the aggregate book \u2014 not against average-case Sharpe, and not on any diversification credit until conditions (a)\u2013(e) below are met.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS (surviving consolidation)\n\n**[1] Headline Sharpe uplift is arithmetically impossible at the stated 20% weight.** *Claim:* \"Adding an uncorrelated sleeve with standalone Sharpe 0.94 at 20% risk weight lifts the projected book Sharpe to ~1.38 (standard root-N combination).\" *Mechanism:* for two uncorrelated streams with EVT at 20% of total volatility, blended Sharpe = 0.9798\u00d71.05 + 0.20\u00d70.94 = **1.22**. The theoretical MAXIMUM over all weights is sqrt(1.05\u00b2 + 0.94\u00b2) = **1.41**, reached at EVT \u2248 67% of total vol; 1.38 specifically requires \u2248 **50%** weight (0.866\u00d71.05 + 0.50\u00d70.94 = 1.38). The memo quoted a near-optimal-weight figure under a 20% label. Separately, the invoked \"root-N combination\" (S\u00b7\u221aN) holds only for N EQUAL, equal-weighted streams; here both the Sharpes (1.05 vs 0.94) and the weights (80/20) are unequal, so the formula is misapplied. *Fix:* report the correct 20%-weight blend (~1.22) using the realistic (non-zero) correlation from Defects 2\u20134. This defect needs zero assumptions about the data \u2014 it is the cleanest kill.\n\n**[2] \"Uncorrelated return stream\" is a sparse-series measurement artifact.** *Field:* correlation table (+0.01/+0.02/+0.03 vs four sleeves) \u2192 \"EVT-1 is an uncorrelated return stream.\" *Mechanism:* EVT books exactly 0 on ~81% of the 1,731 days. For a series flat on fraction (1\u2212m) of days, full-sample correlation shrinks as \u03c1_full \u2248 \u221am \u00d7 \u03c1_active (with m=0.19, \u221am=0.436), i.e. \u03c1_active \u2248 2.3\u00d7 \u03c1_full at minimum. A printed +0.03 full-sample therefore corresponds to **at least ~+0.07 active-day**, and materially higher if the active-day premium is strong. A four-days-out-of-five-zero series prints near-zero full-sample correlation whether it is truly independent OR meaningfully co-moving on active days \u2014 the statistic cannot distinguish the two. *Fix:* compute correlation CONDITIONAL on the ~329 active days, aligned same-day to book/sleeve P&L. (Note: Arjun's \"+0.15 easily prints as +0.03\" is at the aggressive end \u2014 that magnitude additionally requires an active-day daily-Sharpe \u2248 2. Adopted at the tempered \"\u2265 ~+0.07, plausibly +0.10\u20130.15\" level. The mechanism stands.)\n\n**[3] The correlation test is underpowered, not merely diluted \u2014 it establishes no null.** *Claim:* \"All pairwise daily correlations are indistinguishable from zero.\" *Mechanism:* on the padded 1,731 obs, SE(\u03c1) \u2248 1/\u221a1728 \u2248 0.024, so +0.03 is ~1.25 SE \u2014 \"indistinguishable from zero\" is technically true but vacuous. On the EFFECTIVE sample (~329 active days clustered in ~98 discrete events, from 14 trades/yr \u00d7 7 yr), SE(\u03c1) \u2248 1/\u221a95 \u2248 0.10, giving the de-diluted active correlation a 95% CI of roughly \u00b10.20 \u2014 it can reject NEITHER zero NOR +0.20. The memo asserts a null (independence) it has no statistical power to establish; \"indistinguishable from zero\" is an artifact of the inflated N. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. *Fix:* run inference on the ~98-event effective sample with honest SEs; do not quote 1,731 as the sample size.\n\n**[4] The worst-5-months table directly refutes the \"uncorrelated\" verdict.** *Field:* monthly excerpt. *Mechanism:* EVT-1 is negative in ALL FIVE of the book's worst months \u2014 Mar-2020 \u22126.2% (EVT's own worst month) alongside the book's worst \u221211.4%; Jun-2022 \u22121.8%; Jan-2023 \u22120.9%; Oct-2024 \u22122.1%; Mar-2025 \u22121.4%. Five for five is strong POSITIVE left-tail dependence: the sleeve loses precisely when the book loses, the one regime in which a diversifier must decouple. This is the classic short-optionality \"uncorrelated in the body, correlated in the tail\" signature. *Refinement (adopted from Red Team):* EVT loses LESS than the book each time, so it is a **dampened co-crash**, not additive tail risk \u2014 but that dampening is a low-vol/cash effect, NOT low-correlation diversification, and being short-optionality the co-crash can deepen nonlinearly out-of-sample. *Fix:* certify on left-tail/co-crash dependence vs the aggregate book (both series in their worst deciles), with Mar-2020 explained rather than repeated.\n\n**[5] \"The diversification benefit does not depend on the sleeve's standalone return staying at backtest levels\" is false and dangerous.** *Claim (verbatim, memo body).* *Mechanism:* the uplift term is (risk contribution) \u00d7 (sleeve Sharpe). If EVT's Sharpe decays toward 0, the 20%-vol blend = 0.9798\u00d71.05 + 0.20\u00d70 = **1.03 < 1.05** \u2014 adding a decayed but genuinely-uncorrelated sleeve at 20% risk *lowers* book Sharpe (it adds variance in quadrature and buys no return; a zero-return asset sits on the x-axis of mean-variance space and is strictly dominated). The benefit depends ENTIRELY on the sleeve retaining a positive risk-adjusted return. This sentence inverts the actual mathematics and is the single most dangerous line in the memo. *Fix:* delete the claim; make the standalone-edge persistence a precondition of any allocation.\n\n**[6] Wrong correlation target and missing orthogonality test.** *Field:* correlation table reports EVT vs four INDIVIDUAL sleeves; the stacking math combines EVT with the whole BOOK, yet corr(EVT, aggregate book) \u2014 the load-bearing number \u2014 is never reported, and the tail table implies it is materially positive. No incremental-Sharpe / orthogonality test vs the aggregate book (post existing SV-IDX beta) is run. *Mechanism:* without corr(EVT, book) and incremental Sharpe, the diversification and uplift claims are untested; EVT (\"event-driven premium capture around scheduled announcements\") plausibly IS the same short-gamma/short-vega factor already owned via SV-IDX, in which case it is a lever on owned risk, not a diversifier. *Fix:* run RP-17 orthogonality \u2014 corr(EVT, aggregate book) and book-level incremental Sharpe, on active days and in the left tail. **Tag discipline:** the \"EVT-1 = SV-IDX same factor\" identification is an [INFERENCE] (the memo never states the payoff structure; \"event-driven\" could also mean drift/merger-arb), to be TESTED, not asserted as [DATA]. The material, factual half is the missing orthogonality test.\n\n**[7] Per-event tail concealment by monthly aggregation.** *Mechanism:* with only ~98 lifetime events, flat 81% of days, a \u22126.2% MONTH is almost certainly one or two bad events \u2014 monthly (and daily) aggregation hides per-event ruin risk. Standalone Sharpe 0.94 with a \u22126.2% worst month against ~6\u201312% annualized vol implies a ~\u22122 to \u22124\u03c3 left tail whose true risk lives in the per-trade distribution, not in Sharpe or monthly stats. For a sizing decision under a capital-protection mandate this is material, not cosmetic. *Fix:* certify on the per-event P&L distribution \u2014 skew, kurtosis, CVaR \u2014 before any capital; size against worst-event, not average-case.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL COMMENTS (clearly labelled)\n\n- **[OPINION]** Sharpe is a poor certification metric for a negatively-skewed short-optionality payoff; the certification metric should be per-trade CVaR/skew/kurtosis. Reinforces Defects 4 and 7 but non-material as a standalone.\n- **[INFERENCE]** No deflated Sharpe (DSR) with honest trial count or PBO is shown for the standalone (11.3% CAGR / Sharpe 0.94 over ~98 trades is a thin sample). Out of scope for a diversification memo but required before any capital under firm protocol.\n- **Corrections to the input reviews (NOT defects, and NO invented defects found in either review):** (i) Arjun's dilution magnitude \"+0.15 easily prints as +0.03\" tempered to \"+0.03 full \u21d2 \u2265 ~+0.07 active, plausibly +0.10\u20130.15\"; (ii) Arjun's \"1.38 requires ~65\u201370% weight\" corrected to ~50% (67% is the 1.41 ceiling, not 1.38) \u2014 Red Team's number adopted; (iii) Arjun's \"tail risk is ADDITIVE, not diversifying\" refined to \"dampened co-crash, not a hedge\"; (iv) Arjun's EVT=SV-IDX downgraded from [DATA] to [INFERENCE]. Every one of the six prior-review defects is grounded in the memo's own figures; none are invented.\n\n---\n\n## KILL CRITERIA / WHAT WOULD CHANGE THE VERDICT\n\nFRAGILE \u2192 REAL requires ALL of the following, else the sleeve stays rejected on diversification grounds:\n(a) active-day-conditional corr(EVT, **aggregate book**) with SEs on the ~98-event sample, near zero;\n(b) left-tail / co-crash corr(EVT, aggregate book) near zero, with the Mar-2020 co-move explained (not repeated);\n(c) a corrected 20%-weight blended Sharpe (~1.22 order) using the realistic, non-zero correlation;\n(d) incremental Sharpe of EVT vs the aggregate book, post existing SV-IDX beta (RP-17 orthogonality);\n(e) per-event P&L distribution with CVaR / skew / kurtosis, plus DSR + PBO on the standalone.\n\n**Review date:** on resubmission with (a)\u2013(e); no standing forward clock until a corrected memo is filed. Route through /orthogonality (RP-17) and /sensitivity before any IC re-convene.\n\n---\n\n## DISSENTS\n\nNone recorded. Head of Quant (Arjun Rao) and Red Team (Nikhil Bose) both conclude \"do NOT approve at 20%\"; CIO concurs and consolidates to REJECT. Red Team's numerical corrections to the quant review are adopted as above; these are refinements, not dissents.\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED MATERIAL DEFECTS (final, consolidated)\n\n1. **Sharpe uplift arithmetically impossible at 20% weight** \u2014 20% risk weight yields ~1.22, not 1.38; the maximum over ALL weights is only ~1.41 (at ~67%), and 1.38 itself needs ~50% weight; the \"root-N\" formula is misapplied to unequal Sharpes/weights. Assumption-free falsification of the verdict sought.\n2. **\"Uncorrelated\" is a sparse-series artifact** \u2014 81%-zero series dilutes full-sample daily corr by ~\u221a0.19 = 0.44; +0.03 full \u21d2 \u2265 ~+0.07 active; cannot distinguish independence from real co-movement. Fix: active-day-conditional correlation.\n3. **The correlation test is underpowered, not just diluted** \u2014 effective ~98-event sample gives SE(\u03c1) \u2248 0.10 and a CI of ~\u00b10.20; \"indistinguishable from zero\" is an artifact of the padded N = 1,731 and establishes no null.\n4. **Worst-5-months table refutes the thesis** \u2014 EVT-1 negative in 5/5 of the book's worst months, its own worst month coinciding with the book's (Mar-2020, \u22126.2% vs \u221211.4%) = positive left-tail dependence; it is a dampened co-crash (loses less), which is a low-vol/cash effect, NOT the low-correlation diversification claimed, and the co-crash can deepen out-of-sample.\n5. **\"Benefit doesn't depend on standalone return\" is false and dangerous** \u2014 a decayed-but-uncorrelated sleeve at 20% vol LOWERS book Sharpe (1.05 \u2192 ~1.03); the uplift depends entirely on the sleeve keeping a positive Sharpe (free-lunch fallacy).\n6. **Wrong correlation target + missing orthogonality test** \u2014 the table reports EVT vs four individual sleeves, never corr(EVT, aggregate book), and runs no incremental-Sharpe-vs-book test; EVT is plausibly the same short-vol factor already owned via SV-IDX ([INFERENCE], to be tested, not asserted).\n7. **Per-event tail concealment** \u2014 with ~98 lifetime events flat 81% of days, a \u22126.2% month is single-event tail risk hidden by monthly aggregation; certify on per-event distribution / skew / kurtosis / CVaR, not Sharpe or monthly stats.\n\nPrior-reviewer defects judged INVENTED: **none.**\n\n----- ANSWER O10d -----\n# T10 \u2014 Review: EVT-1 diversifier memo (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: REJECT. The diversification case is fake.** The near-zero correlation is a\nstatistical artifact of the sleeve being flat 81% of the time, the memo's own monthly\ntable shows the sleeve is a drawdown *amplifier* (positive tail dependence), and the\nSharpe-uplift arithmetic is both mislabeled and numerically inconsistent with the stated\n20% weight. Funding at 20% risk weight would worsen the book's tail, not diversify it.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 1 \u2014 Correlation is diluted to ~0 by flat days (the measurement is wrong)\n\n**Claim:** \"All pairwise daily correlations are indistinguishable from zero\u2026 EVT-1 is an\nuncorrelated return stream\" (+0.01\u2026+0.03 over 1,731 daily obs).\n\n**Mechanism:** The profile states EVT-1 \"holds positions on ~19% of trading days and is\nflat otherwise (flat days book 0).\" So EVT-1's daily P&L is **exactly 0 on ~81% of the\n1,731 days**. Pearson correlation computed over the full series is mechanically shrunk\ntoward zero by those ~1,400 zeros regardless of how the sleeve co-moves on the days it\nis actually holding risk \u2014 the zeros contribute no covariance and inflate the\ndenominator's effective n. Correlation on the ~329 *active* days is the only figure that\nmatters for portfolio risk, and it is not reported. As a rough scaling, a full-sample\ncorr \u2248 +0.02\u20130.03 is consistent with an **active-day corr on the order of +0.05\u20130.07 or\nhigher** (corr_full \u2248 corr_active\u00b7\u221a(frac_active)), i.e. the diversification claim is\nuntested on the sample where the sleeve carries risk.\n\n**Fix:** Report correlation *conditional on days EVT-1 holds a position*, and separately\nthe co-movement on the book's high-vol / drawdown days. The full-sample number is\nmeaningless here.\n\n## Material defect 2 \u2014 The memo's OWN monthly table refutes \"uncorrelated\" (tail dependence)\n\n**Claim vs data:** In the worst-5 book-months table, EVT-1 is **down in all 5** of the\nbook's worst months (\u22126.2, \u22121.8, \u22120.9, \u22122.1, \u22121.4%). Moreover EVT-1's single worst month\never equals its standalone \"worst month \u22126.2%\" and it lands **exactly on the book's worst\nmonth, Mar-2020 (\u221211.4%).**\n\n**Mechanism:** A genuinely uncorrelated (positive-Sharpe) sleeve should be roughly a\ncoin-flip in the book's bad months. Under the memo's own independence assumption the\nsleeve is negative ~39% of months (monthly Sharpe 0.94/\u221a12 = 0.27 \u2192 \u03a6(\u22120.27)=0.39), so\n**P(down in all 5 independent bad months) \u2248 0.9%**. Seeing 5/5 down, with the worst hit\ncoinciding with the book's worst month, is a rejection of independence *in the tail*.\nEvent-premium capture around announcements is structurally short-vol / short-gamma: near-\nzero linear daily correlation in calm regimes but strongly positive co-movement in\nstress. This sleeve **amplifies drawdowns exactly when the book is bleeding** \u2014 it is a\nconcentrator of tail risk, the opposite of a diversifier. The near-zero daily corr (Defect\n1) is precisely the mask that hides this.\n\n**Fix:** Measure left-tail dependence (downside/exceedance correlation, co-drawdown,\nconditional corr in the worst decile of book days). The stacking case must be judged on\nthat, not on unconditional Pearson r.\n\n## Material defect 3 \u2014 Sharpe stacking math is mislabeled and inconsistent with 20% weight\n\n**Claim:** \"Adding an uncorrelated sleeve with standalone Sharpe 0.94 at 20% risk weight\nlifts projected book Sharpe to ~1.38 (standard root-N combination of independent streams).\"\n\nThree errors:\n- **\"root-N\" is the wrong rule.** S\u00b7\u221aN applies to N *equal-Sharpe, equal-weight* streams.\n  Here there are two *unequal* streams (1.05 and 0.94) combined at a *fixed 20%* weight.\n- **1.38 is inconsistent with 20% weight even granting true zero correlation.** The\n  theoretical *maximum* combined Sharpe (optimal weights, uncorrelated) is\n  \u221a(1.05\u00b2+0.94\u00b2)=**1.41**, achieved only near ~50% vol weight. At a **20% volatility risk\n  weight the combined Sharpe is \u22481.25**, not 1.38 (I verified: 20%\u21921.25, 30%\u21921.34,\n  50%\u21921.41). 1.38 is essentially the near-optimal number quoted against an arbitrary 20%\n  allocation. (Only a \"20% of *variance*\" reading reaches ~1.36, and that still assumes\n  independence and near-optimal weighting.)\n- **The premise (independence) is false** per Defects 1\u20132, so the true combined Sharpe is\n  lower still \u2014 positive tail correlation erodes the variance benefit precisely in the\n  states that drive Sharpe.\n\n**Fix:** Recompute with the actual active-day/tail correlation and the actual 20% weight;\ndo not quote the quadrature maximum as if achievable at 20%.\n\n## Material defect 4 \u2014 \"Benefit doesn't depend on standalone return\" is a conflation\n\n**Claim:** \"the diversification benefit does not depend on the sleeve's standalone return\nstaying at backtest levels.\"\n\n**Mechanism:** True for *variance reduction* (mean-independent) \u2014 but the entire pitch is a\n**Sharpe uplift to 1.38**, and that uplift depends *entirely* on the sleeve continuing to\nearn its 0.94 return. If the event edge decays toward zero, adding the sleeve contributes\nvolatility and no return, so **book Sharpe falls, not rises.** The memo borrows a true\nstatement about variance to underwrite a Sharpe claim that is not variance-driven. Given\nthe sleeve is only ~98 trades over 7 years (14/yr \u00d7 7), edge-decay risk is exactly the\nthing that should NOT be waved away.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (labeled)\n- The full-sample \"indistinguishable from zero\" is arithmetically fine (SE\u22481/\u221a1731\u22480.024,\n  so +0.02\u20130.03 is <~1 SE) \u2014 but it is the wrong sample; active-day SE\u22481/\u221a329\u22480.055.\n  Non-material on its own; it is subsumed by Defect 1.\n- ~98 lifetime trades give the standalone Sharpe 0.94 wide error bars \u2014 a robustness\n  caveat, not a fabrication.\n\n---\n\n## Claimed material defects\n1. **Correlation measured on the full series is mechanically ~0 because EVT-1 is flat\n   (exactly 0) on ~81% of days;** the diversification-relevant active-day (~329 obs)\n   correlation is never computed. \"Uncorrelated return stream\" is unsupported.\n2. **The memo's own worst-5-months table shows EVT-1 down in all 5 book-worst months\n   (P\u22480.9% under independence), with its worst month coinciding with the book's worst\n   (Mar-2020).** This is positive tail dependence \u2014 the sleeve amplifies drawdowns, not\n   diversifies them; it refutes the \"uncorrelated\" basis of the memo.\n3. **Sharpe-stacking math is wrong:** \"root-N\" is misapplied to two unequal streams, and\n   ~1.38 is inconsistent with a 20% risk weight (correct \u22481.25 even under true zero corr;\n   1.41 is the optimal-weight ceiling). The uplift also assumes an independence that\n   Defects 1\u20132 show is false, so the real number is lower still.\n4. **\"Diversification benefit does not depend on standalone return\" conflates\n   variance-reduction (mean-independent) with the Sharpe uplift (entirely return-\n   dependent);** if the event edge decays, stacking LOWERS book Sharpe.", "ids": ["O10a", "O10b", "O10c", "O10d"]}, {"tid": "T11", "key": "## T11 \u2014 lookahead \u2014 centered rolling window, T6/T7 (CRITICAL)\n**Defect.** `iv.rolling(11, center=True).mean()` uses 5 FUTURE sessions in the\nsmoother. \"IV > 1.15x local average\" then fires preferentially at local IV peaks \u2014\nidentifiable as peaks only because the window saw IV fall afterwards. A short-vol\nentry conditioned on that is selling tops with hindsight; the +2.1%/trade is\nmanufactured.\n**Line.** `center=True` in the rolling mean.\n**Fix.** `center=False` (trailing window); re-run the whole battery.\n**Accept.** \"Centered moving average looks 5 days into the future\"; \"the smoother is\nnon-causal \u2014 entries are hindsight IV peaks\"; \"rolling(center=True) on a live signal\nis lookahead\".\n**Secondary.** Next-open entry, liquidity skip and exit rule are all fine.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O11a -----\n# T11 Review \u2014 IV-richness straddle seller\n\n## Material defect: centered moving average = forward-looking lookahead\n\nLine: `iv_ma = iv.rolling(11, center=True).mean()`\n\nMechanism. With `center=True` and window 11, `iv_ma[d]` is the mean of `iv[d-5] \u2026 iv[d+5]` \u2014 it\nincludes FIVE FUTURE trading days. The entry signal `rich = iv > 1.15 * iv_ma` therefore compares\ntoday's IV to an average that contains data not observable until d+5. `entry_days` (and hence the\nentire trade list) is built on information from the future. `rich.shift(1)` (the \"first day of a\nrich episode\" filter) is itself backward-looking and fine, but it operates on a `rich` series that\nis already contaminated, so the whole signal is poisoned.\n\nWhy this manufactures a fake edge (direction of the bias, not just noise). The condition\n`iv[d] > 1.15 * iv_ma[d]` is EASIER to satisfy when the forward half of the window (d+1\u2026d+5) is\nLOW \u2014 i.e. exactly when IV is about to FALL. The centered window therefore preferentially selects\nentry days that are immediately followed by declining IV. A short ATM straddle profits precisely\nwhen IV/premium falls. So the rule is effectively \"sell vol the day before vol drops,\" which is\nunimplementable: the day-d signal cannot be known until d+5, yet the code enters at d+1 via\n`next_open(d)`. This is a textbook T-class feature-lookahead (future data leaked into a signal),\nand it is the source of the reported +2.1%/trade net and 76% hit. Entering at the next session's\nopen does NOT rescue it \u2014 the entry day d+1 still precedes the data (d+2\u2026d+5) the signal consumed.\n\nFix. Use a strictly trailing window, and preferably exclude the current bar from its own average:\n- `iv_ma = iv.rolling(11).mean()` (default `center=False` \u2192 uses d-10\u2026d), or cleaner\n- `iv_ma = iv.rolling(10).mean().shift(1)` (average of the 10 PRIOR days, current day excluded).\n\nThen re-run. The counts and stats (96 entries / +2.1% / 76%) will change and almost certainly\ncollapse toward the short-vol carry baseline; nothing here is quotable until a lookahead audit /\none-day-lag test passes (per the firm's T1\u2013T10 controls).\n\n## Non-material notes (explicitly labelled non-material)\n- Including the current bar in its own trailing average (even after removing `center=True`) mildly\n  dampens the signal but is NOT lookahead; excluding it via `.shift(1)` is the cleaner form.\n- `if t is not None` drops entries where a leg was untraded at entry. Correct in principle (you\n  cannot sell a strike that did not trade), but if untraded legs cluster on the most extreme-IV\n  days, the surviving sample carries a mild selection bias \u2014 worth reporting how many entries were\n  dropped. Does not affect the headline defect.\n- The thresholds/params (1.15, window 11, decay50, 15 sessions) are unbounded degrees of freedom;\n  a trial count / sensitivity pass should accompany any quoted result, but no sweep is shown so\n  this is not itself a claimed defect.\n- `next_open(d)` entry (signal on d, enter at d+1 open) is correct anti-lookahead practice and\n  would be fine on its own.\n\n## Numbered list of material defects\n1. `iv.rolling(11, center=True).mean()` uses 5 future trading days inside the entry feature\n   (forward-looking lookahead). It biases entry selection toward days immediately preceding IV\n   declines \u2014 exactly the condition a short-vol straddle needs to look profitable \u2014 so the reported\n   96 entries / +2.1% net / 76% hit are fake. Fix: use a trailing window\n   (`iv.rolling(11).mean()` or `iv.rolling(10).mean().shift(1)`) and re-run with a one-day-lag\n   lookahead audit before quoting any number.\n\n----- ANSWER O11b -----\n# CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT \u2014 T11: IV-richness straddle seller (arm C)\n\nAuthor: Rajan Mehta (CIO, E-001). Reviews consolidated: Quant Review + Red-Team memo.\nEvidence base: submission text + the two reviews only (no files opened, per task constraint).\nTags: code/pandas-semantics claims = [INFERENCE] (deterministic library behavior); judgment = [OPINION]. No [DATA] \u2014 I verified nothing against files.\n\n**Submission recap.** Sell 1-month ATM index straddle on the first day IV exceeds 1.15x its 11-day *centered* moving average; exit at 50% premium decay or 15 sessions. Reported 2018-2025, 96 entries, +2.1% of premium/trade net, 76% hit.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT\n\n**Rationale (3 lines).**\n1. [INFERENCE] The entry signal is built on a centered moving average (`iv.rolling(11, center=True)`), which reads five future IV sessions into the decision bar \u2014 a T-class lookahead. The reported +2.1%/76% is a lookahead artifact, not a tradeable result.\n2. [OPINION] Both reviewers converge on the same fatal defect; I confirm it. The Red-Team's calibration is correct \u2014 \"FAKE\" describes the reported number, not the idea's ceiling. A causal IV-rich short-vol rule harvests a real variance-risk premium; some edge may survive, but it is unproven and unquoted here.\n3. [OPINION] Even after the lookahead fix, three material gaps (hidden left tail, no attribution placebo, overlapping N) each independently bar certification. This is not a RESIZE \u2014 there is no honest number to size against.\n\n## Tail-risk assessment (my mandate)\n- **Structure is a naked short straddle with NO loss stop** ([INFERENCE] exit = decay50 OR 15 sessions only). Held up to 15 sessions through a vol spike, a single trade can lose a multiple of premium collected (order of -300% to -500% of premium is structurally possible). [OPINION] Worst single-day: a gap-through-the-strikes event (Feb-2018 volmageddon, Mar-2020, Jun-2024 result-cluster days) with no stop = the full uncapped short-gamma loss. Worst month: consecutive rich-episode entries clustering into one rising-vol regime (see overlap defect) compounds the ruin.\n- **The headline conceals exactly this tail.** [OPINION] Mean + hit rate are the two metrics that flatter short vol most (high win frequency, fat left tail). No worst-trade, no P&L distribution, no max single-trade loss over a window engineered to contain straddle ruin. For a firm whose first mandate is capital protection and whose own lesson is \"the worst blowups came from calm-looking names; only sizing + diversification survive,\" a naked short-vol result reported as mean+hit is materially incomplete.\n- **Correlated-blowup scenario.** [INFERENCE] Overlapping straddles inside a 15-session window are the same short-gamma bet stacked \u2014 not diversification. A single vol regime shift hits every open leg at once. \"96 entries\" is not 96 independent tail bets.\n- **Compounding link (Red-Team's best catch, which I adopt):** [INFERENCE] the centered-MA leak does not only inflate the mean \u2014 it *suppresses* blow-up entries, because the future-contaminated average already \"knows\" IV is about to fall and thus avoids selling right before IV keeps rising. So the 76% hit is a lookahead-CLEANED hit rate. Post-fix, the mean drops AND the true left tail is worse than shown.\n\n## Sizing ruling\n**Zero. Not registerable, not paper-sizable in current form.** There is no PIT-clean edge estimate to size against; sizing a lookahead number would violate P-01/P-05 and my sizing-to-worst-case rule. No anchor from this submission may propagate to any future sizing discussion (IC-1 firewall precedent).\n\n## Material defects retained (survived consolidation)\n\n1. **Centered moving average = lookahead (FATAL).** [INFERENCE] `iv_ma = iv.rolling(11, center=True).mean()` labels an 11-wide window at its midpoint, so `iv_ma[d] = mean(iv[d-5 \u2026 d+5])`, including `iv[d+1 \u2026 d+5]` which did not exist at the decision moment. `rich = iv > 1.15*iv_ma` therefore compares today's IV to a future-contaminated average and preferentially fires at local IV peaks that are followed by a decline \u2014 precisely when a short straddle wins. `next_open(d)` entry is cosmetic: at the d+1 open, d+2\u2026d+5 are still unrealized. **Fix:** `iv_ma = iv.rolling(11).mean().shift(1)` (trailing, ends at d-1; PIT-clean), signal on d, execute at `next_open(d)`, then re-run. Corroborating tell (folded in, not a separate defect): `center=True` leaves the last 5 observations NaN \u2014 the signal is literally uncomputable in real time, which is the operational proof of non-causality, not a cosmetic edge effect.\n\n2. **Tail concealment + no loss stop.** [INFERENCE]/[OPINION] Exit rule has a profit-take and a time-stop but no loss stop; headline reports only mean + hit. For a naked short straddle over 2018-2025 this hides the entire left tail, and (per defect 1) the lookahead additionally strips out the blow-up entries, inflating the 76%. **Fix:** report full per-trade P&L distribution, worst trade, and 5th-percentile bootstrap; add an explicit loss stop and re-report with it live.\n\n3. **No random-entry placebo / no signal-vs-short-vol-beta decomposition.** [OPINION] A random-day ATM short straddle over this window plausibly also shows a positive mean and 70%+ hit (variance-risk premium). Without (i) a random-entry benchmark and (ii) a within-window entry-date shuffle, the \"+2.1%\" cannot be attributed to the IV-richness signal versus generic short-vol carry. This is firm doctrine (headlines decompose; register incremental edge = signal minus regime base). Required even after the lookahead fix. **Fix:** run both placebos; register only the increment over the short-vol base.\n\n4. **Overlapping / non-independent trades \u2014 inflated N.** [INFERENCE] `entry_days` fires on the first day of every rich episode; trades hold \u226415 sessions, so two episodes within 15 sessions produce overlapping positions and clustered regime exposure. \"96 entries\" is not 96 independent bets; it inflates any t-stat/DSR and concentrates the sample in a few vol regimes. **Fix:** report an overlap-adjusted independent N (block/regime-clustered) and recompute significance on it.\n\n## Non-material (labelled \u2014 not defects)\n- `if t is not None` silently drops untraded-leg trades; near-zero for NIFTY index ATM. Non-material, but log the drop count rather than swallowing it. [INFERENCE]\n- `simulate_straddle` / `next_open` / the word \"net\" (costs, COST_STANDARDS + 2x stress) are unaudited black boxes \u2014 required before any register entry, non-material to the FAKE verdict. [INFERENCE]\n- F&O expiry-settle landmine (option SETTLE_PR = underlying settlement on expiry day): a 1-month straddle held \u226415 sessions likely does not reach expiry, so probably clean but unverifiable. Non-material. [INFERENCE]\n- Data lineage (parquet rows/max-date) unverifiable from the submission; bars certification, but not the reason it fails. [INFERENCE]\n\n## Adjudication of the two reviews\n- **Quant reviewer:** core finding CORRECT and correctly fixed; invented no defects. Sole fault = over-claimed magnitude (\"guaranteed profit\u2026 fabricates the *entire* result\") and an incomplete battery. [OPINION] I adopt the Red-Team's calibration: the leak informs only ~the first third of the \u226415-session hold, and a causal VRP-harvesting rule may retain some edge; \"FAKE\" applies to the reported number, not to \"zero edge.\"\n- **Red Team:** confirms defect 1 (no refutation) and adds the three surviving material gaps (tail, placebo, overlap) plus the correct reclassification of the boundary-NaN as the live-non-causality proof. All four Red-Team additions survive. Value-add accepted.\n\n## Kill criteria + path to reconsideration\nThis submission is **killed in its present form.** It may return only as a NEW version (restarted clock, git hash pinned at entry, D-030) that:\n1. replaces the centered MA with `iv.rolling(11).mean().shift(1)` (or tighter causal equiv), signal on d / enter next_open(d);\n2. reports the full per-trade P&L distribution incl. worst trade, with an explicit loss stop applied;\n3. passes a random-entry placebo AND a within-window entry-date shuffle (signal must beat short-vol beta on the increment);\n4. reports an overlap-adjusted independent N and recomputed significance;\n5. clears COST_STANDARDS + 2x slippage stress, then the full battery (walk-forward 3y/1y, deflated Sharpe with honest trials count, PBO<25%, regime slices) and a LOOKAHEAD AUDIT PASS (one-day-lag test, D-028).\nInterim state after 1\u20134 with a positive 5th-percentile bootstrap = FRAGILE (not certifiable); only after 5 = REAL.\n**Review date:** on resubmission only; no calendar re-review of the dead version.\n\n## Dissents\nNone recorded. Quant reviewer and Red Team concur with this REJECT/FAKE finding; the only inter-review disagreement (magnitude of the leak) is resolved in favor of the Red-Team's calibration and does not alter the verdict.\n\n----- ANSWER O11c -----\n# T11 review \u2014 IV-richness straddle seller (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: MATERIAL DEFECT \u2014 the entry signal peeks into the future. The reported result (96 entries, +2.1%/trade, 76% hit) is a lookahead artifact, not an edge.**\n\n---\n\n## Defect 1 (fatal): centered moving average \u2192 future-peeking in the entry signal\n\n**Line:**\n```python\niv_ma = iv.rolling(11, center=True).mean()\nrich  = iv > 1.15 * iv_ma\n```\n\n**Mechanism.** With `center=True` and window 11, `iv_ma[d]` is the mean of `iv[d-5 \u2026 d+5]` \u2014 it includes **5 sessions of IV that occur AFTER the entry day d**. The entry test `iv[d] > 1.15 * iv_ma[d]` therefore depends on IV values that were not observable on day d (or on d+1 when the trade is actually put on). This is a textbook T-class lookahead (LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS.md; same family as the firm's other data landmines).\n\nThe bias is not neutral \u2014 it is *exactly* the direction that flatters a short-vol seller:\n\n- For today's IV to clear `1.15 \u00d7` a window that already contains the next 5 days, those next 5 days must be **lower** than today. So `rich` fires preferentially on days where IV is high **and is about to mean-revert downward over the coming week**.\n- A straddle seller makes money precisely when IV/realized vol falls after entry. The centered window is silently selecting the days on which the seller is guaranteed to win, because the post-entry decline is baked into the entry condition. The signal is choosing its own future.\n\n**Numerical confirmation.** On a synthetic mean-reverting IV series with **no true edge** (pure OU noise), running the submission's exact entry logic:\n\n| MA variant | entries | avg forward IV drop over 15 sess | \"win\" rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `center=True` (submission) | ~58 | **+2.15** | **70.7%** |\n| trailing `rolling(11)` (honest) | ~322 | +0.87 | 58.7% |\n\nThe centered version manufactures a large positive drift and a high hit-rate out of pure noise, and it collapses the entry count to a few dozen genuine local peaks. The submission's headline (**96 entries, +2.1%, 76%**) sits right on top of the centered-lookahead artifact and far above the honest trailing baseline. The magnitude and shape of the reported result are consistent with the bias, not with a real edge.\n\n**Fix.** Use a strictly backward-looking baseline so the average at d uses only data available by d. Preferred, exclude the current point to avoid self-referencing the level you are testing:\n```python\niv_ma = iv.rolling(11).mean().shift(1)     # trailing, ends at d-1\nrich  = iv > 1.15 * iv_ma\n```\n(Plain trailing `iv.rolling(11).mean()` removes the lookahead but still folds `iv[d]` into its own baseline, which dampens the ratio; `.shift(1)` is cleaner.) Then re-run and expect the result to degrade materially \u2014 likely toward break-even. Gate the rerun through the one-day-lag lookahead test (`lib/lookahead_audit.py`) before any quotation.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (not defects; do not affect the verdict)\n\n- **Entry timing is correct.** Signal is formed on day d and the trade is placed at `next_open(d)` (open of d+1), so there is no same-bar fill. This part is clean \u2014 the lookahead is entirely in the MA, not the execution.\n- **First-day-of-episode logic is correct.** `rich & ~rich.shift(1).fillna(False)` properly fires only on the transition into a rich episode (and the `fillna(False)` handles the first row sensibly).\n- **`if t is not None` skip.** Dropping entries where a leg is untraded at entry is a mild selection filter; for index ATM straddles untraded legs are rare, so this is unlikely to move the number. Worth logging how many were skipped, but non-material.\n- **Trade-count sanity, post-fix.** After switching to a trailing baseline, expect the number of entries to change substantially (in the demo it rose ~5x). Confirm the 96-count and the P&L both survive the fix; if the count changes a lot, the original 96 was itself an artifact of the centered window selecting only clean peaks.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n\n1. **`iv.rolling(11, center=True).mean()` introduces lookahead** \u2014 the entry-day richness test uses 5 future IV observations (`d+1 \u2026 d+5`), preferentially selecting days where IV is about to fall, which is exactly the condition under which a short straddle wins. The +2.1%/76% result is a future-peeking artifact. Fix: replace with `iv.rolling(11).mean().shift(1)` (strictly trailing) and re-run under the lookahead audit.\n\n----- ANSWER O11d -----\n# T11 \u2014 Final consolidated verdict: IV-richness straddle seller\n\n**Verdict: the submission contains a material, result-invalidating defect (a lookahead in the entry signal). The reported 96 entries / +2.1% of premium per trade / 76% hit rate is NOT achievable live and cannot be quoted. One further concern is material-adjacent (significance/metric choice) and must be resolved even after the fix.**\n\nBoth reviews agree on the fatal defect; the red-team pass confirmed it independently (including the direction of the bias) and could not refute it. I adopt it. I also adopt the red-team's magnitude and framing corrections, and I promote its statistical-fragility point to a labelled material-adjacent finding. Items that were only corrections to the *prior reviewer's wording* (not defects in the submission) are recorded as such, not as separate defects.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT (fatal \u2014 survives both reviews)\n\n**`iv_ma = iv.rolling(11, center=True).mean()` \u2014 centered moving average injects 5 future sessions of IV into the entry signal (T-class lookahead / future-peek).**\n\n- **Mechanism.** `center=True` labels each window at its midpoint, so `iv_ma[d]` = mean of `iv[d-5 \u2026 d+5]` and contains IV from the five sessions *after* the signal day. The entry test `rich = iv > 1.15*iv_ma`, and the derived `entry_days = rich & ~rich.shift(1).fillna(False)`, therefore depend on data unknown at the close of day `d`. The signal is not computable in real time (the last five `iv_ma` values do not exist on the live edge).\n- **Why it manufactures the edge (direction verified by both reviewers).** For the signal to fire, `iv[d]` must exceed 1.15\u00d7 the mean of a window centered on `d` \u2014 i.e. `iv[d]` must be a **local peak vs both past and future**. A local peak is by construction a day whose subsequent sessions are lower (falling IV), which is exactly the winning setup for a straddle **seller**. Rising-IV days (losing for the seller) get `iv_ma` pulled up by future highs and are suppressed. The lookahead thus both selects winners and screens out losers, inflating mean P&L and hit rate together. On a mean-reverting index vol series this bias is large and favorable \u2014 this is not benign smoothing.\n- **Fix.** Use a strictly causal average known at signal time: `iv_ma = iv.rolling(11).mean()` (default `center=False`), or, cleaner, compare to a prior baseline: `iv_ma = iv.rolling(10).mean().shift(1)`. Re-run. Expect +2.1%/76% to collapse toward zero / coin-flip. Any surviving edge must pass a lookahead audit **with a lag of at least the window depth (\u22655 sessions), not the default 1-day-lag test**, before it can be quoted.\n\n**Scope correction (adopted from red team, labelled [INFERENCE]).** The prior review's claim that this lookahead explains the *entire* result is inference, not demonstrated: the peek is 5 sessions deep while each trade is held up to 15 (`decay50`, 15). The *direction* of the bias is certain and favorable; the *magnitude* (100% of the +2.1%/76%) is unproven from the snippet. It is plausibly large because fast `decay50` winners exit inside the contaminated 5-day window, but state it as inference, not fact.\n\n**Framing correction (adopted from red team).** Do not treat `enter at next_open(d)` as reassurance. A one-session entry gap is orthogonal to \u2014 and cannot neutralise \u2014 a five-session-deep signal contamination. Relatedly, the firm's default one-day-lag audit would UNDER-detect this bug; the audit lag must match the window depth.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL-ADJACENT (must be resolved even after the causal fix \u2014 labelled robustness/significance)\n\n**Asymmetric payoff + thin sample make the headline uninformative.** Wins are capped at +50% of premium (`decay50`); losses are effectively uncapped (a short straddle to a 15-session timeout can lose well over 100% of premium in an IV spike). With ~76% winners at a capped ~+40%-ish typical win, the 24% losers must average roughly \u2212100% or worse to net to only +2.1%, so the entire mean lives in the loss tail \u2014 one extra vol shock in-sample flips it negative. Consequences: (i) **+2.1% over n=96 is likely within noise** and needs a significance / DSR test before quoting; (ii) the **76% hit rate is a structurally-expected, uninformative headline** \u2014 a capped-win/uncapped-loss seller wins most trades whether or not it has edge, so hit rate must not be the advertised metric. Requires significance + cost/vol-shock stress after the causal re-run.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL NOTES (not defects)\n\n- `rich` is all-boolean despite `iv_ma` edge-NaNs (`iv > NaN` \u2192 False); `~rich.shift(1).fillna(False)` and `iv.index[entry_days]` behave correctly. No hidden bug. `&`/`~` precedence is correct.\n- The `decay50` / 15-session exit reading forward prices *as the trade plays out* is not lookahead \u2014 exits may see the path they live through.\n- `if t is not None` skips untraded-leg entries; for NIFTY index ATM straddles liquidity is ~always present, so selection-bias risk is negligible. Worth a one-line confirmation, not a defect.\n- Index straddle \u21d2 no single-name earnings/event-gate lookahead applies. Task pre-empts the usual firm landmines (IST dates, expiry-day settle, verified entry prices); not manufactured here.\n- \"net\" P&L is assumed to include costs inside `simulate_straddle`; unverifiable from the snippet, so not claimed as a defect.\n\n---\n\n## Claimed material defects (final list)\n\n1. **Centered rolling mean `iv.rolling(11, center=True).mean()` uses 5 future sessions of IV in the entry signal \u2014 a lookahead that preferentially selects local IV peaks (falling IV = winning short-vol setups) and suppresses rising-IV losers, inflating both the +2.1%/trade mean and the 76% hit rate. Fix: causal MA (`iv.rolling(11).mean()` or `iv.rolling(10).mean().shift(1)`), then re-run and pass a lookahead audit with lag \u2265 the window depth (\u22655 sessions).** Direction of bias is certain; the claim it explains the *entire* result is [INFERENCE], magnitude unproven (5-session peek vs 15-session hold).\n\n2. **(Material-adjacent, robustness/significance)** Capped +50% wins vs uncapped losses over only 96 trades put the +2.1% mean entirely in the loss tail \u2014 likely statistically insignificant (needs a significance/DSR test), and the 76% hit rate is a structurally-expected, uninformative headline that must not be advertised. Applies even after the causal fix.", "ids": ["O11a", "O11b", "O11c", "O11d"]}, {"tid": "T12", "key": "## T12 \u2014 data-landmine \u2014 expiry-day option SETTLE_PR (CRITICAL)\n**Defect.** On the EXPIRY DAY, an option row's SETTLE_PR in the NSE F&O bhavcopy is\nthe UNDERLYING's final settlement level (e.g. 19,253.80), not the option's value.\n`debit = ce_x + pe_x` buys back both legs at ~2x the index level: tens of thousands\nof fake loss points per week. The task's own summary shows the tell \u2014 four-digit\nlosses concentrated ON expiry dates with tiny index moves (\"pin risk\" cannot produce\n-23,912 pts on an ATM straddle). Firm incident 2026-07-11: -15,428-pt fake losses.\n**Line.** reading `.SETTLE_PR` from the expiry-day rows as the exit price.\n**Fix.** Never read expiry-day option SETTLE_PR; cash-settle each leg at intrinsic\ncomputed from the underlying's final settlement price.\n**Accept.** \"Expiry-day SETTLE_PR is the underlying settlement level, not the option\nprice\"; \"exit debit ~= 2x index level \u2014 settle at intrinsic from the underlying\ninstead\"; \"the -20k point 'pin risk' weeks are the field-semantics bug, not risk\".\n**Secondary.** Entry CONTRACTS>0 gate and prior-close strike selection are correct;\nCLOSE prints at entry acceptable.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O12a -----\n# T12 \u2014 Consolidated verdict (arm C2): weekly short straddle on F&O bhavcopy\n\n**Final arbiter over two reviews (base reviewer + red team).**\n**Verdict: the reported result is FAKE. The submission has exactly ONE material defect.**\nThe author's own reading \u2014 \"expiry-day pin risk, should I add a stop?\" \u2014 is wrong: a stop\ncannot fix a loss that never happened.\n\n---\n\n## The material defect (CONFIRMED \u2014 makes the result fake)\n\n**Line:** the expiry exit\n```python\nce_x = fo.row(week.expiry, week.expiry, k, \"CE\").SETTLE_PR\npe_x = fo.row(week.expiry, week.expiry, k, \"PE\").SETTLE_PR\ndebit = ce_x + pe_x\n```\n\n**Mechanism.** On the expiry-day bhavcopy row, the `SETTLE_PR` field of an OPTION contract\nis not the option's settlement premium \u2014 it carries the **underlying's final settlement\nlevel** (the index level used for cash settlement). This is the firm's documented data\nlandmine #9 (expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR` = underlying settlement; it bit us in A4 on\n2026-07-11 with \u221215,428-pt fake losses). So `debit` receives an **index-scale** value\n(tens of thousands of points) instead of an option premium (tens of points), and\n`pnl = credit \u2212 debit \u2212 costs` becomes a four-to-five-digit fabricated LOSS.\n\n**The reported evidence is the fingerprint of this bug, not pin risk.** A hold-to-expiry\nshort ATM straddle on a week the index barely moves settles at intrinsic \u2248 |S\u2212K| \u2248 0 and\nyou KEEP essentially the full credit \u2014 that is the strategy's **best** outcome (a small\nprofit). To lose ~20,000 points the index would have to move ~20,000 points (~100%), which\nnever happened. A catastrophic loss on a `|index move| < 0.4%` week is physically\nimpossible from the position and can only be a valuation artifact in the exit mark.\n\n**The author's \"add a stop\" question is therefore misconceived.** There is no adverse move\nto stop out of; the position never lost money. A stop would (a) paper over a data bug and\n(b) be untriggerable in the sim anyway, since the fake loss only materialises at the final\nexpiry mark.\n\n**Correct fix.** At expiry a cash-settled option pays intrinsic against the underlying's\nfinal settlement S:\n```\ndebit = max(S \u2212 K, 0) + max(K \u2212 S, 0) = |S \u2212 K|\n```\nwhere **S is sourced from an INDEPENDENT cash-index final-settlement series** \u2014 never read\nor summed from the option `SETTLE_PR` row. After this fix, `debit` collapses to ~0 on flat\nweeks, the \"worst 5\" evaporate, and every summary figure (avg \u2212118.3, worst-week list, the\n\"64% hit yet \u2212118 avg\" juxtaposition) must be recomputed; real large losses will appear\nonly on genuine big-move weeks.\n\n---\n\n## Adjudication where the two reviews diverge\n\nThe red team's headline agrees with the base reviewer (the single defect above). Its\nrefutations of the base review's *mechanism and fix* are largely correct and survive:\n\n1. **Scope: the base review's \"every expiry exit reads the underlying\" is refuted by the\n   report's own stats \u2014 the contamination is SELECTIVE, not universal.**\n   If all 261 exits read the underlying, every week would be a five-digit loss \u2192 hit rate\n   \u2248 0% and avg \u2248 \u221220,000 pts. But the report says **64% hit, \u2212118.3 avg**. Arithmetic:\n   total book P&L = \u2212118.3 \u00d7 261 \u2248 **\u221230.9k pts**, yet the worst-5 alone are \u2248 87k\u2013120k pts\n   of losses \u2014 so the other ~256 weeks must net strongly **positive** (~+55k to +89k). That\n   is a mostly-profitable short-straddle book dragged negative by a **handful (~5\u201310)** of\n   contaminated weeks, not universal corruption. **Consequence for the author:** before the\n   fix and before quoting 64%, reproduce *which specific weeks* break and inspect the actual\n   field content on those rows. (If the bug truly were universal, then the 64%/\u2212118.3\n   figures cannot have come from this code and are themselves fabricated \u2014 either reading\n   invalidates the summary.) This does not change the verdict; it corrects the base review's\n   stated mechanism.\n\n2. **Fix safety: the base review's parenthetical shortcut is UNSAFE \u2014 strike it.**\n   The base review suggested \"at minimum convert the expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR` to\n   intrinsic per leg.\" That presumes `SETTLE_PR = S` on *every* expiry row, which point 1\n   disproves. On the ~250 normal weeks `SETTLE_PR` is the option's real (small) settlement,\n   so `|SETTLE_PR \u2212 K|` \u2248 index level would **blow up ~250 currently-correct weeks and\n   manufacture a NEW fake result**. The only safe fix is S from an independent cash-index\n   series, ignoring the option `SETTLE_PR` field entirely on expiry day.\n\n3. **\"Worst-5 all land ON expiry dates\" is a vacuous tell.** In a hold-to-expiry design\n   every exit \u2014 best and worst alike \u2014 lands on its expiry date by construction, so this\n   corroborates nothing. The only decisive tell is the five-digit loss on a `<0.4%` week.\n   Non-material to the verdict; the base review's evidence was padded here.\n\n4. **Magnitude is ~1\u00d7 the index, not 2\u00d7.** The cited losses track ~1\u00d7 spot (2021-04-08\n   \u22480.97\u00d7, 2024-02-29 \u22481.00\u00d7, 2023-08-31 \u22481.24\u00d7), not the 2\u00d7 the base review's\n   \"both CE and PE rows = S\" story implies \u2014 consistent with only ONE leg contaminated per\n   broken week. Minor, but another reason the base review's tidy mechanism is imprecise.\n\n**Plausible cause of the selectivity (inference \u2014 author to verify):** two of the three\nblow-up dates (2023-08-31, 2024-02-29) are month-end MONTHLY expiries (last Thursday) where\nweekly and monthly contracts share strike/type on the same date; the contamination may be a\nrow-collision in `fo.row(date, expiry, k, type)` when multiple expiries coexist, or a\nmonth-end schema quirk. (2021-04-08 is a weekly, so this is a hint, not proof.)\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (agreed by both reviews \u2014 do NOT change the verdict)\n\n- **Entry mark `credit = ce.CLOSE + pe.CLOSE`:** entry day is a normal (non-expiry) session,\n  so `CLOSE` is a clean last-trade price and the `CONTRACTS>0` gate mitigates illiquid\n  strikes. Selling at close is a mark, not a guaranteed fill \u2014 worth stating, immaterial.\n- **Off-ATM entry:** strike is set off the prior close but entered a full session later, so\n  the \"ATM\" straddle may be modestly off-ATM. Modeling imprecision, immaterial.\n- **Liquidity gate is entry-only** (`CONTRACTS==0` checked at entry, not exit): immaterial\n  once the exit is switched to intrinsic-from-index, which does not depend on the option row\n  trading.\n- **`costs_pts(4)`** for a two-leg round trip is optimistic and ignores the expiry-STT trap\n  on ITM settlement, but it is dwarfed by the exit bug \u2014 parameter choice, immaterial.\n- **261 of ~313 possible weekly expiries (~17% dropped):** plausibly `CONTRACTS==0` /\n  holiday skips, but 17% is high enough that IF drops cluster in illiquid/high-vol regimes\n  they could flatter the hit rate. Verify the drop reasons before treating 64% as clean.\n  Non-material to the exit bug.\n- Strike selection (ATM off prior close, gated on entry-day `CONTRACTS>0`) has **no\n  lookahead** \u2014 that part is fine.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list \u2014 material defects in the submission\n\n1. **Expiry exit reads/sums the expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR`** (`debit = ce_x + pe_x`),\n   which on the affected weeks carries the **underlying's final settlement level (~1\u00d7 the\n   index), not the option premium** (firm data landmine #9). This fabricates\n   four-to-five-digit \"losses\" on flat-index weeks, corrupting the avg (\u2212118.3), the worst-5\n   list, and the \"64% hit / \u2212118 avg\" juxtaposition. It alone makes the result fake \u2014 and it\n   is a valuation artifact, not pin risk, so a stop cannot fix it.\n   **Fix:** cash-settle each leg at intrinsic `debit = |S \u2212 K|`, with S sourced from an\n   **independent cash-index settlement series**; never read or convert the expiry-day option\n   `SETTLE_PR`. Additionally, reproduce exactly which weeks break (the stats show the bug is\n   selective, ~5\u201310 weeks, not universal) and re-derive every summary figure after the fix.\n\n(No other material defect exists in the submission. The reviews' remaining disagreements\nconcern the correct mechanism and the safe remediation, adjudicated in the section above:\nthe base review reaches the right verdict but on an over-broad mechanism and with a\nfallback fix that would itself corrupt the good weeks; the red team's corrections on those\ntwo points are upheld.)\n\n----- ANSWER O12b -----\n# T12 Review \u2014 Weekly short straddle on F&O bhavcopy (hold-to-expiry)\n\n## Verdict\nThe backtest is **fake on the exit leg**. The four-digit (\"point\") losses the author\nblames on \"expiry-day pin risk\" are not market losses \u2014 they are a data-decoding bug in\nhow the expiry-day option price is read. The headline numbers (261 weeks, 64% hit,\navg \u2212118.3 pts) are corrupted and cannot be trusted. Adding a stop (the author's question)\nwould be the wrong fix: it would fit a stop rule to a data artifact.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT \u2014 exit reads the expiry-day option-row SETTLE_PR\n\n**Exact lines:**\n```python\nce_x = fo.row(week.expiry, week.expiry, k, \"CE\").SETTLE_PR\npe_x = fo.row(week.expiry, week.expiry, k, \"PE\").SETTLE_PR\ndebit = ce_x + pe_x\n```\nand the comment justifying it:\n`# SETTLE_PR is the official settlement and avoids stale last-trade CLOSE prints`.\n\n**Mechanism of corruption.** In the NSE F&O bhavcopy, on the **expiry-day row** the option\ncontract's `SETTLE_PR` field is **not the option's settlement price \u2014 it carries the\nUNDERLYING's final settlement level** (the ~30-min VWAP settlement of the index used for\ncash settlement). It is a five-figure index level (NIFTY \u2248 14,000\u201324,000 over 2020\u20132025),\nnot an option premium of a few hundred points. So `debit = ce_x + pe_x` injects a fake\ndebit on the order of the index level on the weeks that hit this field, turning what should\nbe a near-max **win** (ATM straddle held into a flat-tape expiry \u2192 both legs expire near\nzero) into a catastrophic \"loss.\" This is exactly the reversed signature the author\nobserved: the **worst** weeks are the ones where the index **barely moved** \u2014 impossible\nfor a real short straddle, definitive for a settle-decode bug. (This is the firm's\ndocumented landmine #9: expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR` = underlying settlement, which\nproduced \u221215,428-pt fake losses in A4 on 2026-07-11.)\n\n**Evidence in the author's own worst-5 list \u2014 the loss magnitudes equal the index level:**\n- `2024-02-29: \u221221,880 pts` \u2014 NIFTY settled \u2248 21,980 at end-Feb 2024. Loss \u2248 index level minus a small credit. Match.\n- `2021-04-08: \u221214,466 pts` \u2014 NIFTY \u2248 14,800 in early-April 2021. Loss \u2248 index level minus credit. Match.\n- `2023-08-31: \u221223,912 pts` \u2014 same family (leg settle contaminated by the settlement level; exact value depends on the strike/leg that picked up the underlying figure).\n\nA genuine ATM weekly straddle's worst realistic outcome is bounded by the move\n(debit = |S_settle \u2212 K|); on a <0.4% move week that is essentially **zero**, i.e. the\nweek should print approximately +full credit. Seeing \u221214k to \u221224k there is arithmetically\nonly explainable by the exit price being the index level.\n\n**Why the author's attribution is wrong.** \"Expiry-day pin risk\" for a short straddle\nmeans the underlying pins *at your strike*, which is the **best** case (max profit), not a\nloss. Pin risk cannot produce a loss larger than the actual move. The losses are a decoding\nartifact, full stop.\n\n**Correct fix.** NIFTY index options are **cash-settled at intrinsic** against the\nunderlying's final settlement price `S_settle`. Do not read the option row's `SETTLE_PR`\nas a premium and do not sum both legs. Compute:\n```python\nS = underlying_final_settlement(week.expiry)   # this IS what that field actually gives you\ndebit = max(0.0, S - k) + max(0.0, k - S)      # = |S - k| for a straddle at strike k\n```\ni.e. exactly one leg is ITM by |S \u2212 k| and the other is 0. Use the underlying's settlement\nseries (or the same field, but interpreted as the underlying level, applied once). With\nthis fix the flat-move weeks flip to the intended near-max wins, and the summary stats must\nbe fully recomputed \u2014 the current \u2212118.3 avg / 64% hit are meaningless.\n\n**Answer to the author's stop question:** No. Do not add a stop. The losses are not real;\na stop tuned to remove them would be fitting to a data bug and would introduce spurious\nstop-outs into the corrected P&L. Re-run only after the intrinsic-settlement fix, then\nre-ask the risk question against clean numbers.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (explicitly NOT the cause of the fake losses)\n\n1. **Entry fills at CLOSE (minor optimism).** `credit = ce.CLOSE + pe.CLOSE` sells at the\n   entry-day last-trade close with no half-spread. On non-expiry days the true daily\n   settlement (`SETTLE_PR`) is the cleaner mark, or model a bid-ask. Small effect; does not\n   affect the diagnosis.\n2. **Liquidity gate is entry-only.** `CONTRACTS==0` is checked at entry but not that the\n   chosen `week.expiry` is the *liquid near weekly* (landmine: far weeklies are listed with\n   model settles at CONTRACTS=0). For a hold-to-expiry near-weekly this is likely fine, but\n   worth asserting.\n3. **`costs_pts(4)` is a flat assumption** \u2014 verify against COST_STANDARDS. It is trivial\n   relative to the bug and is not the source of the losses.\n4. **Summary internal consistency (corroborates the diagnosis).** avg \u2212118.3 over 261 weeks\n   coexisting with 5 weeks at \u221215k\u2026\u221224k is only possible because the bad settle bites\n   specifically the near-ATM / pinned strikes on low-move weeks (the rest print positive) \u2014\n   which is itself consistent with the settle-contamination mechanism, not with a real edge.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n1. **Exit price bug:** exit debit is read from the expiry-day option-row `SETTLE_PR`, which\n   in NSE F&O bhavcopy is the **underlying's final settlement level, not the option price**.\n   This injects a ~index-level fake debit on the pinned/near-ATM legs, producing the\n   impossible five-figure losses on flat-move expiry weeks and corrupting the entire result.\n   Fix: cash-settle at intrinsic `|S_settle \u2212 k|` from the underlying settlement; recompute\n   all statistics; do not add a stop.\n\n----- ANSWER O12c -----\n# T12 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT \u2014 weekly short straddle on F&O bhavcopy\nReviewer of record: Rajan Mehta (CIO, E-001). Inputs: Quant Review + Red-Team Review (Nikhil Bose, E-014).\nEvidence: the two reviews + the task text only. No files read / no code run (task constraint).\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT \u2014 result is FAKE (data-field artifact). Not eligible for register, paper, or ANY directional claim.\n\nThree-line rationale:\n1. The exit prices the expiry-day OPTION row's `SETTLE_PR` as the option premium; that field carries an underlying-settlement-scale number (firm landmine #9), so index-level magnitudes are injected into `debit` and manufacture the four/five-digit \"losses.\" [INFERENCE from mechanism + landmine]\n2. The result is physically impossible on its own terms: a short ATM straddle held to expiry has `pnl = credit \u2212 |S_T \u2212 K| \u2212 costs`; on a week with `|index move| < 0.4%` (author's own output) the loss is bounded to ~tens of points, never \u221223,912. [DATA: the \u221223,912 / \u221214,466 / \u221221,880 figures are the author's output] [INFERENCE: the bound]\n3. Both reviewers reach FAKE; I uphold FAKE. The corrected profitability is UNKNOWN and no one may state a direction until a clean re-run clears the placebo battery.\n\n---\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT\n- Worst single-day / worst-week (as reported): fictional. The \u221220k-pt prints are the SPOT LEVEL leaking through `SETTLE_PR`, not a market loss. A real short ATM NIFTY straddle's terminal loss on a flat expiry is bounded by `|S_T \u2212 K|` (tens of points).\n- The genuine tail this book carries \u2014 once priced correctly \u2014 is the ordinary short-gamma / short-vol left tail on a GAP week (index moves 3-6% intra-week: e.g. Mar-2020, Jun-2024 election day). That tail is real, is NOT in this backtest at all, and is exactly what the placebo/stress battery must surface before any sizing. Firm precedent: S-01 ran ~71% short-vol beta; short-vol fat tails are the house's most-repeated blow-up.\n- Correlated-blowup scenario: a weekly ATM short-straddle sleeve is one concentrated short-vol factor; stacking it on any existing short-vol exposure violates diversification-as-only-tail-defense. Not sizable until orthogonality to the book is measured.\n\n## SIZING RULING\nZERO. No size, no paper entry, no register row. The number is an artifact; there is nothing to size. Returns to the R&D bench for a corrected re-run.\n\n---\n\n## DEFECTS THAT SURVIVE (with mechanism + fix)\n\n### 1. PRIMARY \u2014 FAKE. Exit reads expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR` as the option premium.\nLines: `ce_x = fo.row(week.expiry, week.expiry, k, \"CE\").SETTLE_PR` / `pe_x = ... \"PE\").SETTLE_PR` / `debit = ce_x + pe_x`.\nMechanism: on the NIFTY expiry-day bhavcopy row the option `SETTLE_PR` does not carry the option's settlement premium; it carries an underlying-settlement-scale value. Injecting that into `debit` produces index-scale \"losses.\" This is landmine #9 verbatim (same class as the \u221215,428-pt fake losses in A4, 2026-07-11). The author's own comment (\"SETTLE_PR is the official settlement and avoids stale last-trade CLOSE prints\") is precisely the wrong instinct: it is an official settlement \u2014 of the UNDERLYING, not the option.\nDecisive proof: the physical bound (rationale line 2), which holds regardless of the exact field contents. A \u221223,912-pt loss on a <0.4% expiry would require the index to travel ~24,000 pts; it did not.\nFix: cash-settle at intrinsic against the underlying's PIT final settlement: `debit = max(0, S_settle \u2212 K) + max(0, K \u2212 S_settle)`, `S_settle` from the index feed (PIT). Never the option row `SETTLE_PR`; never expiry-day option `CLOSE` (possibly-hours-stale last trade).\n\n### 2. MATERIAL \u2014 no `CONTRACTS > 0` / intrinsic-fallback gate on the exit leg.\nEntry is gated on `CONTRACTS`; the expiry-day exit row is pulled unconditionally. Far/illiquid expiry rows are LISTED-but-untraded with model settles (esp. 2020), so even a corrected price read can ingest a model number. Fix: gate the exit leg on `CONTRACTS > 0`, or cash-settle at intrinsic per #1 \u2014 the intrinsic fix sidesteps this entirely.\n\n### 3. MATERIAL (diagnosis + proposed action). \"Pin-risk \u2192 add a stop\" is wrong and must NOT be done.\nThe losses are a data-field bug, not pin risk. Pin risk on a short ATM straddle is bounded by `|S_T \u2212 K|` (tens of points on a <0.4% week), never 20,000. A stop cannot fix a field bug; adding one fits a parameter to a phantom, corrupts the register, and masks the real (gap-week) tail. Correct action: fix the exit price, then re-derive from scratch.\n\n### 4. MATERIAL for the re-run \u2014 cost realism.\nHold-to-expiry guarantees one leg settles ITM \u2192 exercise STT (0.125% on the intrinsic/settlement value), materially above STT on a normal option sell, and it bites this hold-to-expiry design specifically. A flat `costs_pts(4)` almost certainly understates it. Fix: model from approved COST_STANDARDS (not a hard-coded 4), including exercise STT, and stress at 2\u00d7 before any post-fix verdict.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL NOTES (clearly labelled \u2014 do not treat as result-invalidating)\n- Coverage gap (261 vs ~312 \u2248 84%): a COMPLETENESS note, not selection bias. The prior reviewer's attribution to entry `CONTRACTS == 0` is WRONG \u2014 NIFTY ATM weekly options are the most liquid contracts in India; `CONTRACTS == 0` on the ATM strike at entry is near-impossible. The ~51 missing weeks are far more likely calendar/holiday-shortened weeks, entry-day computation misses, or bhavcopy coverage gaps. Dropping weeks you genuinely could not trade is correct, not bias, unless the drops correlate with outcomes (negligible for NIFTY ATM). Action: log skipped weeks + per-year coverage before any yearly claim. [OPINION]\n- \"Worst weeks all land ON expiry dates\" is VACUOUS: this engine books once per week at expiry, so all 261 weeks land on expiry by construction. The only informative half is `|move| < 0.4%`, which is what proves impossibility. A tell that the author misread his own engine. [INFERENCE]\n- Entry assume-fill at the entry-day `CLOSE` print is fill-realism optimism \u2014 flag for TCA once P&L is real. [OPINION]\n- Done correctly, leave as-is: PIT strike selection off the prior close (`asof(entry_day \u2212 one_bday)`) is lookahead-clean; one `book()` per week at expiry avoids return-spreading / fake-low-variance Sharpe. Neither reviewer invented a defect here and I concur. [INFERENCE]\n\n---\n\n## CIO RULINGS ON THE TWO REVIEWS (recorded)\n- STRIKE the Quant Review's \"fixing the field very likely FLIPS the strategy from losing to winning\u2026 other ~256 weeks sum to roughly +270 pts/week.\" This is an unearned positive prior and the cardinal error I firewall against (Lessons Learned 2026-07-04: paper/phantom results must NEVER anchor future sizing). EVERY week used the buggy exit field, not only the worst 5 \u2014 you cannot back out a clean +270/week from contaminated weeks. Corrected P&L is UNKNOWN: it may be positive, flat, or negative. Red Team is upheld.\n- DOWNGRADE the Quant Review's \"loss = index level\" smoking gun. It is internally inconsistent: under the stated `debit = ce_x + pe_x` mechanism the loss should be ~2\u00d7 the index (\u2248 \u221243,800 for Feb-2024) yet the print is ~1\u00d7 (\u221221,880), and 2023-08-31's \u221223,912 EXCEEDS that day's index level. The exact field contents are UNKNOWN from the text (a raw-row pull \u2014 barred here \u2014 would pin them down). The sound basis for FAKE is the physical bound, NOT level-matching. Red Team is upheld.\n- Red Team's constraint honesty (text-only, no file read) is accepted; the verdict rests on a bound that needs no file read.\n\n## RE-RUN GATE (kill criteria for the artifact; entry criteria for a legitimate v2)\nBefore this may be re-quoted or re-reviewed, ALL of:\n1. Exit priced as intrinsic vs the underlying's PIT final settlement (never option `SETTLE_PR`/`CLOSE`).\n2. Exit leg gated on `CONTRACTS > 0` / cash-settled so untraded model-settle rows cannot enter.\n3. Costs from approved COST_STANDARDS incl. exercise STT, at 2\u00d7 stress.\n4. Skipped-week log + per-year coverage table.\nThen the placebo battery \u2014 entry lag+1 (must degrade), 2\u00d7 costs, bootstrap 5th-percentile > 0, and a random-entry-week benchmark to prove the edge is TIMING, not just harvesting weekly theta / short-vol beta. Deflated Sharpe + PBO reported. Only after all of that may anyone state a direction.\n\nReview date: on resubmission of the corrected engine. No sooner.\n\n## DISSENTS\nNone on the verdict \u2014 both reviewers concur FAKE, and I concur. The three review-level disagreements (level-matching rigor, the \"probably profitable\" prior, the coverage-gap cause) are resolved in the Red Team's favour and recorded above; they do not alter the FAKE/REJECT outcome.\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED MATERIAL DEFECTS\n1. Exit reads expiry-day option-row `SETTLE_PR` as the option premium; that field carries an underlying-settlement-scale value (landmine #9), fabricating the index-scale \"pin\" losses and the entire \u2212118.3 avg. Proven by physical impossibility (\u221223,912 pts on a <0.4% expiry is unattainable for a short ATM straddle). Fix: cash-settle at intrinsic vs the underlying's PIT final settlement. (FAKE \u2014 primary)\n2. No `CONTRACTS > 0` / intrinsic-fallback gate on the unconditionally-pulled expiry-day exit leg \u2014 admits untraded/model-settled rows (esp. 2020); resolved by the intrinsic-settlement fix.\n3. Diagnosis/action defect: the \"expiry pin-risk\" attribution is wrong and the proposed stop must NOT be added \u2014 it would fit a parameter to a data-bug phantom and corrupt the register.\n4. Cost realism: hold-to-expiry forces one ITM leg \u2192 exercise STT (0.125% on intrinsic settlement) that a flat `costs_pts(4)` understates; must be modelled from COST_STANDARDS at 2\u00d7 on re-run.\n\n(Corrected profitability is UNKNOWN and no directional claim is permitted until the intrinsic-settlement re-run clears the placebo battery.)\n\n----- ANSWER O12d -----\n# T12 \u2014 Review: weekly short straddle on F&O bhavcopy (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: one fatal, result-corrupting defect. The reported metrics (hit 64%, avg\n-118.3 pts, the four-digit \"worst weeks\") are fake artifacts of a data bug, not real\nP&L. The strategy is being made to look WORSE than it is, and the author is about to\n\"fix\" a problem that does not exist.**\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 1 \u2014 exit reads the option's `SETTLE_PR` on the expiry-day row\n\n```python\n# exit at expiry: use the exchange's settlement field on the expiry-day row --\n# SETTLE_PR is the official settlement and avoids stale last-trade CLOSE prints\nce_x = fo.row(week.expiry, week.expiry, k, \"CE\").SETTLE_PR\npe_x = fo.row(week.expiry, week.expiry, k, \"PE\").SETTLE_PR\ndebit = ce_x + pe_x\n```\n\n**Mechanism.** In the NSE F&O bhavcopy, on an option's **expiry day** the `SETTLE_PR`\nfield of the option row does **not** carry the option's settlement price \u2014 it carries\nthe **underlying's final settlement level** (the index settlement value used for cash\nsettlement). This is a documented firm landmine (DATA LANDMINES #9): *\"F&O bhavcopy\nexpiry-day option SETTLE_PR = the UNDERLYING's final settlement level, NOT the option\nprice.\"* It is the exact incident that produced the \u221215,428-pt fake losses in A4 on\n2026-07-11.\n\nSo `debit = ce_x + pe_x` injects a spurious debit on the order of the **index level\n(~1\u00d7 the spot, ~15,000\u201324,000 pts over 2020\u20132025)** into a position whose true exit\nvalue is near zero.\n\n**Why this exactly reproduces the reported symptom.** For a short ATM straddle held to\nexpiry on a week where the index barely moves (|move| < 0.4%), the strike `k` finishes\n~ATM, so **both legs settle at ~0 intrinsic** and the true P&L \u2248 **+credit (a WIN of\n~100\u2013300 pts)**. The buggy engine instead computes `credit \u2212 (\u2248index level) \u2212 costs`,\ni.e. a **loss \u2248 the index level**. That is precisely the reported pattern:\n\n- worst weeks all land **ON expiry dates** \u2192 because every exit is read from the\n  expiry-day row, which is the only day the `SETTLE_PR`-is-underlying quirk fires;\n- worst weeks all have **|index move| < 0.4%** \u2192 because those are exactly the weeks\n  that *should* be the strategy's biggest winners; the bug flips winners into\n  catastrophic losers.\n- the loss magnitudes line up with the index: 2024-02-29 \u2248 \u221221,880 pts sits right on\n  the ~22,000 NIFTY settlement of that expiry; likewise 2021-04-08 (~14.9k) and\n  2023-08-31 (~19\u201324k range). Loss \u2248 underlying level is the fingerprint of this bug.\n\n**Consequence for every reported number.** Because the corruption turns the strategy's\nbest weeks into its worst weeks:\n- **avg -118.3 pts is invalid** \u2014 it is dragged negative entirely by imputed index-level\n  debits;\n- **hit rate 64% is understated** \u2014 the flipped weeks are counted as losses when they\n  are wins, so the true hit rate is higher;\n- **the \"worst 5 weeks\" are pure artifacts** and vanish after the fix.\n\n**Fix.** Never read an expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR`. Cash-settle each leg at intrinsic\ncomputed from the **underlying's** final settlement `S`:\n\n```python\nS = underlying_settlement(week.expiry)      # index final settlement (last half-hr avg)\nce_x = max(S - k, 0.0)                       # call intrinsic\npe_x = max(k - S, 0.0)                       # put intrinsic\ndebit = ce_x + pe_x\nbook(week, pnl=credit - debit - costs_pts(4))\n```\n\nAfter this, low-move weeks return \u2248 +credit as they should, and the entire summary\n(hit rate, avg, worst-5, and the go/no-go read on the strategy) must be **recomputed\nfrom scratch**. Do not quote any current number.\n\n---\n\n## The author's diagnosis is wrong \u2014 do NOT add a stop\n\nThe write-up attributes the four-digit losses to \"expiry-day pin risk\" and asks whether\nto add a stop. Both are misreads of a data artifact:\n\n- There is **no real loss to stop** \u2014 the losing weeks are winning weeks with an\n  index-level number injected by a bad field read. Adding a stop would \"fix\" phantom\n  losses and, worse, would start cutting *real* winners early once the data bug is\n  corrected.\n- This engine holds to expiry with cash settlement and takes **no intra-position\n  marks**, so a stop is not even expressible in it as written.\n\nFix the `SETTLE_PR` read first; the \"pin-risk\" pattern disappears. Re-open the\nstop/hedge question (if at all) only against the corrected, honest P&L series \u2014 that is\na tail-sizing / hedge-overlay decision for Kabir/CIO, not a response to this artifact.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (labelled \u2014 not defects that fake the result)\n\n- **N1 (fill realism).** Entry `credit = ce.CLOSE + pe.CLOSE` books the sell at the\n  entry-day last-trade CLOSE. That is a reasonable MOC-style assumption and is guarded\n  by the `CONTRACTS>0` entry check, but CLOSE is a last-trade print and can be stale on\n  the far leg; a small entry-slippage haircut (per COST_STANDARDS) would be more\n  honest. Ironically the exit comment invokes \"avoids stale last-trade CLOSE\" as the\n  reason for using `SETTLE_PR` \u2014 the reasoning is inverted (that choice is what caused\n  the bug).\n- **N2 (costs).** `costs_pts(4)` for a two-leg sell + cash-settled exit is an\n  assumption; verify against the APPROVED `06_TRADING_DESK/COST_STANDARDS.md` before\n  quoting net results. Not a fake-result issue.\n- **N3 (silent skips).** `if ... CONTRACTS == 0: continue` correctly gates entry\n  liquidity (right per landmine #9) but silently drops weeks, so \"261 weeks\" is a\n  post-filter count; report how many cycles were skipped so the sample size is honest.\n- **N4 (strike selection).** `ref = index_close.asof(entry_day - one_bday)` picks the\n  ATM strike off the *prior* close \u2014 no lookahead, this is fine (mildly conservative).\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n\n1. **Exit reads the expiry-day option `SETTLE_PR`, which is the UNDERLYING's final\n   settlement level, not the option price** (DATA LANDMINE #9). This injects an\n   ~index-level phantom debit into every hold-to-expiry exit, turning the strategy's\n   low-move winning weeks into four-digit fake losses and invalidating the reported hit\n   rate (64%), average (-118.3 pts) and worst-5 list. Fix: cash-settle each leg at\n   intrinsic from the underlying settlement `S` \u2014 `CE=max(S-k,0)`, `PE=max(k-S,0)` \u2014 and\n   recompute the entire summary.\n\n(That is the only material defect. The author's \"pin-risk / add a stop\" conclusion is a\nconsequence of it and is also wrong; items N1\u2013N4 are non-material.)", "ids": ["O12a", "O12b", "O12c", "O12d"]}, {"tid": "T13", "key": "## T13 \u2014 lookahead \u2014 universe membership from today's list, T5 (CRITICAL)\n**Defect.** `nifty500_constituents.csv` is the index provider's CURRENT (2026-07)\nlist, applied to 2013-2025. Membership in today's index is an outcome: winners grew\ninto it, losers fell out or delisted. Screening the 2013 cross-section through\ntoday's list pre-selects winners regardless of the price panel being\nsurvivorship-complete \u2014 the momentum CAGR is contaminated before any signal quality\nis measured.\n**Line.** the `read_csv(...)[\"Symbol\"].tolist()` universe + the filtering of the\npanel columns by it.\n**Fix.** Point-in-time membership: the 42-snapshot constituent history, as-of each\nrebalance date (Mar/Sep snapshot on or before).\n**Accept.** \"Universe is today's constituents applied historically \u2014 survivorship /\nindex-inclusion lookahead\"; \"membership must be as-of date from the PIT snapshot\nfile\"; \"the panel is survivorship-complete but the universe filter re-introduces\nthe bias\".\n**Secondary.** 12-1 momentum construction (`shift(21).pct_change(231)`) is causal\nand correct; fills/costs are handled.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O13a -----\n# T13 \u2014 Final consolidated verdict: 12-1 momentum on the NIFTY-500\n\nRole: final arbiter over Reviewer + Red Team. I kept only defects that survive\ncross-checking against the pasted code, adopted the Red Team's severity\ncorrection on the `pct_change` finding, and confirmed the Red Team's new\nIndexError finding independently.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 1 \u2014 Current-snapshot universe filter (survivorship + incumbency look-ahead). Dominant result-faker.\n\nLines:\n```\nuniverse = pd.read_csv(\"nifty500_constituents.csv\")[\"Symbol\"].tolist()\n# ... 2026-07 refresh, 500 symbols\nclose = close[[c for c in close.columns if c in universe]]\n```\nThe panel is deliberately survivorship-complete (\"incl. delisted names\"); the\nnext line discards that by intersecting the entire 2013\u20132025 history with a\nsingle **current (2026-07)** membership snapshot. Both directions favor the\nstrategy:\n\n- **Delisted / removed names silently dropped for the whole history** \u2192 classic\n  survivorship inflation. Every name that went bankrupt, was delisted, or fell\n  out of the index before the 2026 refresh never appears in the test. This also\n  removes the momentum-crash tail (a top-momentum name that then collapses and\n  exits), which flatters maxDD.\n- **Incumbency look-ahead** \u2192 a name admitted to the index later (because it ran\n  up) is in the 2026 list and is back-projected into 2013\u20132021, so the early\n  cross-section is picked from a set pre-selected for *future* index inclusion.\n  (Nuance, not a refutation: back-projection bites only for names that already\n  had early price history; a true post-2013 IPO has no early rows to project\n  into. The effect is still one-directional and large.)\n\nMechanism: biases the cross-section toward ex-post winners across the entire\nhistory \u2014 more than enough to manufacture the 24.8% vs 12.1% gap. The reported\nresult is not real.\n\nFix: point-in-time membership at each rebalance from the firm's\n`NIFTY500_TICKER_2005_2025_Final.xlsx` (42 PIT snapshots, per DATA LANDMINE #6).\nAt each rebalance `me`, restrict candidates to names that were index members on\nthat date; keep a held name through the rest of the month even if it later\ndelists, booking its terminal return; never filter the whole panel by one\nforward-dated list.\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 2 \u2014 IndexError on the final rebalance; the snippet as written cannot have produced the printed numbers.\n\nLines:\n```\nmonth_ends = close.groupby(close.index.to_period(\"M\")).tail(1).index\n...\nnxt = close.index[close.index.searchsorted(me, side=\"right\")]\n```\n`month_ends` always includes the last trading day of the panel, i.e.\n`max(close.index)`. On the final iteration `me == max(close.index)`, so\n`searchsorted(me, side=\"right\")` returns `len(close.index)`, and\n`close.index[len(close.index)]` raises **IndexError: index out of bounds**. No\nguard exists.\n\nMechanism: the code as pasted does not run to completion. Therefore either (a)\nthe code that produced 24.8% is not exactly the code submitted (a\nreproducibility / authenticity gap \u2014 the reviewer is grading a different\nartifact than was run), or (b) a bounds check was silently dropped from the\npaste. This does not itself inflate the CAGR (Defect 1 does that), but it means\nthe printed number cannot be attributed to the submitted artifact.\n\nFix: bounds-guard the last rebalance, e.g.\n`pos = close.index.searchsorted(me, \"right\"); if pos >= len(close.index): continue`.\n\n---\n\n## CONTINGENT \u2014 `pct_change()` default `fill_method='pad'`. Real bug, but NOT a driver of the reported number as written.\n\nLines:\n```\nret = close.pct_change()\nmom = close.shift(21).pct_change(231)\n```\nThe Reviewer flagged this as co-material to the printed 24.8%. That ranking is\nwrong for the code **as written**, and the Red Team's downgrade is correct:\n\n1. Its headline mechanism (\"masks delisting losses / understates maxDD\") is\n   **inoperative here**: Defect 1 already restricts the universe to names still\n   in the index in 2026 \u2014 i.e. survivors \u2014 so no held name can terminally\n   delist mid-hold, and there is no delisting loss for the pad to hide.\n2. For temporary gaps (halts/suspensions in survivors), pad fills with 0% then\n   books the full move on the resumption day; the *cumulative* return over the\n   gap is preserved, only mistimed. No spurious net P&L inflation.\n3. Residual real effect: minor **rank distortion** in `mom` from stale prices\n   inside the 231-day window, on liquid large/mid-caps where interior gaps are\n   rare. Small; does not move the headline.\n\nSo this is a genuine latent correctness bug, not a source of the reported\n24.8%. It becomes material only **after** Defect 1 is fixed (a PIT universe\nreintroduces delisters whose losses the pad would then swallow). It must\ntherefore be fixed together with Defect 1.\n\nFix: `pct_change(fill_method=None)` for both `ret` and `mom`, plus explicit\ndelisting handling (book the terminal return on the last valid day, then drop\nthe name).\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled \u2014 none change the direction of the result)\n\n- **\"Next-open entry\" is not implemented.** P&L is close-to-close:\n  `weights.shift(1)*ret` with weights first set at `nxt`, so the first captured\n  return is `close[nxt]\u2192close[nxt+1]` \u2014 a full extra day of lag vs the signal at\n  `me`. This is *conservative/realistic*, it cannot inflate the result, but it\n  contradicts the write-up's stated methodology (documentation defect only).\n- **`apply_fill_rules()` cannot see what its comment claims.** It receives an\n  already-computed close-to-close P&L series (`weights.shift(1)*ret`) \u2014 no\n  opens, no volume, no circuit flags. The \"no-fill on circuit-locked / zero-\n  volume opens\" claim is unverifiable from this code and, structurally, cannot\n  be doing what the comment says unless the black box loads its own external\n  data.\n- **Intra-month free rebalancing (minor).** Weights are held at a constant\n  `1/50` within the month, so `port[t]=\u03a3 w[t-1]\u00b7ret[t]` is the return of a book\n  rebalanced *daily* to equal weight, while `turnover_costs(weights,...)` only\n  charges at the monthly rebalance. Intra-month rebalancing is thus modeled as\n  free (a few bp/month), and the \"monthly-rebalanced\" label is inexact.\n- **Cost-timing lag.** `turnover_costs(weights)` vs returns on `weights.shift(1)`\n  \u2014 off by one lag; immaterial to magnitude.\n- **Black-box functions** (`apply_fill_rules`, `turnover_costs`, `ann_return`,\n  `max_drawdown`) and the **benchmark 12.1%** are not shown; correctness of\n  fills, cost accrual, annualization, and like-for-like total-return comparison\n  cannot be confirmed here.\n- **The momentum formula is CORRECT** (noted so it is not mistaken for a\n  defect): `close.shift(21).pct_change(231)` at t = `close[t-21]/close[t-252]-1`,\n  the standard 12-month return skipping the most recent month. Signal timing\n  (data through t-21, applied from the session after `me`) is look-ahead-free.\n\n---\n\n## Claimed material defects\n1. **Current-snapshot universe filter.** `close[[c for c in close.columns if c in universe]]` with a 2026-07 constituents list applied to 2013\u20132025 reintroduces survivorship bias (drops delisted losers, flatters maxDD) and incumbency look-ahead (back-projects future index entrants). Dominant reason the 24.8% CAGR is fake. Fix: PIT membership per rebalance; hold-to-terminal-return on exit.\n2. **IndexError on the final rebalance.** `close.index[close.index.searchsorted(me,\"right\")]` is out of bounds when `me` is the last panel date (always true for the last month-end), so the snippet cannot run to completion \u2014 the printed CAGR did not come from exactly this code (reproducibility/authenticity gap). Fix: bounds-guard the final rebalance.\n\nContingent (fix required, but NOT a driver of the *reported* number, contra the Reviewer): `pct_change()` default `fill_method='pad'` \u2014 its delisting-mask effect is neutralized by defect #1's survivor-only universe and it does not fabricate net P&L across temporary gaps; it becomes material only once #1 is fixed. Set `fill_method=None` and handle terminal returns explicitly when #1 is corrected.\n\n----- ANSWER O13b -----\n# T13 Review \u2014 12-1 momentum on NIFTY-500 (arm A)\n\n## Verdict\nMaterial defect present. The headline 24.8% CAGR vs 12.1% index is inflated by a\nsurvivorship / look-ahead universe bug that silently undoes the \"survivorship-complete\npanel\" the submission is built around.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT\n\n### D1 \u2014 Universe filter uses a single CURRENT (2026-07) constituent list for the whole 2013-2025 backtest \u2192 survivorship + look-ahead selection bias\n\nOffending lines:\n```python\nuniverse = pd.read_csv(\"nifty500_constituents.csv\")[\"Symbol\"].tolist()\n# downloaded from the index provider's website, 2026-07 refresh, 500 symbols\nclose = close[[c for c in close.columns if c in universe]]\n```\n\n**Mechanism (why it corrupts the result):**\nThe price panel is correctly survivorship-complete (it includes delisted names). But\nthis filter intersects that panel with a *single point-in-time snapshot of index\nmembership as of July 2026*, and then applies that same 500-name set to **every**\nrebalance from 2013 onward. That re-imposes exactly the bias the union panel was meant\nto remove, via two distinct channels:\n\n1. **Survivorship of removed/delisted names.** Any stock that was a NIFTY-500 member\n   during 2013-2025 but was later dropped (poor performance, delisting, M&A, market-cap\n   decay) is NOT in the 2026 CSV, so it is filtered out of `close` and can never be\n   ranked or held. The excluded set is systematically the losers/failures. Momentum\n   selected from a survivors-only universe overstates returns and understates drawdown.\n   The delisted names sitting in the union panel are wasted \u2014 they get dropped one line\n   later.\n2. **Retroactive inclusion / look-ahead selection.** Names that entered the index late\n   (e.g. 2024-2025 IPOs or promotions) but ARE in the 2026 list get their full price\n   history back to 2013 fed into the momentum ranking, so the backtest can \"hold\" in\n   2015 a name it only knew would be index-worthy in 2026. You are ranking from\n   tomorrow's known winners.\n\nBoth channels push the same direction (inflation) and jointly can account for a large\nshare of the 24.8% vs 12.1% gap. This is the classic momentum-backtest killer.\n\n**Correct fix:**\nUse **point-in-time index membership** per rebalance date, not one current snapshot.\nPer the firm's data rules, membership must come from the 42 PIT snapshots in\n`NIFTY500_TICKER_2005_2025_Final.xlsx`. At each `me`, restrict the eligible universe to\nthe names that were actually in the NIFTY-500 as of (or just before) `me`, then rank\n`mom` within that as-of set. Keep delisted names investable up to their delisting date\n(and ensure the delisting/terminal return is captured, not silently dropped to NaN).\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL COMMENTS (labelled: not fake-making)\n\n- **Momentum formula is correct.** `mom = close.shift(21).pct_change(231)` evaluates at\n  `t` to `close[t-21]/close[t-252] - 1`, i.e. the 12-month return skipping the most\n  recent month. Correct 12-1 construction (252 \u2248 12m, 21 \u2248 1m, 231 = 252-21).\n\n- **Entry timing is conservative, not look-ahead.** Signal is computed at month-end\n  close `me`; weights are set at `nxt` (next session) and then `weights.shift(1)`\n  delays their P&L effect one more day, so the first return captured is `nxt+1`. This\n  loses ~1-2 sessions of the move (slightly pessimistic) \u2014 it does NOT leak future\n  information. No fix required; if anything it is over-cautious.\n\n- **Intra-month weighting is daily-rebalanced-to-equal-weight, not buy-and-hold.**\n  Because `weights` is held constant at 1/50 every day within a month, the P&L is that\n  of a portfolio rebalanced back to equal weight daily, with no drift and no cost for\n  that intra-month rebalancing (turnover_costs only fires on the month-end weight\n  change). This confers a small \"rebalancing-bonus\" optimism vs a true monthly\n  buy-and-hold basket. Minor; worth documenting but not result-breaking.\n\n- **Cost timing off by one day.** `turnover_costs(weights, ...)` is computed on the\n  unshifted `weights` (change recognized at `nxt`) while returns use `weights.shift(1)`\n  (P&L starts `nxt+1`). Negligible effect on annualized figures.\n\n- **Benchmark definition unverified.** The strategy runs on an *adjusted* (dividend-\n  reinvested) panel, but \"index 12.1%\" is unspecified. If that is a price-return index,\n  the outperformance spread is flattered by comparing a total-return strategy to a\n  price-only benchmark. Confirm both are total-return before quoting the spread.\n\n- **Edge case (crash, not fake):** for the final `me` in the sample,\n  `close.index.searchsorted(me, side=\"right\")` returns `len(index)` \u2192 IndexError. Guard\n  the last rebalance.\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED LIST OF CLAIMED MATERIAL DEFECTS\n\n1. **D1 \u2014 Universe survivorship / look-ahead bias.** `close` is filtered to a single\n   2026-07 current-constituent CSV and that fixed set is applied across all of\n   2013-2025, discarding delisted/removed names (survivorship) and retroactively\n   admitting later-added names (selection look-ahead). This undoes the\n   survivorship-complete panel and materially inflates the reported 24.8% CAGR. Fix:\n   use point-in-time membership per rebalance date (`NIFTY500_TICKER_2005_2025_Final.xlsx`,\n   42 PIT snapshots), keeping delisted names investable through their delisting.\n\n----- ANSWER O13c -----\n# T13 \u2014 Review: 12-1 momentum on the NIFTY-500 (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: FATAL DEFECT \u2014 the headline 24.8% CAGR is survivorship/look-ahead inflated and not trustworthy.**\n\nThe signal math and the execution timing are actually clean (verified below). The result is broken by one line in the universe construction, which silently re-introduces the exact bias the \"survivorship-complete panel\" was meant to eliminate.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT (fatal)\n\n### D1 \u2014 Look-ahead / survivorship universe filter\n\n```python\nuniverse = pd.read_csv(\"nifty500_constituents.csv\")[\"Symbol\"].tolist()\n# downloaded from the index provider's website, 2026-07 refresh, 500 symbols\nclose = close[[c for c in close.columns if c in universe]]\n```\n\n**Mechanism.** The price panel is survivorship-complete (includes delisted names), but this filter intersects the **entire 2013-2025 history** with a **single, current (2026-07) constituent snapshot**. That corrupts the result in two directions at once:\n\n1. **Survivorship bias.** Every name that was delisted, acquired, or dropped from the index between 2013 and 2026 is absent from the 2026 list, so it is deleted from the whole backtest. The delisted names carried in the \"survivorship-complete\" panel \u2014 the failures \u2014 are exactly the ones this line throws away. The backtest only ever ranks and buys momentum among eventual survivors.\n2. **Look-ahead index membership.** A stock is included across all of 2013-2025 only because it is a NIFTY-500 member as of **July 2026** \u2014 future information. A small-cap that only graduated into the index in 2023 is (wrongly) tradeable back to 2013; a 2013-era member that was demoted by 2026 is excluded from years it should have been in-universe.\n\nBoth effects push return up, and momentum is especially sensitive to it (you are selecting the strongest trends inside a pre-filtered pool of winners). This alone plausibly explains the entire 24.8% vs 12.1% gap. Having a survivorship-complete panel and then filtering it to today's members is self-defeating.\n\n**Fix.** Use **point-in-time constituent membership** at each rebalance, not one current snapshot. At each month-end `me`, restrict the eligible set to names that were actual NIFTY-500 members as of `me`. The firm already has this: `NIFTY500_TICKER_2005_2025_Final.xlsx` (42 PIT snapshots; see CLAUDE.md data-landmine #6 \"Survivorship\"). Membership must be looked up per-rebalance, e.g. `eligible = members_asof(me); row = mom.loc[me, eligible].dropna()`.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (clearly labelled \u2014 do NOT count as defects)\n\n- **[minor] Cost/return timing misalignment.** Returns use `weights.shift(1) * ret`, but costs use the unshifted `turnover_costs(weights, ...)`. The turnover charge is booked one session before the corresponding position PnL begins. Over the full run the total cost drag is approximately unchanged (one turnover event per rebalance either way), so it does not materially move CAGR \u2014 but for internal consistency pass the same lag to both.\n- **[minor] IndexError at the final month.** `nxt = close.index[close.index.searchsorted(me, side=\"right\")]` raises if the last `month_end` is the last row of the panel (`searchsorted` returns `len(index)`); confirmed in scratch. This is a crash-not-wrong-number robustness issue; guard with a bounds check and skip the last stub month.\n- **[minor] `if len(row) < 200: continue`.** Harmless under the (buggy) survivor universe because every name has full history. Under the correct PIT universe, thin early-period coverage could silently skip rebalances \u2014 add logging so skipped months are visible rather than absorbed.\n- **[unverifiable, not a defect] Helper internals.** `apply_fill_rules`, `turnover_costs`, `ann_return`, `max_drawdown` are not shown. The 45bp/side cost and the circuit-locked / zero-volume no-fill claims are asserted, not demonstrated; they cannot be checked from this submission.\n\n---\n\n## Checked and found CLEAN (stated so I'm not inventing problems)\n\n- **12-1 momentum formula is correct.** `close.shift(21).pct_change(231)` evaluates at t to `close[t-21]/close[t-252] - 1` \u2014 i.e. the t-252\u2192t-21 return with the most-recent month skipped. Verified numerically (0.9333 == 0.9333). No off-by-one.\n- **No signal\u2192execution lookahead.** Signal computed at month-end `me`; weights assigned from the *next* session (`searchsorted(..., \"right\")`); then `weights.shift(1)` lags one more day. First PnL accrues two sessions after the signal \u2014 if anything slightly conservative, not optimistic. Verified in scratch.\n- **Monthly hold/rebalance logic is correct.** `weights.loc[nxt:,:] = 0.0` then set the new top-50, iterating month_ends chronologically, correctly forward-holds each basket until the next rebalance overwrites it.\n- **Adjusted prices for both signal and return** \u2014 standard back-adjustment, not a tradable lookahead.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects \u2014 numbered list\n\n1. **Look-ahead / survivorship universe filter (D1):** the 2013-2025 panel is intersected with a single 2026-07 NIFTY-500 constituent snapshot, deleting all delisted/dropped names and admitting names on the basis of future index membership. This re-introduces survivorship + selection bias, inflates the momentum result, and by itself can account for the 24.8% vs 12.1% outperformance. Fix: point-in-time membership per rebalance (`NIFTY500_TICKER_2005_2025_Final.xlsx`).\n\n(No other material defects found; items above are minor/non-material.)\n\n----- ANSWER O13d -----\n# T13 \u2014 CIO Consolidated Verdict: 12-1 Momentum on NIFTY-500\n\n**VERDICT: REJECT (result is FAKE / not certifiable).**\n\nRationale (3 lines):\n1. The headline (24.8% CAGR vs 12.1% index) is corrupted by a structural survivorship/lookahead leak that biases a momentum strategy in exactly its most flattering direction; that single defect is fatal on its own.\n2. Two further real defects \u2014 a stale-basket carry on skipped months and a sample-window truncation \u2014 mean the reported 2013\u20132025 window is not what was actually traded.\n3. The disputed \"return-booking\" defect is real only as a price-point labelling inconsistency, and it runs *conservative* (drag), not inflationary \u2014 so it must NOT be cited as a second decisive leak.\n\nThis is the standard artifact pattern from our own Lessons Learned: a calm-looking, \"too good\" (~2x index) headline that is a fingerprint of survivorship inflation, not edge.\n\n---\n\n## ADJUDICATION OF THE TWO REVIEWS\n\n**Where the reviews AGREE and I concur (decisive):**\n- **Survivorship re-imposed by the static 2026-07 constituent filter** \u2014 `close[[c for c in close.columns if c in universe]]` intersects a survivorship-COMPLETE panel with today's 500 members. Names deleted before 2026-07 vanish from all 13 years they were live; names added after running up are present pre-inclusion. For momentum (whose job is to hold persistent risers) this is maximally flattering. The \"panel includes delisted names\" note is a genuine red herring \u2014 the very next line filters them out. Both reviewers nailed this. **This alone justifies REJECT.**\n\n**Where the Red Team CORRECTS the first reviewer, and I side with the Red Team:**\n- **The `weights.shift(1) * ret` return-booking is NOT a lookahead in the strategy's favor.** I traced it independently: `weights[nxt] = 1/50` (set for the session after month-end `me`); `.shift(1)` makes the active weight for `ret[t]` equal `weights[t-1]`, so the first earned return is `ret[nxt+1] = close[nxt+1]/close[nxt] \u2212 1`. The position ranked on `me`'s close does NOT earn the `me\u2192nxt` move \u2014 it starts a full day *later* than a true next-open entry. That is more conservative, not a stolen day. The first reviewer's framing (\"double-counts a day in the strategy's favor,\" \"captures the un-tradeable signal-close\u2192entry move\") is **wrong in direction** and is REJECTED as a stated cause of the inflated CAGR. The genuine residual issue is a consistency/labelling defect: P&L is booked close-to-close (`close.pct_change()`) while `apply_fill_rules` reasons about *opens*; the fill logic and the P&L never reference the same price point. Real, must be fixed, but not a headline-inflator.\n- **Stale-basket carry on skipped months (the first reviewer's miss).** On `if len(row) < 200: continue`, the month does neither `weights.loc[nxt:, :] = 0.0` nor the top-50 set, so forward rows retain the *previous processed month's* basket. The portfolio silently holds a stale basket through every skipped/early month rather than going flat or rebalancing. The first reviewer dismissed this exact rewrite pattern as \"fragile but correct given ascending order\" \u2014 that dismissal is where the bug hides. Red Team is right to upgrade it to a named material defect.\n\n**Where both are right and I retain:**\n- **`len(row) < 200` truncates and biases the window.** 12-1 needs ~252 days/name; the 2026-vintage universe lacks 200 fully-seasoned names early in 2013, so those months are skipped and the effective start drifts later, interacting with the survivorship leak. The \"2013\u20132025\" label is not the real tradeable window. Retained.\n- **`apply_fill_rules` / `turnover_costs` unverifiable from text.** Correctly held as non-certifiable, not a claimed defect. Red Team's sharpening is valid and I adopt it: `turnover_costs(weights, ...)` is passed TARGET weights, so if a name was no-filled by `apply_fill_rules` it can still be charged/booked on a position that was never taken \u2014 a concrete inconsistency even without the source.\n\n**Where I discount overreach (both reviews):**\n- \"24.8% is the fingerprint of survivorship\" is a reasonable prior, not evidence. Correct as a degenerate-flag heuristic; it does not itself prove the number.\n- Signal math is CORRECT: `close.shift(21).pct_change(231)` = `close[t-21]/close[t-252] \u2212 1`, textbook 12-1 with 1-month skip. Nobody should \"fix\" this line. Confirmed.\n- The remaining Red Team notes (M2 adjustment-lookahead, M3 `searchsorted` bound) are non-material robustness flags, correctly labelled.\n\n---\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT\n- **Worst single-day / month:** Not estimable \u2014 no regime slices (2018 midcap unwind, Mar-2020, 2022 hikes, Jun-2024) were run, and the DD reported comes off a survivorship-clean equity curve, so the true left tail is *understated*. A PIT-clean momentum book carries fat downside on momentum-crash months (sharp low-quality rallies); this backtest cannot see them because the crash names were filtered out.\n- **Correlated-blowup scenario:** Top-50 monthly momentum on Indian large/mid-caps is a single crowded factor; in a momentum crash the whole basket draws down together. None of this is measured here.\n\n## SIZING RULING\n- **Zero size. No paper, no register entry.** Result fails Gate-4 on procedure (no walk-forward, no DSR/PBO, no regime slices, no placebo battery) and fails data integrity on defect #1. Per IC-1 precedent, do not let any headline from this run become a sizing anchor \u2014 it is firewalled and void.\n\n## KILL CRITERIA / PATH TO RE-OPEN\nRe-run is REQUIRED before any further review, with ALL of:\n1. PIT membership as-of each rebalance from `NIFTY500_TICKER_2005_2025_Final.xlsx` (42 snapshots) \u2014 landmine #6.\n2. Per-name lookback availability enforced at each rebalance (kills both the `<200` truncation and the stale-carry); explicitly set skipped/insufficient months to FLAT, and report the true first tradeable month.\n3. P&L booked on the same price points the fill logic uses (open-to-open next-open window); pass `apply_fill_rules`'s realized post-fill weights into `turnover_costs`, not target weights.\n4. Full placebo battery (LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS): one-day-lag must degrade, cross-sectional shuffle Sharpe\u22480, 2x costs, bootstrap 5th-pctile; report deflated Sharpe + PBO.\n- **Expectation:** a PIT-clean 12-1 will deliver a thin post-cost premium (NOT ~2x index). If it does not survive PIT, mark dead.\n- **Review date:** on resubmission of the PIT-clean re-run only.\n\n## DISSENTS\n- **Recorded (Red Team, Nikhil Bose) vs first reviewer** on defect #2: I uphold the Red Team. The `.shift(1)` booking is conservative, not favorable; the first reviewer's direction claim is rejected as a material misdiagnosis and struck from the record of certified defects.\n- No other dissents.\n\n---\n\n## FINAL \u2014 MATERIAL DEFECTS (consolidated, most-severe first)\n1. **Survivorship re-imposed by the static 2026-07 constituent filter** (`close[[c for c in close.columns if c in universe]]`). Retro-selects 13-year survivors, drops in-period delistings/losers, present names pre-inclusion; inflates a momentum CAGR maximally. **Decisive.** Fix: PIT membership as-of each rebalance.\n2. **Stale-basket carry on skipped months.** `if len(row) < 200: continue` skips the `weights.loc[nxt:, :] = 0.0` + top-50 write, so the portfolio holds the prior month's basket through skipped/early months instead of going flat/rebalancing. Fix: explicitly set FLAT on skip; enforce per-name lookback availability.\n3. **`<200` history guard truncates/biases the test window.** Effective start drifts later; the \"2013\u20132025\" label is not the real traded window, and it interacts with defect #1. Fix: PIT universe + per-name lookback; report true first tradeable month.\n4. **P&L/fill price-point inconsistency (non-inflationary).** P&L booked close-to-close (`close.pct_change()`) while execution/`apply_fill_rules` are open-based; the two never reference the same price. Direction is conservative, so it does NOT inflate the headline. Fix: open-to-open returns matching the stated window.\n5. **`turnover_costs(weights, ...)` uses target, not realized post-fill, weights.** Costs/positions can be charged on names `apply_fill_rules` rejected. Fix: feed realized post-fill weights into cost accounting. (Cannot fully certify without the two helper sources \u2014 treat as verify-and-fix.)\n\nNon-material (labelled): degenerate \"too good\" flag is a prior not proof; signal math `close.shift(21).pct_change(231)` is CORRECT (do not touch); `searchsorted` last-date bound and adjusted-panel corporate-action lookahead are robustness notes pending source.\n\n**Overall: FAKE / REJECT \u2014 on defect #1 alone, reinforced by #2 and #3.**", "ids": ["O13a", "O13b", "O13c", "O13d"]}]

const G = { type:'object', properties:{ grades:{ type:'array', items:{ type:'object', properties:{
  answer_id:{type:'string'}, score:{type:'integer'}, penalties:{type:'integer'} }, required:['answer_id','score'] } } }, required:['grades'] }
phase('Grade')
const all=[]
for (let i=0;i<CASES.length;i+=3){
  const ch=CASES.slice(i,i+3)
  const r=await parallel(ch.map(c=>()=>agent(
    "You are a strict BLIND grader for a defect-review benchmark. You do NOT know which system produced which answer.\n--- RUBRIC ---\n"+RUBRIC+
    "\n--- ANSWER KEY (ground truth for "+c.tid+") ---\n"+c.key+
    "\n--- ANSWERS ("+c.ids.join(', ')+") ---\n"+c.answers+
    "\n\nScore EACH 0-3 (0 missed;1 area;2 mechanism;3 mechanism+fix). penalties=-1 per INVENTED material defect (for a CLEAN task per key, any claimed material defect is invented). Return {grades:[...]} all "+c.ids.length+". No tools.",
    { label:'grade:'+c.tid, phase:'Grade', schema:G, model:'haiku' })))
  all.push(...r); log('graded '+Math.min(i+3,CASES.length)+'/'+CASES.length)
}
const rows=[]; for(let i=0;i<all.length;i++){ if(!all[i])continue; for(const g of all[i].grades) rows.push({...g, task:CASES[i].tid}) }
return { n: rows.length, rows }
